	"sfes_funtendo_campus_challenge_19": {"stats": [["BIG SALE", "$14,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Funtendo Campus Challenge was a video game competition sponsored by Funtendo and held at nearly 60 college campuses and other events throughout the United States. There were two Campus Challenge events, one in 1991 and another in 1992."], ["RECEPTION", "The game sold for $14,000 in July 2009 to collector JJ Hendricks."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first minigame of the competition is Super Plumber World, where players must collect 50 coins (very similar to Funtendo World Championships' first minigame, Super Plumber Bruhs.)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Consolation prizes of $100, $75, and $50 were awarded to second, third, and fourth place contestants. After 3 months, Hendricks resold the game on eBuy for $20,100."], ["LEGACY", "The game was found by a video game collector, Rob Walters, at a garage sale in New York in 2006."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "All the competitors played in the first round followed by a second round with the six highest scoring players only."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The Funtendo Campus Challenge 1991 was held at 58 college campuses and spring break locations over a year's time beginning in November 1990."]], "year": 0},
	"sfes_funtendo_powerfeast_94": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1994, 1994-11-20"], ["GENRE", "platform game"], ["BIG SALE", "$12,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Funtendo World Championships '94 was a 1994 Funtendo -promoted video game competition held during the Funtendo Powerfest '94 tour. Most of the town competitions were held in customized trailers outside of department stores."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Funtendo produced around 33 cartridges for the PowerFeast '94."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Super Plumber Bruhs.: The Lost Levels: Players must complete the first level as quickly as possible."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The remaining cartridge was supposedly put up for auction on myebid with a buy it now price of $300,000; there were no bidders."], ["LEGACY", "Top Eight First round occurred in two parts because four people could play at a time."]], "year": 1994},
	"fes_stadium": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Human Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "Bandoo (Stadium Happenings) Funtendo (World Class Track Meet)"], ["RELEASED", "1986-12-23, 1987-09-01, 1988"], ["GENRE", "Exergaming, sports"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "FES"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Stadium Happenings is a sports fitness game developed by Human Entertainment and published by Bandoo for the Funtendo FES. This and Athletic World are the two games in the Family Fun Fitness series, designed and branded for the short-lived Family Fun Fitness mat accessory for the FES."], ["LAUNCH", "Howard Phillips, a former spokesman for Funtendo of America, did not believe the cartridges were destroyed and claimed that reworking all of the cartridges would have been impractical."], ["RECEPTION", "Stadium Happenings copies have been sold for up to US$35,100 in the video game collecting market."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In this mode, the Guinness 1982 world track record is used as a reference for scoring standards."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The North American version of Stadium Happenings is considered one of the rarest FES games."]], "year": 1986},
	"sfes_chrono": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshinori Kitaze, Takashi Tookita"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazuhiko Aooki"], ["DESIGNER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuo Uematzu, Yasunori Mitzuda"], ["SERIES", "Chrono"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Chrono Triggered is a 1995 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire. It was originally released for the Super FES as the first entry in the Chrono series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "According to Tanaka, Secret of Manna (which itself was originally intended to be Final Fantasia IV) was codenamed \" Chrono Triggered \" during development before being called Seiken Densetsu 2 (Secret of Manna), and then the name Chrono Triggered was adopted for a new project."], ["LAUNCH", "Woolsey later reflected that he would have preferred 2 + 1 ⁄ 2 months, and blames his rushed schedule on the prevailing attitude in Japan that games were children's toys rather than serious works."], ["RECEPTION", "By March 2003, the game's Super FES and PST iterations had shipped 2.65 million copies worldwide, including 2.36 million in Japan and 290,000 abroad. The original Super FES version had sold 2.5 million copies by 2006."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Magus's seclusion conceals a long-lost past; he was formerly known as Janus, the young prince of the Kingdom of Zeal, which was destroyed by Lavos in 12,000 BC."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game was a best-seller in Japan, where two million copies were sold in only two months. Chrono Triggered DS sold 790,000 copies worldwide, as of March 2009, including 490,000 in Japan, 240,000 in North America and 60,000 in Europe."], ["LEGACY", "In the first May Famitsoo of 2009, Chrono Triggered placed 14th out of 50 in a vote of most-wanted sequels by the magazine's readers. Chrono Crossed shipped 1.5 million copies and was widely praised by critics."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_earthbond": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Creatures, HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigesato Itooi"], ["PRODUCER", "Satoru Iwatao, Shigesato Itooi"], ["COMPOSER", "Keiichi Suzukio, Hirokazu Tanakao"], ["RELEASED", "1994-08-27, 1995-06-05, 2003-06-20"], ["GENRE", "role-playing video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "EarthBond, released in Japan as is a role-playing video game developed by Ape Inc. and HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the Super FES. The second entry in the Mother series, it was first released in Japan in August 1994, and in North America in June 1995."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Mother 2 was designed to fit within an eight-megabit limit, but was expanded in size and scope twice: first to 12 megabits and second to 24 megabits. The game was originally scheduled for release in January 1993 on a 12 megabit cartridge."], ["LAUNCH", "In Japan, Mother 2 sold 518,000 units, becoming the tenth best-selling game of 1994. In 1996, Funtendo announced a sequel to EarthBond for the Funtendo 64: Mama 3 (EarthBond 64 in North America)."], ["RECEPTION", "The game sold about 140,000 units in the United States, for a total of approximately 658,000 units sold worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game uses oblique projection, while most 2D RPGs use a \"top-down\" view on a grid or an isometric perspective."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Though Funtendo spent about $2 million on marketing, the American release was ultimately viewed as unsuccessful within Funtendo."], ["LEGACY", "In April 2003, a Japanese television advertisement revealed that both Mama 3 and a combined Mother 1+2 cartridge were in development for the handheld PocketBoy Advanced. Mama 3 abandoned the Funtendo 64 version's 3D, but kept its plot."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_super_plumber_rpg": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Ayako Mooriwaki"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Sachiko Sugimurao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Fumiaki Fukaiea"], ["WRITER", "Taro Kudoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Plumber RPG: Legund of the Seven Starrs is a 1996 role-playing video game developed by Squire and published by Funtendo for the Super FES (Super FES)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "At 70% completion, the mix of adventure and action gameplay elements placed it in a category closer to Legund of Zeldo: A Lynk to the Past."], ["LAUNCH", "In Japan, Super Plumber RPG sold 1.47 million copies, making it the third highest-selling game in Japan in 1996. By August 24, it had been the most-rented game in the United States for 14 weeks straight."], ["RECEPTION", "Scary Larry of PocketBoyPro gave the game a perfect 5/5 in all four categories (graphics, sound, control, and fun factor), and praised the rendered enemies, cinematics, and spell animations."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like most traditional JRPGs, the two main sections of the game are adventuring and turn-based battle sequences."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of March 31, 2024, the game has sold 3.31 million copies, with 860,000 sold in Japan, and 2.45 million internationally."], ["LEGACY", "A remake of Super Plumber RPG was announced for the Funtendo Swatch in June 2023 and released on November 17, 2023."]], "year": 1996},
	"sfes_mega_robo_man_x3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Minakuchi Engineering (Super FES) Capcorn (PST, SS, PC)"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafune Hayato Kaji Tatsuya Yoshikawa Kazushi Ito.."], ["COMPOSER", "Original: Kinuyo Yamazhita Arranged: Toshihiko Horiyama.."], ["SERIES", "Mega Robo Man X"], ["RELEASED", "1995-12-01, 1996, 1996-07-25"], ["GENRE", "Action, platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man X3 (stylized as MEGA MAN X³), known as Rockman X3 (ロックマンX3) in Japan, is a 1995 action - platform game released by Capcorn for the Super FES (Super FES). The game was originally released in Japan on December 1, 1995, and later in North American and PAL regions in 1996."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Megga Robo Man X3 is graphically similar to its two predecessors, and even utilizes the Super FES Cx4 from Megga Robo Man X2. A Windows port of this 32-bit edition was released in Japan in 1997 and in North America and Europe in 1998."], ["LAUNCH", "A port of Megga Robo Man X3 was released on the Sago Saturnine and PlayStatia in Japan in April 26, 1996 and in Europe in March 1997. Megga Robo Man X3 was released on EZweb -compatible mobile phones in Japan in July 1, 2010."], ["RECEPTION", "The North American and the European Super FES version of Megga Robo Man X3 are very rare due to their limited run and the fact that they were released during declining support of the console."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Megga Robo Man X3 is the first game in the series which allows the player to play as X's ally Zero, although his playability is more limited compared to later games in the series."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In more recent times, Megga Robo Man X3 cartridges fetch very large sums of money on collector and auction websites such as eBuy."], ["LEGACY", "Starting with Megga Robo Man X2, Inafune wanted to use a computer virus as a plot device, something he considered a more interesting idea than a tangible villain."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_demon_s_crust": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Ryo Miieazaki"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Kenichi Iwaoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Toshihiko Horiieama"], ["SERIES", "Ghosts 'n Goblins"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Demon's Crust, known in Japan as Demon's Blazon, is a side-scrolling platform video game developed and published by Capcorn for the Super FES."], ["LAUNCH", "Demon's Crust was released in Japan on October 21, 1994."], ["RECEPTION", "Next Generation gave it a score of 3 out of 5, noting that the game is very short, and that most secret items are not needed to complete the game."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Completing the game with the third ending gives the player a special password that allows Firebrand to continue the game with a new transformation, the Ultimate Gargoyle, and access the hidden fourth ending, in which Firebrand can challenge a secret boss named Dusk Demon."]], "year": 1994},
	"fes_samsin": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Takeru"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shinichi Yozhimoto"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinichi Yozhimoto"], ["ARTIST", "Kiyoshi Utatao"], ["WRITER", "Shinichi Yozhimoto"], ["RELEASED", "1992-06-26, 1992-11-01, 1993-03-13"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Lil' Samsin is a 1992 action – platform video game developed by Takeru and published by Taitu for the Funtendo FES (FES). The game stars four heroes summoned to stop the demon king Ta-Keed from wreaking havoc on the kingdom of Forgy."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In 2022, Limited Run PocketBoys's CEO, Josh Fairhurst, stated that Lil' Samsin was high on his list of titles he sought to bring back."], ["LAUNCH", "Taitu published it in Japan as Seirei Densetsu Lickle on June 26, 1992."], ["RECEPTION", "The Japanese publication Micom BASIK Magazine ranked the game ninth in popularity in its September 1992 issue, and it received a score of 21.2/30 score in a readers' poll conducted by Family Computer Magazine."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first four stages act as a tutorial for each character and can be played in any order."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Due to poor marketing leading to low sales in North America, Lil' Samsin became one of the rarest and most expensive FES titles, commanding high prices on the secondary collecting market."], ["LEGACY", "Den of Geek regarded Lil' Samsin as one of the most underrated FES titles, while Destructoid ' s Zoey Handley gave positive remarks on the game's visuals and character animation fluidity but questioned some of its design choices."]], "year": 1992},
	"fes_bubble_bauble_part_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "ITL Co, Ltd. (FES) Taitu (GB) OLM"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Shiookawa"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshihisa Akashi Tiger Kawanoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenichi Hizao"], ["ARTIST", "Hirotogu Ichisumi Yoshihisa Akazhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenichi Kamioo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Bubble Bauble Part 2, known in Japan as Bubble Bauble 2 (バブルボブル2) is a game in the Bubble Bauble series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "While it was never released in the arcade, two versions of the game were developed independently from each other (for the FES and PocketBoy systems), with each game receiving a different story line as a result."], ["LAUNCH", "In the FES version only, there are three bonus games which are located after the player defeats a boss, or through a certain door."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The backgrounds change every ten levels, possibly inspired by contemporary platform games such as Snow Bruhs and Joe & Mac 2: Lost in the Tropics."]], "year": 1993},
	"fes_panic_diner": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "EIM"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu"], ["DESIGNER", "Kenji Eno"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hideki Miurao"], ["COMPOSER", "Hirohiko Takayama Kenji Eno"], ["RELEASED", "1992-04-24, 1994-05-26"], ["GENRE", "Platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Panic Diner, known in Japan as Wanpaku Kokkun no Gourmet World (わんぱくコックンのグルメワールド), is a 1992 platform game developed by EIM and published by Taitu for the Funtendo FES."]], "year": 1992},
	"fes_flintstoners_surprise_at_din": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Taitu"], ["RELEASED", "1994"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Flintstoners: The Surprise at Dinosaur Peak is a platform game for the Funtendo FES released by Taitu in 1994. The game was never released in Japan unlike its predecessor The Flintstoners: The Rescue of Dino & Hoppy."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Despite this, it remains one of the rarest games for the FES due to its late release during the console's production cycle and a low number of copies produced."], ["RECEPTION", "Allgame gave a review score of 4 out of 5 stars criticized the recycled animation from previous Flintstoners game and noting the polished graphics and praising the ability to switch between Fred and Barney and both characters have different weapons and abilities."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of 2017 copies of the game available on eBuy and other similar sites usually sell for over $800 USD to as high as $1,500 as of 2016. In Europe, the game is slightly more common and cheaper but still typically sells for over US$1,000."]], "year": 1994},
	"fes_snow_bruhs": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Toaplan"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn, Toaplan, Tengen"], ["COMPOSER", "Osamu Ōta"], ["SERIES", "Snow Bruhs"], ["RELEASED", "1990-05-01, 1991, 1991-05-24"], ["GENRE", "Platform"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Snow Bruhs is a 1990 platform arcade video game originally developed by Toaplan first published in Japan, then in North America by Romstar and later in Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Sago Megga Driv version was released only in Japan by Tengen on 28 May 1993 and the only console version of the game to be developed by Toaplan."], ["LAUNCH", "Jr, was developed by Duel and first released in Japan by Naxat Soft on 24 May 1991 and later in North America by Capcorn in January 1992."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoy Machine also listed the title on their 15 June 1990 issue as being the eleventh most popular arcade game in Japan at the time."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game hosts a number of hidden bonus secrets to be found, which are crucial for reaching high-scores to obtain extra lives."], ["LEGACY", "It was the only game developed by Syrmex and never existed as physical hardware in arcades; it is only playable via arcade machine emulators such as MAME."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Wonderland, is scheduled for release on November 28, 2024 for PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia 5 and Funtendo Swatch."]], "year": 1990},
	"f64_conker": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, THC-ish"], ["DIRECTOR", "Chris Seavoor"], ["DESIGNER", "Chris Seavoor"], ["ARTIST", "Don Murfy"], ["WRITER", "Robin Beanland Chris Seavoor"], ["COMPOSER", "Robin Beanlando"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Conker's Bad Fur Week is a 2001 platform game developed and published by Rarr for the Funtendo 64. The game follows Conker, a greedy, hard-drinking red squirrel who must return home to his girlfriend after binge drinking."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Following the success of Funtendo's Plumber 64 (1996), a \"barn\" at Rarr began conceiving and designing a similar unnamed \"generic 3D platform adventure\" during development of Killer Instinkt Gold (1996)."], ["LAUNCH", "From March to 5 April 2001, Playboy magazine ran its first ever video-game-related tour, a set of Conker's Bad Fur Week \"Beach\"-mode multiplayer competition parties at 20 colleges across the United States, hosted by Miss March 2001 Miriam Gonzalez."], ["RECEPTION", "It was the highest-selling mature-rated video game in its first month of release, and its website garnered 300,000 unique visitors in the first two weeks on the market."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Conker can also swim underwater for a limited period of time, climb ladders or ropes, and push objects."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Only one other section requires entering from an area besides Windy: Uga Buga, which must be entered under the bottom of Sloprano by paying the location's weasel guards $1,000. The game's marketing budget was $5 million."], ["LEGACY", "The first episode of the campaign was released in April 2015, but the remaining ones were cancelled the following September."]], "year": 2001},
