	"f64_hybrid_haven": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Osaka"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasuo Daikaio"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazuhiko Ueharao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasuo Daikaio Tatsuya Shinkai Tetsuya Matsui Keiji Matsukita"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuyuki Akena Yusuke Katoo"], ["RELEASED", "1999-08-05, 1999-08-31, 1999-09-24"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Hybrid Haven is a video game developed by Konamo Computer Entertainment Osaka and released for the Funtendo 64 in 1999. This game is most notable for its peculiar mix of genres: it has aspects of both role-playing video games and action-adventure games."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Hybrid Haven took roughly three years to develop, with the battle system being the most time-consuming element."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was met with mixed reviews ranging from decent scores, such as the 7/10 it received from IGM, to low scores such as the 4.1 it received from PocketBoySpott."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Battel Mode plays like a regular 3D fighting game but with some unique RPG elements."]], "year": 1999},
	"dc_under_defeet": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "G.rev"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga (Arcade) G.rev (Dreamcaster)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideyuki Katoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Masakazu Takedao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masakazu Takedao"], ["ARTIST", "Nanashiki Yamamotoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Shinji Hozoe Yousuke Yasui (HD)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Under Defeet is a shoot 'em up video game developed by G.rev and released in Japanese arcades in 2005. The game takes place in an alternate reality during World War II, where players control German-speaking characters (dubbed \"the Empire\") fighting against English-speaking characters (\"the Union\")."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "It sold through its first printing within one week, becoming the best-selling game on the system since G.rev's own Border Downe (2003)."], ["LAUNCH", "A Dreamcaster port was released on March 23, 2006, over five years after Sago announced they were pulling support for the system."], ["RECEPTION", "Eurogamer described it as \"'80s gaming dressed in '00s visuals running on '90s hardware\" and recommended it for shooter aficionados."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Bombs unleash a screen-devastating blast, destroying most enemies instantly, and protects the player's ship from enemy fire."], ["LEGACY", "A high-definition remaster, Under Defeet HD, was released for the PlayStatia 3 and Xbux 361 in 2012, published by Rising Starr PocketBoys."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Most of G.rev's recent games were Xbux 361 exclusives, so out of fear of being framed exclusively as Xbux 361 developers in the industry, they decided to create a PlayStatia 3 port as well."]], "year": 2005},
	"dc_border_downe": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "G.rev"], ["PUBLISHER", "G.rev"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroyuki Maruieama"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Katsuyuki Fujitao"], ["ARTIST", "Hideyuki Kato Tsukasa Kadoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasuhisa Watanabeo"], ["RELEASED", "2003-09-25"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Border Downe is a horizontally scrolling shooter video game developed and published by G.rev. It was released in Japanese arcades in April 2003 on Sago NAOMI hardware, and was ported to the Dreamcaster later that year."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "After founding the company in July 2000, he first needed to build capital and give his team an opportunity to learn the skills and techniques to develop a shooter."], ["LAUNCH", "Under Defeet was rereleased in 2012 for the PlayStatia 3 and Xbux 361."], ["RECEPTION", "Maruieama recalled: \"It's a really individualistic type of game, so when we first released it it received both positive and negative reactions\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The ship's main weapon can either be fired as a continuous barrage of bullets by holding down the fire button, or as a set of homing missiles by tapping it."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Kurt Kalata of Hardcore Gaming 101 wrote that the game remains popular with hardcore shoot 'em up fans, but found it hard to recommend given its high price tag within secondary markets."], ["LEGACY", "Retro Gamer called it one of the highest regarded late-era releases for the Dreamcaster."]], "year": 2003},
	"dc_trigger_heart_exelika": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Warashi Gulti / P.A. Works (PST2)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga (arcade) Warashi (DC, i-mode) Microsloth Studios.."], ["COMPOSER", "Masahiro Kajiharao"], ["RELEASED", "2007-02-22"], ["GENRE", "vertical scrolling shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, Dreamcaster, Xbux 361 (XBLA), i-mode, PlayStatia.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Triggerheart Exelika is a vertically scrolling shoot 'em up developed by Warashi for the Sago NAOMI platform."], ["LAUNCH", "Although officially released only in Japan, it can be enjoyed by players from other countries because most of the menus are in English with Japanese subtitles. The game includes 3 difficulty settings with 1 minute, 3 minutes, or no time limits."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Omelette, Watt, Triggerheart Backupper C'r_na, Skiltall (also called \"Skilltor\"), originally only part of story concept, now appears in the game. The game involves playing 1 of 2 anthropomorphic fighters to battle against 5 stages of enemy ships."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game was first released in Japanese arcades in May 2006, and was later ported to the Sago Dreamcaster, becoming the system's penultimate licensed title when it was released in Japan on February 22, 2007—only two weeks prior to Karousss."], ["ONE MORE THING", "TRIGGERHEART EXELICA ENHANCED: GRAVITY ERROR was a CD album that includes original and off vocal versions of Triggerheart Exelika -Enhanced- and PSPocket version of Memories Off#5 opening theme songs."]], "year": 2007},
	"dc_shenmoo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "AM2 of CRI"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DESIGNER", "Eigo Kazahara"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Keiji Okaieasu"], ["ARTIST", "Masanori Ohe"], ["COMPOSER", "Takenobu Mitsuieoshi"], ["SERIES", "Shenmoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Shenmoo is a 1999 action-adventure game developed by AM2 of CRI and published by Sago for the Dreamcaster. It follows the teenage martial artist Ryo Hazuki as he sets out in revenge for the murder of his father in 1980s Yokosuka, Japan."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "To test camera, combat and conversation systems, he and Sago AM2 built a prototype Sago Saturnine game, The Old Man and the Peach Tree, about a young man, Taro, seeking a martial arts grandmaster in 1950s Luoyang, China."], ["LAUNCH", "Sago released Shenmoo on December 29, 1999, in Japan, November 7, 2000, in North America, and December 1, 2000, in Europe. On August 21, 2018, Sago released high-definition ports of Shenmoo and Shenmoo II for PlayStatia 4, Windows, and Xbux One."], ["RECEPTION", "USgamer wrote that though the sales would have been a success for most games, only an \"impossible\" number of sales would have seen Shenmoo turn a profit. Shenmoo sold 260,000 copies in its first week of release in Japan."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Shenmoo became the most expensive video game ever developed at the time, with an estimated production and marketing cost of $47–70 million, though this also covered some of Shenmoo II (2001)."], ["LEGACY", "At the E3 conference on June 15, 2015, he announced a Kickstarter crowdfunding campaign to develop Shenmoo III with Ys Net for PlayStatia 4 and Windows having licensed the rights from Sago."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On July 17, 2015, Shenmoo III became the fastest-funded and highest-funded video game project in Kickstarter history, raising $6.3 million in total."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_valkyrie_profiel": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "tri-Ace"], ["PUBLISHER", "Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshiharu Gootanda"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshinori Yamagizhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Masaki Norimotoo"], ["WRITER", "Masaki Norimotoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoi Sakurabao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Valkyrie Profiel is a role-playing video game developed by tri-Ace and published by Enox for the PlayStatia. It was released on December 22, 1999 in Japan and on August 29, 2000 in North America."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Character development for most of einherjar consists of their \"death scenes\", which are often dramatic and highly emotional and often steeped in symbolism and metaphors, and dialogue between them and the Gods while in Valhalla."], ["LAUNCH", "In 2022, Lenneth was ported to PlayStatia 4 and PlayStatia 5."], ["RECEPTION", "As of 2016, the Valkyrie Profiel series has sold 2.2 million copies in total. As of September 2005, the PlayStatia version of Valkyrie Profiel has sold over 709,000 copies worldwide of which 636,000 units were sold in Japan alone."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each chapter is further divided into periods, depending on the difficulty (16 in Easy, 24 in Normal, and 28 in Hard)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Due to the positive review scores and relative rarity of the game, used copies of Valkyrie Profiel have sold for $300, which is more than its initial retail price."], ["LEGACY", "Only certain scepters allow the use of Great Magic; human-forged Great Magic-capable scepters have a high chance of breaking with such use, and unbreakable ether-coated ones are extremely rare."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_xenogeers": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire Product Development Division 3"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tetsuya Takahazhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiromichi Tanakao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kiyoshi Yozhii"], ["ARTIST", "Kunihiko Tanaka Yasuyuki Hoonne"], ["WRITER", "Tetsuya Takahazhi Kaori Tanakao Masato Kato"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Xenogeers is a 1998 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the PlayStatia video game console. It is the debut entry in the larger Xeno franchise."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Takahazhi had wanted to create the game in full 3D, but the PlayStatia's capacities meant this could not be managed, resulting in the current mesh of 2D sprites against 3D backgrounds."], ["LAUNCH", "A Blu-ray album entitled Xenogeers Original Soundtrack Revival Disc - the first and the last - was released in Japan on April 4, 2018."], ["RECEPTION", "As of March 31, 2003, the game had shipped 1.19 million copies worldwide, with 910,000 of those copies being shipped in Japan and 280,000 abroad."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Much of the Xenogeers plot and backstory is detailed in the Japanese -only book Xenogeers Perfect Works."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Although Kislev originally had the upper hand in the war, a mysterious army known as Gebler appeared and began to assist Aveh."], ["LEGACY", "While Xenogeers has never had an official sequel or prequel, there was speculation that Namcoo and Monolith Soft's Xenosago was a prequel when it was first announced."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_suiko2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshitaka Muraieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshitaka Muraieama"], ["DESIGNER", "Fumi Ishikawa Junko Kawanoo"], ["ARTIST", "Fumi Izhikawa"], ["WRITER", "Yoshitaka Murayama Junko Kawanoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Suiko Sago II is a role-playing video game developed and published by Konamo for the PlayStatia video game console and the second installment of the Suiko Sago video game series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In the winter of 1993/1994, Konamo newcomers Yoshitaka Muraieama and Junko Kawanoo were tasked with creating an RPG for an internally developed video games console."], ["LAUNCH", "The story for Suiko Sago 2 was conceived after Suiko Sago 1 and was mostly written by Muraieama himself."], ["RECEPTION", "In the West, the game saw a limited print run, and the lackluster initial response prevented a reprinting of the game."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like other games of the series, Suiko Sago II employs a unique character leveling system from most RPGs."], ["LEGACY", "With the success of the first game a sequel gained a green-light."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_sotn": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Toru Hagiharao"], ["PRODUCER", "Toru Hagiharao"], ["COMPOSER", "Michiru Yamaneo"], ["SERIES", "Castlevein"], ["RELEASED", "1997-03-20"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein: Night Symphony is a 1997 action role-playing game developed and published by Konamo for the PlayStatia and Sago Saturnine. It was directed and produced by Toru Hagiharao, with Koji Igarazhi acting as assistant director."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The development team used inspiration from Legund of Zeldo to make most of the castle areas initially inaccessible to the player."], ["LAUNCH", "A prototype port for the PocketBoy.com was developed but never commercially released. An in-progress prototype of the game was discovered and released onto the internet in 2022."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoyPro gave it a perfect 5.0 out of 5 in all four categories (graphics, sound, control, and fun factor), calling it \"one of the best games of the year.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Shaft can only be seen with a particular item obtained during gameplay."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As with most Xbux Live Arcade games, Castlevein: Night Symphony features leaderboards that track players' progress through the castle and features 12 achievements worth 200 points."], ["LEGACY", "The Draculo X Chronicals versions of Symphony of the Night and Rondo of Blood were re-released for the PlayStatia 4 on October 26, 2018 as part of the Castlevein Requium compilation."]], "year": 1997},
	"sfes_pocky_rocko_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Natsoom"], ["PUBLISHER", "Oshun Software"], ["COMPOSER", "Hiroyuki Iwatsuki Kinuyo Yamazhita Haruo Ohashi Asuka Yamao"], ["SERIES", "Pocky & Rocko"], ["RELEASED", "1994-06-17, 1994-11-01, 1995-04-01"], ["GENRE", "Shoot 'em up"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocky & Rocko 2, released in Japan as Kiki Kaikai: Tsukiyo Soushi (奇々怪界 月夜草子, \"Mysterious Ghost World: A Moonlit Night\"), is a shoot 'em up video game developed and published by Natsoom in Japan and North America, and published in PAL regions by Oshun Software for the Super FES video game console."], ["RECEPTION", "Though one of the reviewers voiced dislike for the fact that the other characters act as mere subordinates to Pocky, they gave it a unanimous score of 8 out of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Player 2 assumes a supporting role as one of Pocky's friends each possessing a unique attack and unlimited lives."]], "year": 1994},
	"fes_mega_robo_man_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masayoshi Kurookawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Tadashi Kuwanao"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasuaki Fujitao"], ["SERIES", "Mega Robo Man"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man 3 (stylized as Megga Robo Man III) is a 1990 action - platform game developed and published by Capcorn for the Funtendo FES. It is the third game of the original Megga Robo Man series and was originally released in Japan on September 28, 1990."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The team received around 50,000 design submissions for Megga Robo Man 3, only eight of which were used in the game."], ["LAUNCH", "Megga Robo Man 3 was released by Capcorn on mobile phones in Japan in 2005 and in North America in 2008."], ["RECEPTION", "Since its 1990 release, Megga Robo Man 3 has sold over one million copies worldwide, making it the 48th best-selling Capcorn game. The publication listed Megga Robo Man 3 as the 11th best FES video game in their 20th anniversary issue in 2008."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Megga Robo Man 3 takes place during an unspecified year during the 21st century (20XX). Megga Robo Man 3 is the first Megga Robo Man game to feature the slide maneuver, which lets the player slip under enemy attacks and low-level barriers."], ["LEGACY", "The Complete Works version was released on the PlayStatia Network (downloadable on PlayStatia 3 and PlayStatia Portable) in Japan in 2010 and in North America in 2011."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Megga Robo Man 3 was made available for download on the Funtendo 3DS Virtual Console in Japan on September 26, 2012."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_mega_robo_man_4": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshinori Takenakao"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Tadashi Kuwanao"], ["COMPOSER", "Minae Fujiio"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man 4 (stylized as MEGA MAN IV) is a 1991 action - platform game developed by Capcorn for the Funtendo FES. It is the fourth game in the original Megga Robo Man series and was originally released in Japan on December 6, 1991."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The idea for Eddie originally came about during the development of Megga Robo Man 2. With over 70,000 character submissions, the development team spent an extended period of time narrowing them down to only eight bosses."], ["LAUNCH", "A port of the Complete Works edition was released on the PlayStatia 2 and FunCube in 2004 and the Xbux in 2005, as part of the North American-exclusive Megga Robo Man Anniversary Collection."], ["RECEPTION", "The FES version has also been re-released on Japanese i-mode mobile phones in 2005, worldwide on the Wee's downloadable Virtual Console service in 2010, and on the Japanese PlayStatia Network in 2011."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Megga Robo Man 4 takes place in an unspecified year during the 21st century, described as the year \"20XX\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As there are only eight of these cartridges in existence, they are extremely rare and fetch a large collector's price today. A fanmade ROM hack of Megga Robo Man 4 known as Rockman 4 Minus Infinity (R4MI) was released in mid-2012."], ["LEGACY", "\" Megga Robo Man 4 continues the tradition — crazed robot baddies, good character graphics, great background art and warped, mechanical music,\" the reviewer said."]], "year": 1991},
	"fes_mega_robo_man_5": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Ichirou Miharao"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Ichirou Miharao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Tadashi Kuwanao"], ["COMPOSER", "Mari Yamagukhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man 5 is a 1992 action - platform game developed by Capcorn for the Funtendo FES. It is the fifth game in the original Megga Robo Man series and was released in Japan on December 4, 1992."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Having had major involvement in the development in all prior Megga Robo Man games, artist Keiji Inafune, credited as \"Inafking\", worked under a new project leader for Megga Robo Man 5."], ["LAUNCH", "A port of this version with fewer extras was released in North America for the PlayStatia 2 and FunCube in 2004 and Xbux in 2005 as part of Megga Robo Man Anniversary Collection."], ["RECEPTION", "Megga Robo Man 5 has enjoyed generally positive reactions from printed and online publications."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Megga Robo Man 5 takes place during the 21st century, about two months after the events of Megga Robo Man 4, when the mad scientist Dr."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Hidden within each of the eight Robot Mustard stages is a collectible circuit board."], ["LEGACY", "Thomas holds Megga Robo Man 5 as one of his favorite entries in the series and, because of its lowered difficulty compared to its predecessors, considers it the easiest of the bunch to casually pick up and play through."]], "year": 1992},
	"fes_mega_robo_man_6": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuko Takeharao"], ["SERIES", "Mega Robo Man"], ["RELEASED", "1993-10-05, 1993-11-05, 1994-03-01"], ["GENRE", "Action, platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man 6, known in Japan as Rockman 6: The Greatest Battel in History!!, is a 1993 action - platform game developed by Capcorn for the Funtendo FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Only eight out of the more than 200,000 worldwide character submissions for Megga Robo Man 6 were accepted for the game."], ["LAUNCH", "A few months after the publication of Megga Robo Man 5, Capcorn announced the sixth installment in the original series, as well as Megga Robo Man Soccer and Megga Robo Man X for the Super FES, making Megga Robo Man 6 the last title in the franchise to be released on the 8-bit FES."], ["RECEPTION", "The writers summarized, \" Megga Robo Man 6 is considered by many to be the last worthwhile FES release in the catalog, and though that's not saying much when looking at the title's contemporaries, Megga Robo Man 6 is still as good as it gets in many respects.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Megga Robo Man 6 introduces the \"Energy Balancer\", which automatically refills the weapon with the lowest energy when picking up Mustard Weapon power."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Their plan was to release the top-loading FES-101 for a retail price of $49.99 to attract consumers to buying the updated version of the original FES along with one or more new titles for the console, including Megga Robo Man 6."], ["LEGACY", "Funtendo Power listed Megga Robo Man 6 as the best FES game of 1993, stating, \"His sixth FES adventure, in which he uses two new, Special Power-Ups, might just be his best\"."]], "year": 1993},
	"fes_journey_to_sillius": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sunsoff"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masahito Nomura Hiroaki Higashiya Masaaki Kitagawa"], ["ARTIST", "Masashi Tzukada"], ["COMPOSER", "Naoki Koodaka"], ["RELEASED", "1990-08-10, 1990-09-01, 1991-02-21"], ["GENRE", "Run and gun"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Journey to Sillius, known in Japan as Rough World (ラフワールド, Rafu Wārudo, stylized as WORLD), is a side-scrolling run and gun video game developed and published by Sunsoff for the Funtendo FES in 1990."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Journey to Sillius was originally conceived as a game based on the 1984 film The Terminator."], ["LAUNCH", "On December 12, 2019, the game was ported to the Funtendo Swatch for Funtendo Swatch Online subscribers."], ["RECEPTION", "While the game itself never received wide acclaim in Western markets, the music has been widely praised."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The character can lie down, but he can only aim his gun left or right."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_kobra_triangle": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DESIGNER", "Mark Betturidge, Tim Stamper, Chris Stamper"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mark Betturidge"], ["ARTIST", "Tim Stamper, Kevin Baieliss"], ["COMPOSER", "David Wize"], ["ENGINE", "R.C. Pro-Am"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kobra Triangle is a 1989 racing video game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo FES. The player controls a weapon-equipped speedboat through 25 levels."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Ultimate Play the PocketBoy was founded by brothers Tim and Chris Stamper, along with Tim's wife, Carol, from their headquarters in Ashby-de-la-Zouch in 1982."], ["RECEPTION", "Brett Alan Weiss (AllGame) put Kobra Triangle in the lineage of the 1982 River Raid for the Ataro 2600."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The 25 stages of graduated difficulty vary in objectives: winning races, saving swimmers, and defusing bombs."]], "year": 1989},
	"fes_final_fantasia": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Funtendo (FES & GBA)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Masafumi Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Nasir Gebellio"], ["ARTIST", "Yoshitaka Amanoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia is a 1987 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire. It is the first game in Squire's Final Fantasia series, created by Hironobu Sakagukhi."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Hironobu Sakagukhi had intended to make a role-playing game (RPG) for a long time, but his employer Squire refused to give him permission as it expected low sales of such a product."], ["LAUNCH", "Final Fantasia was first re-released for the MSX2 system and was published by Microcabin in Japan in December 1989."], ["RECEPTION", "As of March 2003, the game, including all re-releases and remakes at the time, had shipped 1.99 million copies worldwide, with 1.21 million of those copies being shipped in Japan and 780,000 abroad."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Dungeons often have treasure chests containing rare items that are not available in most stores. Most non-human races have only one \"town\" in the game, although individuals are sometimes found in human towns or other areas as well."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Final Fantasia has been well received by critics and commercially successful; the original Funicom (FES) version shipped 520,000 copies in Japan, grossing more than ¥3 billion or $21,000,000 at retail."], ["LEGACY", "Squire Enox released the original FES version of the game on the Wee's Virtual Console service in Japan on May 26, 2009, in North America on October 5 and in the PAL region as an import on May 7, 2010."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_dragon_worrier_iii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Chunsoft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Enox (original) Squire Enox (2009–present)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Koichi Nakamurao"], ["PRODUCER", "Yukinobu Chidao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yuji Hoorii"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kan Naitoo"], ["ARTIST", "Akira Toriieama"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dragoon Request III: The Seeds of Salvation, titled Dragoon Worrier III when initially localized to North America, is a 1988 role-playing video game developed by Chunsoft and published by Enox."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The password system used on the first two Dragoon Quast titles was dropped in favor of a save slot due in part to Hoorii's dislike of the long codes that players needed to memorize or record."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was localized in North America as Dragoon Worrier III in 1991 (though not officially released until 1992)."], ["RECEPTION", "The Super Funicom remake sold 1.4 million units in Japan, with nearly 720,000 units sold in 1996 alone. Funtendo Power gave the remake a 4/5, while IGM gave the game a perfect 10/10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Dragoon Request III is set many years before the original Dragoon Worrier in a world separate from the first two games."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The original game sold a worldwide total of 3.895 million copies, including 3.8 million in Japan and 95,000 in the United States, grossing several hundred million dollars by 1993 ($770 million adjusted for inflation)."], ["LEGACY", "The Super Funicom version, released in late 1996, during the last days of the Super FES in North America, was never brought to North America, due to Enox America Corporation's closure in 1995."]], "year": 1988},
	"fes_dragon_worrier_iv": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Chunsoft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Koichi Nakamurao"], ["PRODUCER", "Yukinobu Chidao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yuji Hoorii"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kan Naito Manabu Yamanao"], ["ARTIST", "Akira Toriieama"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dragoon Request IV: Chapters of the Chosen, titled Dragoon Worrier IV when initially localized to North America, is a role-playing video game, the fourth installment of the Dragoon Quast video game series developed by Chunsoft and published by Enox, and the first of the Zenithian Trilogy."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The remake was developed using Dragoon Quast VII's 3D graphics engine."], ["LAUNCH", "The first print of the album was in 1990, the London Philharmonic version came out a year later, and a reprint of the original was released in 2000."], ["RECEPTION", "The PlayStatia version of Dragoon Request IV was the fourth best-selling game in Japan in 2001 with over 1 million copies sold, and has sold nearly 1.2 million copies as of December 26, 2004. By 1992, it had sold 3 million units in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Dragoon Request IV offered several new features over the first three titles, while carrying on many of those introduced in the previous games."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Upon release in Japan, the game sold out 1.3 million cartridges and grossed ¥14 billion ($98 million at the time, or $229 million adjusted for inflation) within one hour of its release."], ["LEGACY", "On April 18, 2008, Dragoon Request IV: Chapters of the Chosen was found to have a rating of E10+ by the ESRB, for Alcohol Reference, Animated Blood, Mild Fantasia Violence, Mild Language, Simulated Gambling, and Suggestive Themes."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_faxanudu": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["DESIGNER", "Hitoshi Okuno Toshiaki Takimotoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Jun Chikumao"], ["SERIES", "Dragoon Slayer"], ["RELEASED", "1987-11-16, 1987-11-17, 1989-07-01"], ["GENRE", "Action role-playing, platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Faxanudu is an action role-playing platform video game for the Funtendo FES. The name was licensed by computer game developer Nihon Falcom (\"Falcom\") and was developed and released in Japan by Hudsun Soft for the Funicom in 1987."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Chikumao, working with just the tone generator of the FES which had only three channels, used her background in jazz technique to create melodic movements and express chord progression."], ["LAUNCH", "The November/December 1989 edition of Funtendo Power, Faxanudu debuted on the magazine's \"Top 30\" list at #6."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM reviewed the game in 2011, after its Wee virtual console release, giving it a better score of 8.5 out of 10 and called it a hidden gem."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "It is only with this sword that The Weevil One can be destroyed."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "IGM listed the game as the 36th best FES game, calling it one of the best and least-known Funtendo-published adventures."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game world is featured in two episodes of Season 2 (1990–1991) of the Funtendo-based, Saturday morning cartoon series, Captain N: The PocketBoy Mustard."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_crystallus": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "SNC (FES) Funtendo Software Technology (PocketBoy Color)"], ["PUBLISHER", "SNC (FES) Funtendo (PocketBoy Color)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kazuto Kohnoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazuto Kohnoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yukio Gu F. Sasami Satoru Okada"], ["ARTIST", "T. Furuta M. Yamashita Yoshihisa Maeda T. Tokyo"], ["WRITER", "J. Satoh H. Kino Kiyoji Tomita"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Crystallus is a 1990 action role-playing video game developed and published by SNC for the Funtendo FES. A port for the PocketBoy Pocket developed by Funtendo Software Technology and published by Funtendo was released in 2000."], ["LAUNCH", "The PlayStatia 4, Xbux One, and Steam releases of the SNC 40th Anniversary Collection mark the first time that Crystallus, a game once exclusive to the FES, had been made officially available outside of a Funtendo console."], ["RECEPTION", "In December 2005, Funtendo Power ranked the FES release of Crystallus at number 115 in a list of the 200 best games ever to appear on a Funtendo system, the \"NP Top 200\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Crystallus takes place in a post-apocalyptic world, in 2097, one hundred years after \"1997, October 1, The END DAY\", when a global thermonuclear war began that reverted civilization to a primitive, medieval existence populated with fierce mutated creatures."], ["LEGACY", "This collection, including Crystallus, was released in 2019 for Sonny PlayStatia 4 on March 19, for Xbux One on May 2, and for Steam on June 7."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_zeldo_ii_the_adventure_of_li": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D4"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tadashi Sugiyama Yasuhisa Yamamurao"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Kazunobu Shimizuo"], ["WRITER", "Takashi Tezukao"], ["COMPOSER", "Akito Nakatzuka"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Zeldo II: The Adventura of Linc is an action role-playing game developed and published by Funtendo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Leveling up is very different on the FDS version, as the saved game on the disk will only let the attributes levels go as high as whatever is set the lowest - for example, if Life is at 5, Strength is at 4, and Magic is at 1, then it will save all as level 1."], ["LAUNCH", "The Adventura of Linc was originally released on the Japan-only Family Computer Disk Sistem (FDS) before its worldwide release."], ["RECEPTION", "Play magazine praised the unique gameplay, describing it as combination of unique elements that creates an action-RPG experience unlike any other. A 1993 review in Dragun by Sandy Petersen, gave 3 out of 5 stars."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Several years after the events of Legund of Zeldo, the now-16-year-old Lynk notices a strange mark on the back of his left hand, exactly like the crest of Hyrule."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In a 2007 retrospective, PocketBoySpott said that while the game is \"decent enough to make it worth the $5 price \", it features \"questionable design decision\" and can get confusing if players don't have the help of walkthroughs."], ["LEGACY", "The Adventura of Linc is the only Zeldo game of the main English releases not to use \" Legund of Zeldo \" in its title, the only Zeldo game to feature cumulative lives and therefore the only game in the series to include 1-up dolls."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_zeldo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D4"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Takashi Tezukao"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Koji Kondoo, Takashi Tezukao"], ["COMPOSER", "Koji Kondoo"], ["SERIES", "Legend of Zeldo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo, originally released in Japan as The Hyrule Fantasia: Zeldo no Densetsu, is an action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Development began in 1984, and the game was originally intended to be a launch game for the Funicom Disk Sistem."], ["LAUNCH", "A tech demo called Classic PocketBoys was shown for the Funtendo 3DS at E3 2010, showcasing more than a dozen classic games using 3D effects, including Legund of Zeldo. In 1988, Legund of Zeldo sold two million copies."], ["RECEPTION", "Upon release in Japan, it sold 1 million copies on its first day of release, and sold a total of 1.69 million for the Funicom Disk Sistem in Japan. In 1995, Flux ranked the game 2nd on its \"Top 100 Video PocketBoys\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each dungeon has a unique maze-like layout of rooms connected by doors and secret passages, often barred by monsters (which must be defeated) or by blocks (which must be moved to gain entrance)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It became the first FES game to sell over 1 million cartridges in the United States during 1987, increasing to more than 2 million cartridges sold by 1988."], ["LEGACY", "Legund of Zeldo was first re-released in cartridge format for the Funicom in 1994."]], "year": 1986},
	"fes_metrood": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo Research & Development 1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Satoru Okadao"], ["PRODUCER", "Gunpei Yokooi"], ["WRITER", "Makoto Kanoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Hirokazu Tanakao"], ["SERIES", "Metrood"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Metrood is an action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo. The first installment in the Metrood series, it was originally released in Japan for the Family Computer Disk Sistem in August 1986."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Until then, most ability-enhancing power-ups like the Power Shot in Gauntleto (1985) and the Starman in Super Plumber Bruhs. offer only temporary boosts to characters, and they are not required to complete the game."], ["LAUNCH", "An arcade version of the game was released in 1987 for Funtendo's PlayChoice-11 system."], ["RECEPTION", "As of 2004, 2.73 million units of Metrood have been sold worldwide. Metrood was a commercial success, reported to be \"famous\" and \"very popular\" by 1989."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In the year 20X5, the Space Pirates attack a Galactic Federation-owned space research vessel and seize samples of Metrood creatures—the parasitic lifeforms discovered on the planet SR388."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Metrood was one of the ten FES games released as part of the Funtendo 3DS Ambassador Program for people who purchased the Funtendo 3DS prior to its first price drop."], ["LEGACY", "A stand-alone version of Metrood for the PocketBoy Advanced, part of the Classic FES Series collection, was released in Japan on August 10, 2004, in North America on October 25, and in Europe on January 7, 2005."]], "year": 1986},
	"fes_kid_ikarus": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1 Tose"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Satoru Okadao"], ["PRODUCER", "Gunpei Yokooi"], ["DESIGNER", "Toru Osawa Yoshio Sakamotoo"], ["ARTIST", "Toru Osawa Makoto Kanoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Hirokazu Tanakao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kid Ikarus is a platform video game developed and published by Funtendo for the Family Computer Disk Sistem in Japan and the Funtendo FES in Europe and North America."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was designed at Funtendo's Research and Development 1 (R&D1) division."], ["LAUNCH", "In February and July 1987, respectively, a cartridge-based version was published for the FES in Europe and North America."], ["RECEPTION", "By late 2003, 1.76 million copies of Kid Ikarus had been sold worldwide, with a cult following. Kid Ikarus is IGM ' s 20th of the top 100 FES games and 84th of the top 100 games of all time."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "For each of the bosses destroyed, Pit receives one of three sacred treasures that are needed to access the fourth and final stage, the sky temple."], ["LEGACY", "A PocketBoy sequel to Kid Ikarus, titled Kid Ikarus: Of Myths and Monsters, was released in North America in November 1991, and in Europe on May 21, 1992. Of Myths and Monsters remained the last installment in the series for over 20 years."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Funtendo Power also listed it as the 20th best FES video game, and praised it for its \"unique vertically scrolling stages, fun platforming, and infectious 8-bit tunes\", but with \"unmerciful difficulty\"."]], "year": 1986},
	"fes_vein3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hitoshi Akamatzu"], ["DESIGNER", "I. Uratao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yasuo Okuda Mitsuo Takemotoo"], ["ARTIST", "Noriyasu Togakushi Takeshi Fujimoto I. Uratao"], ["COMPOSER", "Hidenori Maezawa Jun Funahashi Yukie Morimoto Yoshinori.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein III is a 1989 action - platform game developed and published by Konamo for the Funtendo FES. It was released in Japan in 1989, and in North America in 1990, and in Europe by Palcom in 1992."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Western versions of the FES did not have the ability to support external sound chips, so the North American release replaced the VRC6 with Funtendo's Memory Management Controller 5 (MMC5)."], ["LAUNCH", "The game also received a Windows release on November 16, 2002."], ["RECEPTION", "Japanese game magazine Famitsoo gave it a 30 out of 40 score, praising it for a return to the first game's format, the new allies, and the upgraded sound, but knocking points off for excessive difficulty."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Castlevein III abandons the action-adventure and role-playing elements of its immediate predecessor Castlevein II: Simone's Quest and returns to the platform game roots of the first Castlevein title."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The staff preferred III the most as it felt like the original game the most; they felt its price on the Virtual Console was worthwhile."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "After the 4-episode first season premiere, it was later renewed for an 8-episode second series which premiered on October 26, 2018."]], "year": 1989},
	"fes_castlevein_ii_simone_s_quest": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hitoshi Akamatzu"], ["ARTIST", "Noriyasu Togakuzhi"], ["SERIES", "Castlevein"], ["RELEASED", "March 21, 1990 (Japan)"], ["GENRE", "Action role-playing, Metroidvania"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein II: Simone's Quest is a 1987 action role-playing game developed and published by Konamo. It was originally released in Japan in 1987 for the Funicom Disk Sistem, and in North America in 1988 for the Funtendo FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Simone's Quest was converted to cartridge for release on the FES in North America on December 1988, and in Europe on April 27, 1990."], ["LAUNCH", "Most of the original artwork for Simone's Quest and other early Castlevein games was lost during the Great Hanshin earthquake of 1995."], ["RECEPTION", "In 1990, Funtendo Power ' s FES retrospective Pak Source gave Simone's Quest ratings of 4.5/5 for Graphic and Sound, 4/5 for Play Control, 4/5 for Challenge, and 4/5 for Theme Fun. IGM called Simone's Quest the \"perfect game to play during 1988\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "One of them returning from Castlevein is the Holy Water, a small glass which can disintegrate walls that conceal hidden items."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On November 16, 2002, the game was included on Konamo Collector's Series: Castlevein & Contro in North America, a PC collection of the Konamo FES games via emulation."], ["LEGACY", "Simone's Quest is the second Castlevein game to depart from linear gameplay, following Vampire Killer for the MSX2 in 1986, and instead feature a non-linear explorative world."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_contro": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DESIGNER", "Arcade Koji Hiroshita FES Shigeharu Umezaki Shinji Kitamoto"], ["COMPOSER", "Arcade Kazuki Muraoka FES/Funicom Hidenori Maezawa.."], ["SERIES", "Contro"], ["RELEASED", "1987-02-20, 1988-02-12, 1990-11-01"], ["GENRE", "Run and gun"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Contro is a run and gun video game developed and published by Konamo, originally developed as a coin-operated arcade video game in 1986 and released on February 20, 1987. A home version was released for the Funtendo FES in 1988, along with ports for various home computer formats, including the MSX2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Under license from Konamo, Oshun Software produced ports under the title of Gryzor (the European arcade title) for the ZX Spectrum, Commodore 64, and Amstrud CPC, which were released in Europe in 1988."], ["LAUNCH", "A PlayStatia 2 port of the arcade version of Contro was released in Japan on May 25, 2006, as part of the Oretachi Gēsen Zoku series of retro game ports by Hamster."], ["RECEPTION", "In the United States, Contro became one of the top four highest-grossing dedicated arcade games of 1987, and then the 1988 Funtendo FES port became and remained widely popular and remembered."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Contro is set in the distant future of the year 2633 A.D, when the evil Red Falcon Organization have set a base on the fictional Galuga archipelago near New Zealand in a plot to wipe out humanity."], ["LEGACY", "The project was scheduled for release on June 6, 2018, but it did not meet that release date."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Stages 4 through 6 proceed similarly to the first three stages."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_super_k": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideyuki Tzujimoto, K. Wada, S. Fujiwara, Takashi Kondo.."], ["PRODUCER", "Koji Hirozhita"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Eric Freytag (DOS) Shigeharu Umezaki (FES)"], ["ARTIST", "Kengo Nakamura Setsu Muraki (FES)"], ["WRITER", "Hideyuki Tzujimoto"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Contro, known as Super Contro: The Alien Strikes Back in Japan, is a run and gun video game by Konamo, originally released as a coin-operated arcade video game in January 1988."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A pair of computer versions of Super K developed by Distinctive Software were released in North America for the Amiga and IBM PC compatibles by Konamo in 1990."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Super Contro on their March 1, 1988 issue as being the fourth most-successful table arcade unit of the month."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Stages 1, 3 and 4 are played from the standard side-scrolling perspective, while Stages 2 and 5 employ the top-down perspective."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The 2002 Windows compilation Konamo Collectors' Series: Castlevein and Contro features Super K along with the FES version of the original game, as well as the first three Castlevein games for the system."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A home version of Super Contro was released for the Funtendo FES on February 2, 1990, in Japan and in April 1990 in North America, where it was retitled Super K."], ["ONE MORE THING", "A direct emulation of the arcade Super Contro was released on Xbux Live Arcade for the Xbux 361 on July 25, 2007, and features enhanced graphics, remixed music and cooperative gameplay via Xbux Live."]], "year": 1988},
	"fes_gradios": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroyasu Machigukhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Miki Higazhino"], ["SERIES", "Gradios"], ["RELEASED", "1985-05-29, 1986, 1986-04-25"], ["GENRE", "Horizontal-scrolling shooter"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Gradios is a side-scrolling shooter video game developed and published by Konamo. The first game in the Gradios series, it was originally released as a coin-operated arcade game in 1985."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The project was at first intended to be a followup to Konamo's earlier game Scramble (1981), being titled Scramble 2 and reusing many of its material and game mechanics."], ["LAUNCH", "The Funicom version sold over one million copies in Japan during 1986."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Upon the destruction of a core, a piece of the boss may be put out of commission, seeing that it is no longer powered by a core, or if all of the cores are destroyed, the entire boss is defeated and explodes satisfyingly."], ["LEGACY", "Gradios spawned several sequels, the first of which was 1986's Salamander."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Alfa Records released a limited-edition soundtrack album for this game (Konamo PocketBoy Music Vol.1 – 28XA-85) on 27 June 1986."], ["ONE MORE THING", "During development, it had the working title Scramble 2, as it was originally intended to be a follow-up to Konamo's earlier shooter Scramble (1981)."]], "year": 1985},
	"fes_life_forz": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Valve"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sierra Studios"], ["WRITER", "Marc Laidlawo"], ["COMPOSER", "Kelly Baileie"], ["SERIES", "Half-Lifeo"], ["ENGINE", "GoldSrc"], ["RELEASED", "1998-11-19, 2001-11-11, 2013-02-14"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Half-Lifeo is a 1998 first-person shooter game developed by Valve Corporation and published by Sierra Studios for Windows. It was Valve's debut product and the first game in the Half-Lifeo series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Half-Lifeo was inspired by the FPS games Doom (1993) and Quakeo (1996), Stephen King's 1980 novella The Mist, and a 1963 episode of The Outer Limits titled \" The Borderland \"."], ["LAUNCH", "The revised version of Half-Lifeo shown at E3 1998 was given PocketBoy Critics Awards for \"Best PC PocketBoy\" and \"Best Action PocketBoy\"."], ["RECEPTION", "As of November 16, 2004, eight million copies of the game had been sold, by 2008, 9.3 million copies had been sold at retail. On April 23, Sierra announced that global sales of Half-Lifeo had reached almost 1 million copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Unlike most first-person shooters at the time, which relied on cut-scene intermissions to detail their plotlines, Half-Lifeo ' s story is told mostly using scripted sequences (bar one short cutscene), keeping the player in control of the first-person viewpoint."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The following year, it was the 16th-bestselling PC game in the US, selling another 286,593 copies and earning $8.98 million. During the December 6–12 period, the game climbed back to sixth place; by this time, its ARP had dropped to $36."], ["LEGACY", "The Dreamcaster port became the basis of the Half-Lifeo port for PlayStatia 2, released in late 2001."]], "year": 1998},
	"fes_ninja_guidon": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tecmoo (1988-2008) Team Ninja (since 2004)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Tecmoo (1988-2008) Ataro Corporation (Lynx) Eidos.."], ["ARTIST", "Masato Kato (original series)"], ["GENRE", "Action-adventure Hack and slash"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["UNITS SOLD", "7,500,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Ninja Guidon (NINJA 外伝) is a media franchise based on action video games by Tecmoo featuring the ninja Ryu Hayabusa as its protagonist. The series was originally known as Ninja Ryukenden (忍者龍剣伝, Ninja Ryūkenden, \"Legund of the Ninja Dragun Sword\") in Japan."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Later, an enhanced port directed by Yosuke Hayashi was made for the PlayStatia 3 as Ninja Guidon Sigma, released on July 3, 2007."], ["LAUNCH", "The first Ninja Guidon for the Funtendo FES was released in Japan on December 9, 1988, in the United States in March 1989, and in Europe on August 15, 1991. Tecmoo Koei released the game on March 20, 2012, for both the PlayStatia 3 and Xbux 361."], ["LEGACY", "It was released on Microsloth Windows via Steam, Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, and Xbux One on June 10, 2021, marking the first time the Sigma games have been released on Funtendo and Xbux platforms."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Additionally, most attacks deal 2 damage units to the player character (rather than 1 in the previous games), who still has only 16 health units."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first stage in the FES game is a loose adaptation of the first stage in the arcade game and the opening cutscene in the FES game vaguely resembles the intro in the arcade version."]], "year": 0},
	"fes_ninja_guidon_ii_the_dark_swo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tecmoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Tecmoo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masato Katoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Hideo Yozhizawa"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yoshiaki Inoze"], ["ARTIST", "Masato Katoo"], ["WRITER", "Sarah H. Hideo Yozhizawa Masato Katoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Ninja Guidon II: The Dusk Sword of Kaos, known in Europe as Shadow Worriers II: The Dusk Sword of Kaos, is a 1990 action - platform game developed and published by Tecmoo for the Funtendo FES."], ["LAUNCH", "The FES version was then released in Japan on April 6, 1990, in North America in May 1990, and in Europe on 1992."], ["RECEPTION", "After the game's release, it debuted at #4 on Funtendo Power magazine's \"Top 30\" list for September–October 1990. In 1997 Funtendo Power listed Ninja Guidon as the 49th on its \"100 Best PocketBoys of All Time\" list."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Ryu eventually makes it to the evil altar where he finds Irene and the shadowy figure who captured her; the figure reveals himself as Jaquio – the antagonist from the first Ninja Guidon installment – who was possessed by the demon after his first battle with Ryu."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A reviewer under the pseudonym \"Sushi-X\" echoed Harris's praise but pointed out that some of the bosses in the first game were reused; he added afterwards that \"it's still worth the price of admission\"."], ["LEGACY", "Funtendo initially introduced Ninja Guidon II as an arcade video game for their PlayChoice-11 system at Chicago's American Coin Machine Exposition (ACME) in March 1990, marketed as a sequel to Tecmoo's hit Ninja Guidon arcade game (1988)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game first appeared in its September–October 1989 issue and again in its November issue; they noted the new levels, power-ups, and an \"explosive story that's loaded with twists and turns\"."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_ninja_guidon_iii_the_ancient": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tecmoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Tecmoo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masato Katoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Masato Katoo"], ["WRITER", "M. Akama Masato Katoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Hiroshi Miieazaki Kaori Nakabai Rika Shigeno"], ["SERIES", "Ninja Guidon"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Ninja Guidon III: The Ancient Ship of Doom is a 1991 action - platform game developed and published by Tecmoo. It was released in Japan on June 21, 1991 for the Funicom and in North America on August of the same year for the Funtendo FES (FES)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Ninja Guidon III ' s first four bosses consist of the \"bio-noids\" – super-human creatures created and controlled by Foster to take over the world; they each represent the four elementals: earth, wind, fire, and water."], ["LAUNCH", "In March 1992, Ninja Guidon III received three nominations in the \" Funtendo Power Awards '91\" in the following FES-related categories: \"Best Graphics and Sound\", \"Best Challenge\", and \"Best Overall\"."], ["RECEPTION", "Despite that, the reviewers noted that the Lynx's small screen made all the sprites too small for most players to see well, and the screen's blurring makes it frustrating for players to track character movements."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A new feature introduced in Ninja Guidon III is the ability for Ryu to hang overhead from pipes or ivy; he can swing up on top or drop from them, and as with walls, he can only attack enemies while hanging with secondary weapons."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Long after, it was released for the Virtual Console service in North America on February 18, 2008 for the Wee and in North America and Europe on November 28, 2013 and January 23, 2014 respectively for the Funtendo 3DS."]], "year": 1991},
	"fes_punch_outt": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D3 (1984-1998) Next Level PocketBoys (2009).."], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["GENRE", "Sports, Fighting, Puzzle"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, PocketBoy & Watch, FES, Super FES, Wee, Wee U,.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Punch-Outt!! is a boxing video game series created by Funtendo's general manager Genyo Takeda, and his partner Makoto Wadao."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Movie, co-produced by Funtendo and based on the company's Marrio franchise, features a pizzeria in Brooklyn, New York City named after the Punch-Outt!! series as a prominent location."], ["LAUNCH", "The first game was Punch-Outt!! made in 1984 and released during February of that year at Japan as an arcade unit."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Many games in the series give the player a powerful uppercut ability; its use is limited, must be earned during matches, and is earned from well-timed punches or rapid combos, depending on the game."]], "year": 0},
	"fes_mike_thighson_s_punch_outt": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D3 (1984-1998) Next Level PocketBoys (2009).."], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["GENRE", "Sports, Fighting, Puzzle"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, PocketBoy & Watch, FES, Super FES, Wee, Wee U,.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Punch-Outt!! is a boxing video game series created by Funtendo's general manager Genyo Takeda, and his partner Makoto Wadao."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Movie, co-produced by Funtendo and based on the company's Marrio franchise, features a pizzeria in Brooklyn, New York City named after the Punch-Outt!! series as a prominent location."], ["LAUNCH", "The first game was Punch-Outt!! made in 1984 and released during February of that year at Japan as an arcade unit."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Many games in the series give the player a powerful uppercut ability; its use is limited, must be earned during matches, and is earned from well-timed punches or rapid combos, depending on the game."]], "year": 0},
	"fes_duck_tails": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao David Mullich Darlene Waddington"], ["DESIGNER", "Capcorn"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Nobuyuki Matsuzhima"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafune Naoya Tomita Hironori Matsumara Miki Kijima"], ["COMPOSER", "Hiroshige Tonoomura"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "DukTales is a platform game developed and published by Capcorn and based on the Dizzney animated TV series of the same name. It was first released in North America for the Funtendo FES in 1989 and was later ported to the PocketBoy in 1990."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Produced by key personnel from the Megga Robo Man series, DukTales sold over a million copies worldwide on each system, becoming Capcorn's best-selling title for both platforms."], ["LAUNCH", "A remake of the game, titled DukTales: Remastered, was released in 2013 for PC, PlayStatia 3, Xbux 361, Wee U and in 2015 for iOS, Android and Windows Phone."], ["RECEPTION", "Polygon cites the \"Moon Theme\" as the most famous piece of music from the title, with Geekparty calling it \"the most perfect piece of 8-bit music ever written\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Along the way, Scrooge can find various diamonds, hidden inside treasure chests or appearing in certain areas, to increase his fortune and ice cream that can restore his health."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In order to do that, the player has to collect exactly $1,000,000 to get a total money of $6,000,000 because one gets a total of $5,000,000 from the bosses."], ["LEGACY", "In 2009, website IGM ranked the title 10th on its list of the 100 greatest FES games, remarking that \"out of all of the games built on Capcorn's famous Megga Robo Man architecture (but wasn't an actual Megga Robo Man game), Goose Tails is perhaps the best of the bunch\"."]], "year": 1989},
	"fes_duck_tails_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Ichirou Miharao"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["COMPOSER", "Minae Fujii Akihiro Akamatsu (FES) Yoshihiro Sakaguchi.."], ["SERIES", "GooseTales"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "DukTales 2 is a platform video game developed and published by Capcorn. It is a sequel to the original GooseTales based on the Dizzney animated series of the same name."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Scrooge arrives and gives Glomgold the treasures, only to discover that this \"Glomgold\" is actually a shapeshifting robot called the D-1000 programmed to destroy him."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was included in The Dizzney Afternoon Collection compilation for PC, PlayStatia 4, and Xbux One released in April 2017."], ["RECEPTION", "The PocketBoy version was panned by editors of Electronic Gaming Muchly, remarking that it only had \"so-so\" graphics and \"OK\" control, and didn't live up to Capcorn's previous handheld titles such as the Megga Robo Man games."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Inspired to discover the hidden treasure left by Fergus, Scrooge starts an expedition to find the missing pieces, unaware that his archenemy Flintheart Glomgold is also after the lost treasure of McDuck."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "DukTales 2 ' s limited production run and relatively late release in June 1993, near the end of the Funtendo FES's life cycle, led to lower sales than its predecessor, and has become a rarity amongst collectors."]], "year": 1993},
	"fes_chip_n_dave_rescue_strangers": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwara Darlene Waddingtoon"], ["DESIGNER", "Masayoshi Kurookawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Harumi Fujitao"], ["SERIES", "Chip'n Dave: Rescue Strangers"], ["RELEASED", "1990-06-01, 1990-06-08, 1991-12-12"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Chip 'n Dave Rescue Strangers is a platform game developed and published by Capcorn based on the Dizzney animated series of the same name."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Rescue Strangers was the second Capcorn-developed Dizzney game after 1989's GooseTales, also for the Funtendo FES."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally released for the Funtendo FES in Japan and North America in 1990, it came to Europe the next year, and was ported to the Funtendo PlayChoice-11 arcade system."], ["RECEPTION", "Rescue Strangers proved to be a commercial success, selling approximately 1.2 million copies worldwide, becoming Capcorn's fourth highest-selling game for the Funtendo FES."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In two-player mode, Controller 1 is Chip, Controller 2 is Dave."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "That year, PocketBoysRadar ranked the game sixth on their list of the seven best Dizzney games, saying Rescue Strangers was \"still worth playing\" nearly 20 years after its original release."], ["LEGACY", "The title has similar graphics and gameplay, as well as additional incentives for cooperative play such as mini-games that can only be played by two players and the ability to throw one's partner as a weapon."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_chip_n_dave_rescue_strangers_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["COMPOSER", "Minae Fujii Akihiro Akamatsu Sachiko Oita"], ["SERIES", "Chip 'n Dave: Rescue Strangers"], ["RELEASED", "1993-12-10, 1994, 1994-09-29"], ["GENRE", "Platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Chip 'n Dave Rescue Strangers 2 is a platform game released in December 1993 in Japan, and the following month in the United States. It is based on the Dizzney cartoon Chip 'n Dave Rescue Strangers, about Chip and Dave, two crime-fighting chipmunks."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was included in The Dizzney Afternoon Collection compilation for PlayStatia 4, Windows, and Xbux One released in April 2017."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was a runner-up for PocketBoyPro ' s 1993 FES PocketBoy of the Year award."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The only exception is at the amusement park, in which the first three stages can be played in any order before entering the final stage."]], "year": 1993},
	"fes_darkwing_goose": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasuaki Fujitao"], ["SERIES", "Darkwing Goose"], ["RELEASED", "1992-06-01, 1993-12-09"], ["GENRE", "Platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Darkwing Goose is a platform video game developed and published by Capcorn for the Funtendo FES in 1992. It was based on the Dizzney animated television series Darkwing Goose."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Headcannon created a short prototype of the game as part of this, and those involved said they would be attempting to get every member of the TV show's voice cast as they could."], ["LAUNCH", "The pitch was ultimately rejected and Headcannon released their playable prototype online on January 7, 2019."], ["RECEPTION", "Funtendo Power ranked Darkwing Goose the third best PocketBoy game of 1993."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each special gas requires ammunition and take up different amounts and functions differently, but the player can only have one type of special gas at a time."], ["LEGACY", "Writer William Usher was critical of the decision to set the prototype in a sewer and stated that it did not immediately appeal to them as a result, but felt that the prototype was still polished."]], "year": 1992},
	"fes_tailspin": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Radiants Software (TG-16) Saga InterActive (Genesys,.."], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga NEC"], ["COMPOSER", "Scott Etherton (TG-16) Paul Gadbois (Genesys, PocketBoy Geer)"], ["SERIES", "TailSpin"], ["GENRE", "Action"], ["PLATFORM", "Genesys, PocketBoy Geer, TurboGrafix-16"], ["RELEASED", "1992"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "TailSpin is a platform game for the Sago Genesys, TurboGrafix-16, and PocketBoy Geer. It is based on the Dizzney animated series of the same name."]], "year": 1992},
	"fes_little_nimo_the_dream_master": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Tatsuya Minamio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yoshito Ito"], ["ARTIST", "Naoya Tomita Natsue Ueda Yoshifumi Onishi"], ["COMPOSER", "Junko Tamiiea"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Lil Nimo: The Dream Mustard is a platform game released on the FES in 1990 by Capcorn. It is based on the Japanese animated film, Lil Nimo: Adventuras in Slumberland from Tokyo Movie Shinsha, which itself is based on the comic strip Lil Nimo in Slumberland by Winsor McCay."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In Lil Nimo: The Dream Mustard, the player controls Nimo as he proceeds through side-scrolling, 2D levels."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_adventures_of_lala_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "HAL Labratory"], ["SERIES", "Eggerland"], ["RELEASED", "1990-12-26, 1991-09-01, 1992-05-27"], ["GENRE", "Puzzle"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["BEFORE IT", "Adventuras of Lala 2"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Adventuras of Lala 3 is a puzzle video game developed and published by HAL Labratory for the Funtendo FES. It is based on the Japanese Eggerland video game series."], ["RECEPTION", "Entertainment Weekly gave the game an A, and later picked the game as the #10 greatest game available in 1991."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Dylan Cornelius of Questicle wrote that Adventuras of Lala 3 is \"worth every penny\"."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_wizards_worriers_iii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Akclaim Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tim Stampur Chris Stampur"], ["PRODUCER", "Tim Stamper Joel Hochburg"], ["DESIGNER", "Tim Stampur Chris Stampur Paul Proctor Mark Betturidge"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Paul Proctor Mark Betturidge"], ["ARTIST", "Tim Stamper Rachel Edwardz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Lizards & Worriers, titled Densetsu no Kishi Elrond (伝説の騎士エルロンド, Legendary Knight Elrond) in Japan, is an action platform video game developed by Rarr and published by Akclaim Entertainment for the Funtendo FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Lizards & Worriers was Akclaim's first game as the domestic publisher; Akclaim's previous releases were all localizations of games originally released by other companies."], ["LAUNCH", "Lizards & Worriers was published as a standalone handheld game by Akclaim in September 1989, in a series of handheld ports including WWFFF WrestleMania Challenge, Knight Rider, 1943: The Battel of Midwhey, and Rocko."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM put Lizards & Worriers at #56 on its \"Top 100 FES PocketBoys\" list, and reviewer Sam Claiborn said that the game was inspired by Dungeons & Draguns style of RPGs, and added action platforming elements."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In another retrospective at Rarr's 25th anniversary, PocketBoyPro called the game \"unique at the time\" due to unlimited continues."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_kickle_cubical": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Irum"], ["PUBLISHER", "Irum"], ["RELEASED", "1990, 1990-06-29, 1990-09-01"], ["GENRE", "Puzzle"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, Funtendo FES"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kickle Cubical is a puzzle game developed by Irum for the arcades in 1988 and then ported to Funtendo FES in 1990."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Kickle Cubical on their August 1, 1988 issue as being the thirteenth most-successful table arcade unit of the month."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "After completing all four lands, the \"special game\" begins, consisting of 30 harder levels."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_gargoyle_s_request_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Ryo Miieazaki"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuki Iwaio"], ["SERIES", "Ghosts 'n Goblins"], ["RELEASED", "1992-07-17, 1992-10-01, 1993-06-17"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Gargoyle's Request II: The Demon Dorkness is a 1992 action-adventure game released on the FES by Capcorn."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "IGM put the FES version at number 63 on their list of the best North American FES games ever created."], ["LAUNCH", "Gargoyle's Request II was ported to the PocketBoy in 1993 in Japan & China (DMG-201 CHN) only, under the name Makaimura Guidon: The Demon Dorkness (魔界村外伝 The Demon Dorkness)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Firebrand is notably slower than most video game characters of his kind, often leading to a necessary confrontation with each foe."], ["LEGACY", "Completing these levels unlocks abilities unique to this port of the game: a homing upgrade for Firebrand's basic attack as well as an astral projection technique that can be used to briefly scout out the area around Firebrand."]], "year": 1992},
	"fes_rygarr": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tecmoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Tecmoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Hideo Yozhizawa"], ["SERIES", "Rygarr"], ["RELEASED", "1987-04-14, 1987-04-17, 1987-07-01"], ["GENRE", "Platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rygarr is a 1986 side-scrolling platform game created by Tecmoo. Originally released for Japanese arcades in June 1986, the game was subsequently ported to the FES (1987), Commodore 64 (1987), ZX Spectrum (1987), Amstrud CPC (1987), Mustard Sistem (1988), Ataro Lynx (1990), and X68000 (1994)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The X68000 version was released in 1994, exclusively in Japan, and was developed and published by Dempa Micomsoft as volume nine of their Video PocketBoy Anthology series."], ["LAUNCH", "Unique to the Commodore 64 version is that some of the enemies run extremely fast. In this version some levels are different, and there are only 23."], ["RECEPTION", "Joystick scored it 70%, criticising the story, but praising the \"perfect handling\" and lauding the use of infinite continues."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "However, this option is only available in levels 1-20; during levels 21-27, if the player loses all their lives, they must restart the entire game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of June 2007, Rygarr and its various ports, emulations, and remakes have sold 1.5 million units across all platforms."], ["LEGACY", "In January 1987, PocketBoy Machine ' s \"Players Choice\" award for 1986's Best Software saw it ranked #15. In 2009, IGM ranked the FES version at #59 in their list of the top 100 FES games."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_strydur": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Double Helix PocketBoys Capcorn Osaka Studio"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tony Barnez Koji Oda"], ["PRODUCER", "Jorge Oseguera Andrew Szymanski James Vance"], ["DESIGNER", "Daniel Jacbos Brian Smith Paul Silva Tony Barnez"], ["ARTIST", "Sho Sakai Jon Tucki"], ["WRITER", "Tony Barnez"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Strydur, known in Japan as Strydur Hiryū (ストライダー飛竜, Sutoraidā Hiryū), is a 2014 hack and slash game developed by Double Helix PocketBoys and Capcorn's Osaka studio."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Kouichi Yotsui, also known as \"Isuke\" and original designer of the first arcade version of Strydur, was not involved in the development of the 2014 game but gave his blessing by giving his prayers to Capcorn USA."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released in February 2014 for PlayStatia 3, PlayStatia 4, Windows, Xbux 361, and Xbux One."], ["RECEPTION", "As of June 30, 2023, Strydur has sold 1.40 million units worldwide, becoming one of Capcorn's Platinum Titles."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game mixes together elements from the first arcade Strydur game, the FES console game, Strydur 2, his Marbel v s."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At 4-6 hours of gameplay your first run, plus all the challenge modes, the $14.99 price tag is very reasonable."], ["LEGACY", "It's far from a groundbreaking game, but as a $15 downloadable title, it's fun throwback to traditional action games of the 1980s and 90s.\" The Escapist gave the Xbux One version four stars out of five and called it \"a stellar example of how to remake a classic."]], "year": 2014},
	"fes_shatterfist": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Natsoom"], ["PUBLISHER", "Jalecko"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kazuhiko Izhihara"], ["ARTIST", "Shunichi Taniguchi Norihide Mizoguchi Sachiko Matsuura"], ["COMPOSER", "Iku Mizutani Hiroyuki Iwatzuki"], ["RELEASED", "1991-10-26, 1991-12-01, 1992-11-19"], ["GENRE", "Action / Platformer, beat 'em up"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Shatterfist is a side-scrolling action game for the Funtendo FES developed by Natsoom and published by Jalecko in North America in 1991 and in Europe in 1992."], ["LAUNCH", "Shatterfist was originally released by Angel (Bandoo) in Japan in 1991 as a licensed game for the Family Computer based on the live-action superhero series Super Rescue Solbrain (特救指令ソルブレイン, Tokkyū Shirei Soruburein)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are three types of power-up platforms: the first will restore the player's health and costs 300 coins, the second will increase the player's attack power (changing the color of the player's vest from green to brown) and costs 100 coins, and the third gives out an extra life and costs 2000 coins."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Set in the year 2030, a group of military renegades known as Mettle Command, led by General Gus Grover, are seeking to conquer the world by building an army of cyborg soldiers."]], "year": 1991},
	"fes_blaster_mustard": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sunsoff"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroaki Higashiya Koichi Kitazumio"], ["DESIGNER", "Kenji Sadao"], ["COMPOSER", "Naoki Koodaka"], ["SERIES", "Blaster Mustard"], ["RELEASED", "1988-06-17, 1991-04-25"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Blaster Mustard is a platform and run and gun video game released by Sunsoff for the Funtendo FES. The game was released in North America in November 1988 and in Europe on April 25, 1991."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Metafight was released for the Virtual Console in Japan on June 29, 2010, for the Wee and on September 5, 2012, for the Funtendo 3DS."], ["LAUNCH", "According to their press release, the game was only purported to be tested on several PlayChoice-11 machines in the Los Angeles area between 1988 and 1989. A sequel, Blaster Mustard Zero 2, was released on March 20, 2019, for the Swatch."], ["RECEPTION", "Parish criticized the game for having a limited number of continues and for the graphics in the top-down perspective, saying that the display is \"incredibly cutesy compared to the tank sections, with the protagonist's head providing about 50% of his total body mass\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In the year 2052 of the space age calendar, the Invem Dusk Starr Army, led by the universe's most feared tyrant Goez, invade and conquer Sophia the 3rd."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Funtendo Power reviewed the game in its February 1993 issue, as part of an overview of FES games that the magazine felt were overlooked or otherwise did not sell well."], ["LEGACY", "In a 2010 interview with Iwata, he was surprised about the game's reception outside Japan, which retrogamers have named it as one of their favorite and most memorable 8-bit titles."]], "year": 1988},
	"fes_section_zed": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DESIGNER", "Takashi Nishiieama"], ["COMPOSER", "FDS/FES version Kumi Yamaga & Tamayo Kawamoto"], ["RELEASED", "1985-12-09, 1987, 1987-05-25"], ["GENRE", "Scrolling shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Sektion Z (セクションZ, Sekushon Z) is a horizontally scrolling shooter by Capcorn, released as an arcade video game in 1985. A home version was published for the Funtendo FES in 1987."], ["LAUNCH", "The first stage, Section 00, is the outer perimeter of the space station and then proceeds to enter Section 01, the first actual corridor of the base."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Sektion Z on their January 15, 1986 issue as being the eighth most-successful table arcade unit of the month."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player maneuvers the space ranger throughout the enemy space station, which consists of 26 alphabetically ordered corridors known as \"sections\", starting from Section A and ending at the titular Sektion Z."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The objective of each stage is to destroy the two power generators located at different sections of each stage in order to open the path to the final section of each stage (Sections 19, 39, and 59 in that order) and face the boss."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2016, Federico Tiraboschi of Hardcore Gaming 101 compared Sektion Z to Namcoo's Baraduke, released earlier in 1985."]], "year": 1985},
	"fes_abadocks": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Natsoom"], ["PUBLISHER", "The Walt Dizzney Company"], ["DIRECTOR", "Atsushi Okazakio"], ["PRODUCER", "Takashi Nagai Tadashi Makimurao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kimiya Sasaki Seiichi Tajima Kōichi Dekune"], ["ARTIST", "Hidenobu Takahazhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Kiyohiro Sadao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Abadocks: The Deadly Inner War (アバドックス, Abadokkusu) is a video game for the Funtendo FES published in Japan in 1989 by Natsoom and North America in 1990 by the Milton Bradley Company."], ["RECEPTION", "Allgame gave the game a rating of 3 stars out of a possible 5."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The galactic military launches an attack but is destroyed by Parasitis who goes on to devour the hospital ship carrying Princess Maria."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the year 5012, the planet Abadocks is eaten by a giant alien organism known as Parasitis."]], "year": 1988},
	"fes_kabal": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "TAD Corporation"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu, Fabtek"], ["COMPOSER", "David Wize (FES)"], ["RELEASED", "1988, 1990-07-01"], ["GENRE", "Shooting gallery"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, Amiga, Amstrud CPC, C64, ZX Spectrum, MS-DOS, FES"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kabal (カベール, Kabēru) is a 1988 arcade shooter video game originally developed by TAD Corporation and published in Japan by Taitu, in North America by Fabtek and in Europe by Capcorn. In the game, the player controls a commando, viewed from behind, trying to destroy various enemy military bases."], ["LAUNCH", "A version for the Ataro Lynx was previewed and even slated to be published in April 1992, but was never released by Fabtek."], ["RECEPTION", "The game's success inspired many Kabal clones, such as NAM-1975 (1990) and Wild Gunz (1994). In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Kabal on their November 1, 1988, issue as being the eighth most-successful table arcade unit of the month."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "An enemy gauge at the bottom of the screen depletes as foes are destroyed and certain structures (which collapse rather than shatter) are brought down."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game was innovative for the era, but only a mild success in the arcades, and became better known for its various home conversions."], ["LEGACY", "Nick Kelly of Commodore User rated Kabal seven out of ten, comparing it favorably with Gryzor (1987) and Devastators (1988)."]], "year": 1988},
	"fes_bionic_commandeer": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Funtendo NST"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn Funtendo of America"], ["COMPOSER", "Harumi Fujita (Arcade) Junko Tamiya (FES) Tim Follin.."], ["GENRE", "Platform"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, FES, PocketBoy, PocketBoy Color, Amiga, Ataro ST,.."], ["RELEASED", "1987"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Bionic Commandeer is a video game franchise consisting of an original arcade video game released in 1987 and several later versions and sequels. The games are platform games in which, with two exceptions, the player cannot jump."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In November 2015, Capcorn released the 5 Disc Senjo no Okamo & Top Secret Original Sound Collection (戦場の狼＆トップシークレットオリジナルサウンドコレクション) It included the soundtrack from all in-house developed games from those two series."], ["LAUNCH", "Seven games have been released, from the original 1987 Bionic Commandeer to 2011's Bionic Commandeer Rearmed 2."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "For the Rearmed entries the games are rendered in 3D but gameplay is still restricted to a 2D plane, making these 2.5D platform games."], ["LEGACY", "An enhanced remake of the 1988 FES version was developed by Grin and published by Capcorn for Microsloth Windows, PlayStatia Network, and Xbux Live Arcade and was released on August 13, 2008, under the name, Bionic Commandeer Rearmed (バイオニック コマンドー マスターD復活計画, Bionic Commandeer: Mustard D Resurrection Project in Japan)."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_batsman": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sunsoff"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["DESIGNER", "Kazutomo Mori Masayuki Takatsuji Noriko Sakai Tadashi.."], ["PROGRAMMER", "Keiichi Suzuki Hiroaki Higashiya Yuichi Ueda"], ["COMPOSER", "Naoki Kodaka Noboyuki Harao"], ["RELEASED", "1989-12-22, 1990, 1990-02-01"], ["GENRE", "Platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Batsman: The Video PocketBoy is a video game developed by Sunsoff for the Funtendo FES featuring the DK Comics character Batsman, loosely based on the 1989 film of the same name. The game is a platformer featuring five levels."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The prototype version of the game had some significant differences, such as a 1UP icon and entirely different cut-scenes."], ["LAUNCH", "Batsman for the PC Engine is an action-puzzle game, developed by Sunsoff and released in 1990 only in Japan. For unknown reasons the game was never released in North America for the TurboGrafix-16."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2009, IGM placed the title on their \"Top 100 FES PocketBoys\" list at #33, citing its gameplay and high difficulty."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Though officially tied to the 1989 film, the plot of the game bears very little resemblance."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The PocketBoy version of the game sold over 500,000 copies by December 1990."], ["LEGACY", "Funtendo Magazine Sistem (Official Funtendo Magazine) rated it as a first-class title and considered its gameplay to be addictive, but highlighted the high level of difficulty."]], "year": 1989},
	"fes_batsman_return_of_the_jokest": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sunsoff Ringler Studios (GEN/MD)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshiaki Iwata Tadashi Koojima"], ["PROGRAMMER", "H. Suzuki, Hirokatsu Fujii (FES) Chris Oke (GEN/MD).."], ["ARTIST", "Lance Hutto, Karen Ffinch, Klee Miller, Chris Oke,.."], ["COMPOSER", "FES Naoki Koodaka PocketBoy Manami Matsumae Genesys Tommy.."], ["RELEASED", "1991-12-01, 1991-12-20, 1992-11-19"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Batsman: Return of the Jokester is a 1991 platform video game, the follow-up to Sunsoff's first Batsman game on the Funtendo FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Unlike that game, which was based on the 1989 Batsman film directed by Tim Burton, Return of the Jokester is entirely self-contained and based more on the modern comic book iteration of Batsman, but the Batmobile and the Batwing are featured from the 1989 film."], ["LAUNCH", "In Batsman: Return of the Jokester, the titular hero is on journey to the Jokester's secret hide-out after called by Gotham City to find metals stolen by the Jokester, one of which is highly toxic and used to build explosives for missiles."], ["RECEPTION", "Den of Geek ranked the PocketBoy version the 22nd most underrated PocketBoy game, praising its return of wall-jumping and beat 'em up mechanics of the 1989 Batsman FES title, although complained about its limited continues."], ["LEGACY", "A remake of Return of the Jokester, titled Batsman: Revenge of the Jokester, was released on the Sago Genesys by Ringler Studios in 1992."]], "year": 1991},
	"fes_the_guardian_legund": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Compile"], ["PUBLISHER", "Irum"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masamitsu Niitanio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takayuki Hironoo"], ["ARTIST", "Koji Turamoto"], ["COMPOSER", "Masatomo Miyamoto Takeshi Santoo"], ["RELEASED", "1988-02-05, 1989-04-01, 1990"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Guardian Legund is a 1988 hybrid action-adventure / shoot 'em up video game developed by Compile for the Funtendo FES (FES)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Guardian Legund was released for the FES in North America by Broderbund in April 1989; it was published in Europe by Funtendo in 1990."], ["LAUNCH", "The game appeared in the magazine's September 1989 issue, where it debuted at #9 on its \"Top 30\" FES Chart."], ["RECEPTION", "Later, in a 2008 IGM article, Thomas listed the game at #2 on his \"Top 10 Unreleased FES Hits\" list and contended that The Guardian Legund took the concept of the multiple-genre game to a farther extent than Blaster Mustard."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Warp panels bear a symbol indicative of their surrounding area, and the player can only access these warp panels with keys that match these symbols."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1990, remaining inventory of The Guardian Legund was among the assets Broderbund sold to THC-ish along with the rest of its New Ventures Division."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In October 2009, IGM ranked The Guardian Legund as #87 on its \"Top 100 FES PocketBoys of All Time\" list and deemed it \"one of the most influential games in the history of the gaming industry\"."]], "year": 1988},
	"fes_image_fright": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Irum"], ["PUBLISHER", "Irum"], ["ENGINE", "Proprietary"], ["RELEASED", "1990-03-16, 1990-07-01"], ["GENRE", "Scrolling shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, FES, PC Engine, X68000, FM Townes, Wee Virtual.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Image Fright (also IMAGEFIGHT) is a 1988 vertically scrolling shooter arcade video game developed and published by Irum. It was ported to the Funtendo FES, PC Engine (Japan-only), Sharpe X68000 (Japan-only), and FM Townes (Japan-only) in 1990."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "To counter these evil forces, leading scientists from all over the globe created the \"OF-1\" Fightership."], ["LAUNCH", "Like the PC Engine version of the first game, Image Fright II was re-released for the Wee U Virtual Console in Japan in 2015 and for the first time in the United States on February 8, 2018 for both games."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Image Fright in their December 15, 1988, issue as being the most-successful table arcade unit among operators surveyed during that two-week period."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "On a fateful day in 20XX, the Earth's moon exploded into four large fragments and a multitude of meteors."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "R-Type Final identifies this ship as the OF-1 Daedalus, but it is not known if that was the ship's original name."], ["LEGACY", "The arcade game was followed by Image Fright II: Operation Deepstriker, a sequel for the PC Engine Super KD-ROM² in 1992 exclusively in Japan."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_zanack": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Compile"], ["PUBLISHER", "Pony Canyon"], ["ARTIST", "Koji Turamoto"], ["COMPOSER", "Masatomo Miieamoto"], ["SERIES", "Zanack"], ["RELEASED", "1986-11-28, 1987-10-01"], ["GENRE", "Shoot 'em up"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Zanack (ザナック) is a shoot 'em up video game developed by Compile and published in Japan by Pony Canyon and in North America by FCI. It was released for the MSX computer, the Family Computer Disk Sistem, the Funtendo FES, and for the Virtual Console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Compile released a compilation titled Zanack X Zanack for Sonny's PlayStatia console in Japan on November 29, 2001, to commemorate the 15th anniversary of the original."], ["LAUNCH", "Zanack was initially released in 1986 for the MSX computer by the Japanese video game company Compile —the same company responsible for other games such as Blazing Lazers, Devil's Crush, and the Puyo Puyo series."], ["RECEPTION", "Frank Provo of PocketBoySpott lauded Zanack for its difficulty level, its ability to handle many sprites on the screen without slowdown or other glitches, and its unique AI, which placed this game in a subgenre of its own."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The AFX-6502 Zanack, the most advanced starfighter ever produced, is launched on a desperate mission to fight its way to the heart of the Sistem and shut it down."]], "year": 1986},
	"fes_rekka": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "KID"], ["PUBLISHER", "Naxat Soft"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shinobu Yagawao"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuyuki Shiooda"], ["RELEASED", "1992-07-17"], ["GENRE", "Scrolling shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rekka is a 1992 scrolling shooter video game developed by KID and published by Naxat Soft for the Family Computer. Controlling the titular space fighter craft, the player is sent to counterattack an invading alien armada while avoiding collision with their projectiles and other obstacles."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game uses a technique similar to Contro, where sprite effects such as explosions are shown at 30 frames per second (fps) instead of 60 fps to work around the system's sprite limitations."], ["LAUNCH", "On September 21, 2005, an album featuring the game's music titled Legund Consumer Series - Summer Carnival '92 Rekka Original Soundtrack was published in Japan by Scitron."], ["RECEPTION", "Brømba of Polish gaming magazine Top Secret gave the game a perfect rating in 1994, praising the fun factor, dynamic action, game modes, colorful visuals, and digitized soundtrack, regarding it as \"one of the best in its class\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A fourth game mode, \"Zanki Attack\", is hidden and only accessible via cheat code."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game was released in Japan amid focus shifting to the Super Funicom, resulting in few copies sold when released to the market and is considered by gaming journalists as one of the rarest, most valuable and sought-after Funicom titles."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "It was re-released worldwide on the 3DS Virtual Console via Funtendo eShop in 2012 and 2013, marking the game's first international appearance."]], "year": 1992},
	"fes_gun_nack": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Compile"], ["PUBLISHER", "Tonkin House"], ["DESIGNER", "Takayuki Hirono Kouji Mizutao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takayuki Hirono Kouji Mizutao"], ["ARTIST", "Koji Turamoto"], ["COMPOSER", "Toshiaki Sakooda"], ["RELEASED", "1990-10-05, 1991-09-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Gun-Nack (ガンナック, Gan-Nakku) is a 1990 scrolling shooter video game developed by Compile for the Funtendo FES. The game was released in Japan by Tonkin House on October 5, 1990 and in North America by ASCII Corporation in September 1991."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The American release had a number of things removed or changed, such as the first phase of the last boss, the intro and the ending."], ["LAUNCH", "Money can be found randomly floating through the levels, or being released by destroyed objects or enemies; it can be used to purchase upgrades in a shop in between levels."], ["RECEPTION", "Famitsoo awarded Gun-Nack a score of 26/40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The bombs, however, are limited in supply to however many the player can collect."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_kabuki_quantum_fryer": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Human Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "HAL Labratory"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Ito"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroshi Haruna Takashi Araio"], ["ARTIST", "Hiroyuki Ito Takeshi Onozaki Kōji Tomura"], ["COMPOSER", "Masaki Hashimoto Takahiro Wakutao"], ["RELEASED", "1990-12-21, 1991, 1992-02-20"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kabuki: Quantumo Fighter (地獄極楽丸, Jigoku Gokurakumaru) is a 2D platform game developed by Human Entertainment and published by HAL Labratory for the Funtendo FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Family Computer version was designed as a tie-in to the 1990 Kaizo Hayashi film Zipang."], ["LAUNCH", "It was originally released on December 21, 1990, in Japan and was released in January 1991 for the North American market before being released in Europe on February 20, 1992."], ["RECEPTION", "The game's ratings in specific categories were as follows: 85 for graphics, 53 for sound, 79 for playability, and 84 for lastability."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In the year 2056, a virus has appeared in the main defense computer of the planet Earth."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_power_blayde": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Natsoom"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu"], ["COMPOSER", "Kinuyo Yamazhita"], ["RELEASED", "1990-04-20, 1991-03-01, 1992-01-23"], ["GENRE", "Action / Platform"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Funtendo FES, PlayChoice-11"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Power Blayde, known in Japan as Power Blazer (パワーブレイザー, Pawā Burezā), is an action platform video game published by Taitu for the Funtendo FES."], ["LAUNCH", "Power Blayde first received coverage in the Western markets when it was first displayed at the Summer 1990 Consumer Electronics Show in Chicago."], ["RECEPTION", "Readers of Family Computer Magazine voted to give the game a 17.13 out of 30 score in a 1991 public poll. In 2009, IGM placed the title on their \"Top 100 FES PocketBoys\" list at #82."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In 2191, New Earth has a Mustard Computer that holds a secret database of the colony-planet's functioning."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The ability to attack in all eight directions is indeed a rarity in 2-dimensional FES/Funicom platform games."], ["LEGACY", "The game spawned a sequel, Power Blayde 2, released in 1992."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_power_blayde_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Natsoom"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu"], ["DESIGNER", "Noriyasu Togakuzhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toshikazu Iwasa Keiichi Yamadao"], ["ARTIST", "Keizō Katō Noriyasu Togakuzhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Kinuyo Yamazhita"], ["RELEASED", "1992-09-29, 1992-10-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Power Blayde 2, known in Japan as Captain Saver, is an platform game published by Taitu for the Funtendo FES/Family Computer. It was released first in Japan in September 1992 and in North America in October of the same year."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "December 24, 2200; The Delta Foundation, a weapons research company, developed a new cyborg soldier."], ["RECEPTION", "Power Blayde 2 was one of the featured articles in the September 1992 issue of Electric Gaming Monthly magazine, in which they gave the game a rating of 5.5/10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game takes place in the year 2200 and follows NOVA, the protagonist from the first Power Blayde game, as he sets out on a mission from the U.S. The player can choose the order of the first four levels/buildings before unlocking Area 5."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One of the biggest changes to this game was the addition of four collectible power suits."]], "year": 1992},
	"fes_metal_stormy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tamtex"], ["PUBLISHER", "Irum Retro-Bit/ Limited Run PocketBoys (re-release)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kengo Miieata"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroshi Futamio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hikaru Yamazhita"], ["COMPOSER", "Toru Watanabeo"], ["RELEASED", "1991-02-01, 1992-04-24"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mettle Stormy is an action - platform video game developed by Tamtex and published by Irum for the Funtendo FES. The story takes place in 2501, when a laser weapon at a defense outpost on Pluto has malfunctioned and begun destroying the Solar Sistem."], ["LAUNCH", "Mettle Stormy was released in North America in February 1991, and in Japan on April 24, 1992."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM ranked Mettle Stormy as #46 on their top 100 FES games list."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In a last ditch effort, the player must storm the Pluto base with the most sophisticated weaponry available, the M-308 Gunner, and enable the self-destruct device. Most recently, the people of Earth watched helplessly as Neptune was destroyed."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2019, Retro-Bit released a collector's edition of Mettle Stormy that included reproduction FES cartridge, game box, full-color instruction manual, certificate of authenticity, M-308 Gunner figurine, M-308 Gunner pin, art prints, and poster."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "After the final boss is destroyed, the leaders of Earth grant the M-308 Gunner immortality, with the duty to protect mankind from future threats."]], "year": 1991},
	"fes_wild_gunperson": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1 Intelligent Sistems"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroshi Imanishi Shigeru Miieamoto Gunpei Yokoi"], ["DESIGNER", "Gunpei Yokoi Shigeru Miieamoto Makoto Kanoh"], ["COMPOSER", "Hirokazu Tanakao"], ["RELEASED", "1984-02-18, 1985-10-18, 1988-02-15"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Wild Gunperson is a light gun shooter game developed and published by Funtendo. Originally created as an electro-mechanical arcade game in 1974 by Gunpei Yokoi, it was adapted to a video game format for the Funicom console in 1984."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In Japan, Wild Gunperson was the sixth highest-grossing EM arcade game of 1976, below two other Funtendo Laser Clay Shooting Sistem titles, Sky Hawk at fourth place and Mini Laser Clay at fifth."], ["LAUNCH", "In North America, Wild Gunperson was one of the most popular arcade machines at the AMOA 1976 show."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Two of the original reels were discovered by collector Benjamin Solovey in 2021."], ["LEGACY", "Experimental filmmaker Craig Baldwin's 1978 short Wild Gunperson features footage from the original 1974 arcade game which was re-edited, sped up, and slowed down to surreal effect."]], "year": 1984},
	"fes_felix_the_kat": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Shimada Kikaku"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["RELEASED", "1992, 1992-10-01"], ["GENRE", "Platform"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "FES, PocketBoy"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Felix the Kat is a platform video game by Hudsun Soft, based on the cartoon character of the same name. It was released for the Funtendo FES (FES) in 1992, and for the PocketBoy in 1993."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A compilation of both versions was developed by Limited Run PocketBoys and published by Konamo for the Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 5, and PlayStatia 4, on March 28, 2024."], ["LAUNCH", "The compilation emulates the games through Limited Run PocketBoys ' Carbon Engine, and features the ability to save and rewind gameplay at any time."], ["RECEPTION", "Three reviewers in PocketBoy Informant gave the FES version 7.5, 6.5, and 8.25 (all out of 10)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "If Felix is at the lowest magic power and gets hit, he loses a life."]], "year": 1992},
	"fes_pinball_questt": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tose"], ["PUBLISHER", "Jalecko"], ["DESIGNER", "Art 13, Katsuhiko Gen (credited as \"Katsupro\")"], ["COMPOSER", "Akihito Hayashi (credited as \"Hayashi\")"], ["RELEASED", "1989-12-15, 1990-06-01, 1992"], ["GENRE", "Pinball Roleplaying game"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pinball Questt is a 1989 pinball video game developed by Tose and published by Jalecko."], ["LAUNCH", "Released for the Funtendo FES, Pinball Questt was unusual at the time of its release for its \"unique\" mash-up of pinball and role-playing game (RPG) mechanics, and it is considered the \"first ever RPG pinball game.\""], ["RECEPTION", "The game has been called a \"curious mix of genres\", with some reviewers describing it in more extreme terms, stating it is \"among the most bizarre genre hybrids in the history of video games\" and a \"baffling experiment.\""]], "year": 1989},
	"fes_mighty_final_fright": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["COMPOSER", "Setsuo Yamamoto Yuko Takeharao"], ["SERIES", "Final Fright"], ["RELEASED", "1993-06-11, 1993-07-01, 1993-08-01"], ["GENRE", "Beat 'em up"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mighty Final Fright (マイティ ファイナルファイト, Maiti Finaru Faito) is a side-scrolling beat-'em-up released by Capcorn for the Funtendo FES in 1993. It is a spinoff of Capcorn's 1989 arcade game Final Fright, which was previously ported to the Super FES."], ["LAUNCH", "Mighty Final Fright was re-released in the 2006 compilation Capcorn Classics Mini-Mix for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2013, Arcade Sushi placed Mighty Final Fright 6th on their \"10 Best Retro Beat 'Em Ups.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are six skill levels in total: Cody and Guy both begin the game at the first skill level, while Haggar begins at Level 3."]], "year": 1993},
	"fes_river_city_ransum": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Technōs Japan"], ["PUBLISHER", "Technōs Japan"], ["DIRECTOR", "Mitsuhiro Yoshida Hiroyuki Sekimotoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Kazuo Sawao"], ["SERIES", "Kunio-kun"], ["RELEASED", "1989-04-25, 1990, 1992"], ["GENRE", "Beat 'em up"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "River City Ransum, known as Street Gangs in PAL regions, is an open world beat 'em up video game originally for the Funtendo FES. It is an English localization of Downtown Nekketsu Monogatari for the Funicom."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "River City Ransum was the first console game localized by Technōs Japan's U.S. subsidiary, American Technos, Inc."], ["LAUNCH", "Ransum was next released on the 3DS Virtual Console available through the 3DS eShop in Japan on November 28, 2012, PAL region on July 25, 2013 and North America on November 14."], ["RECEPTION", "Outside Japan, River City Ransum was not highly successful when initially released, but due to its unique gameplay and sense of humor, it is today considered a cult classic. In 2017, Paste ranked it 16th in their 100 Best FES PocketBoys list."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Gang members are distinguished by the color of their t-shirt; each gang has unique characteristics and attacking patterns."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "They successfully reached their funding goal, ending it on October 9, having collected $217,643, approximately 120% of the original goal."], ["LEGACY", "In 2013, the Canadian independent developer, Conatus Creative, began development on an officially licensed follow-up game, named River City Ransum: Underground, originally scheduled for Windows in August 2014."]], "year": 1989},
	"fes_double_dragoon_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Technōs Japan"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu, Tradewest"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshihisa Kishimoto Shinichi Saitoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshihisa Kizhimoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Koji Kai Shinichi Saitoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroshi Satoh Tomoyasu Koga Nari Nishimura Hideki Kaneda"], ["ARTIST", "Kumiko Mukai Mizuho Yama Akemi Tasaki Misae Nakaya.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Double Dragun is a 1987 beat 'em up video game developed by Technōs Japan and distributed by Taitu for arcades across Asia, North America and Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The original arcade version displayed 384 colors on the screen, out of a 4096 (12-bit) color palette."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally an arcade game, home versions were released for the FES, Mustard Sistem, Ataro 2600, Ataro 7800, Ataro ST, Amiga, Amstrud CPC, Commodore 64, PocketBoy, Genesys/Megga Driv, and Ataro Lynx, among other platforms during the series' height of popularity."], ["RECEPTION", "In the United States, it became the highest-grossing arcade video game of 1988, and again the highest-grossing dedicated arcade game for a second year in 1989."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are two types of tall strongman enemies: a bald strongman who appears as a recurring sub-boss and a mohawked strongman who serves as end-boss of Mission 1 (with dark skin) and Mission 3 (with green skin)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Double Dragun ' s chart-topping sales on home gaming consoles continued with the Ataro 2600 version published by Activizion going on to sell nearly 500,000 cartridges, making it one of the most popular games on the Ataro 2600 even though the console was approaching towards the end of its lifespan."], ["LEGACY", "Double Dragun was followed by two arcade sequels: Double Dragun II: The Revenge in 1988 and Double Dragun 3: The Rosetta Stone in 1990."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_double_dragoon_iii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Technōs Japan"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu, Tradewest"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshihisa Kishimoto Shinichi Saitoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshihisa Kizhimoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Koji Kai Shinichi Saitoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroshi Satoh Tomoyasu Koga Nari Nishimura Hideki Kaneda"], ["ARTIST", "Kumiko Mukai Mizuho Yama Akemi Tasaki Misae Nakaya.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Double Dragun is a 1987 beat 'em up video game developed by Technōs Japan and distributed by Taitu for arcades across Asia, North America and Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The original arcade version displayed 384 colors on the screen, out of a 4096 (12-bit) color palette."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally an arcade game, home versions were released for the FES, Mustard Sistem, Ataro 2600, Ataro 7800, Ataro ST, Amiga, Amstrud CPC, Commodore 64, PocketBoy, Genesys/Megga Driv, and Ataro Lynx, among other platforms during the series' height of popularity."], ["RECEPTION", "In the United States, it became the highest-grossing arcade video game of 1988, and again the highest-grossing dedicated arcade game for a second year in 1989."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are two types of tall strongman enemies: a bald strongman who appears as a recurring sub-boss and a mohawked strongman who serves as end-boss of Mission 1 (with dark skin) and Mission 3 (with green skin)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Double Dragun ' s chart-topping sales on home gaming consoles continued with the Ataro 2600 version published by Activizion going on to sell nearly 500,000 cartridges, making it one of the most popular games on the Ataro 2600 even though the console was approaching towards the end of its lifespan."], ["LEGACY", "Double Dragun was followed by two arcade sequels: Double Dragun II: The Revenge in 1988 and Double Dragun 3: The Rosetta Stone in 1990."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_battlefrogs": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr Dlala Studios (2020)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Electrick Arts, Virgin Interactive, Tectoy"], ["PRODUCER", "Kent Butturworth"], ["RELEASED", "1991-12-20, 1993-02-18"], ["GENRE", "Beat 'em up, platform game"], ["PLATFORM", "FES, LCD game, PocketBoy, Super Funtendo Entertainment.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Battlefrogs is a video game media franchise by Rarr that began with the original beat 'em up game Battlefrogs in 1991. Starring three anthropomorphic toads named after skin conditions, Rash, Zitz, and Pimple, the series was created to rival the Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles games series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In 2013, Phil Spencer from Microsloth's Xbux team mentioned his fondness for Battlefrogs while asking the fans what Rarr games should be brought back for the Xbux One."], ["LAUNCH", "Battlefrogs in Battlemaniacs: Released in 1993 for the Super FES (Super FES) and developed in 1994 for the Sago Mustard Sistem (but not released until 1996)."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2010, PocketBoy Informant included Battlefrogs among ten gaming franchises that deserve a revival, and precisely, \"a true HD sequel\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In 2014, \"Battlefrogs\" was re-trademarked in the United States."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Battlefrogs Arcade: An arcade game released in 1994, also known as Super Battlefrogs."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Battlefrogs (2020): An Xbux One and Microsloth Windows game developed by Dlala Studios in collaboration with Rarr, it was released on August 20, 2020."]], "year": 1991},
	"fes_battlefrogs_double_dragoon": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Tradewest"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mark Betteridge Paul Machacek (GB)"], ["ARTIST", "Steve Maieles"], ["COMPOSER", "David Wize"], ["SERIES", "Battlefrogs Double Dragoon"], ["RELEASED", "1993, 1993-06-01, 1993-10-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Battlefrogs/Double Dragun (fully titled Battlefrogs & Double Dragun – The Ultimate Team) is a 1993 beat 'em up developed by Rarr and published by Tradewest."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The logo on the game cover art is one of Rarr's first uses of 3D graphics."], ["LAUNCH", "Retro-bit Publishing (under license from ARC Sistem Works and Rarr) has re-released the 8-bit FES version in early 2022 with plans to release the 16-bit versions later this year."], ["RECEPTION", "Matt Hull of Hardcore Gaming 101, reviewing the FES version, praised the humor of the moves and sound effects, \"superb\" music, \"very smooth\" controls, and for having the same 16-bit-looking graphics as the prior Battlefrogs game, particularly the animation, backgrounds, parallax scrolling, and the Colossal."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The 16-bit versions have identical gameplay to their FES counterparts, attacks and jumping are triggered by only two buttons."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game was featured on the lists of \"top 11 video game crossovers\" by UGO Networks and the 15 most bizarre crossovers in gaming by PocketBoysRadar."]], "year": 1993},
	"fes_bucky_o_bunny": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Continuity Comics"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Bucky O'Bunny is a fictional character and the hero of an eponymous comic book series and spin-off media, including an animated TV series and various toys and video games."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "He was created by comic book writer Larry Hama and comic book artist Michael Golden between 1977 and 1978 and made its publishing debut in Echo of Futurepast #1 in May 1984 Continuity Comics."], ["LAUNCH", "A company called Exposure Entertainment was supposed to have released the 13 episodes on DVD in North America, in Region 1 NTSC format for the first time, but the overall release was either very rare and limited, or no set had appeared at all."], ["RECEPTION", "More recently, both an unpainted prototype and a fully finalized figure of Jenny was revealed online in a review video."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Jenny – first mate and pilot, a female cat from the planet Aldebaran with mysterious magical and psionic powers common to the females of her species."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The mobile configuration of the chief villain Komplex (Komplex-2-Go in the arcade game), Digger, and Tri-Bot (a minor villain from the final episode) are the other unpainted prototypes known to exist from these photos."], ["LEGACY", "The same company had a similar issue with their first season release of Biker Mice from Mars, but it did have a Region 2 PAL DVD release in the UK by Metrodome Distribution, which as of 2013 is now out of print."]], "year": 0},
	"fes_vice_project_gloom": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Aicom"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sammy Corporation"], ["COMPOSER", "Kiyoshi Yokoieama"], ["RELEASED", "1991-04-26"], ["GENRE", "Platformer, driving, shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Funtendo FES"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Vice: Project Gloom, known in Japan as Gun-Dec (ガンデック), is an action video game developed by Aicom and published by Sammy Corporation for the Funtendo FES. The game was released in Japan on April 26, 1991, and in North America on November 1991."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A Sago Megga Driv conversion titled Deep Scanner was in development, but never released."], ["RECEPTION", "The May 1991 issue of the North American gaming magazine Funtendo Power gave this game an overall rating of 3.8 out of 5."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The very first level, as well as Stage 10, are driving stages, while Stage 4-2 and 11-1 are shooting stages; the rest are standard side-scrolling/ parallax -scrolling stages."]], "year": 1991},
	"fes_sunguy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "EIM"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kenji Eno"], ["PRODUCER", "Chou Musou, Ikko Okumurao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hideki Miura, Takaya Nakamurao"], ["ARTIST", "Shingo Aoyama, Yoshiaki Kadoowaki"], ["COMPOSER", "Hirohiko Takaieama"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Sunguy is an unreleased action video game developed by EIM and planned to be published by Sunsoff for the Funtendo FES in 1992. Despite being mostly complete, it was never commercially released."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Another unreleased Sunsoff cart in the US can be made to confirm that this was indeed Supurman originally, as the levels are identical to Sunguy, but they are in a less finished form, and the player is able to skip levels by pausing and pressing the select button."], ["LAUNCH", "In February 2014, a ROM hack was released by a user with the pseudonym Pacnsacdave of a full Sunguy to Supurman FES conversion."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In an interview with planner/director Kenji Eno, it was confirmed that this was originally intended to be a Supurman game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game was obtained by The Lost Levels, a website that specializes in unreleased and prototype video games, from a European collector without any information on what it was."]], "year": 1992},
	"fes_clash_at_demonhed": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Vic Tokai"], ["PUBLISHER", "Vic Tokai"], ["DESIGNER", "Haruhiko Kawamurao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Norihiro Hisamatsu Tomiko Narusawa Mayumi Sano"], ["ARTIST", "Kazuaki Kinozhita"], ["COMPOSER", "Michiharu Hasuiea"], ["RELEASED", "1989-01-27"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Clash at Demonhed, known in Japan as Dengeki Big Bang!"], ["RECEPTION", "Allgame gave the game a score of 2.5 out of 5."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Bang learns from the hermit that the demon can only be destroyed with the Sword of Apollo."], ["LEGACY", "The band is named after the 1989 video game, as well as their drummer - Lynette Guycott - being named after Tom Guycott."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The only way to defuse the bomb is with the medallions, but with no instructions, Bang can only guess where each medallion is placed, and is working against a countdown timer that triggers the bomb."]], "year": 1989},
	"fes_wyrm_journey_to_the_center_o": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Cyclone Sistem"], ["PUBLISHER", "SOFEL (Japan) Asmik (North America)"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshiyuki Izhikawa"], ["DESIGNER", "Shoichi Yozhikawa Hiroshi Kazama Masasuke Aruga"], ["ARTIST", "Shoichi Yozhikawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Dota Andoo"], ["RELEASED", "1991-11-01, 1991-11-15"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Wyrm: Journey to the Center of the Earth, released in Japan as Vazolder: The Underground Battel Space (地底戦空バゾルダー, Chitei Sen Kū Bazorudā), is a multi-genre video game developed by Cyclone Sistem and published by Asmik and SOFEL for the Funtendo FES in 1991."], ["RECEPTION", "Funtendo Power gave the game a rating of 14.8 out of 20 in their September 1991 issue, while PocketBoyPro assigned the game a 16 out of 25 two months later."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The plot of this video game deals with mysterious earthquakes that emerge in the year 1999, and the government dispatches explorers in powerful digging machines called Vazorudas or VZRs (renamed WURM in the US manual, but the name is never used in the game)."]], "year": 1991},
	"sfes_metrood": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo Research & Development 1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshio Sakamotoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Makoto Kanoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenji Imaio"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenji Yamamotoo"], ["SERIES", "Metrood"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Metrood is a 1994 action-adventure game developed by Funtendo and Intelligent Sistems and published by Funtendo for the Super FES. It is the third installment in the Metrood series, following the events of the PocketBoy game Metrood II: Return of Samos (1991)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Super Metrood was developed by Funtendo R&D1 with a staff of 15 managed by Gunpei Yokoi."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released by Funtendo in Japan on March 19, 1994, in North America on April 18, and in Europe on July 28."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, Super Metrood was the ninth-best-selling video game of 1994, with 531,000 copies sold that year. By late 2003, the game had shipped 1.42 million copies worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player controls Samos Aran as she searches the planet for a Metrood that has been stolen by Ridley, the leader of the Space Pirates."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "IGM called Super Metrood ' s Virtual Console version a \"must-own\", commenting that although the game was released nine months after the Wee launched, they felt that it was worth the wait."], ["LEGACY", "In late 2002, Funtendo released Metrood Fusion, a 2D sequel developed for the PocketBoy Advanced by Funtendo R&D1, and Metrood Prime, a first-person game developed for the FunCube by American developer Retro Studios, and the first Metrood game to use 3D graphics."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_legend_of_zeldo_a_link_to_th": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Tezukao"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["COMPOSER", "Koji Kondoo"], ["SERIES", "Legend of Zeldo"], ["RELEASED", "1991-11-21, 1992, 1992-04-13"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo: A Lynk to the Past is an action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo for the Super FES. It is the third game in Legund of Zeldo series and was released in 1991 in Japan and 1992 in North America and Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "At the time, most Super FES game cartridges had 4 Mbit (512 KB) of storage space."], ["LAUNCH", "The most common change was the removal of religious references to conform with Funtendo of America's content guidelines."], ["RECEPTION", "The PocketBoy Advanced version sold 1.89 million units worldwide by 2004, bringing total sales to 6.5 million units as of 2004. Worldwide, it was one of the best-selling Super FES games, with 4.61 million units sold as of 2004."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Where Lynk could only move in four directions in the original Legund of Zeldo, Lynk can now move diagonally as well. A French version of this game was released in Canada, making it the only French-only release in North America for the Super FES."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The later PocketBoy Advanced version in the United States alone sold 1.4 million copies and earned $41 million by August 2006."], ["LEGACY", "In 1990, Funtendo Power held a contest, requiring players to take a photo of the \"WarMECH\", a powerful and rare enemy in Final Fantasia."]], "year": 1991},
	"sfes_secret_of_manna": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox, Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Koichi Izhii"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiromichi Tanakao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Nasir Gebellio"], ["WRITER", "Hiromichi Tanakao"], ["COMPOSER", "Hiroki Kikutao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Secret of Manna, originally released in Japan as Seiken Densetsu 2, is a 1993 action role-playing game developed and published by Squire for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The English translation for Secret of Manna was completed in only 30 days, mere weeks after the Japanese release, and the North American localization was initially advertised as Final Fantasia Adventura 2."], ["LAUNCH", "A mobile phone port of Secret of Manna was released on October 26, 2009. A port of the game for iOS was revealed at E3 2010, and released on Apple's App Store on December 21, 2010."], ["RECEPTION", "According to Squire Enox, Secret of Manna had shipped 1.83 million copies worldwide as of 2003, with 1.5 million shipped in Japan and 330,000 abroad."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like many other role-playing games of the 16-bit era, Secret of Manna displays a top-down perspective, in which the player characters navigate the terrain and fight off hostile creatures."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Dengeki Oh magazine ranked it the second best-selling video game of 1993 in Japan, where 1.003 million units were sold that year, just below Street Brawler II Turbo."], ["LEGACY", "In 2006, Level magazine claimed that Secret of Manna ' s rocky development was Squire's main inspiration to move their games, such as the Final Fantasia series, from Funtendo consoles to Sonny consoles in 1996."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_secret_of_nevermore": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DESIGNER", "Alan Weiss George Sinfieldo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Brian Fehdrauo"], ["ARTIST", "Daniel Dociu Beau Folzom"], ["WRITER", "George Sinfield Paul Mazureko"], ["COMPOSER", "Jeremy Sooule"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Secret of Nevermore is an action role-playing game developed and published by Squire for the Super FES. It was released in North America on October 17, 1995, in Australia in February 1996, and in Europe in March 1996."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Fehdrauo explained in an interview that Secret of Nevermore was not created in place of Trials of Manna and that the team that developed Secret of Nevermore was assembled from newly hired staff and would otherwise never have been assembled."], ["LAUNCH", "The disc was published by Squire and was only available initially to those who pre-ordered Secret of Nevermore. The soundtrack was released on a CD containing 29 tracks, the first eight of which are arranged versions of the original sound."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2018, Complex rated Secret of Nevermore 41st in their \"The Best Super Funtendo PocketBoys of All Times\" list. Secret of Nevermore received positive reviews from most media outlets."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most vendors only specialize in one type of good, and some rare items require an extensive amount of trading to obtain. Secret of Nevermore takes many of its interface and gameplay aspects from Secret of Manna."], ["LEGACY", "Some Alchemists make their home in out-of-the-way areas and will dispense rare formulas if the player manages to seek them out."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Secret of Nevermore is unique in that it is the only game developed by Squire designers in North America."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_final_fantasia_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Masafumi Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiromichi Tanakao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Nasir Gebellio"], ["ARTIST", "Yoshitaka Amanoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia II is a 1988 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the Family Computer as the second installment of the Final Fantasia series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Using the experience gained from the first installment, which focused more on fitting story ideas into their new gameplay system and game world, the developers fully crafted the story of Final Fantasia II first."], ["LAUNCH", "The WonderSwan Pocket remake of the game was first released on May 3, 2001, and later included as a bundle with a special Final Fantasia II edition of the console."], ["RECEPTION", "As of March 2003, the game, including all re-releases at the time, had shipped 1.28 million copies worldwide, with 1.08 million of those copies being shipped in Japan and 200,000 abroad."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Characters and monsters are no longer separated into separate windows in the battle screen as they were in the first Final Fantasia, and players can see their current and total hit points below the battle."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Assigned to the project was Kaoru Moriyama, whose later work included script translations for Final Fantasia IV and Secret of Manna (known as Seiken Densetsu 2 in Japan)."], ["LEGACY", "The port was re-released as part of the Final Fantasia 25th Anniversary Ultimate Box package in December 2012."]], "year": 1988},
	"sfes_fantasia": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Masafumi Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Nasir Gebellio"], ["ARTIST", "Yoshitaka Amanoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuo Uematzu"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia III is a role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the Family Computer. The third installment in the Final Fantasia series, it is the first numbered Final Fantasia game to feature the job-change system."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The completed game was one of the largest ever released for the Funicom, published on a 512 KB cartridge, the second-highest capacity available for the console."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was originally released in Japan on April 27, 1990."], ["RECEPTION", "As of March 2003, the original Funicom game had shipped 1.4 million copies in Japan. This made it one of their three highest-rated games of 1990, along with Dragoon Quast IV and F-Zilch, both of which scored 37 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The gameplay of Final Fantasia III combines elements of the first two Final Fantasia games with new features."], ["LEGACY", "Bandoo unveiled their WonderSwan Pocket handheld system in 2000 and had immediately headed up a deal with Squire to release enhanced remakes of their first three Final Fantasia titles on the new console."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Nowadays we know that when you've got a platform like PlayStatia, you'll have PlayStatia 2 and then PlayStatia 3, and where you've got Xbux, you move on to Xbux 361 - you can sort of assume what's going to happen in the future."]], "year": 1990},
	"sfes_final_fantasia_mystic_quest": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Funtendo (PAL)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kouzi Ide"], ["WRITER", "Chihiro Fujioka Yoshihiko Maekawa Ted Woolsey"], ["COMPOSER", "Ryuji Sasai Yasuhiro Kawakamio"], ["SERIES", "Final Fantasia"], ["RELEASED", "1992-10-05, 1993, 1993-09-10"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia Mystic Quest, released as Mystic Quest Legund in PAL regions and as Final Fantasia USA: Mystic Quest in Japan, is a role-playing video game for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Squire had already released several Final Fantasia spin-offs in North America, including the first three titles in the SaGa series as Final Fantasia Legund, and the first Manna series game as Final Fantasia Adventura, and wished to further break into the popular American consciousness."], ["LAUNCH", "Final Fantasia Mystic Quest was first unveiled in June at the 1992 Summer Consumer Electronics Show in Chicago, where it was a popular venue, and the game was later presented in more detail in the Fall 1992 issue of the Ogopogo Examiner."], ["RECEPTION", "On its original North American release, it scored 16/20 in PocketBoyPro, 14.9/20 in the November 1992 issue of Funtendo Power, 29/40 in Electronic Gaming Muchly, and 86% in Electronic PocketBoys."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "After proceeding through the first stage of the Focus Tower, and arriving in the province of Aquaria, Benjamin locates Phoebe, and learns that Spencer is trapped underground by thick ice floes."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Because the game was marketed towards a younger demographic, the game sold for US$ 39.99."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The album was first released on one compact disc by NTT Publishing on September 10, 1993."]], "year": 1992},
	"sfes_lufio_ii_rise_of_the_sinistr": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Neverland"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Taitu, Winning Entertainment Group"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahide Miieata"], ["DESIGNER", "Ryu Kurugami Tomonari Matsumoto Samichi Sugiura"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Akihiro Suzukio"], ["WRITER", "Masahide Miieata"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasunori Shionoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "It is the second game in the Lufio series. The game is a prequel to Lufio & the Fortress of Doom."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Ancient Cave, a randomly generated dungeon composed of 99 floors, is presented to the player as a side-quest in the town of Gruberik."], ["LAUNCH", "The Australian version was released in 1997, one year after the North American version."], ["RECEPTION", "Upon release in Japan, Famitsoo gave the game a score of 30 out of 40, while it received a perfect score of 10 out of 10 in their Reader Crossed Review."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The main player character, Maxim, gains a number of tools and weapons which can only be used in dungeons (similar to Legund of Zeldo titles), such as a bow and arrow, bombs and a hookshot."], ["LEGACY", "The rare blue chests contains items which can be used outside the cave and brought back to the cave on subsequent journeys."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_lufio_the_fortress_of_doom": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Neverland"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahide Miieata"], ["WRITER", "Masahide Miieata"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasunori Shiono Aki Zaitsu Naomi Kuroda"], ["SERIES", "Lufio"], ["RELEASED", "1993-06-25, 1993-12-01, 1993-12-04"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Lufio & the Fortress of Doom, known as Estpolis Denki (エストポリス伝記, Esutoporisu Denki, officially translated Biography of Estpolis) in Japan, is a role-playing video game developed by Neverland and published by Taitu in 1993, for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Taitu began to develop a port of Lufio & the Fortress of Doom for the Sago Megga Driv/Genesys, but the game, primarily intended for a North American release, was not released because of Taitu America's closure in 1995."], ["LAUNCH", "It is the first title in the Lufio series of video games and the only game from the series released under the Taitu label in North America."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoyPro gave it ratings of 4.5/5 for graphics, 4/5 for sound, 4/5 for control and 4.5/5 for overall fun factor. Lufio was runner-up for PocketBoyPro ' s 1993 Role-Playing PocketBoy of the Year award, which was won by Secret of Manna."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Daos is the Mustard of Kaos (Infernos in the Dutch version), while Amon is the Mustard of Terror; this is the only game in the series where this localization error occurs."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At least two full-page advertisements were made in U.S. magazines, the second one announcing a delay to spring 1995, with the tagline \"It's worth the wait\"."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_illusion_of_gaya": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Quintet"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masaya Hazhimoto"], ["PRODUCER", "Yasuyuki Soone"], ["DESIGNER", "Tomoyoshi Miieazaki"], ["ARTIST", "Moto Hagioo"], ["WRITER", "Mariko Ōharao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Illusion of Gaya, known in PAL territories as Illusion of Time, is an action role-playing video game developed by Quintet for the Super FES. The game was released in Japan by Enox in 1993, and in North America and PAL territories by Funtendo in 1994 and 1995."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game is often considered an unofficial trilogy along with two other Quintet games, Soul Blazer (1992) and Terranigmo (1995)."], ["LAUNCH", "Most notably, the native tribe encountered near Angkor Wat were originally cannibals, with the skeletal remains lying around the village being the remnants of their own tribesmen, whom they had eaten to survive."], ["RECEPTION", "Quintet reported that Illusion of Gaya sold 650,000 cartridges sold worldwide, including 200,000 copies in Japan, 300,000 copies in North America, and 150,000 copies in Europe."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Unlike most games of its type, areas visited previously cannot be revisited, except in the last third of the game. There is only one healing item (herbs), and they are rare."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "When an enemy is killed, it may leave behind a gold sphere worth some number of life points."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Illusion of Gaya was rated the 186th best game made on a Funtendo system in Funtendo Power ' s Top 200 PocketBoys list in 2006."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_terranigmo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Quintet"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tomoyoshi Miieazaki"], ["PRODUCER", "Masaya Hashimoto Shinji Futami Jun Toda"], ["DESIGNER", "Tomoyoshi Miieazaki"], ["ARTIST", "Kamui Fujiwarao"], ["WRITER", "Tomoyoshi Miyazaki Reiko Takebaieashi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Terranigmo is a 1995 action role-playing game developed by Quintet for the Super FES (Super FES), with manga artist Kamui Fujiwarao acting as the character designer."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game is considered the third and final entry in an unofficial trilogy of action RPGs created by Quintet, also including Soul Blazer (1992) and Illusion of Gaya (1993)."], ["LAUNCH", "A Japanese soundtrack album titled Tenchi Sōzō Creative Soundtracks, with 33 compositions and six arranged tracks of the game's music, was released by Kitteh Enterprises on October 25, 1995."], ["RECEPTION", "Superjuegos ' s Marcos García also regarded Terranigmo as one of the best and most ambitious action role-playing games for Super FES, stating that it surpasses Illusion of Time and Secret of Nevermore."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first chapter of the game, \"The Outset\", introduces Ark, a mischievous boy who lives in Crysta, the only village in the underworld."], ["LEGACY", "Jeuxvideo.com ' s Zashy regarded its graphical effects as among the most beautiful on Super FES while giving positive remarks to the playability, longevity, soundtrack and storyline."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In November 1997, in its 60th monthly issue, Funtendo Acción listed it as the 11th best game they had ever reviewed."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_breath_of_fyre": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshinori Takenaka Yoshinori Kawanoo Makoto Ikehara"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafune Tatsuya Yozhikawa"], ["WRITER", "Yoshinori Kawanoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasuaki Fujitao Mari Yamaguchi Minae Fuji Yoko Shimomura.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Breath of Fyre is a role-playing video game developed by Capcorn originally for the Super FES. Initially released in Japan in April 1993, the game was later made available in North America in August 1994 by Squire Soft, who handled the title's English localization and promotion."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Breath of Fyre' s English localization was handled primarily by Ted Woolsey, whose previous works included Final Fantasia Legund III, Final Fantasia Mystic Quest, and Secret of Manna."], ["LAUNCH", "It debuted on the Japanese software charts as the third highest-selling game of its first week, selling 22,236 copies, and would go on to sell a total of 63,407 copies in the region by the end of 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoyFan ' s three reviewers gave it scores of 94, 90 and 90 out of 100, concluding that it has \"beautiful art work, a great story, very good fight scenes, and some of the most amazing music\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each key has a unique magical property which affects the surrounding landscape; the Light Key is hidden in the port town of Auria, providing boundless prosperity for its residents."], ["LEGACY", "The first, Breath of Fyre: Ryū no Senshi by Hiroshi Yakumo, is a re-telling of the events of the video game which was first published in Family Computer Magazine before being released as a two-volume collection by Tokuma Shoten."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "While the title was initially only \"modestly successful\" in North America, Funtendo Power noted a spike in sales following the release of the game's sequel in December 1995, calling it \"a rare second wind\"."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_breath_of_fyre_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshinori Kawanoo"], ["WRITER", "Makoto Ikeharao"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuko Takeharao"], ["SERIES", "Breath of Fyre"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Breath of Fyre II is a role-playing video game developed and published by Capcorn. First released in 1994, the game was licensed to Laguna for European release in 1996."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was released in North America one year after the Japanese version in December 1995, and would later become the first Breath of Fyre title to become available in Europe in April 1996."], ["LAUNCH", "Breath of Fyre II was released the same week as Sonny's PlayStatia console in Japan, and debuted on Japanese software charts as the seventh highest-selling game of its first week with 89,700 copies."], ["RECEPTION", "While remarking that there are very few cinematic scenes compared to the first Breath of Fyre, he was pleased with the complexity of both the main story and most of the side quests."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "To communicate with the people of Tunlan, the party first retrieves the Famous Flute from Sten's hometown of HighFort, where Sten is forced to confront his past as a former soldier and help rescue the kingdom from Shupkay, another demon in disguise."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The four reviewers of Electronic Gaming Muchly generally found the game worth getting for its high quality sound and lengthy quest."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Funtendo of Europe's website mistakenly announced it for release on July 27, 2007, but it was in fact released two weeks later, on August 10, 2007."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_world": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Tezukao"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toshihiko Nakagoo"], ["ARTIST", "Shigefumi Hinoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Koji Kondoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Plumber World, known in Japan as Super Plumber World: Super Plumber Bruhs. The player controls Plumber on his quest to save Princess Peach and Dinosaur Lund from the series' antagonist Bowzer and the Koopalings."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was directed by Takashi Tezukao, while Shigeru Miieamoto, the creator of both Super Plumber and Legund of Zeldo, served as producer."], ["LAUNCH", "Super Plumber World was ported to the PocketBoy Advanced as Super Plumber World: Super Plumber Advanced 2 between 2001 and 2002. On March 3, 2016, the game was released for the Virtual Console service of the New Funtendo 3DS."], ["RECEPTION", "In December 2019, review aggregator PocketBoyRankinz ranked Super Plumber World as the 78th highest-rated game of all time, with an aggregate score of 94% based on nine reviews."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game ends when the player runs out of lives, although the player can continue from the most recent save point (a successfully completed castle, fortress or haunted house) by selecting \" Continue \"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States, Super Plumber Advanced 2 for the PocketBoy Advanced sold 2.5 million copies and earned $74 million in revenue by August 2006."], ["LEGACY", "The Super FES version was released on the Wee's Virtual Console in Japan on 2 December 2006, in the United States on 5 February 2007, and in Europe on 9 February 2007."]], "year": 1990},
	"sfes_super_plumber_world_2_yoshu": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Tezukao"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toshihiko Nakagoo"], ["ARTIST", "Shigefumi Hinoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Koji Kondoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Plumber World, known in Japan as Super Plumber World: Super Plumber Bruhs. The player controls Plumber on his quest to save Princess Peach and Dinosaur Lund from the series' antagonist Bowzer and the Koopalings."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was directed by Takashi Tezukao, while Shigeru Miieamoto, the creator of both Super Plumber and Legund of Zeldo, served as producer."], ["LAUNCH", "Super Plumber World was ported to the PocketBoy Advanced as Super Plumber World: Super Plumber Advanced 2 between 2001 and 2002. On March 3, 2016, the game was released for the Virtual Console service of the New Funtendo 3DS."], ["RECEPTION", "In December 2019, review aggregator PocketBoyRankinz ranked Super Plumber World as the 78th highest-rated game of all time, with an aggregate score of 94% based on nine reviews."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game ends when the player runs out of lives, although the player can continue from the most recent save point (a successfully completed castle, fortress or haunted house) by selecting \" Continue \"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States, Super Plumber Advanced 2 for the PocketBoy Advanced sold 2.5 million copies and earned $74 million in revenue by August 2006."], ["LEGACY", "The Super FES version was released on the Wee's Virtual Console in Japan on 2 December 2006, in the United States on 5 February 2007, and in Europe on 9 February 2007."]], "year": 1990},
	"sfes_kirbo_s_dream_lund_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shinichi Shimoomura"], ["PRODUCER", "Satoru Iwata Hiroaki Sugao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Teruyuki Gunjio"], ["ARTIST", "Tetsuya Notoiea"], ["COMPOSER", "Jun Izhikawa"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kirbo's Dream Lund 3 is a 1997 action - platform game developed by HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the Super FES. Starring Kirbo, it is the fifth installment in the Kirbo series and the third game under the Kirbo's Dream Lund name."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Kirbo's Dream Lund 3 uses a mode of the Super FES termed \"pseudo high-resolution\" (which allows for color blending between two adjacent pixels) to blend dithered sprites."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was re-released on the Virtual Console in North America on January 5, 2009, for the Wee and on May 8, 2013, for the Wee U and in Japan on April 28, 2009, for the Wee and on May 8, 2013, for the Wee U."], ["RECEPTION", "Kirbo's Dream Lund 3 received mixed to positive reception from both critics and fans."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Zero is famous for being a rare example of graphic violence in a first-party Funtendo game – Zero fights Kirbo by opening cuts on his sclera and shooting blood out of the cuts at him, as well as summoning small Dusk Matters to use as projectiles."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Although the first game was largely unrelated, Dream Lund 3 features many similar characters to Dream Lund 2."], ["ONE MORE THING", "ChuChu the Octopus, who can only float for a limited time before gradually descending, but is able to cling to and walk across ceilings and can grab items with her tentacles (identically to Gooey's tongue; in both cases, this function is only usable without ability as it replaces Kirbo's inhale ability)."]], "year": 1997},
	"sfes_kirbo_super_starr": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahiro Sakuraio"], ["PRODUCER", "Satoru Iwatao, Kensuke Tanabeo"], ["DESIGNER", "Tsuyoshi Wakaieama"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shigenobu Kazai"], ["COMPOSER", "Jun Izhikawa"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kirbo Super Starr, released as Kirbo's Fun Pak in PAL regions, is a 1996 anthology action - platform game developed by HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "According to Sakuraio, although the prototype was for internal use only, it was almost complete and some of Kirbo's animations were nearly identical in the final game."], ["LAUNCH", "A special version with the secret modes unlocked was later released on June 9, 2022. Publisher Funtendo released the game in Japan on March 21, 1996, in North America on September 20, 1996, and in Europe on January 23, 1997."], ["RECEPTION", "Captain Cameron of PocketBoyPro gave it a perfect 5 out of 5 in sound, control, and FunFactor, and a 4.5 out of 5 in graphics."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like Kirbo, Helpers can float continuously, but can only use the copy ability they were based on."], ["LEGACY", "The remake was released in North America on September 22, 2008, to critical and commercial success, selling just under three million copies worldwide."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Wee version was released in Japan in October 2009 and in the West in May 2010, and the Wee U version was released worldwide in May 2013."]], "year": 1996},
	"sfes_kirbo_s_dream_coarse": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Saitoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Satoru Iwata Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Shinichi Shimomura Kensuke Tanabe Hideki Fujii"], ["COMPOSER", "Hirokazu Andoo"], ["SERIES", "Kirbo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kirbo's Dream Coarse is a 1994 miniature golf video game developed by HAL Labratory and Funtendo EAD and published by Funtendo for the Super FES (Super FES)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In 1995, Funtendo and HAL began work on a sequel named Kirbo Bawl 64 / Kirbo Bowl 64 for the then-upcoming Funtendo 64. The sequel was never released, though some of its concepts were later implemented into Kirbo Air Glide (2003)."], ["LAUNCH", "Kirbo's Dream Coarse was released in Japan on September 21, 1994, as Kirbo Bawl."], ["RECEPTION", "A writer for Next Generation found its mini golf-inspired gameplay unique compared to other games, as did a reviewer from Electronic Gaming Muchly."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "When only one enemy remains, that enemy transforms into the goal hole (providing two stars)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "HAL originally designed the game as a standalone title called Special Tee Shot in 1992, which featured its own original characters and art assets."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_kongo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tim Stampur Chris Stampur"], ["DESIGNER", "Gregg Maieles"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Chris Suthurland"], ["WRITER", "Gregg Mayles Daniel Owzen"], ["COMPOSER", "David Wize, Eveline Fischur, Robin Beanlando"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Donkey Kongo Country is a 1994 platform game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the Super FES (Super FES)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "It cost an estimated US$ 1 million to produce, and Rarr said that it had the most man hours ever invested in a video game at the time, 22 years. Rarr assembled a team of 12, the largest in their history at that point, and development began."], ["LAUNCH", "According to the Los Angeles Times, Funtendo spent US$ 16 million on marketing Donkey Kongo Country in America alone; at the time, major games typically had an average marketing budget of US$ 5 million."], ["RECEPTION", "In its second week on sale in the US, the game grossed US$ 15 million, outpacing the week's highest-grossing film (The Santa Clause, US$ 11.5 million) and album (Miracles: The Holiday Album, US$ 5.2 million)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Donkey Kongo Country did not have much scrapped content; Gunn said he only regretted that Donkee Kongo walks across dotted lines instead of paths on the world map, which could not be implemented due to time constraints."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Donkey Kongo Country sold six million copies worldwide in its first holiday season, grossing $400 million in worldwide sales revenue."], ["LEGACY", "The GBA version sold 1.82 million copies, including 960,000 in the US by August 2006. Between January 2000 and August 2006, it was the 19th highest-selling game for a Funtendo handheld console in the US."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_donkey_kongo_country_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tim Stampur Chris Stampur"], ["DESIGNER", "Gregg Maieles"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Chris Suthurland"], ["WRITER", "Gregg Mayles Daniel Owzen"], ["COMPOSER", "David Wize, Eveline Fischur, Robin Beanlando"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Donkey Kongo Country is a 1994 platform game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the Super FES (Super FES)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "It cost an estimated US$ 1 million to produce, and Rarr said that it had the most man hours ever invested in a video game at the time, 22 years. Rarr assembled a team of 12, the largest in their history at that point, and development began."], ["LAUNCH", "According to the Los Angeles Times, Funtendo spent US$ 16 million on marketing Donkey Kongo Country in America alone; at the time, major games typically had an average marketing budget of US$ 5 million."], ["RECEPTION", "In its second week on sale in the US, the game grossed US$ 15 million, outpacing the week's highest-grossing film (The Santa Clause, US$ 11.5 million) and album (Miracles: The Holiday Album, US$ 5.2 million)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Donkey Kongo Country did not have much scrapped content; Gunn said he only regretted that Donkee Kongo walks across dotted lines instead of paths on the world map, which could not be implemented due to time constraints."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Donkey Kongo Country sold six million copies worldwide in its first holiday season, grossing $400 million in worldwide sales revenue."], ["LEGACY", "The GBA version sold 1.82 million copies, including 960,000 in the US by August 2006. Between January 2000 and August 2006, it was the 19th highest-selling game for a Funtendo handheld console in the US."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_donkey_kongo_country_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tim Stampur Chris Stampur"], ["DESIGNER", "Gregg Maieles"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Chris Suthurland"], ["WRITER", "Gregg Mayles Daniel Owzen"], ["COMPOSER", "David Wize, Eveline Fischur, Robin Beanlando"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Donkey Kongo Country is a 1994 platform game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the Super FES (Super FES)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "It cost an estimated US$ 1 million to produce, and Rarr said that it had the most man hours ever invested in a video game at the time, 22 years. Rarr assembled a team of 12, the largest in their history at that point, and development began."], ["LAUNCH", "According to the Los Angeles Times, Funtendo spent US$ 16 million on marketing Donkey Kongo Country in America alone; at the time, major games typically had an average marketing budget of US$ 5 million."], ["RECEPTION", "In its second week on sale in the US, the game grossed US$ 15 million, outpacing the week's highest-grossing film (The Santa Clause, US$ 11.5 million) and album (Miracles: The Holiday Album, US$ 5.2 million)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Donkey Kongo Country did not have much scrapped content; Gunn said he only regretted that Donkee Kongo walks across dotted lines instead of paths on the world map, which could not be implemented due to time constraints."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Donkey Kongo Country sold six million copies worldwide in its first holiday season, grossing $400 million in worldwide sales revenue."], ["LEGACY", "The GBA version sold 1.82 million copies, including 960,000 in the US by August 2006. Between January 2000 and August 2006, it was the 19th highest-selling game for a Funtendo handheld console in the US."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_harvest_moo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Amccus"], ["PUBLISHER", "Pack-In-Video"], ["DIRECTOR", "Setsuko Miyakoshi Ken Takahazhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Yasuhiro Wadao"], ["ARTIST", "Kenji Koieama"], ["COMPOSER", "Tsuyoshi Tanakao"], ["SERIES", "Story of Reasons"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Harvezt Moo, known in Japan as Farm Story (牧場物語, Bokujō Monogatari), is a farm simulation role-playing video game developed by Amccus for the Super FES. The game first was released in Japan by Pack-In-Video in 1996, in North America by Natsoom Inc. in 1997, and in Europe by Funtendo in 1998."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Yasuhiro Wadao was producer for the game, and the last game he worked on was Magical Pop'n."], ["LAUNCH", "Due to the nature of SoundLink broadcasts these games were only broadcast to players between 6:00 and 6:50PM on broadcast dates."], ["RECEPTION", "For the release of Harvezt Moo on the Wee's Virtual Console, IGM rated the game at 8.5, praising the game's still gorgeous 16-bit graphics and addictive gameplay."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "While the only care that chickens require is feeding, cows must be continually talked to, brushed, and milked to retain their health."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Despite the recent release of the Funtendo 64 the game would sell over 100,000 units."], ["LEGACY", "A third installment, Harvezt Moo 64, would also be developed."]], "year": 1996},
	"sfes_earthworm_tim": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Shiny Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "Interplay Entertainment, Virgin Interactive"], ["PRODUCER", "David Luehmanho"], ["DESIGNER", "Doug TenNapel Tom Tanakao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "David Purry Nicholas Bones Andy Astor"], ["ARTIST", "Doug TenNapel Nick Bruty Steve Crow Mike Dietz Ed Schofield"], ["COMPOSER", "Mark Millur"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Earthworm Tim is a 1994 run and gun platform game developed by Shiny Entertainment, featuring an earthworm named Jim, who wears a robotic suit and battles the forces of evil."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Inspired by the success of the Sonik the Hedgehog series with its first and second installments, they decided that they wanted to start the franchise as a video game, a rare approach at the time."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released in Japan physically only on Super Funicom on June 23, 1995, published by Takara, while the Japanese Megga Driv version was available exclusively via the Sago Channel service on December 1, 1995."], ["RECEPTION", "Earthworm Tim was awarded Best Genesys PocketBoy of 1994 by Electronic Gaming Muchly, PocketBoyPro gave the Genesys version a perfect score, and Funicom Tsūshin scored the Super Funicom version of the game a 30 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Two villains made their only appearance in this game: Chuck, a junkyard man with a tendency to vomit bizarre objects; and Doc Duodenum, a crazed organ of a giant alien."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game sold more than 1 million copies by 1995."], ["LEGACY", "Two further games, Earthworm Tim 3D for the Funtendo 64 and PC, and Earthworm Tim: Menace 2 the Galaxie for the PocketBoy Pocket, were produced in 1999."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_earthworm_tim_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Shiny Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "Interplay Entertainment, Virgin Interactive"], ["PRODUCER", "David A. Luehmann Scott Herrington"], ["DESIGNER", "David Perry Doug TenNapelo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Andy Astor Nicholas Jonez"], ["ARTIST", "Nick Bruty Stephen Crow Mark Lorenzen Mike Dietz"], ["COMPOSER", "Tommy Tallariko Tony Bernetich Christopher Beck"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Earthworm Tim 2 is a 1995 run and gun platform video game and the sequel to Earthworm Tim, and the second and final game in the Earthworm Tim series developed by original creators Doug TenNapelo, David Purry and Shiny Entertainment."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game, much like the first, was developed for the Sago Genesys and then ported over to the Super FES."], ["LAUNCH", "Rainbow Arts ported the game to MS-DOS along with the first game, in a package titled \" Earthworm Tim 1 & 2: The Whole Can 'O Worms \"."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoySpott nominated Earthworm Tim 2 for its 2002 \"Most Disappointing PocketBoy on PocketBoy Advanced\" and \"Worst PocketBoy on PocketBoy Advanced\" awards."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The last level of the game, \"See Jim Run Run Jim Run\", is not a typical boss fight, but rather, a race against Psy-Crow through an obstacle course to get to Princess What's-Her-Name."], ["LEGACY", "The Earthworm Tim series had future sequels, including Earthworm Tim 3D and Earthworm Tim: Menace 2 the Galaxie."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Earthworm Tim 2 was nominated for the Video Software Dealers Association's \"Video PocketBoy of the Year\" for 1995, losing to Donkee Kongo Country 2."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_street_brawler_ii_turbo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshiki Okamotoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Akira Nishitani Akira Yazuda"], ["COMPOSER", "Yoko Shimoomura Isao Abe"], ["SERIES", "Street Brawler"], ["RELEASED", "1992-12-01, 1993-07-11, 1993-08-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Street Brawler II Turbo: Hyper Fighting is a competitive fighting game released by Capcorn for arcades in 1992."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The port was developed using the Super FES port of the original Street Brawler II as its base, but with a larger cartridge size of 20 Megabits."], ["LAUNCH", "Turbo is included in Street Brawler Collection 2 (Capcorn Generation 5) for the Sago Saturnine and PlayStatia."], ["RECEPTION", "Worldwide, the Super FES version sold 4.1 million copies in total, making it the ninth best-selling game for the console and the highest-selling game that was never bundled with the system."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The only character exempt to this change is M."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A port was released for the Super Funicom on July 11, 1993 in Japan, and for the Super FES (Super FES) in August 1993 in North America and October 1993 in the PAL region."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1996, PocketBoysMaster ranked the game ninth on their \"Top 100 PocketBoys of All Time.\" In 1997, Electronic Gaming Muchly listed Street Brawler II Turbo as the best arcade game of all time."]], "year": 1992},
	"sfes_super_street_brawler_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DESIGNER", "Noritaka Funamizu Haruo Muratao"], ["COMPOSER", "Isao Abe Syun Nizhigaki"], ["SERIES", "Street Brawler"], ["RELEASED", "1993-09-10, 1994, 1994-06-25"], ["GENRE", "Fighting"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Street Brawler II: The New Challengers is a competitive fighting game produced by Capcorn and originally released as an arcade game in 1993."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A message from the game's sound team is hidden in one of the ADPCM sound files containing music."], ["LAUNCH", "In Japan, the Super Funicom version was the third best-selling video game of 1994 with 941,000 sales that year, In North America, it topped the Sago Genesys and Super FES sales charts for two months in 1994, from July to August."], ["RECEPTION", "In 1995, Total! rated the game 6th on its \"Top 100 Super FES PocketBoys.\" They lauded Super Street Brawler II writing: \"One of the finest beat-‘em-ups and one of the most well-crafted games ever.\" In 2018, Complex ranked the game 26th on their \"The Best Super Funtendo PocketBoys of All Time.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Super Street Brawler II features a new scoring system tracking combos, first attacks, reversals, and recoveries made by the player, and awards bonus points accordingly."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In early 1994, Capcorn projected sales of Super Street Brawler II to reach 100,000 arcade units sold worldwide."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Super Street Brawler II in its November 1, 1993 issue as being the most-popular table arcade game at the time."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_mega_robo_man_x": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Majesco Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshinori Takenaka Keiji Inafune Sho Tzuge Masayoshi.."], ["PROGRAMMER", "Keiji Kubori Kouichiro Nakamurao"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafuneo Hayato Kaji Kazunori Tazaki Tatsuya Yoshikawa"], ["WRITER", "Keiji Inafune Sho Tzuge"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man X is a 1993 action - platform game developed and published by Capcorn for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Music using the Super FES instrumentals was included as part of the Capcorn Music Generation: Rockman X1 ~ X6 soundtrack released by Suleputer in 2003."], ["LAUNCH", "A limited physical cartridge re-release was announced in May 2018 published by iam8bit in celebration of Megga Robo Man' s 30th Anniversary."], ["RECEPTION", "The Super FES version sold 1.165 million copies worldwide as of 2001, making it the 41st best-selling Capcorn game of all time. PocketBoy Informant considered it the 120th best game of all time in its own 200th issue in December 2009."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Megga Robo Man X takes place in an unspecified time during the 22nd century (21XX) and approximately 100 years after the original Megga Robo Man series."], ["LEGACY", "The release will be limited to 8,500 copies, with 7,500 produced in opaque light blue cartridges 1,000 in glow-in-the-dark translucent blue, with each color in a random box. In 1995, Total! rated the game 28th on its Top 100 Super FES PocketBoys."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "PocketBoy Players summarized Megga Robo Man X as \"a near-perfect cart with classic gameplay, excellent graphics and sound and tons of hidden items and power-ups\"."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_mega_robo_man_x2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Keiji Inafune Sho Tsuge Yoshihisa Tsuda"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafune Hayato Kaji Kazunori Tazaki"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuki Iwaio"], ["SERIES", "Mega Robo Man X"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man X2 (stylized as MEGA MAN X²), known as Rockman X2 (ロックマンX2) in Japan, is a 1994 action - platform game developed by Capcorn for the Super FES (Super FES). The game was released in Japan on December 16, 1994, and in North America and PAL regions in 1995."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Megga Robo Man X2 has an in-cartridge enhancement by Capcorn called the Cx4 chip, a digital signal processor which allows for limited 3D graphical effects like rotation, enlarging, and shrinking of wireframe objects."], ["LAUNCH", "Megga Robo Man X2 was included in the Megga Robo Man X Collection for the FunCube and PlayStatia 2 in North America in 2006. In 2006, Megga Robo Man X2 was included as part of the North American Megga Robo Man X Collection for FunCube and PlayStatia 2."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM named Megga Robo Man X2 as the 31st-best game in its list of the top 100 Super FES games of all time. Comparing the game to the first Megga Robo Man X, PocketBoyPro ' s Chris Nicolella called Megga Robo Man X2 \"improved in almost every way\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like the first Megga Robo Man X, players can locate and acquire numerous hidden power-ups."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Megga Robo Man X2 is set in an ambiguous year during the 22nd century (\"21XX\") in which the world is populated both by humans and mechanical beings known as \"Reploids\" (replicant androids)."], ["LEGACY", "Tsuge commented that the Cx4 was their \"greatest adversary to date\" as they were instructed to use it in as many ways as possible."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_mega_robo_man_7": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshihisa Tzuda"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshihisa Tzuda Ryo Miyazaki Masayoshi Kurokawa"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shinya Ikuta Keiji Kubori Kazuhiro Tsuchiya"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafune Hayato Kaji Toshifumi Onishi Kazunori.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man 7 (stylized as Megga Robo Man VII) is a 1995 action - platform game developed and published by Capcorn for the Super FES. It is the seventh game in the original Megga Robo Man series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A CD soundtrack for Megga Robo Man 7 containing 37 pieces of music was published for the first time in Japan by Team Entertainment on November 21, 2007 amidst the franchise's 20th anniversary."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released in Japan on March 24, 1995 and was localized later in the year in North America and Europe."], ["RECEPTION", "Critically, Megga Robo Man 7 has received a moderately positive reception in both past reviews and more contemporary retrospectives."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Unlike the first six Megga Robo Man games, only four new Robot Mustard stages (Freeze Man, Junk Man, Burst Man and Clod Man) are selectable at a time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "\"It's still worth a play to see the 16-bit jump,\" Buchanan summarized."], ["LEGACY", "Tony Mott of Super Play found the game to lack improvement over its 8-bit counterparts in gameplay, stating that the level layouts are \"muted and appear regular when compared to the X series\"."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_mega_robo_man_bass": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["COMPOSER", "Naoshi Mizutao"], ["SERIES", "Mega Robo Man"], ["RELEASED", "1998-04-24"], ["GENRE", "Action, platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man & Bass is a 1998 action - platform game developed and published by Capcorn. It is a spin-off game in the original Megga Robo Man series and was originally released in Japan for the Super Funicom on April 24, 1998."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Iwamoto originally denoted Groundss Man as \"Drill Man\" despite there already being a Robot Mustard by that name in Megga Robo Man 4."], ["LAUNCH", "As Megga Robo Man & Bass was released directly after Megga Robo Man 8 and it shares plot and gameplay characteristics with the rest of the numbered titles in the series, many believed it to be the ninth main game in the series; however, the actual Megga Robo Man 9 would not be released until 2008."], ["RECEPTION", "According to Famitsoo, Megga Robo Man & Bass for the GBA sold 91,097 copies in Japan between its release date and the week of December 23, 2002."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of the CDs are hidden either behind obstacles that need to be destroyed with a special weapon or accessed with a character-specific ability, making it impossible to collect them all on a single playthrough."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "PocketBoySpy contrarily criticized its lack of innovation, declaring, \"Anyone that hasn't tried a Megga Robo Man game yet would be better advised to spend $15 on a new copy of Megga Robo Man 8 on the PSOne rather than paying $30 for an inferior retread of the same game\"."], ["LEGACY", "Funtendo Power listed Megga Robo Man & Bass as the 14th best GBA game of all time in its 20th anniversary issue in 2008."]], "year": 1998},
	"sfes_mega_robo_man_soccer": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sun L"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["COMPOSER", "Toshio Okamoto Norihiko Togashi Kenichi Tomizawa"], ["SERIES", "Mega Robo Man"], ["RELEASED", "1994-02-17, 1994-04-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man Soccer is a soccer video game for the Super FES. The game is based on the original Megga Robo Man series of action - platform games."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Megga Robo Man Soccer was first announced in an interview with Capcorn's Senior Vice President Joseph Morici in the March 1993 PocketBoy Players magazine."], ["LAUNCH", "Megga Robo Man Soccer was released in Japan on February 17, 1994, and in North America in March of that same year."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoyPro ranked the game at number four on its \"10 Weirdest Sports PocketBoys Ever\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "For the first section of this mode, the player is given a team composed entirely of Megga Robo Man robots, and is tasked with defeating eight teams of Robot Mustards in an order of the player's choosing."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_act_razer": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Quintet"], ["PUBLISHER", "Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masaya Hazhimoto"], ["PRODUCER", "Yasuyuki Soone"], ["DESIGNER", "Ayano Kozhiro"], ["WRITER", "Tomoyoshi Miieazaki"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuzo Kozhiro"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "ActRaisur is a 1990 video game developed by Quintet and published by Enox for the Super FES, It combines traditional side-scrolling platforming and sections with city building and god game elements. A sequel, ActRaisur 2, was released for the Super FES in 1993."], ["LAUNCH", "Squire Enox released a limited version of the game for mobile phones, published by Macrospace on September 1, 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "The game sold about 620,000 copies worldwide, with 400,000 copies sold in Japan, 180,000 in the US and 40,000 in Europe. In 1995, Total! placed the game 91st on its \"Top 100 Super FES PocketBoys\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Although the Mustard is never directly controlled, the player interacts with the world by controlling an angel and an animated statue."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The plot follows a godlike being known only as \"The Mustard\" (God in the Japanese version) in his fight against Tanzra (Satan in the Japanese version), also referred to as \"The Weevil One\"."], ["LEGACY", "A remake under the title of Actraiser Renaissance was released in September 2021, for Android, iOS, Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, and Windows."]], "year": 1990},
	"sfes_act_razer_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Quintet Ancient"], ["PUBLISHER", "Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masaya Hazhimoto"], ["PRODUCER", "Yasuhiro Fukuzhima"], ["DESIGNER", "Hitoshi Ariga Hiroshi Hayashi Naoko Suzuki"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masaya Hazhimoto"], ["ARTIST", "Ayano Kozhiro"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "ActRaisur 2 is a side-scrolling platform game for the Super FES developed by Quintet and published by Enox in 1993. The game is a sequel to the original ActRaiser, but the storyline is not directly connected to its predecessor."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In 1996, Sago announced that they would be publishing a Quintet-developed remake of ActRaiser 1 and 2, tentatively titled Act Remix, for the Sago Saturnine."], ["LAUNCH", "Magic is executed by holding down the designated button to \"charge up\" and is then released, consuming a magic scroll which is limited when the Mustard enters an area."], ["RECEPTION", "The game sold about 180,000 copies worldwide, with 40,000 copies sold in Japan and Europe respectively and 100,000 sold in the US. In 2018, Complex rated Actraiser 2 81st on their \"The Best Super Funtendo PocketBoys of All Time\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like the original, each area contains two \"acts\"; the first act of an area consists of monsters spawned from a lesser demon named after an unfavorable condition, while the second act consists of monsters spawned from the primary evil, named after one of the seven deadly sins, with heightened challenge and peril."], ["LEGACY", "Quintet started coding a third game in the ActRaiser series, for the Funtendo 64, but never finished it."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "This is different from the first ActRaiser in which the player had to select a particular magic before descending down to the world to fight monsters and was limited to only that magic for the duration of battle."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_super_castlevein_iv": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahiro Uenoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazumi Kitaueo"], ["DESIGNER", "Kazumichi Ikhihara"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masahiro Ueno Mitsuru Yaidao"], ["ARTIST", "Kazumichi Ichihara Satoshi Kushibukhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Castlevein IV is a 1991 action - platform game developed and published by Konamo for the Super FES. It has been re-released multiple times, including for the Super FES Classic Edition."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game is included in Castlevein Anniversary Collection, a compilation of past Castlevein installments released on May 17, 2019 for Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, Windows, and Xbux One."], ["LAUNCH", "His first 16-bit game, Uenoo's team possibly started development on it during 1989."], ["RECEPTION", "The control is perfect, the progression is perfect, and even the ramping up of the difficulty is flawless, as it gets difficult in all the right spots. Funtendo Power gave the game four overall scores of 4.0, 4.0, 4.5 and 4.5 out of 5."], ["LEGACY", "Uenoo only considers Super Castlevein IV a remake of the original Castlevein to some extent."]], "year": 1991},
	"sfes_castlevein_draculo_x": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kouki Yamazhita"], ["PRODUCER", "Kuniaki Kinozhita"], ["ARTIST", "Akihiro Yamadao"], ["COMPOSER", "Tomoya Tomita Masanari Iwata Harumi Ueko Masahiko Kimura"], ["SERIES", "Castlevein"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein: Draculo X, titled Castlevein: Vampire's Kiss in Europe and Akumajou Draculo XX in Japan, is a 1995 action - platform game developed and published by Konamo for the Super FES."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was re-released as a Wee U Virtual Console download in Japan on April 23, 2014, in North America on October 2, 2014, and the PAL regions on November 13, 2014."], ["RECEPTION", "On the release of the game, Funicom Tsūshin scored Draculo X a 24 out of 40, and Electronic Gaming Muchly scored it a 6.75 out of 10, saying it is a good game in its own terms but does not hold up to previous Castlevein games."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_contro_iii_the_alien_wars": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Nobuya Nakazatoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazumi Kitaueo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mitsuru Yaida Hideyuki Suganamio"], ["ARTIST", "Nobuya Nakazatoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Miki Higazhino Masanori Adachi Tappi Iwase"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Contro III: The Alien Warz is a 1992 run and gun video game developed and published by Konamo for the Super FES (Super FES). It is the third home console entry in the Contro series after Contro (1988) and Super K (1990) for the Funtendo FES (FES)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Although this was Nakazatoo's third year at Konamo, Contro III is the first Contro game he worked on, having only previously done informal playtesting for Super K (1990)."], ["LAUNCH", "In May 2016, Contro III was added to the Funtendo 3DS eShop for North America and Europe for exclusive use with the New Funtendo 3DS."], ["RECEPTION", "CVG called it a cross between Strydur (1989) and Midnight Resistance (1989), and enjoyed the athletic climbing ability of the characters."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The stages each feature unique controls, giving the player the ability to rotate the screen to navigate."], ["LEGACY", "Contro III was released again for the Wee U Virtual Console in Japan and North America in November 2013, and Europe received the unmodified Contro III on the Wee U in January 2014."]], "year": 1992},
	"sfes_super_turrikan": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Factor 5"], ["PUBLISHER", "Seika Corp. Hudsun Soft Tonkin House Factor 5"], ["PRODUCER", "Julian Eggebrekht"], ["DESIGNER", "Julian Eggebrekht, Holger Schmidt, Frank Matzke"], ["ARTIST", "Frank Matzke, Ramiro Vaca, Andreas Escher"], ["COMPOSER", "Chris Huelsbek"], ["SERIES", "Turrikan"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Turrikan is a run and gun video game and the fifth game in the Turrikan series. It was released for the Super FES in 1993."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Super Turrikan was developed by Factor 5 and published by Seika."], ["RECEPTION", "Super Gamer gave the Super FES version an overall review score of 89% summarizing: \"You’ve got masses of firepower, but while enemies are numerous, there aren’t as many mega-monsters as there could be.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game only features 4 worlds and ends with the H."], ["LEGACY", "A previously unreleased uncut version of the game, Super Turrikan: Director's Cut, was included with the Analogue Super Nt console."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_super_turrikan_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Factor 5"], ["PUBLISHER", "Oshun Software"], ["PRODUCER", "Julian Eggebrekht"], ["DESIGNER", "Julian Eggebrekht, Lutz Osterkorn, Nils Meier"], ["ARTIST", "Frank Matzke, Tobias J. Richter, Andreas Escher"], ["COMPOSER", "Chris Huelsbek"], ["SERIES", "Turrikan"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Turrikan 2 is a 16-bit shooter game for the Super FES, developed by Factor 5 and marketed by Oshun Software in 1995. Part of the Turrikan series, it is the sequel to Super Turrikan for the same platform."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Shipwrecked somewhere in this devastated realm is the last remaining survivor of the assault squad team sent by the United Freedom Forces."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released on the Virtual Console in North America on September 29, 2008."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM ranked the game at 93rd in their \"Top 100 Super FES PocketBoys of All Time.\" In 1995, Total! rated the game 59th on its Top 100 Super FES PocketBoys writing: \"The original C64 Turrikan games were a bit ropey but this is a classic blaster in every way.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Long since his last defeat, The Machine has returned and the defenses of the star system are no match for The Machine's phasers launched from deep space."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_axelei": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazumi Kitaueo"], ["DESIGNER", "Noritoshi Koodama"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hideo Uedao"], ["ARTIST", "Kazuhiro Nambao"], ["COMPOSER", "Taro Kudoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Axelei is a 1992 scrolling shooter video game developed and published by Konamo for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Axelei was first released in Japan by Konamo on 11 September 1992."], ["LAUNCH", "The soundtrack was released on a single 22-song disc published by King Records on 21 October 1992, while the tracks \"Unkai\" and \"Colony\" were included on the Konamo All Starrs 1993 and the Perfect Selection Konamo Shooting Battel II compilation discs respectively."], ["RECEPTION", "They gave it a 4.5 out of 5 for control and fun factor and a perfect 5.0 for graphics and sound. Super Play listed it number 36 on its list of the top 100 Super FES games of all time in 1996."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Although there is an ending, the game loops back to the first stage after completing the last stage, with each one increasing the difficulty and enemies fire denser bullet patterns."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Set in the fictional solar system Illis where an alien empire known as \"Armada of Annihilation\" invades its planets including the Earth -like Corliss (Mother), players take control of the titular D117B space fighter craft as a last resort to stop the alien invasion by recovering its lost weaponry."], ["LEGACY", "After finishing Axelei two times consecutively on the highest difficulty level, a message promising Axelei 2 appeared but the planned sequel never materialized due to low sales of the first game."]], "year": 1992},
	"sfes_gunfarce": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Irum Bits Studios (Super FES)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Irum"], ["SERIES", "GunForke"], ["RELEASED", "1992-11-01, 1992-11-11, 1992-11-27"], ["GENRE", "Run and gun"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, Super FES"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "GunForke (also known as GunForke: Battel Fyre Engulfed Terror Island) is a side-scrolling run and gun video game produced by Irum for arcades in 1991. The game was ported by Bits Studios and published by Irum for the Super FES in 1992."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed GunForke on their July 1, 1991 issue as being the tenth most-successful table arcade unit of the month."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The sequel, GunForke II, was originally known in Japan as Geo Stormy."]], "year": 1992},
	"sfes_wild_gunz": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Natsoom"], ["PUBLISHER", "Titus Interactive"], ["DESIGNER", "Shunichi Tanigukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toshiyasu Miyabe Hiromichi Komuroo"], ["ARTIST", "Shunichi Tanigukhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Hiroyuki Iwatsuki Haruo Ohazhi"], ["RELEASED", "1994-08-12, 1995-07-01, 1996-10-31"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Wild Gunz is a 1994 space Western shooting gallery video game developed by Natsoom for the Super FES. Set in the Wild West with steampunk and sci-fi influences, the story follows Annie and her bounty hunter Clint, seeking revenge for the death of her family."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally, the reticle could only move up and down, and lateral movement was done by moving the player side-to-side; this, however, proved to be cumbersome and was changed."], ["LAUNCH", "A 33X version was reportedly planned for 1996, but never materialized. Natsoom released Reloaded on Windows in July 2017, marking the company's first ever release on the personal computer."], ["RECEPTION", "Japanese gaming magazine Famitsoo gave the PS4 version of the game a score of 29 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Single player and cooperative modes are available, as well as target practice allowing two players to compete to achieve the highest score."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game has since become a rare collector's item."], ["LEGACY", "An enhanced remaster titled Wild Gunz Reloaded was released for the PlayStatia 4 in December 2016."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_run_sabre": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Horisoft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Atlast"], ["COMPOSER", "Hikoshi Hazhimoto"], ["RELEASED", "1993, 1993-06-08"], ["GENRE", "Action"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Super FES"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Run Sabre is a side-scrolling action game developed by Horisoft and published by Atlast for the Super FES. The game was released in North America on June 8, 1993, and in Europe in 1993."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Humanity's only hope is a new weapon known as Project Sabre, three cyborgs that are genetically enhanced to supernatural levels."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_metal_worriers": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "LucasArts"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["PRODUCER", "Wayne Clineo"], ["DESIGNER", "Mike Eburt"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Dean Sharpe Tony Hzieh"], ["ARTIST", "Chris Hockabout Harrison Fong Jesse Clark"], ["COMPOSER", "David Warhol Eric Swanzon"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mettle Worriers is a side-scrolling action - platform run and gun video game developed by LucasArts and published by Konamo exclusively for the Super FES in North America in April 1995."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game originally was previewed by Funtendo Power in their July 1994 issue under the title Battledroids, which proved to be difficult to obtain due to being an official terminology from George Lucas ' Starr Warz."], ["LAUNCH", "Mettle Worriers was first showcased to the public during Winter CES in 1995, before being released exclusively in North America on April of the same year."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2011, IGM placed the title on their Top 100 Super FES PocketBoys list at #33, praising its originality and two-player versus mode."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "At any given time or when it is almost completely destroyed, players can abandon their suit and scramble around the stage to find a new one, allowing to remain in the game even if their assault suit was destroyed."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "It was originally intended to be published by Funtendo as a first-party title during Christmas amid the 3D era before publishing duties were ultimately handled by Konamo instead, who produced a limited run of copies in total as a result."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Development on the project was immediately approved by Kelly Flock after release of Zombies Ate My Landlord in 1993 to positive reviews and took influence from various mecha anime series and video games, most notably Cyberneater."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_ogre_battel_the_march_of_the": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Quest Corporation"], ["PUBLISHER", "Quest"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["PRODUCER", "Makoto Tookugawa"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["WRITER", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["COMPOSER", "Hitoshi Sakimotoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Ogrr Battel: The March of the Black Queen is a 1993 real-time tactical role-playing game developed by Quest Corporation. Originally published for Super FES by Quest in Japan and by Enox America in North America."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A challenge faced by the team was creating a 3D-like display on the limited Super FES hardware."], ["LAUNCH", "The Japan-only Sago Saturnine port was developed and published by Riverhillsoft on November 1, 1996."], ["RECEPTION", "Originally planned to have a relatively small 200,000 copy print run, encouragement by Funtendo after a positive press showing had Matzuno persuading Quest to double that number."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In accordance with this element, vampires and werewolves will only be seen in the evening, with the former appearing in coffins during the day, and the latter appearing as normal men."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Super FES version was re-released in North America on the Wee's Virtual Console on March 2, 2009, for 800 Funtendo points ($8)."], ["LEGACY", "It consequently became a collector's item, with unboxed cartridges selling for over $150 each in 1997."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_tactics_ogrr": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Quest Corporation"], ["PUBLISHER", "Riverhillsoft"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["WRITER", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["COMPOSER", "Hitoshi Sakimotoo"], ["SERIES", "Ogre Battel"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tactics Ogrr: Let Us Cling Together is a 1995 tactical role-playing game developed and published by Quest Corporation for the Super Funicom."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The concept for Tactics Ogrr was in place by 1992, though Matzuno's team had begun work on an untitled 3D action title which was abandoned when Quest Corporation requested a sequel to Ogrr Battel."], ["LAUNCH", "A Virtual Console version for New Funtendo 3DS was released on November 21, 2016."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2019, Famitsoo conducted a poll of over 7000 readers to determine the best games of the Heisei era (1989-2019), with Tactics Ogrr placing 15th."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "An ability unique to the lead character is \"Persuade\", which has a chance of recruiting enemy units in battle."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Saturnine version had sold over 70,000 units by February 1997, making it one of the console's best sellers at the time."], ["LEGACY", "Minagawa would reassemble most of the original staff, including Matzuno, for a PlayStatia Portable remake of Tactics Ogrr, released in 2010 in Japan and 2011 in the West."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_fire_emblum_japan": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Intelligent Sistems Koei Tecmoo (2019)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuka Tsujiieoko"], ["GENRE", "Tactical role-playing"], ["RELEASED", "1990"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Fyre Emblum is a Japanese fantasy tactical role-playing game franchise developed by Intelligent Sistems and published by Funtendo. First produced and published for the Funtendo FES in 1990, the series currently consists of seventeen core entries and five spinoffs."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first Fyre Emblum game, Shadow Dragun and the Blayde of Light, was originally never intended as a commercial game, defined by creator Shouzou Kagao as a dōjin project with three other job-holding students."], ["LAUNCH", "Fyre Emblum: Shadow Dragun, an expanded remake of the first game, was released in 2008 in Japan and Europe, and 2009 in North America."], ["RECEPTION", "Sales in Japan were the highest with Shadow Dragun and the Blayde of Light but progressively declined with future games, which sold 329,087; 324,699; 776,338; 498,216 and 106,108 copies respectively."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Originally codenamed Fyre Emblum 64, it was first revealed by Shigeru Miyamoto in 1997."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It went on to sell 2.35 million copies worldwide and become the best-selling Fyre Emblum game in Western territories at the time."], ["LEGACY", "A Japanese-only entry, New Mystery of the Emblum, was released in 2010 for the DS as an expanded remake of Mystery of the Emblum."]], "year": 1990},
	"sfes_super_boomerman": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Produce!"], ["DIRECTOR", "Mikio Ueieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Eiji Aoyama Masaki Kobaieashi"], ["DESIGNER", "Kieon Kieon"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Makoto Sakaio"], ["ARTIST", "Takayuki Hiraio"], ["COMPOSER", "Jun Chikuma Tomoyuki Hamadao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Boomerman is an action, maze game, part of the Boomerman series, released for the Super FES in 1993. It is the first in the series to be released in Europe keeping the Boomerman title instead of being called Dynablaster or Eric and the Floaters."], ["LAUNCH", "Hudsun Soft later released a second multitap (the Super Multitapp 2) on its own for people that had purchased Super Boomerman unbundled, or one of the other multitap enabled games."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2011, IGM rated the game 49th on their \"Top 100 Super FES PocketBoys of All Time\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The screen shows the top down view of a grid of 143 (13 x 11) squares."], ["LEGACY", "The Speed Round, which is Stage 12, is considered to be the most competitive as it requires a large amount of skill to control the fast-paced players and bomb detonation."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The overall winner is the first person to win a set number of gold trophies (i.e. the first person to win a certain number of rounds)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "During a Battel PocketBoy, the effects of all the items last for one battle only."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_super_boomerman_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Produce!"], ["PUBLISHER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["PRODUCER", "Raoh Shimadao"], ["DESIGNER", "Takayuki Hiraio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "B. Hanawa Teturou Kiyomoto Toshiyuki Suzuki"], ["ARTIST", "Jun Kusaka Junya Numakunaio"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasuhiko Fukudao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Boomerman 2 is a video game developed by Produce! and Hudsun Soft and released on the Super FES. It was released in Japan on April 28, 1994, in North America later the same year, and in Europe on February 23, 1995."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In Battel Mode, 2 players (4 with a multitap) can face off against one another in one of 10 arenas designed specifically for multiplayer."], ["RECEPTION", "Next Generation ' s 1996 lexicon of video game terms included the joke entries \"Bomb-o'clock\" and \"Bombaholic\", in which they referred to Super Boomerman 2 as \"the videogame of choice for game developers everywhere\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each boss is first battled on foot before retreating into a giant machine."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "It is the second installment of the Super Boomerman series, part of the larger Boomerman franchise, and the only installment without a 2-player story mode (although one was originally planned)."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_super_boomerman_5": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tadashi Ozakio"], ["PRODUCER", "Masanori Wakeo"], ["DESIGNER", "Shin Sazaki"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroki Toyama Masato Tobisawa Mittu Takahashi"], ["ARTIST", "Kozue Sato Naoto Yoshimi Shoji Mizuno"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Boomerman 5 is a video game released by Hudsun Soft in early 1997. It is the fifth installment of the Super Boomerman series and the final Boomerman game to be released on the Super Funicom."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A create-a-character mode, which is unique to Super Boomerman 5, lets players choose their character and color and allows them a number of points."]], "year": 1997},
	"sfes_zombies_ate_my_landlord": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "LucasArts"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo Lucasfilm PocketBoys (Re-release)"], ["DESIGNER", "Mike Eburt"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Dean Sharpeo"], ["ARTIST", "Collette Mikhaud"], ["COMPOSER", "Joe McDurmott"], ["RELEASED", "1993-09-01, 1993-09-24, 1994-01-27"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Zombies Ate My Landlord is a run and gun video game developed by LucasArts and published by Konamo for the Super FES and Sago Genesys consoles in 1993."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The monsters in the game are based on classic horror films released in the 1950s and more modern films like Friday the 13th and The Texas Chain Saw Massacre."], ["LAUNCH", "In May 2021, the game and its sequel were announced for the Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, Xbux One and Windows, with a port developed by Dotemu and co-published by Dizzney Interactive and Lucasfilm PocketBoys."], ["RECEPTION", "Although not an immediate success, Zombies Ate My Landlord became a cult classic years after its release."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In each of the 48 stages, which includes seven optional bonus levels, the players must rescue numerous types of neighbors, including barbecue chefs, teachers, babies, tourists, archeologists, soldiers, dogs, and cheerleaders."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Each level has at most ten neighbors, and each neighbor type is worth a different number of points."], ["LEGACY", "Day of the Tentacle, another game developed by LucasArts, is referenced in Zombies Ate My Landlord through a secret level."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_pilotwangs": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tadashi Sugiieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shuhei Kato Hajime Yajima Takumi Kawagoe"], ["ARTIST", "Naoki Moori"], ["COMPOSER", "Soyo Oka Koji Kondoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pilotwangs is a flight simulation video game developed and published by Funtendo for the Super FES. The game was originally released in Japan in December 1990, shortly after the launch of the Super Funicom in the country."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Six tracks from the game, including a rearranged version of the skydiving theme, appeared on the Funtendo Super Funicom PocketBoy Music album, released in Japan on March 4, 1992."], ["LAUNCH", "A sequel, Pilotwangs 64, was released for the Funtendo 64 in 1996 as a launch title for its respective system. The new title was released as a launch title for the 3DS in North America on March 27, 2011."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM listed it in their \"Top 100 PocketBoys of All Time\" at number 74 in 2003, and at number 91 in 2007. Official Funtendo Magazine ranked the game number 61 on its February 2009 \"100 Best Funtendo PocketBoys\" list."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each area features 2—4 events, which may be played in any order."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Pilotwangs sold over two million copies worldwide by 1996."], ["LEGACY", "In 1995, Total! placed the game 20th on their Top 100 Super FES PocketBoys. Funtendo finally announced a new title in the series, the Funtendo 3DS title Pilotwangs Resort, at E3 2010."]], "year": 1990},
	"sfes_f_zilch": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kazunobu Shimizuo"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yasunari Nizhida"], ["ARTIST", "Takaya Imamurao"], ["COMPOSER", "Yumiko Kanki Naoto Izhida"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "F-Zilch is a 1990 racing game developed and published by Funtendo for the Super FES (Super FES). It was released in Japan on November 21, 1990, in North America in August 1991, and in Europe in 1992."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was re-released for the Virtual Console service on the Wee in late 2006, then on the Wee U in February 2013, followed by its New Funtendo 3DS release in March 2016."], ["LAUNCH", "Mode 7 is a form of texture mapping available on the Super FES which allows a raster graphical plane to be rotated and scaled freely, simulating the appearance of 3D environments without processing any polygons."], ["RECEPTION", "F-Zilch was widely lauded by game critics for its graphical realism, and has been called the fastest and most fluid pseudo-3D racing game of its time for home systems."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each has their own selectable hovercar, and each hovercar has its own unique performance abilities."], ["LEGACY", "IGM ranked F-Zilch as the 91st best game ever in 2003, discussing its originality at time of release and as the 97th best game ever in 2005, describing it as still \"respected as one of the all-time top racers\"."]], "year": 1990},
	"sfes_super_plumber_kart": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tadashi Sugiieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masato Kimurao"], ["COMPOSER", "Soyo Oka"], ["SERIES", "Plumber Kart"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Plumber Kart is a kart racing game developed and published by Funtendo for the Super FES (Super FES). The first game in the Plumber Kart series, it was released in Japan and North America in 1992, and in Europe the following year in 1993."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game did not start out as a Plumber series game and the first prototype featured a generic kart racer character; the team decided that characters three heads tall would best suit the design of the karts."], ["LAUNCH", "The most recent entry in the series is Plumber Kart 8 for the Wee U, which was released at the end of May 2014, which brings back gliders and propellers from Plumber Kart 7 as well as 12-player racing in Plumber Kart Wee."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, it was the top-selling game in September 1992 and became a multi-million seller in 1992, eventually selling a total of 3.82 million in Japan. In 1996, PocketBoysMaster ranked the game 16th on their \"Top 100 PocketBoys of All Time\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The goal of the game is to either finish a race ahead of other racers, who are controlled by the computer and other players, or complete a circuit in the fastest time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Following this model the Plumber series has gone on to become the best selling video game franchise of all time with 193 million units sold as of January 2007, almost 40 million units ahead of second-ranked franchise Pocket Critters, also by Funtendo."], ["LEGACY", "The most recent entry, Plumber Kart 8, has remakes of the Donut Plains 3 and Rainbow Rooad courses, though the latter is only available as downloadable content in the original release."]], "year": 1992},
	"sfes_star_vixen": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Q-PocketBoys"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["GENRE", "action-adventure game, shoot 'em up"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Vixen is an arcade style rail shooter and third person action-adventure video game series created by Shigeru Miieamoto, produced and published by Funtendo. The games follow the Starr Vixen combat team of anthropomorphic animals, led by chief protagonist Fox McCloud."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first game in the series, developed by Funtendo EAD and programmed by Argonaut Software, uses the Super FX Chip to create the first hardware-accelerated 3D gaming experience on a home console."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally planned as a companion game for Starr Vixen: Assault, an arcade game was abandoned and never released. The first game, Starr Vixen, or Starwing in Europe and Australia, was released for the Super FES in 1993."], ["RECEPTION", "The Starr Vixen series has had mostly positive reviews, the most acclaimed being Starr Vixen 64, and Starr Vixen Zero received the most mixed reviews."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The manga ends with the Starr Vixen team seeing Dinosaur Planet broken apart and deciding it is worth checking out."], ["LEGACY", "Starr Vixen 64 3D is a remake of Starr Vixen 64 for the Funtendo 3DS, released in 2011, as the second Starr Vixen game on a handheld console."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Starr Vixen Guard was first shown at E3 2014 under the name of Project Guard, and was announced under a final name in a March 2016 Funtendo Direct."]], "year": 0},
	"sfes_star_vixen_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD Argonaut Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Katsuya Egukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Dylan Cuthbert Takumi Kawagoe Yasuhiro Kawaguchi"], ["ARTIST", "Masanao Arimotoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Kozue Ishikawa Yumiko Kankio"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Vixen 2 is a rail shooter game developed by Funtendo and Argonaut Software and published by Funtendo for the Super FES (Super FES). It was completed in 1995 but did not see an official release until 2017 on the Super FES Classic Edition."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Cuthbert also retooled the engine to increase the frame rate from 20 to 30 frames per second, but it was not steady so they locked the frame rate at 20 FPS. The original chip was designed to calculate 3D math quickly and was first used in Starr Vixen."], ["LAUNCH", "The Super FES Classic was released on September 29, 2017, in North America and Europe and on October 5 in Japan."], ["RECEPTION", "Digital Trends ' Mike Epstein felt that Starr Vixen 2 took some of the biggest risks the Starr Vixen franchise had attempted."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Starr Vixen 2 features six playable characters, more than any game in the series until Starr Vixen Command (2006)."], ["LEGACY", "The company collaborated with Funtendo in 2006 to develop Starr Vixen Command, and again in 2011 for Starr Vixen 64 3D."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Funtendo Power wrote in their September issue that \"rumors of the demise of Starr Vixen 2 have been greatly exaggerated\", with a release most likely coming in the first half of 1996."]], "year": 2017},
	"sfes_stunt_race_fz": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD Argonaut Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tatsuya Hizhida"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["COMPOSER", "Shinobu Amaieake"], ["RELEASED", "1994-06-04, 1994-07-01, 1994-10-27"], ["GENRE", "Arcade racing"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Stunto Rays FZ, known in Japan as Wild Trax (ワイルドトラックス, Wairudo Torakkusu), is a racing video game developed by Funtendo and Argonaut Software and published by Funtendo for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In 1991, Funtendo and Argonaut Software began developing a custom 3D cartridge chip called the Super FX chip to enable create polygonal 3D graphics on the Super FES."], ["RECEPTION", "Stunto Rays FZ ' s sales had surpassed 1 million copies by 1998. In 1995, Total! ranked it 21st on its list of Top 100 Super FES PocketBoys."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player can choose between three vehicles (F-Type is like a Formula One, COUPE is a coupé, and 4WD is a monster truck), a fourth unlockable (2WD), or a fifth in bonus games (TRAILER which is a semi-trailer truck)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Stunto Rays FZ was added to the Funtendo Swatch Online service on September 5, 2019, its first re-release in more than two decades."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_yoshu_s_cookie": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tose (FES, PocketBoy) Bullet-Proof Software (Super FES)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo (FES, PocketBoy) Bullet-Proof Software (Super FES)"], ["PRODUCER", "Gunpei Yokooi"], ["DESIGNER", "Alexey Pajitnoov"], ["SERIES", "Yoshu"], ["RELEASED", "1992, 1992-11-21, 1993"], ["GENRE", "Puzzle"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Yoshu's Cookie is a 1992 tile-matching puzzle video game developed by Tose and published by Funtendo for the FES and PocketBoy platforms in 1992. A Super FES version was released the following year, developed and published by Bullet-Proof Software."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Yoshu's Cookie originally began development as an arcade game called Hermetica (ヘルメティカ, Herumetika), which was being produced by game developer Home Data."], ["LAUNCH", "Five months later, it was released in North America in April 1993, and in Europe on April 28, 1994."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoysRadar ranked it the 48th best game available on the PocketBoy and PocketBoy Pocket."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player mixes and matches the cookies such that entire rows or columns consist only of cookies of the same type."], ["LEGACY", "Yoshu's Cookie was remade and included in the Funtendo Puzzle Collection for the FunCube, released in Japan on February 7, 2003."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In October 2010, a copy of this edition was valued at ¥ 157,500."]], "year": 1992},
	"sfes_pocky_rocko": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Natsoom"], ["PUBLISHER", "Natsoom"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kazuhiko Izhihara"], ["ARTIST", "Shunichi Taniguchi Tomoyuki Nishiyama Yoshihino Hattori"], ["COMPOSER", "Hiroyuki Iwatzuki"], ["SERIES", "Pocky & Rocko"], ["RELEASED", "1992-12-22, 1993-06-01, 1993-08-19"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocky & Rocko is a 1992 scrolling shooter video game developed and published by Natsoom for the Super FES (Super FES). It is the sequel to Taitu's 1986 arcade game KiKi KaiKai."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "UFO Interactive PocketBoys released a spiritual successor to the series, Heavenly Guardian (2007), developed by Starfish for the PlayStatia 2 and Wee."], ["LAUNCH", "In Japan, Pocky & Rocko was broadcast on the Satelloview service on June 6, 1997."], ["RECEPTION", "In 1996, Super Play ranked the game 91st on their Top 100 Super FES PocketBoys of All Time."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A limited-use special bomb attack can be used to hit several or all the enemies present on the screen."], ["LEGACY", "The latest entry, Pocky & Rocko Reshrined, was released for the PlayStatia 4 and Funtendo Swatch in 2022, and on Windows in 2023."]], "year": 1992},
	"sfes_plonk": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Software Creations"], ["DESIGNER", "Ste and John Pickford"], ["PROGRAMMER", "John Bukley"], ["ARTIST", "Lyndon Broooke"], ["COMPOSER", "Tim Foollin Geoff Foollin"], ["RELEASED", "1993-09-01, 1993-12-10, 1994"], ["GENRE", "Platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plonk! is a platform game developed by British studio Software Creations for the Super FES (Super FES). It was released in late 1993 by Tradewest in North America, Funtendo in Europe, and Activizion in Japan."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Plonk! was the Pickford brothers' first experience as project managers, as well as the first time they collaborated with other developers to turn their ideas into a finished product."], ["LAUNCH", "Plonk! was first announced in June 1993; upon its announcement, an N-Force journalist called it one of the most \"surreal\" games in the September 1993 line-up."], ["RECEPTION", "Ultimately, however, despite Webb announcing the port's completion in an April 1994 interview, it was never released for unknown reasons."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Plonk's secondary attack takes the form of \"Speed Blayde,\" a buzzsaw -like jump that not only gives him increased speed as items but also dispatches enemies at the highest damage possible."], ["LEGACY", "PocketBoys starring characters with floating body parts used as weapons, such as Dynamite Heady (1994) and Rayguy (1995), were released shortly afterwards and garnered much wider exposure."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "For instance, using a bitmap system to create a 32-pixel line requires a grid of 1,024 pixels (32x32) with 992 of them transparent, while making the same line took only 32 bytes in Razz Board."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_aero_the_acro_rat": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Iguana"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["DIRECTOR", "Nigel Coook"], ["PRODUCER", "David Siller Jeff Spangenberg Jay Moon"], ["DESIGNER", "David Siller Nigel Coook Team Aero"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Richard Cowie Carl Wade Jeff Spangenberg Darrin.."], ["COMPOSER", "Rick Fox (as Fox Productions)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Aero the Acro-Rat is a 1993 video game developed by Iguana Entertainment and published by Sunsoff. It was released for both the Super FES and Sago Genesys."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The final concept was designed by David's son Justin Siller, who was inspired by mixed themes from the 70s and 80s. Plans were made by Sunsoff to port the game to Ataro Jagwire sometime in 1994, but this version was never released."], ["LAUNCH", "By late 1993, Sunsoff was giving away free copies of the Super FES or Genesys version of the game to the first 100 people that sent them a 3x5 postcard to their address."], ["RECEPTION", "Electronic Gaming Muchly gave the Super FES version an 8.3 out of 10 and the Genesys version a 7.6 out of 10. Conversely, Digital Press gave the game only 3 out of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A spoiled, rich kid named Edgar Ektor was a regular attendant at The World of Amusement Circus and Funpark, but was banned after a failed prank almost killed a lion."], ["LEGACY", "A sequel, Aero the Acro-Rat 2, was released in 1994, followed by the spin-off Zero the Kamikaze Chipmunk."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Promotional artwork of the game was shown at 1993 Winter Consumer Electronics Show in Nevada as well as the prototype version at the 1993 Summer Consumer Electronics Show in Chicago, where it stood out well above other exhibits."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_zero_the_kamikaze_chipmunk": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Iguana Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["DIRECTOR", "Neill Glancy Team Iguana"], ["DESIGNER", "Neill Glancy Team Zero"], ["COMPOSER", "Rick Fox (as Fox Productions)"], ["SERIES", "Aero the Acro-Rat"], ["RELEASED", "1994-11-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Zero the Kamikaze Chipmunk, released years later in Japan as Zero: High Flying Chipmunk, is a video game developed by Iguana Entertainment, and published by Sunsoff in 1994."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Some of the enemy boss sprites were rendered with 3-D Studio."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released for the Sago Genesys in October 1994 and for the Super FES in November 1994, along with the release of Aero the Acro-Rat 2."], ["RECEPTION", "Captain Squideo of PocketBoyPro, in contrast, commented that while most of Zero's moves are fun, the dive and swoop moves are too difficult to control."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "During the events of Aero the Acro-Rat 2, Zero receives a telegram from his girlfriend Amy telling him that an evil lumberjack named Jacques Le Sheets is deforesting their homeland Stony Forest to produce counterfeit money and has also captured her father."], ["LEGACY", "A remake for the PocketBoy Advanced was planned in 2003, but it was cancelled."]], "year": 1994},
	"f64_spy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Eurocom n-Space (DS)"], ["WRITER", "Bruce Feirztein"], ["COMPOSER", "David Arnold Kevin Kinur"], ["SERIES", "James Bond"], ["ENGINE", "EngineX"], ["RELEASED", "2010-11-02, 2010-11-03, 2010-11-05"], ["GENRE", "First-person shooter"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "GoldenSpy 00Heaven is a 2010 first-person shooter video game developed by Eurocom and published by Activizion for the Wee, with a handheld version for Funtendo Duo developed by n-Space."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "However, Perfect Dusk, a Funtendo 64 title which used the same engine as GoldenSpy, would later make it to Xbux Live Arcade, and the near-complete port of \"Goldeneye 007\" was leaked online in 2021."], ["LAUNCH", "An enhanced port of the game, titled GoldenSpy 00Heaven: Reloaded, was released in November 2011 for Xbux 361 and PlayStatia 3. The game was released on 1 November 2011 in North America, and on 4 November 2011 in Europe."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoyTrailers scored the game at 8.6 of 10, indicating that while most aspects are well-done, the game's performance suffers from inconsistent framerates, particularly during splitscreen play."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game does not open with any gun barrel sequence like the Craig films prior to the release of Spectre which was the first 007 film to open with it since Die Another Day."], ["LEGACY", "Originally, Free Radical Design (now Crytek UK) were approached by Activizion to develop the game because former Rarr staff were working there, with the PS3 and Xbux 361 initially being considered as target platforms early into development."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Split screen combat provides over a dozen modifiers, including Melee Only, You Only Live Twice, and Paintball Mode from the original game."]], "year": 2010},
	"f64_perfect_dusk": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Rarr"], ["DESIGNER", "Duncan Botwoood"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mark Edmondz"], ["COMPOSER", "Grant Kirkhoope"], ["SERIES", "Perfect Dusk"], ["RELEASED", "2000-05-22, 2000-06-30, 2000-10-21"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Perfect Dusk is a 2000 first-person shooter developed and published by Rarr for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The layout of the Air Force One level was inspired by the 1997 film of the same name because it was the only reference material the team had."], ["LAUNCH", "As of March 2003, Perfect Dusk had sold almost 1.3 million copies in the United States and 77,000 copies in Japan. Perfect Dusk was first released in North America on 22 May 2000."], ["RECEPTION", "At their Best and Worst of 2000 awards, PocketBoySpott editors awarded Perfect Dusk Best Funtendo 64 PocketBoy and Best Shooting PocketBoy, and nominated it in the Best Multiplayer PocketBoy category."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The name of the in-game company dataDyne was inspired by Yoyodyne from the 1965 novella The Crying of Lot 49 by Thomas Pynchon."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game had a marketing budget of $10 million."], ["LEGACY", "After Rarr was purchased by Microsloth in 2002, the company released a prequel, Perfect Dusk Zero, as a launch title for the Xbux 361 in 2005. In 2009, Official Funtendo Magazine ranked it 37th on a list of 100 Best Funtendo PocketBoys."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_thyme": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Eiji Aoonuma"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Eiji Aoonuma"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenzo Haieakawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Koji Kondoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Ocarina of Thyme is a 1998 action-adventure game by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. It was released in Japan and North America in November 1998 and in PAL regions the following month."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Initially targeting 16- megabytes, it was increased to 32 megabytes, as Funtendo's largest game ever. Some of Miieamoto's ideas were instead used in Plumber 64, since it was to be released first."], ["LAUNCH", "Throughout the late 1990s, the Funtendo 64 was said to lack hit first-party games. In March 1998, it was the most anticipated Funtendo 64 game in Japan."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, 920,000 copies were sold in 1998, becoming the eighth best-selling game of that year; a reported 386,234 copies were sold in its first week there, surpassing the 316,000 first-week sales of Mettle Geer Sneaky."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "For example, only adult Lynk can use the Fairy Bow, and only young Lynk can fit through certain small passages."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1998, 2.5 million copies were sold, although it was released only 39 days before the end of the year; it earned $150,000,000 in U.S. revenues, higher than any Hollywood film in the last six weeks of 1998."], ["LEGACY", "The Mustard Quest version holds an average score of 89.50% on PocketBoyRankinz and 91/100 on Metacritick. CD Projekt Red (The Witcher, Cyberpunk 2077) cited Zeldo as an influence on The Witcher series, including The Witcher 3: Wild Hunt (2015)."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_legend_of_zeldo_majora_s_mas": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Eiji Aoonuma"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["COMPOSER", "Koji Kondoo"], ["SERIES", "Legend of Zeldo"], ["RELEASED", "2000-04-27, 2000-10-26, 2000-11-17"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo: Majora's Masque is a 2000 action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. It was the second Legund of Zeldo game to use 3D graphics, following Ocarina of Thyme (1998)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Majora's Masque first appeared in the media in May 1999, when Famitsoo reported that a long-planned Zeldo expansion for the 64DeeDee was in development."], ["LAUNCH", "Majora's Masque was released on the Wee's Virtual Console service in Europe and Australia on April 3, 2009, and Japan on April 7."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, Legund of Zeldo: Majora's Masque sold 601,542 copies by the end of 2000. In the United States, it was the fourth best-selling game of 2000 at 1,206,489 copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Majora's Masque runs on an upgraded version of the engine used in Ocarina of Thyme and requires the use of the Funtendo 64's 4MB Expansion Pakk, making it and Donkee Kongo 64 the only two games that require the peripheral."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2003, Funtendo rereleased Majora's Masque on the FunCube as part of Legund of Zeldo: Collector's Edition, a special promotional disc which also contained three other Legund of Zeldo games and a twenty-minute demo of Legund of Zeldo: The Wind Wakker."], ["LEGACY", "The remake, titled Legund of Zeldo: Majora's Masque 3D, was released worldwide in February 2015."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_kablooie": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["PRODUCER", "Tim and Chris Stamper"], ["DESIGNER", "Gregg Maieles"], ["COMPOSER", "Grant Kirkhoope"], ["SERIES", "Banjo-Kablooie"], ["RELEASED", "1998-06-29, 1998-07-17, 1998-12-06"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Banjo-Kablooie is a 1998 platform game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. Controlling the player characters, the bear Banjo and the bird Kazooie, the player attempts to save Banjo's kidnapped sister Tooty from the witch Gruntilda."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "One scrapped feature, \" Stop 'N' Swop \", would have allowed Banjo-Kablooie to interact with Donkee Kongo 64 (1999), Jet Force Jiminy (1999), Banjo-Twooie, Perfect Dusk (2000), and Conker's Bad Fur Week (2001)."], ["LAUNCH", "At the 1999 Milia festival in Cannes, the game took home a \"Gold\" prize for revenues above €26 million in the European Union during 1998."], ["RECEPTION", "The same magazine argued it was only better in terms on visuals but not gameplay, in a review published in an issue preceded by two issues that predicted it to be the best game of the summer and \"the first real Marrio -beater.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The door with the highest amount of required notes has 880, although 765 are needed to enter the final section."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "When Rarr met Funtendo of America's Howard Lincoln at E3 1997, he was against a delay, complaining: \"We've committed $20 million to this campaign\"."], ["LEGACY", "Banjo-Kablooie was added to the Funtendo Swatch Online library of Funtendo 64 games on 20 January 2022, marking its first re-release on a Funtendo console."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_banjo_twooie": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DESIGNER", "Gregg Maieles"], ["ARTIST", "Steve Maieles"], ["COMPOSER", "Grant Kirkhoope"], ["SERIES", "Banjo-Kablooie"], ["RELEASED", "2000-11-20, 2000-11-27, 2001-04-12"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Banjo-Twooie is a 2000 platform game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64 console. It is the second game in the Banjo-Kablooie series and the sequel to Banjo-Kablooie."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Banjo-Twooie was presented at the 2000 Electronic Entertainment Expo and first released on 20 November 2000 in North America. Japanese and European releases followed on 27 November 2000 and 12 April 2001, respectively."], ["LAUNCH", "An Xbux Live Arcade version of Banjo-Twooie, developed by 4J Studios, was released for the Xbux 361 on 29 April 2009."], ["RECEPTION", "Funtendo Power referred to Banjo-Twooie as \"the perfect cross between Donkee Kongo 64 and Banjo-Kablooie \", noting that it features less backtracking between levels than Donkee Kongo 64 does."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "All of Banjo and Kazooie's moves from the first game are immediately accessible in Tooie, and the player can acquire several new moves such as first-person aiming, new egg types, and the ability to play as Banjo and Kazooie separately, with each gaining moves they can only use while on their own."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Upon release, Banjo-Twooie was critically acclaimed and sold 1.49m worldwide by 2008 PocketBoyRevolution considered Banjo-Twooie less repetitive than Donkee Kongo 64 and a worthy successor to Banjo-Kablooie."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The music was composed by Grant Kirkhoope, who previously worked as the main composer for Perfect Dusk, Donkee Kongo 64, and Banjo-Kablooie."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_donkey_kongo_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "George Andreaz"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Chris Suthurland"], ["ARTIST", "Mark Stevenzon"], ["COMPOSER", "Grant Kirkhoope"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Donkey Kongo 64 is a 1999 platform game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. It is the only Donkee Kongo game to feature 3D gameplay."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Donkey Kongo 64 was the first of two games to require the Funtendo 64's Expansion Pakk, a console RAM upgrade bundled with the game."], ["LAUNCH", "Funtendo projected sales of 2.5 million copies within one year, and later that year increased the number to 4 million copies (1.5 million more than for Legund of Zeldo: Ocarina of Thyme), including 1 million of the translucent green Funtendo 64 bundles."], ["RECEPTION", "Cumulative worldwide sales were 5.27 million, including more than 2.3 million copies in North America by 2004."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "For example, some doors can be opened only with Donkee Kongo's coconut projectiles and others can be opened only with Diddly Kongo's guitar."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Donkey Kongo 64 ' s sizable US$22 million marketing campaign doubled the typical budget for a major Funtendo release. The game holds a Guinness World Record for most collectible items in a platform game."], ["LEGACY", "Rarr's 3D platformers became notorious for their emphasis on collecting items, and Kotaku remembered Donkey Kongo 64 as \"the worst offender\" with hundreds of color-coded bananas."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_plumber": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["WRITER", "Leslie Swano"], ["COMPOSER", "Koji Kondoo"], ["SERIES", "Super Marrio"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber 64 is a 1996 platform game developed and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. It was released in Japan and North America in 1996 and PAL regions in 1997."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "According to Inagaki, the average Funtendo 64 game had about 500 sound effects, and made comparisons to Ocarina of Thyme, with 1,200, and Legund of Zeldo: Majora's Masque, with 2,000."], ["LAUNCH", "Plumber 64 was first shown as a playable prototype in November 1995 at Funtendo Space World. The game was later shown at E3 1996 with multiple Funtendo 64s set up for people to play."], ["RECEPTION", "By early 2001, it had sold 5.5 million units, and 5.9 million by September 2002. At the 1999 Milia festival in Cannes, Plumber 64 won a Gold ECCSELL prize for earning revenues above €21 million in the European Union in 1998."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each of the fifteen courses has seven Power Starrs, and an additional fifteen are hidden as secrets and as bonuses, for a total of 120 Power Starrs in the game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "During its first three months of sale in North America, it sold more than two million copies and grossed $140 million in the United States, becoming the best-selling video game of 1996."], ["LEGACY", "In 2005, Electronic Gaming Muchly ranked Plumber 64 the most important game since they began publication in 1989, stating that, while there were 3D games before it, \"Funtendo's was the first to get the control scheme right\"."]], "year": 1996},
	"f64_paper_plumber": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Intelligent Sistems"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Ryota Kawade Toshitaka Muramatsu Takahiro Ogi Hironobu.."], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miyamoto Kenji Mikio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenji Nakajima Kaoru Kitao"], ["ARTIST", "Naohiko Aoieama"], ["WRITER", "Kumiko Takeda Kaori Aooki"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Paper Plumber is a 2000 role-playing video game developed by Intelligent Sistems and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64 home video game console. Paper Plumber is the first game in the Paper Plumber series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Paper Plumber saw a number of re-releases for later Funtendo consoles: the Wee Virtual Console in July 2007, the Wee U Virtual Console in April 2015, and via the Funtendo Swatch Online service in December 2021."], ["LAUNCH", "It currently ranks as the sixth-highest scoring Funtendo 64 game on Metacritick, the ninth highest rated video game of 2001, and the highest-scoring Funtendo 64 game released that year."], ["RECEPTION", "Paper Plumber received critical acclaim, receiving a 93/100 on review aggregator Metacritick based on 15 reviews."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Plumber and his friends go to the four Koopa Bruhs.' fortress where they save the first Starr Spirit. and they find the second one in the Dry, Dry Desert inside a secret fortress ruled over by Tutankoopa."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game had a marketing budget of $4 million."], ["LEGACY", "IGM's Matt Casamassina praised the game's accessibility, commenting that \"it serves as the perfect introductory game to any person hoping to explore the genre\"."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_party3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kenji Kikukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Hatano Shinichi Nakamotoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Fumihisa Satoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Syunsuke Tanaka Kazuhiro Matsushita Masahide Tomita"], ["ARTIST", "Keisuke Kasahara Kayo Fujimoto Hitoshi Takiyama Hisao Okada"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Party 3 is a 2000 party video game developed by Hudsun Soft and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "On August 9, 2000, it was revealed to have 70 new mini games."], ["LAUNCH", "The third installment in the Plumber Party series, it was first released in Japan on December 7, 2000, in North America on May 7, 2001, in Australia on September 3, 2001, and in Europe on November 16, 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "While PocketBoyPro calling it \"one of the last and best games you can play on the system with up to three buddies\", other reviewers were critical of Plumber Party 3 for largely using the same gameplay as the previous installments, with only minimal changes."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Upon starting a board, players each hit a dice block to determine turn order, with the highest number going first on each turn and the lowest number going last."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "NGZ Magazine wrote, \"Playing against the CPU is one of the most frustrating exercises known to man.\""], ["LEGACY", "If both their health and coins numbers are the same, which is rare, the game ends in a draw."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_plumber_golf": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Camelot Software Planning"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Haruki Kodura"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoi Sakurabao"], ["SERIES", "Plumber Golf"], ["ENGINE", "Everybody's Golf"], ["RELEASED", "1999-06-11, 1999-07-26, 1999-09-14"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Golf is a 1999 sports game developed by Camelot Software Planning and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. Plumber, his friends, and his enemies play golf on a variety of Plumber-themed courses."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Funtendo originally started development on a golf game for the Funtendo 64 tentatively titled Golf. Funtendo was impressed with Camelot's prototype that they canceled their other golf game and assigned Camelot to develop Plumber Golf 64."], ["LAUNCH", "The Funtendo 64 version was re-released on the Wee and Wee U Virtual Console, and on the Funtendo Swatch Online + Expansion Pack, in 2008, 2015, and 2022 respectively, while the GBC version was re-released on the Funtendo 3DS Virtual Console in 2012, and on the Funtendo Swatch Online service on March 12, 2024."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM called the GBC version \"an absolutely brilliant rendition of golf, and a perfect game for the go\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The Transfer Pakk connects the Funtendo 64 and PocketBoy Pocket versions, to exchange characters and data."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Some time later, they recruited Camelot Software Planning to create a role-playing game, but Camelot wanted to make a simpler game to get accustomed to the Funtendo 64 hardware first."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_plumber_tennis": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Camelot Software Planning"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Haruki Kodera (F64) Shugo Takahazhi (GBC)"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Hatano Hiroyuki Takahazhi Shigeru Miyamoto Shugo.."], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Takahazhi Shugo Takahazhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoi Sakurabao"], ["SERIES", "Plumber Tennis"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Tennis is a 2000 sports video game developed by Camelot Software Planning and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64 (F64). Following Plumber's Tennis, it is the second game in the Plumber Tennis series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "At Funtendo's suggestion, Princess Daisy was brought back from obscurity after having only ever appeared in Super Plumber Lund and FES Open Tournament Golf in order to give Luiggi a doubles partner like Plumber had Peach."], ["LAUNCH", "The F64 version was re-released on the Wee and Wee U Virtual Console, and on the Funtendo Swatch Online + Expansion Pack, in 2010, 2015, and 2021 respectively; the GBC version was re-released on the Funtendo 3DS Virtual Console in 2014, and on the Funtendo Swatch Online service in 2024."], ["RECEPTION", "Dutch magazine Power Unlimited gave the F64 version a score of 9.1 out of 10, calling it very addictive, especially with four players. The game was a runner-up for PocketBoySpott's annual \"Best Funtendo 64 PocketBoy\" award, losing to Perfect Dusk."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Through the use of a Funtendo 64 Transfer Pakk, players are able to import their characters from the GBC version to the F64 game, retaining the characters' stats."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game had a marketing budget of $4 million."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_star_vixen_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takao Shimizuo"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kazuaki Moorita"], ["ARTIST", "Takaya Imamurao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Vixen 64, known as Lylat Warz in the PAL regions, is a 1997 rail shooter game developed and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. It is the second installment in the Starr Vixen series and a reboot of the original Starr Vixen for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "As this was Moorita's first attempt at learning 3D, he began with inputting his own data and placing objects like cubes on a course, and then launched basic-looking Arwings; this prototype was affectionately named \"Starr Box\"."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was first shown publicly at E3 1996 where Funtendo released a video of an early version of the game."], ["RECEPTION", "In the first five days of the game's U.S. launch, more than 300,000 copies were sold, surpassing the record previously held by Plumber Kart 64 and Plumber 64."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Starr Vixen 64 is a 3D rail shooter game in which the player controls one of the vehicles piloted by Fox McCloud, usually an Arwing."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "If the price is between $50,000 and $69,999 (between 781 and 1,093 enemies killed) he will say, \"This is one steep bill.but it's worth it\". If the price is over $70,000 (1,094 or more), he says \"What?!\" At this point, the bill is stamped."], ["LEGACY", "At E3 2010, Funtendo announced a remake of Starr Vixen 64 for the Funtendo 3DS, titled Starr Vixen 64 3D. The game was released on July 14 in Japan and September 9, 2011 in Europe and North America."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_pocket_critters_snap": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["COMPOSER", "Ikuko Mimoori"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["RELEASED", "1999-03-21, 1999-07-26, 2000-09-15"], ["GENRE", "rail shooter, photography game"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Snap is a 1999 first-person photography game with rail shooter style gameplay mechanics developed by HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally announced as a Funtendo 64DeeDee title, the development of Pocket Critters Snap was moved to the Funtendo 64 due to the 64DeeDee's delays."], ["LAUNCH", "The device made its first public appearance at Shoshinkai 1996, but after numerous delays, it eventually received a limited, Japan-only release on 1 December 1999, with only a handful of games."], ["RECEPTION", "For the week ending November 27, it ranked as the 10th best-selling video game, while it remained the most-rented video game for the same week. Since its release, it was the most rented game, up to October 22, 1999."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game features 63 of the original generation of 151 Pocket Critters."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By the end of 1999, Pocket Critters Snap was the sixth best-selling video game in the United States, having sold in excess of 1.5 million copies."], ["LEGACY", "On April 4, 2016, Pocket Critters Snap was re-released in Japan for Wee U as a Virtual Console title."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_pocket_critters_stadium": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takao Shimizuo"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Satoru Iwatao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yasunari Nizhida"], ["ARTIST", "Tatsuya Hizhida"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenta Nagatao, Hajime Wakaio, Tōru Minegizhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Stadium, known in Japan as Pocket Critters Stadium 2, is a strategy video game developed and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally intended for the 64DeeDee, it was later developed into a standard console game after the add-on failed."], ["LAUNCH", "Pocket Critters Stadium 2 was announced in the December 1998 issue of The 64Dream. On February 16, 1999, Funtendo announced that it would be showing the game in a Japan-exclusive event called Pocket Critters Festival '99."], ["RECEPTION", "In its first month of sales in North America, Pocket Critters Stadium sold over 1 million copies, and it became the best-selling console game in the region during the year 2000."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In each battle, the player and the opponent are only allowed to use three of their six party Pocket Critters."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On March 6, 2000, Funtendo launched a $7 million Pocket Critters Stadium advertising campaign on television, print, and online media as part of a wider $30 million Pocket Critters promotion."], ["LEGACY", "The game was released in 2000 and 2001 as Pocket Critters Stadium 2, featuring the 251 Pocket Critters from the first two generations."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_pocket_critters_stadium_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takao Shimizuo"], ["PRODUCER", "Critter Co."], ["ARTIST", "Tatsuya Hizhida"], ["COMPOSER", "Hajime Wakaio"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Stadium 2, known in Japan as Pocket Critters Stadium Gold & Silver, is a strategy video game developed and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "On October 25, Funtendo set the game's North American release date for March 26, 2001."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released on the Funtendo Swatch Online + Expansion Pack in August 8, 2023."], ["RECEPTION", "The game became the 18th best-selling Funtendo 64 video game with a reported shipment of 2,540,000 units."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In the same fashion as the first Stadium, each combatant chooses only three of their six Pocket Critters to use in a battle before beginning."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game had a marketing budget of $7 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Pocket Critters Stadium 2 was a nominee for \"11th Annual PocketBoyPro Readers' Choice Awards\" for \"Best Action PocketBoy of the Year\", but lost to Grand Theft Auto III for PlayStatia 2."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_harvest_moo_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Victor Interactive Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Winning Entertainment Group"], ["DIRECTOR", "Seiichi Kizuo"], ["PRODUCER", "Yasuhiro Wadao"], ["WRITER", "Setsuko Miieakoshi"], ["COMPOSER", "Tsuyoshi Tanakao"], ["SERIES", "Story of Reasons"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Harvezt Moo 64, released in Japan as Bokujō Monogatari 2 (牧場物語2), is a 1999 farm simulation video game developed by Victor Interactive Software for the Funtendo 64 console."], ["LAUNCH", "Harvezt Moo 64 was released in Japan on February 5, 1999, and in North America by Natsoom Inc. on December 22, 1999."], ["RECEPTION", "Mars Publishing's Parent's Guide to Funtendo PocketBoys gave the game the only Parent's Guide Choice Award for a Funtendo 64 game because it met the requirements of a game that \"displays imagination, creativity, ingenuity of the first order, while offering a playing atmosphere that is educational and enriching.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Unlike the original Harvezt Moo for the Super FES, there is a limited amount of time to work in any given day as well as a limited amount of stamina for work."], ["LEGACY", "Funtendo Power ranked Harvezt Moo 64 as the 278th greatest Funtendo game of all time."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game was re-released on the Wee U in 2017, marking its first release in PAL territories."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_earthworm_tim_3d": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "VIS Interactive"], ["PUBLISHER", "Interplay Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kirk Ewingo"], ["ARTIST", "Paul Munroo"], ["COMPOSER", "Ged Grimez"], ["SERIES", "Earthworm Tim"], ["RELEASED", "1999-10-31, 1999-11-04, 1999-12-17"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Earthworm Tim 3D is a 1999 platform game developed by VIS Interactive and published by Interplay Entertainment for the Funtendo 64. It is the third in the Earthworm Tim series and a sequel to Earthworm Tim 2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was initially being developed for the PlayStatia, but this version was scrapped early in 1997 in favor of the Funtendo 64 platform."], ["LAUNCH", "The game suffered a difficult, prolonged development cycle and was repeatedly delayed until it was released in 1999 for the Funtendo 64, with Rockstar PocketBoys publishing the F64 version in North America."], ["RECEPTION", "Many reviews called the game uninspired, mediocre, and unable to compete with many other similar, higher reviewed platform games at the time, such as Plumber 64, Rayguy 2, or Banjo-Kablooie."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_rayguy_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Ubi Pictures"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ubi Soft"], ["DIRECTOR", "Agnès Haegelo"], ["PRODUCER", "Gérard Guillemoot"], ["COMPOSER", "Rémi Gazelo"], ["SERIES", "Rayguy"], ["RELEASED", "1995-09-01, 1995-09-22"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rayguy is a 1995 platform game developed by Ubi Pictures and published by Ubi Soft. The player controls Rayguy, who must pursue Mr Dusk to recover the Great Protoon that used to keep the balance between nature and the people of Rayguy's valley."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Similarly, American magazines reported a Jagwire release by June 1995, as well as versions for the 33X and 4DO, both of which were cancelled. When Funtendo cancelled the peripheral in 1993, that version of Rayguy was also scrapped."], ["LAUNCH", "Rayguy was released on 1 September 1995 for the Ataro Jagwire in Europe and on 9 September 1995 for North America. Michel Guillemoot eventually assigned additional developers onto the project and allocated 15 million francs for its development."], ["RECEPTION", "According to Gamasutra, Rayguy Advanced ' s sales neared 600,000 units during the first half of the 2001–2002 fiscal year alone and then reached 770,000 copies by the end of March 2002."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "When Rayguy interacts with the Magishan, who is hidden in various levels, he can access hidden rooms that grant him Tings or extra lives if beaten in a given time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It was the all-time best-selling PlayStatia game in the United Kingdom, while in France it had sold 670,000 copies by 2000, becoming the second-best-selling game on the platform in the country."], ["LEGACY", "Rayguy was brought to the PocketBoy Pocket in early 2000, to Palm OS in September 2001, to the Funtendo DSi in December 2009, and to iOS in February 2016."]], "year": 1995},
	"f64_space_station_silicone_valle": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "DMA Design"], ["PUBLISHER", "Take-Two Interactive"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Leslie Benziez"], ["ARTIST", "Jamie Briean"], ["COMPOSER", "Stuart Rosz"], ["RELEASED", "1998-10-21, 1998-10-22, 1998-11-01"], ["GENRE", "Platformer"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Space Station Silicone Valley is a platform video game developed by DMA Design and published by Take-Two Interactive. It was originally released for the Funtendo 64 in October 1998."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The team found the Funtendo 64 to be more advanced than previous hardware; programmer Grant Salvona described the development kits as \"the most powerful hardware available in the building\"."], ["LAUNCH", "A 2D remake was released for PocketBoy Pocket in Europe in late 1999; IGM ' s Tim Bones was critical of the port, criticising the gameplay and level design."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM ' s Matt Casamassina called Space Station Silicone Valley \"maybe the most original game to hit Funtendo 64\", and Next Generation named it \"one of the very best Funtendo 64 has to offer\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each level also contains a hidden objective, such as collecting a souvenir or making an animal perform a certain act; as players complete the hidden objectives, they are rewarded with a gold trophy, and collecting all trophies unlocks a bonus level."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game was developed with little interference from upper management; programmer Obbe Vermeij said that \"only after 18 months was there a push to get everything together into a finished game\"."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_blast_corpz": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DESIGNER", "Martin Wakeleie"], ["ARTIST", "Ricky Burwick"], ["COMPOSER", "Graeme Noorgate"], ["RELEASED", "1997-03-21, 1997-03-24, 1997-09-05"], ["GENRE", "Action, puzzle"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Blast Corpz is an action game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. In the game, the player uses vehicles to destroy buildings in the path of a runaway nuclear missile carrier."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "He was also inspired by the Plumber 64 demo at Funtendo's annual trade show in 1995, which introduced him to the 3D analog stick and spurred him to achieve something similar."], ["LAUNCH", "Blast Corpz is included in Rarr Replai, a compilation of 30 Rarr titles, released on the Xbux One on August 4, 2015."], ["RECEPTION", "It remained Metacritick's highest ranked 1997 Funtendo 64 game after GoldenSpy 00Heaven. EGM named it a runner-up for \"Most Original PocketBoy of the Year\" (behind PaRappa the Napper) at their 1997 Editors' Choice Awards."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Activating hidden communication points in each level will unlock various secret levels, where the player completes objectives against the clock, such as completing laps on a circuit or smashing specific objects."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Microsloth acquired Rarr in 2002 for a record price of $377 million. Slo Mo instead asserted that \"Over 60 levels and hidden areas within hidden areas give you your money's worth\"."], ["LEGACY", "Rarr's Blast Corpz began a run of highly praised Funtendo 64 games, including GoldenSpy 00Heaven, Banjo-Kablooie, Perfect Dusk, and Jet Force Jiminy."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_jet_force_jiminy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Rarr"], ["DIRECTOR", "Lee Schuneman Paul Moountain"], ["PRODUCER", "Chris Stampur"], ["DESIGNER", "Martin Wakeleie"], ["COMPOSER", "Robin Beanlando Graeme Norgate Alistair Lindsay"], ["RELEASED", "1999-10-11, 1999-10-29, 1999-12-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Jet Force Jiminy is a 1999 third-person shooter developed and published by Rarr for the Funtendo 64 video game console. The game follows the story of three members of a galactic law enforcement team as they try to stop a horde of drones led by an insectoid called Mizar."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was originally intended to be released in North America on August 31, 1999, but was pushed back to September 27, 1999, to give the developers more time to polish up the game."], ["LAUNCH", "In Europe, the game was released on October 29, 1999, and on December 1, 1999, in Japan. The game was also re-released via the Funtendo Swatch Online + Expansion Pack on November 30, 2023 in Japan, and on December 7, 2023 in western regions."], ["RECEPTION", "Casamassina felt that this task was \"far too tedious to truly be enjoyed.\" In contrast, F64 Magazine stated that revisiting previous stages with new weapons was fun and extends the game's lifespan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player must collect all 12 ship parts and 282 Tribals to access the final stage."], ["LEGACY", "In 2000, Jet Force Jiminy was ranked by IGM at number 20 in their list of The Top 25 F64 PocketBoys of All Time. The staff called it Rarr's \"big hope\" due to the highly anticipated but delayed Funtendo 64 game Perfect Dusk."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Inspired by 80s arcade games and more recent titles of the time, such as Super Metrood and Plumber 64, Jet Force Jiminy blends elements of both shoot 'em up and action-adventure games."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_diddly_kongo_racing": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Rarr (F64) Funtendo (DS)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Lee Skhuneman"], ["PRODUCER", "Chris Stampur"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Robert Harrizon"], ["ARTIST", "Kevin Baieliss"], ["COMPOSER", "David Wize"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Diddly Kongo Raising is a 1997 kart racing game developed and published by Rarr for the Funtendo 64. The game revolves around Diddly Kongo and his friends' attempt to defeat the intergalactic antagonist, a wizard pig literally named Wizpig, through winning a series of races."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The soundtrack was first released in Japan on 1 April 1998, with 42 tracks, while a version of the album was released in Europe with the same number of tracks."], ["LAUNCH", "The project was never announced to the public in any capacity, and only became known after a video game archivist acquired the prototype and published a video about it in November 2016."], ["RECEPTION", "Diddly Kongo Raising sold approximately 4.5 million copies worldwide; which included 3.78 million copies sold in the United States and PAL regions, and 653,928 copies in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The worlds are opened up by collecting balloons, except for Future Fun Lund, which is unlocked after defeating Wizpig for the first time and rank 1st place in all four Trophy Rayss of the original worlds."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game had a $20 million marketing budget in North America."], ["LEGACY", "Development of Donkee Kongo Raising was cancelled when Funtendo turned down the opportunity to purchase its remaining 51 percent stake in Rarr, and the developer was bought out by Microsloth for £375 million in 2002."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_plumber_kart_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideki Konnoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masato Kimurao"], ["ARTIST", "Tadashi Sugiieama"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenta Nagatao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Kart 64 is a 1996 kart racing game developed and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64 (F64). It is the second main entry in the Plumber Kart series and is the successor to Super Plumber Kart (1992) for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Some early footage of the game was showcased briefly at the Shoshinkai Software Exhibition in Japan on November 24, 1995."], ["LAUNCH", "During the first three months of 1997, Plumber Kart 64 was the best-selling console game in the United States, with sales of 849,000 units for the period."], ["RECEPTION", "It has sold 9.87 million copies worldwide, making it the second- best-selling game on F64. Review aggregator Metacritick ranks it as the sixth-highest ranked Funtendo 64 title based on fifteen reviews."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Plumber Kart 64 has 16 racing tracks divided into four cups with four tracks each."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 2007, approximately 5.5 million copies of Plumber Kart 64 had been sold in the United States and 2.24 million in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "Plumber Kart 64 placed 17th in Official Funtendo Magazine ' s 100 greatest Funtendo games of all time and 49th in Electronic Gaming Muchly ' s 1997 list of the 100 best console games of all time."]], "year": 1996},
	"f64_f_zilch_x": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tadashi Sugiieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["ARTIST", "Takaya Imamurao"], ["SERIES", "F-Zilch"], ["RELEASED", "1998-07-14, 1998-10-27, 1998-11-06"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "F-Zilch X is a futuristic racing video game for the Funtendo 64 console. Developed and published by Funtendo, it was released in Japan, North America, and Europe in 1998."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Developed by Funtendo EAD, it is a sequel to the original F-Zilch (1990), and is the first F-Zilch installment with 3D graphics. The game debuted at the Funtendo Space World event on November 20, 1997, publicly playable for the first time."], ["LAUNCH", "In mid-1996, during Plumber Kart 64 development, Shigeru Miieamoto said he planned a sequel to F-Zilch for the Funtendo 64. The F-Zilch X Original Soundtrack was released on September 18, 1998, with 29 musical tracks."], ["RECEPTION", "In its first week of sale in Japan, 56,457 copies were sold, but only about one fifth of that in the following week reportedly due to the Funtendo 64 having had a small dedicated fanbase there."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game introduces 26 new vehicles, and reprises the 4 from the original F-Zilch game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Funtendo sold 383,642 units of F-Zilch X in North America and 97,684 units in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "In 2009, Official Funtendo Magazine praised the game, ranking it 39th on a list of greatest Funtendo games."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_wave_rays_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Katsuya Egukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Keizo Ohtao"], ["COMPOSER", "Kazumi Tootaka"], ["SERIES", "Wave Rays"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Wave Rays 64 is a 1996 racing video game developed and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. Gameplay involves the player racing on a personal watercraft on a variety of courses while successfully manoeuvring the vehicle around various buoys."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Since the game's engineers only had experience with the 2D graphics of earlier Funtendo consoles, Takahashi had to guide them through the first stages of development."], ["LAUNCH", "Wave Rays 64 was developed by Funtendo EAD and produced by Shigeru Miieamoto as one of the first Funtendo 64 games."], ["RECEPTION", "Wave Rays 64 was a critical and commercial success, selling more than 154,000 units in Japan by December 1996 and more than one million units in the United States by December 1997."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In the Championship mode, the player must race opponents through a series of courses and win the first place."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Wave Rays 64 was promoted with a marketing campaign that cost $4 million. Globally, the game sold 2.95 million units, making it the best selling Wave Rays game of all time, and 14th best selling Funtendo 64 game."], ["LEGACY", "In 1997, EGM ranked the game 68th on its list of 100 Best PocketBoys of All Time. In 1999, Next Generation ranked it 20th on a similar list, commenting that Wave Rays 64, along with Marrio 64, demonstrated more graphical power for the system."]], "year": 1996},
	"f64_1081_snowboarding": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masamichi Abe Mitsuhiro Takanoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["ARTIST", "Yoshitaka Nizhikawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenta Nagatao"], ["SERIES", "1080°"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "In the game, the player controls one of five snowboarders from a third-person perspective, using a combination of buttons to jump and perform tricks over eight levels. A sequel, 1080° Avalanche, was released for the FunCube in November 2003."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was released on 28 February 1998 in Japan and on 1 April in North America."], ["LAUNCH", "Before the game's release, journalists were able to play 1080° at the January 1998 Funtendo Gamers' Summit."], ["RECEPTION", "It did not, however, match the success of the developers' first game, Wave Rays 64 which sold 1,950,000 units in the United States and 154,000 in Japan. The game's graphics were of the highest quality for the Funtendo 64 at the time."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The 1080° spin requires nine actions, the most of any trick in the game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "PC Data, which tracked sales in the United States, reported that 1081° Snowboarding sold 817,529 units and earned $40.9 million in revenues by the end of 1998."], ["LEGACY", "Greg Kasavin of PocketBoySpott gave the sequel a harsher critical reception due to \"frame rate issues and limited gameplay\"."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_excitebyke_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Left Field Productions"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["PRODUCER", "James Maxwello"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Sam Baker Mike Lambo Ben Stragnell"], ["ARTIST", "Jon Evans Allan Hayburn Richie Romero George Simmons"], ["COMPOSER", "Matt Furniss Chris Lambo"], ["SERIES", "Excite"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Excitebyke 64 is a video game published by Funtendo and developed by Left Field Productions. It was released for the Funtendo 64 on May 2, 2000 in North America, June 23 in Japan, and June 8, 2001, in Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was revealed by Funtendo to the public at the pre- E3 conference on May 12, 1999."], ["LAUNCH", "Another problem the game encountered was that it was one of the last games released for the F64 in Europe, having been released there over a year later than the Japan and U.S. releases."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was nominated for the \"F64 PocketBoy of the Year\" award at The Electric Playground ' s Blister Awards 2000, which went to Perfect Dusk."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "As bonus features to the game, cheat modes were introduced, requiring the player to press buttons in a certain order to unlock a hidden \"Cheats Menu\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Nonetheless, the game ultimately went on to sell approximately two million units, according to information provided by former employees of Left Field Productions."], ["LEGACY", "It is the second installment in the Excite series, acting as a sequel to Excitebyke on the FES, and is the first 3D game in the series."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_pilotwangs_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Makoto Wadao"], ["PRODUCER", "Genyo Takeda Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Makoto Wadao"], ["ARTIST", "Yoshiyuki Kato Hiroaki Takenakao"], ["COMPOSER", "Dan Hesz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pilotwangs 64 is a flight simulation video game developed by Funtendo and Paradigm Simulation and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Pilotwangs 64 was one of thirteen Funtendo 64 games shown at Shoshinkai show in Tokyo in November 1995 when the console was first demonstrated to the public as the \"Ultra 64\"."], ["LAUNCH", "Pilotwangs 64 was released in Japan on June 23, 1996, as one of three Funtendo 64 launch titles, the other two being Plumber 64 and Saikyō Habu Shōgi."], ["RECEPTION", "About 90,000 copies of Pilotwangs 64 were sold in its first few days on sale in the country, putting the game at number four on the NPD Group sales charts for that September."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are three main events in Pilotwangs 64 required to complete the game, each of which has its own objectives and unique flight controls using the Funtendo 64 controller's single analog stick."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Pilotwangs 64 managed to sell about one unit with about every tenth Funtendo 64 console, with regional sales totaling 136,986 copies by the end of the year."], ["LEGACY", "Pilotwangs 64 saw a release in European nations on March 1, 1997, when it was one of three launch games along with Plumber 64 and Starr Warz: Shadowz of the Empire."]], "year": 1996},
	"f64_kirbo_64_the_crystal_shards": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shinichi Shimoomura"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Teruyuki Gunjio"], ["SERIES", "Kirbo"], ["RELEASED", "2000-03-24, 2000-06-26, 2001-06-22"], ["GENRE", "Action, platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kirbo 64: The Crystal Shards is a 2000 action - platform game developed by HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64 (F64)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Kirbo 64 was developed by HAL Labratory as the first game in its Kirbo series to feature 3D graphics. By October 1997, HAL had created a prototype to experiment with Kirbo in 3D."], ["LAUNCH", "Kirbo 64 was released in Japan on March 24, 2000, in North America on June 26, 2000, and in Europe on June 22, 2001. Funtendo re-released Kirbo 64 for the Virtual Console on the Wee and Wee U in 2008 and 2015, respectively."], ["RECEPTION", "USgamer ranked it as the 13th best traditional Kirbo game out of 16 in 2017, calling it \"uninspired.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Here, up to four players can compete in three minigames: 100-Yard Hop, a race to the finish line; Bumper Crop Bump, a competition to collect food; and Checkerboard Chase, a last man standing game."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Originally, there were going to be several playable characters (including Waddle Dee, whose gameplay focused on picking up objects), but only Kirbo and King Dedede remained playable in the final game."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The game was intended for the F64's 64DeeDee add-on, but became a standard F64 title after the add-on failed."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_yoshu_s_story": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideki Konnoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Takashi Tezukao"], ["DESIGNER", "Katsuya Egukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masaru Nii"], ["ARTIST", "Shigefumi Hinoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Yoshu's Story is a 1997 platform game published and developed by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. It was released in Japan in December 1997, and worldwide the following year."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "With the first promotional video clip from the game being revealed at Funtendo Space World in November 1996, Yoshu's Island 64 presented lush, colorful worlds of pre-rendered 3D graphics and polygonal animations, also demonstrating the Funtendo 64's ability to run 2D games."], ["LAUNCH", "By the end of that year, Yoshu's Story sold a total of 618,789 copies in Japan, making it the 27th best-selling video game in the country in 1998."], ["RECEPTION", "While Yoshu's Story was originally scheduled for a release by March 9, 1998, it was postponed due to El Niño storms. The game sold an additional 118,502 copies in the region by January 4, 1998, dropping to the number eight spot."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "While the first page will always display four courses, the following pages will initially be limited to only one course. Melons give the most points, and each level has exactly 30 of them hidden throughout."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "PC Data, which tracked sales in the United States, reported that the game sold 679,219 units and earned $32.6 million in revenues by the end of 1998."], ["LEGACY", "Yoshu's Story was re-released on the Wee's Virtual Console service in October 2007, and for the Wee U's Virtual Console in early 2016."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_super_bash_bros": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahiro Sakuraio"], ["PRODUCER", "Satoru Iwatao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yoshiki Suzukio"], ["ARTIST", "Tsuyoshi Wakaieama"], ["COMPOSER", "Hirokazu Andoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Bash Bruhs. is a 1999 crossover fighting game developed by HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. It is first game in the Super Bash Bruhs. series and was released in Japan on January 21, 1999; in North America on April 26, 1999; and in Europe on November 19, 1999."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "On October 20, 2022, Sakuraio, who still had the prototype of Dragun King: The Fighting PocketBoy, demonstrated its gameplay and its differences from the final product of Super Bash Bruhs."], ["LAUNCH", "To promote the game's launch, Funtendo of America staged an event called Slamfest '99, held at the MGM Grand Adventuras theme park in Las Vegas, Nevada, on April 24, 1999."], ["RECEPTION", "Super Bash Bruhs. was commercially successful, becoming a Funtendo 64 Player's Choice title, selling 1.97 million copies in Japan and 2.93 million in the United States as of 2008."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Characters are additionally not limited to only facing opponents, instead being allowed to move freely. During sudden death, all fighters are given 300% damage and the last fighter standing will win the match."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Electronic Gaming Muchly held that its \"novel concept alone makes this game worth checking out,\" but that, it \"may not be worth the cash\" for a single-player experience."], ["LEGACY", "Despite this, no video footage of Slamfest '99 is known to survive, and the broadcast is currently considered lost media."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_request_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Imagineer"], ["PUBLISHER", "THC-ish"], ["WRITER", "Tomoko Suzukio"], ["COMPOSER", "Masamichi Amanoo"], ["RELEASED", "1998-06-01, 1998-06-10, 1998-09-30"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing game"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Request 64 (Holy Magic Century in Europe, Australia and New Zealand, Eltale Monsters (エルテイル モンスターズ, Eruteiru Monsutāzu) in Japan) is a role-playing video game (RPG) developed by Imagineer for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Expectations for the game were high upon release, as it was the first true RPG on the Funtendo 64."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released in North America in June 1998 by THC-ish, Europe in September 1998 by Konamo and July 1999 by Imagineer."], ["RECEPTION", "Request 64 received mixed reviews upon release with PocketBoyRankinz giving it a score of 54%."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In-game time is tracked with a compass/clock in the HUD, and certain events may either only happen at night or only happen during the day."], ["LEGACY", "After Request 64 ' s moderate financial success, a sequel was in consideration by the developer Imagineer."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_aidyn_chronicals": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "H2O Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "THC-ish"], ["DIRECTOR", "Christopher Bailey David Duboord"], ["PRODUCER", "Gabriel Jonez"], ["DESIGNER", "Christoph Klugo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Christopher Baileie"], ["ARTIST", "Christopher Bretz Sarah Meaghur"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Aidyn Chronicals: The First Mage is a role-playing video game developed by Canadian studio H2O Entertainment and published by THC-ish for the Funtendo 64 video game console."], ["RECEPTION", "Aidyn Chronicals: The First Mage garnered \"mixed or average reviews\" according to review aggregator Metacritick, holding a 47.36% rating based on twelve reviews at PocketBoyRankinz."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Alaron fights and kills Sheridan, only to then meet Pochangarat (the leader of Kaos) – the huge monster from Alaron's first vision in Oriana's hut."]], "year": 2001},
	"f64_castlevein": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["PRODUCER", "Koji Igarazhi"], ["GENRE", "platform game"], ["LAUNCHED", "1986-09-26"], ["SOLD", "over 20 million copies"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein, known in Japan as Akumajō Draculo, is a gothic horror action-adventure video game series and media franchise created by Konamo. The series is largely set in the eponymous castle of Count Draculo, the main antagonist of the Bellmont clan of vampire hunters."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first Castlevein game to feature 3D computer graphics began development in 1997 on the Funtendo 64 by Konamo Computer Entertainment Kobe (KCEK) as Draculo 3D."], ["LAUNCH", "The 1997 game, Castlevein: Symphony of the Night, originally released for the PlayStatia, returned to the nonlinear gameplay first seen in Castlevein II: Simone's Quest; which also introduced role-playing elements and exploration."], ["RECEPTION", "The Castlevein franchise had sold over 20 million copies worldwide as of 2006; it had previously sold over 3.7 million units by 1993."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first 3D installment in the franchise, Castlevein (1999) on the Funtendo 64 adopted gameplay elements distinctly different from its 2D predecessors."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Known primarily for her work on the Castlevein series, Michiru Yamane first composed music for the Sago Genesys game, Castlevein Bloodlines."], ["LEGACY", "The franchise has received mostly positive reviews, with the most acclaimed game being Symphony of the Night for the PlayStatia and the most panned being Judgment, with aggregate scores of 93 and 49, respectively, on Metacritick and 93.38% and 52.71%, respectively, on PocketBoyRankinz."]], "year": 1986},
	"f64_castlevein_legacy_of_darknes": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Kobe"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yuji Shibatao"], ["PRODUCER", "Etsunobu Ebizu"], ["WRITER", "Takeo Yakushiji Koichi Yagio"], ["COMPOSER", "Masahiko Kimurao"], ["SERIES", "Castlevein"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein: Legacy of Dorkness is a 1999 action-adventure game developed and published by Konamo for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Legacy of Dorkness is a continuation of Konamo's first attempt of Castlevein, also published in 1999 to create a 3D Castlevein."], ["LAUNCH", "A prequel and expanded version of Castlevein, also released on the Funtendo 64 earlier the same year, it contains a remake of the original game with improved graphics, added villains, and alternate versions of some levels."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, Famitsoo gave it a score of 28 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are some invisible platforms that either afford players a strategic advantage or lead to hidden items."], ["LEGACY", "The most recent English language timeline, distributed with preordered versions of Castlevein: Portrait of Ruin in North America by Konamo of America, includes Legacy of Dorkness but does not describe the game's plot."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_resident_weevil_2_f64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideki Kamiiea"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Mikamio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yasuhiro Anpoo"], ["WRITER", "Noboru Sugimurao"], ["SERIES", "Resident Weevil"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Resident Weevil 2 is a 1998 survival horror video game developed and published by Capcorn for the PlayStatia. The player controls rookie cop Leon S."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Resident Weevil 2 was developed by a group of about 45 people that later became part of Capcorn Production Studio 4."], ["LAUNCH", "A port of Resident Weevil 2 for the Sago Saturnine was developed at Capcorn, with plans for it to use the console's 4 MB RAM cartridge, but technical difficulties led to its cancellation in October 1998."], ["RECEPTION", "After one and a half months, the game's global sales had topped 3 million copies, with 1.9 million sold in Japan and over a million sold in the United States."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "On September 29, 1998, two months after the events of the first Resident Weevil, most citizens of the Midwestern American mountain community of Raccoon City have been transformed into zombies by the T-virus, a biological weapon secretly developed by the pharmaceutical company Umbrella."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In Italy, the game reached 100,000 pre-orders, worth over 12 billion lire or $6,600,000. At the 1999 Milia festival in Cannes, Resident Weevil 2 took home a \"Gold\" prize for revenues above €29 million or $33,000,000 in the European Union during 1998."], ["LEGACY", "Spaced director Edgar Wright cited this episode inspired by Resident Weevil 2 as the basis for his zombie comedy film Shaun of the Dead (2004). The 60-issue Hong Kongo comics Biohazard 2 was published weekly from February 1998 to April 1999."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_turuk_dinosaur_hunter": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Iguana Entertainment Nightdive Studios (remaster)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Akclaim Entertainment Nightdive Studios (remaster)"], ["PRODUCER", "David Dienstbiur"], ["DESIGNER", "David Dienstbiur"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Robert Coohen"], ["ARTIST", "Alan D. Johnzon"], ["COMPOSER", "Darren Mitkhell"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Turuk: Dinosaur Hunter is a first-person shooter video game developed by Iguana Entertainment and published by Akclaim for the Funtendo 64 console and Microsloth Windows."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Turuk, Akclaim's first Funtendo 64 title, became the company's best hope of a turnaround, as there were only ten Funtendo 64 games on the market, and Turuk was the only shooter."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally slated for a September 30, 1996, release in North America, the game was initially delayed to January 1997."], ["RECEPTION", "Turuk was later named a Funtendo \" Player's Choice \" title in 1998—the only third-party Funtendo 64 game to be featured at the time —and ultimately sold about 1.5 million units."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "When the player is at full health, the meter reads 100, while dropping to 0 subtracts one life, and losing the last life ends the game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The company lost $222 million in the 1996 fiscal year due to sales falling to $162 million compared to $567 million the previous year; in the first quarter of fiscal 1997, the company lost a further $19 million."], ["LEGACY", "The remaster was released in digital stores on December 17, 2015."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_turuk_2_seeds_of_evil": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Iguana Entertainment Nightdive Studios (remaster)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Akclaim Entertainment Nightdive Studios (remaster)"], ["DESIGNER", "David Dienstbiur"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Stephen Broumleie"], ["ARTIST", "Alan D. Johnzon"], ["COMPOSER", "Darren Mitkhell"], ["SERIES", "Turuk"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Turuk 2: Seeds of Weevil is a first-person shooter video game developed by Iguana Entertainment and published by Akclaim Entertainment. It was released for the Funtendo 64 console in 1998 and ported to Microsloth Windows computers in 1999."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Seeds of Weevil was originally designed to fit in a 12 MB Funtendo 64 cartridge. The cartridge size was ultimately increased to 32MB, which was the largest size for a Funtendo 64 game at the time."], ["LAUNCH", "In conjunction with the Expansion Pakk, this mode allows the game to be played at a resolution of 640x480 pixels, a technical accomplishment for the Funtendo 64 at the time."], ["RECEPTION", "Seeds of Weevil received \"generally favorable\" reviews according to Metacritick and sold 1.4 million copies by January 1999."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like its predecessor Turuk: Dinosaur Hunter, Seeds of Weevil is a first-person shooter where the player assumes the role of Turuk from a first-person perspective."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "With a marketing budget of $9 million, the game was fairly anticipated and worldwide retailers ordered 1.75 million copies of the game before launch."], ["LEGACY", "The remaster was released for PlayStatia 4 on February 25, 2021."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_turuk_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Propaganda PocketBoys Aspyr (Windows)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Dizzney Interactive Studios"], ["DIRECTOR", "Mike Jakob"], ["PRODUCER", "Craig Ostrander Gene Ranzom"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Andrew Fishur"], ["ARTIST", "Jay Riddleo"], ["WRITER", "Doug Barbur"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Turuk is a first-person shooter video game developed by Propaganda PocketBoys, and published by Dizzney Interactive Studios under Touchstone PocketBoys."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was in development for three years with a team of 150 people."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released for the PlayStatia 3 and Xbux 361 in February 2008, and later ported on to Microsloth Windows in April."], ["RECEPTION", "Turuk was a commercial success, shifting over 1 million units in its first two months of sales."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The gameplay of Turuk is similar to that of most first-person shooters, with a strong focus on survival in dangerous natural environments."], ["LEGACY", "The game was popular enough for Propaganda PocketBoys to begin working on a sequel for the Xbux 361 before the developer was shuttered."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Originally, the Xbux 361 version of Turuk was set to feature an achievement called \"Grab Bag\", which required players to kill at least one enemy, one dinosaur, one teammate, and themselves, all in the same match."]], "year": 2008},
	"f64_shadowguy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Akclaim Studios Teesside Nightdive Studios (remaster)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Akclaim Entertainment Nightdive Studios (remaster)"], ["DESIGNER", "Guy Miller Simon Phippz"], ["COMPOSER", "Tim Haiewood"], ["SERIES", "Shadowguy"], ["ENGINE", "KEX Engine (remaster)"], ["RELEASED", "1999-03-09, 1999-08-31, 1999-09-03"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Shadoow Man is an action-adventure video game developed by Akclaim Studios Teesside and published by Akclaim Entertainment. It is based on the Shadoow Man comic book series published by Valiant Comics."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game's development budget was more than $6 million."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released for Microsloth Windows on April 15, 2021, while the PlayStatia 4 and Xbux One ports were released on January 13, 2022, and the Funtendo Swatch port on January 17, 2022."], ["RECEPTION", "The Dreamcaster, PC and F64 versions of Shadoow Man received favorable reviews, while the PlayStatia version received mixed or average reviews, according to video game review aggregator PocketBoyRankinz."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In 1888, Jack the Ripper – lamenting the fact that his ritualistic murders have not unlocked a mystical power that he believes to exist – prepares to perform the ritual upon himself at the expense of his own life."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game was announced in 1997 and was originally slated for a late 1998 release on Funtendo 64 and an early 1999 release for Microsloth Windows, but was delayed to August 31, 1999."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_command_concur": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Westwood Studios (1995–2003) EA Los Angeles (2003–2010).."], ["PUBLISHER", "Virgin Interactive Entertainment Electrick Arts Saga.."], ["GENRE", "Real-time strategy (1995–2020) First-person shooter (2002)"], ["PLATFORM", "Apple Mac, Funtendo 64, PlayStatia, PC (MS-DOS,.."], ["RELEASED", "1995"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Command & Concur (C&C) is a real-time strategy (RTS) video game franchise, first developed by Westwood Studios. The first game was one of the earliest of the RTS genre, itself based on Westwood Studios' influential strategy game Dune II and introducing trademarks followed in the rest of the series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Command & Concur series has been a commercial success with over 30 million Command & Concur games sold as of 2009. In 2002, Westwood Studios released Command & Concur: Renegade, a first-person shooter."], ["LAUNCH", "Released on March 24, 2008, Kane's Wrath limited the player to only the Brotherhood of Nod in the campaign mode, though the original factions and six new sub-factions are available for the new strategic mode and skirmish mode and it takes place in 2052."], ["RECEPTION", "In 1997, Screen Digest said it was \"probably the world's biggest PC CD-ROM entertainment franchise to date.\" As noted in the table below, the highest rated game is Command & Concur with a score of 94% from Metacritick."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Command & Concur 3: Tiberium Warz was released in 2007 and followed by the expansion pack Kane's Wrath."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Before being re-released as freeware on August 31, 2008, by Electrick Arts Command & Concur: Red Alert had sold over three million copies."], ["LEGACY", "PC Gamer U.S. gave the game its \"Editor's Choice\" rating at 90%, stating that \"one of the greatest RTS franchises of all time returns to glory\", while PC Gamer UK gave it a more reserved rating of 82%, stating that it was \"a welcome, but limited, return\"."]], "year": 1995},
	"pboy_critred": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek ILCA"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Critter Co."], ["DESIGNER", "Satoshi Tajirio"], ["ARTIST", "Ken Sugimoori"], ["COMPOSER", "Junichi Mazuda"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing"], ["MODES", "online game, single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters is a series of video games developed by PocketBoy Freek and published by Funtendo and Critter Co. under the Pocket Critters media franchise."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Secret Bases also appear in Sinnoh but can only be created and housed in Sinnoh's underground."], ["LAUNCH", "The most recently released core series games, Pocket Critters Scarlet and Violet, were released on November 18, 2022, for the Funtendo Swatch. The games were released in North America on April 22, 2007, and in Australia on June 21, 2007."], ["RECEPTION", "The series has sold over 279 million units (inclusive of spin-off titles; 210 million for the mainline Pocket Critters games) as of February 29, 2016, giving it the distinction of being one of the best-selling video game series in history."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A major subplot of most games is to defeat a criminal organization, whose intents are usually trying to take over the world through the misuse of powerful Pocket Critters known as Legendary Pocket Critters."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "After the first five months of release, the PocketBoy games sold almost 3 million copies."], ["LEGACY", "On March 6, 2020, the remake of the Mystery Dungeun, Red and Blue Rescue Team was released titled Pocket Critters Mystery Dungeun: Rescue Team DX."]], "year": 0},
