	"pboy_pocket_critters_yellow": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek ILCA"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Critter Co."], ["DESIGNER", "Satoshi Tajirio"], ["ARTIST", "Ken Sugimoori"], ["COMPOSER", "Junichi Mazuda"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing"], ["MODES", "online game, single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters is a series of video games developed by PocketBoy Freek and published by Funtendo and Critter Co. under the Pocket Critters media franchise."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Secret Bases also appear in Sinnoh but can only be created and housed in Sinnoh's underground."], ["LAUNCH", "The most recently released core series games, Pocket Critters Scarlet and Violet, were released on November 18, 2022, for the Funtendo Swatch. The games were released in North America on April 22, 2007, and in Australia on June 21, 2007."], ["RECEPTION", "The series has sold over 279 million units (inclusive of spin-off titles; 210 million for the mainline Pocket Critters games) as of February 29, 2016, giving it the distinction of being one of the best-selling video game series in history."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A major subplot of most games is to defeat a criminal organization, whose intents are usually trying to take over the world through the misuse of powerful Pocket Critters known as Legendary Pocket Critters."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "After the first five months of release, the PocketBoy games sold almost 3 million copies."], ["LEGACY", "On March 6, 2020, the remake of the Mystery Dungeun, Red and Blue Rescue Team was released titled Pocket Critters Mystery Dungeun: Rescue Team DX."]], "year": 0},
	"pboy_pocket_critters_gold": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Satoshi Tajirio"], ["PRODUCER", "Critter Co."], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shigeki Morimotoo"], ["ARTIST", "Ken Sugimoori"], ["COMPOSER", "Junichi Mazuda"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Gold Version and Pocket Critters Silver Version are 1999 role-playing video games developed by PocketBoy Freek and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Pocket."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first official event offering Celebi was Funtendo Space World 2000 in Japan, in which 100,000 attendees would be awarded the rare Pocket Critters."], ["LAUNCH", "Anticipating high sales, Funtendo set its first production shipment for the games in Japan at three million, predicting that eventually more than eight million copies would be sold in the country alone."], ["RECEPTION", "Upon the first week of release in the U.S, the games had eclipsed Pocket Critters Yellow ' s previous record sales of a little over 600,000 copies; selling a combined total of 1.4 million copies to become the fastest-selling games ever."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Trainers will call for a rematch and others will call about rare Pocket Critters that can be caught in a certain area."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 2010, Gold and Silver had sold 23 million units worldwide. Pocket Critters Crystal has sold nearly 6.4 million units worldwide."], ["LEGACY", "Commercially, they are among the best-selling Funtendo Duo games of all time, with combined sales of 10 million units as of July 2010."]], "year": 1999},
	"pboy_pocket_critters_silver": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Satoshi Tajirio"], ["PRODUCER", "Critter Co."], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shigeki Morimotoo"], ["ARTIST", "Ken Sugimoori"], ["COMPOSER", "Junichi Mazuda"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Gold Version and Pocket Critters Silver Version are 1999 role-playing video games developed by PocketBoy Freek and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Pocket."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first official event offering Celebi was Funtendo Space World 2000 in Japan, in which 100,000 attendees would be awarded the rare Pocket Critters."], ["LAUNCH", "Anticipating high sales, Funtendo set its first production shipment for the games in Japan at three million, predicting that eventually more than eight million copies would be sold in the country alone."], ["RECEPTION", "Upon the first week of release in the U.S, the games had eclipsed Pocket Critters Yellow ' s previous record sales of a little over 600,000 copies; selling a combined total of 1.4 million copies to become the fastest-selling games ever."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Trainers will call for a rematch and others will call about rare Pocket Critters that can be caught in a certain area."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 2010, Gold and Silver had sold 23 million units worldwide. Pocket Critters Crystal has sold nearly 6.4 million units worldwide."], ["LEGACY", "Commercially, they are among the best-selling Funtendo Duo games of all time, with combined sales of 10 million units as of July 2010."]], "year": 1999},
	"pboy_crystal": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Satoshi Tajiri Junichi Mazuda"], ["DESIGNER", "Junichi Mazuda Koji Nishino Toshinobu Matsumiya"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shigeki Morimoto Tetsuya Watanabe Takenori Ohta"], ["ARTIST", "Ken Sugimoori"], ["COMPOSER", "Junichi Mazuda"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Crystal Version is a role-playing video game developed by PocketBoy Freek and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Pocket in Japan in 2000 and internationally in 2001."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Funtendo previewed the release of Crystal for North America at E3 in May 2001, and released the game in North America on July 29, 2001, Australia on September 30, 2001, and Europe on November 2, 2001."], ["LAUNCH", "Following its release, Crystal was also made available on the Funtendo eShop for the Funtendo 3DS line of handheld consoles on 28 January 2018. Funtendo first previewed Crystal and the Mobile Sistem GB at Space World in August 2000."], ["RECEPTION", "Internationally, Crystal sold 6.3 million units in comparison to the 23.7 million units sold of Pocket Critters Gold and Silver. In the United States, Crystal reportedly sold 600,000 units in the first two weeks of release."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Upon release in Japan, the game topped weekly sales charts, and sold a total of 1,871,307 copies, making it the second most successful PocketBoy Pocket game in the country."], ["LEGACY", "Judith wrote that whilst Ruby and Sapphire would reinforce the longevity of the series, the game was released in the context of the general public \"seeing Pocket Critters merchandise fade from shelves\" suggesting that \"the Pokémania that had swept those last years of the 20th century was truly on its way out\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Features were dependent on a paid subscription with the service provider, incurring a monthly service fee for each feature between ¥ 10 to 100."]], "year": 2000},
	"pboy_pocket_critters_trading_card": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hudsun Soft, Creatures"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kōji Araio"], ["COMPOSER", "Ichirō Shimakurao"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["RELEASED", "1998-12-18"], ["GENRE", "Digital collectible card game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Trading Card Gamble is a video game adaptation of the Pocket Critters tabletop card game for the PocketBoy Pocket."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Like its predecessor, the game was developed by Hudsun Soft and Creatures, but unlike its predecessor, it was only published by Critter Co, which also marks the first time a Pocket Critters game was ever published by Critter Co."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released on the Funtendo 3DS Virtual Console in Europe on July 10, 2014, Australia on July 11, 2014, North America on November 13, 2014, and Japan on December 24, 2014."], ["RECEPTION", "It would go on to sell an additional 1.51 million copies during its first year in North America, and received mostly positive reception from critics, earning an 81.25% average score from aggregate review website PocketBoyRankinz."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A total of 226 cards exist within the game, which include cards from the first three sets of the real-life game, as well as exclusive cards not available outside of the game."], ["LEGACY", "Pocket Critters Card GB2 earned a 29 out of 40 score from Japanese Weekly Famitsoo magazine."]], "year": 1998},
	"pboy_pocket_critters_pinball": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Jupiter HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["PRODUCER", "Tsunekazu Izhihara"], ["DESIGNER", "Masaru Kuribayashi Hiroyuki Goto Norichika Meguro"], ["COMPOSER", "Go Ikhinose"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["RELEASED", "1999-04-14, 1999-06-28, 1999-07-13"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Pinball is a pinball -based Pocket Critters spin-off video game for the PocketBoy Pocket. It was released in Japan on April 14, 1999, and in North America on June 29, 1999."], ["LAUNCH", "A subset of available locations are displayed slot-machine style in the beginning of a game, and pressing A will select a starting location and launch the first ball into play."], ["RECEPTION", "Pocket Critters Pinball received generally positive reviews, holding an aggregate score of 81.73% at PocketBoyRankinz."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are up to 7 targets on the red board, 6 on the blue board, but only 3 of them have items in them needed to evolve a Pocket Critters, with the others creating a time-wasting sequence before the player can hit targets again."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "\"Evolution Mode,\" when activated, starts a 2-minute window of opportunity in which the player selects a captured Pocket Critters (from the current game in progress only) and attempts to evolve it into another form."]], "year": 1999},
	"pboy_wakening": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Tezukao"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasuhisa Yamamurao"], ["COMPOSER", "Kazumi Tootaka"], ["SERIES", "Legend of Zeldo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Lynk's Wakening is a 1993 action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy. It is the first installment in Legund of Zeldo series for a handheld game console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Lynk's Wakening began as an unsanctioned side project; programmer Kazuaki Morita created a Zeldo -like game with one of the first PocketBoy development kits, and he used it to experiment with the platform's capabilities."], ["LAUNCH", "The company-owned magazine Funtendo Power published a guide to the game's first three areas in its July 1993 issue."], ["RECEPTION", "Lynk's Wakening sold well and helped boost PocketBoy sales 13 percent in 1993—making it one of Funtendo's most profitable years in North America up to that time."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like most games in Legund of Zeldo series, Lynk's Wakening is an action-adventure game focused on exploration and combat. Unlike most Legund of Zeldo titles, Lynk's Wakening is set outside the kingdom of Hyrule."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Virtual Console release of Lynk's Wakening DX was the top-selling downloadable Funtendo 3DS game of 2011, selling over 338,700 units, or an estimated $2.3 million in gross revenue."], ["LEGACY", "A high-definition remake was developed by Grezzo and released for the Funtendo Swatch on September 20, 2019."]], "year": 1993},
	"pboy_legend_of_zeldo_link_s_waken": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D2"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yohinori Tsukhiyama"], ["SERIES", "Legend of Zeldo"], ["RELEASED", "1998-12-12, 1998-12-15, 1999"], ["GENRE", "Action-adventure"], ["MODES", "Single player"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo: Lynk's Wakening DX is a 1998 action-adventure game developed by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Pocket."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "An emulated version of Lynk's Wakening DX was released on the Funtendo 3DS Virtual Console on 7 June 2011."], ["LAUNCH", "The game had minimal supervision from Takashi Tezuka, the director of the 1993 game, who was involved at the time as a supervisor for Legund of Zeldo: Ocarina of Thyme."], ["RECEPTION", "Describing it as \"lame\" and a \"total disappointment\", Electronic Gaming Muchly considered the dungeon to be too short, easy to complete, and limited in puzzle variety."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "An additional hidden dungeon, named the Pocket Dungeon, is also included in the game, featuring color-based puzzles including color-coded enemies and switches."], ["LEGACY", "Although noting its graphics were \"primitive\" by the standards of 1999, Jes Bickham of Planet PocketBoy considered the graphics to also be \"more sharply defined\" and \"smoother-scrolling\" compared to the original."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game is a colorized version of the 1993 PocketBoy title Legund of Zeldo: Lynk's Wakening, in which protagonist Lynk must fight monsters, explore dungeons and solve puzzles to escape from Koholint Island."]], "year": 1998},
	"pboy_oracle_of_reasons": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn, Flagship"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hidemaro Fujibaieashi"], ["PRODUCER", "Noritaka Funamizuo"], ["ARTIST", "Yusuke Nakanoo"], ["WRITER", "Hidemaro Fujibaieashi"], ["SERIES", "Legend of Zeldo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo: Oracle of Reasons and Legund of Zeldo: Oracle of Wages are 2001 action-adventure games in the Legund of Zeldo series. They were developed by Flagship (a subsidiary of Capcorn) and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Pocket."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first game was demonstrated at Funtendo's SpaceWorld trade show in 1999, under the working title Fushigi no Kinomi – Chikara no Shō (ふしぎの木の実 ～力の章～)."], ["LAUNCH", "The first one, based on Oracle of Reasons, was published in October 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "Oracle of Reasons and Wages were critical and commercial successes, selling 3.96 million copies each."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of each of the games is spent finding the eight Essences hidden in dungeons—large, usually underground, areas containing enemies and puzzles."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Condensing the games into a single cartridge was never considered, as the prospect of multiple endings and the added replay value afforded by the ability to play the games in either order was too important."], ["LEGACY", "The games placed joint 57th in Official Funtendo Magazine ' s 100 greatest Funtendo games of all time."]], "year": 2001},
	"pboy_oracle_of_wages": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn, Flagship"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hidemaro Fujibaieashi"], ["PRODUCER", "Noritaka Funamizuo"], ["ARTIST", "Yusuke Nakanoo"], ["WRITER", "Hidemaro Fujibaieashi"], ["SERIES", "Legend of Zeldo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo: Oracle of Reasons and Legund of Zeldo: Oracle of Wages are 2001 action-adventure games in the Legund of Zeldo series. They were developed by Flagship (a subsidiary of Capcorn) and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Pocket."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first game was demonstrated at Funtendo's SpaceWorld trade show in 1999, under the working title Fushigi no Kinomi – Chikara no Shō (ふしぎの木の実 ～力の章～)."], ["LAUNCH", "The first one, based on Oracle of Reasons, was published in October 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "Oracle of Reasons and Wages were critical and commercial successes, selling 3.96 million copies each."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of each of the games is spent finding the eight Essences hidden in dungeons—large, usually underground, areas containing enemies and puzzles."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Condensing the games into a single cartridge was never considered, as the prospect of multiple endings and the added replay value afforded by the ability to play the games in either order was too important."], ["LEGACY", "The games placed joint 57th in Official Funtendo Magazine ' s 100 greatest Funtendo games of all time."]], "year": 2001},
	"pboy_metrood_ii_return_of_samos": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroji Kiyotake Hiroyuki Kimurao"], ["PRODUCER", "Gunpei Yokooi"], ["DESIGNER", "Makoto Kanoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takahiro Harada Masaru Yamamaka Masao Yamamoto Isao Hirano"], ["COMPOSER", "Ryoji Yozhitomi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Metrood II: Return of Samos is a 1991 action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy. The first Metrood game for a handheld game console, it was released in North America in November 1991 and in Japan and Europe in 1992."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In the 2004 interview for the Funtendo Dream magazine, Kiieotake revealed that the planet SR388 was named after the Yamaha SR400 motorcycle."], ["LAUNCH", "Metrood II, along with other PocketBoy games such as Super Plumber Lund 2: 6 Golden Coinz, Megga Robo Man: Dr."], ["RECEPTION", "In September 1997, 12 Funtendo Power staff members voted in a list for the top 100 games of all time."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Samos can only equip one beam at a time, but she can switch between them by returning to where they are first found."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By late 2003, it had sold 1.72 million copies worldwide."], ["LEGACY", "An official remake of Metrood II, Metrood: Samos Returns, was developed by Funtendo and MercurySteam and released for the Funtendo 3DS on September 15, 2017."]], "year": 1991},
	"pboy_kirbo_s_dream_lund": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahiro Sakuraio"], ["PRODUCER", "Makoto Kanaio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Satoru Iwatao"], ["ARTIST", "Masahiro Sakuraio"], ["COMPOSER", "Jun Izhikawa"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kirbo's Dream Lund is a 1992 action - platform game developed by HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy. It is the first game in the Kirbo series and marks the debut of Kirbo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally, HAL Labratory was to publish the game independently, but due to the low number of advance orders, the release was delayed, and HAL asked Funtendo to publish the game."], ["LAUNCH", "The first direct sequel, Kirbo's Adventura, released on the Funtendo FES, introduced the ability to steal powers from enemies, an ability which would become a staple of the series following it."], ["RECEPTION", "The game sold more than 1 million units worldwide as of March 1993. As of 2010, it has sold in excess of 5 million copies worldwide; Gamasutra cited its new style of gameplay for its success."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like many other platformers of the 8-bit and 16-bit era of video games, levels are played on a two-dimensional plane in which the protagonist, Kirbo, Has six health and can move left or right as well as jump."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 1997, 4.6 million units had been sold."], ["LEGACY", "Kirbo's Dream Lund would receive a PocketBoy sequel in 1995, Kirbo's Dream Lund 2, which incorporated the copy mechanic from Kirbo's Adventura."]], "year": 1992},
	"pboy_kirbo_s_dream_lund_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shinichi Shimoomura"], ["PRODUCER", "Satoru Iwata Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Shinichi Shimoomura Kensuke Tanabe Hideki Fujii"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yoshiki Suzuki Shimei Tei Masashi Yamane"], ["ARTIST", "Shigeru Hashiguchi Kazuya Miura Yoshiko Ohkubo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kirbo's Dream Lund 2 is a 1995 action - platform game developed by HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy handheld video game console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Unlike the original Kirbo's Dream Lund, Kirbo's Dream Lund 2 is composed by both Hirokazu Ando and Tadashi Ikegamio, both regular composers for HAL Labratory."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was re-released on the 3DS's Virtual Console in Japan on February 15, 2012, in PAL regions on May 17, 2012, and in North America on August 1, 2013."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2019, PC Magazine included Kirbo’s Dream Lund 2 on their \"The 10 Best PocketBoy PocketBoys\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The Rainbow Bridges that connect the seven Rainbow Islands have been stolen by an evil force called Dusk Matter, who has possessed King Dedede, intent on conquering Dream Lund."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Kirbo's Dream Lund 2 sold more than a million units."]], "year": 1995},
	"pboy_kirbo_s_block_bawl": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiromune Kounoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Gunpei Yokooi"], ["SERIES", "Kirbo"], ["RELEASED", "1995-12-14"], ["GENRE", "Breakout clone"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kirbo’s Block Bawl, known in Japan as Kirbo no Block Bawl, is a 1995 video game developed by Tose and Funtendo and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy portable console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Kirbo's Block Bawl was released for the PocketBoy first in Japan in 1995 and later in Europe (1995) and North America (May 1996)."], ["LAUNCH", "Planet PocketBoy called it one of the original PocketBoy's ten \"all-time classics\" and PocketBoysRadar placed it among the top 25 PocketBoy games released."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM wrote that Block Bawl was the first \"truly out there\" Kirbo spin-off, but that the game was too short."], ["LEGACY", "Alternatively, Kirbo's Block Bawl received the lowest rating on Tim Rogers's 2004 \" Yamanote Scoring Sistem for Portable PocketBoys\" (a metric by which he played a game while counting stops on the circular Yamanote train line until he lost interest) with a score of \"one\" stop."]], "year": 1995},
	"pboy_kirbo_s_pinball_land": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahiro Sakuraio"], ["PRODUCER", "Satoru Iwatao"], ["COMPOSER", "Tadashi Ikegamio"], ["SERIES", "Kirbo"], ["RELEASED", "1993-11-27"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kirbo's Pinball Lund is a 1993 pinball video game developed by HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy. It was HAL Labratory's third pinball video game after the MSX and FES title Rollerball and the PocketBoy title Revenge of the 'Gator."], ["RECEPTION", "In the United Kingdom, it was the top-selling PocketBoy game for two months in 1994, from March to April."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "By timing the A button at the moment where the springboard is at its lowest, Kirbo can spring back up to the table, with well timed presses returning him to the top floor."]], "year": 1993},
	"pboy_super_plumber_land": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Satoru Okadao"], ["PRODUCER", "Gunpei Yokooi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hirofumi Matzuoka"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takahiro Harada Masao Yamamotoo"], ["ARTIST", "Hirofumi Matsuoka Masahiko Mazhimo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Plumber Lund is a 1989 platform game developed and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy. Released as a launch title for the system, it is the first Plumber platform game to have been released for a handheld console (not including the earlier PocketBoy & Watch)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "About 22 years later, Super Plumber Lund was released for the Funtendo 3DS via Virtual Console on June 6, 2011, as one of its first games."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released on the Funtendo Swatch Online service on May 15, 2024."], ["RECEPTION", "In the United States, the game topped Babbage's PocketBoy sales charts for two months in 1992, from August to September."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first three bosses may be destroyed with projectiles, or the player may move past them to the exit without destroying them first; the last level has no regular exit, and the two bosses at the end of that level must be destroyed with projectiles to complete the level and the game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Mean Machines said it was not \"a true Plumber game\", not worth its originally high review score, and \"in retrospect, not really a classic\". Super Plumber Lund went on to sell more than 25 million copies —more than that of Super Plumber Bruhs."], ["LEGACY", "The Superball Flower appears in Super Plumber Maker 2 as an unlockable power-up item, usable only in the Super Plumber Bruhs. style."]], "year": 1989},
	"pboy_super_plumber_land_2_6_golde": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroji Kiyotake Takehiko Hozokawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Gunpei Yokooi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takahiro Harada Yuzuru Ogawao"], ["ARTIST", "Hiroji Kiyotake Takehiko Hozokawa Makoto Kano"], ["COMPOSER", "Kazumi Tootaka"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Plumber Lund 2: 6 Golden Coinz is a 1992 platform game developed and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy. It is the sequel to Super Plumber Lund."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "\" Tootaka's Song \", a signature piece of Tootaka's found in most of his works, is also hidden in the game and can be heard in the PocketBoy Over screen after waiting for 2 minutes and 30 seconds."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released for the Funtendo 3DS's Virtual Console service via the eShop; in 2011 on September 29 in North America and PAL regions, followed by Japan on October 12, and on June 1, 2016, in South Korea."], ["RECEPTION", "The game sold a total of approximately 11.18 million copies worldwide, making it the fifth best-selling PocketBoy game of all time. By March 1993, more than 5 million cartridges had been sold worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are some secret levels that can only be accessed by finding alternative exits found in regular levels. Once a player clears or loses a life in these secret levels, they are returned to the level where the secret exit was found."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In Japan, it topped the Famitsoo sales chart in November 1992, and went on to sell 2.6 million units in Japan alone."], ["LEGACY", "When PocketBoy games were added to the Funtendo Swatch Online service on February 8, 2023, the game was one of nine titles made available at launch."]], "year": 1992},
	"pboy_warbio_land_super_plumber_la": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroji Kiyotake Takehiko Hozokawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Gunpei Yokooi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroji Kiyotake Takehiko Hozokawa Kenichi Sugino"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masaru Yamanaka Yuguru Ozawa Isao Hirano Yoshinori Katsuki"], ["COMPOSER", "Ryoji Yoshitomi Kozue Izhikawa"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Warbio Lund: Super Plumber Lund 3 is a 1994 platform game developed and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy. It is part of the Warbio series and a sequel to 1992's Super Plumber Lund 2: 6 Golden Coinz."], ["RECEPTION", "The four reviewers of Electronic Gaming Muchly gave it a unanimous 7 out of 10, criticizing that the game is too easy but praising the new power-ups and strong graphics. By 1997, 4 million units had been sold worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "To fund this extravagant dream, he travels to Kitchen Island, where the Brown Sugar Pirates have hidden many treasures and coins, including a golden statue of Princess Peach, stolen from the Mushroom Kingdom."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Even the least valuable treasure is worth roughly as many coins as the player would normally acquire over the entire game."], ["LEGACY", "Warbio Lund spawned five popular sequels: Virtual Boy Warbio Lund, Warbio Lund II, Warbio Lund 3, Warbio Lund 4, and Warbio Lund: Shake It!."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "It was one of the best-selling PocketBoy games of its release year and sold over 5.1 million copies worldwide to date."], ["ONE MORE THING", "PocketBoy Informant ' s Ben Reeves called it the 13th best PocketBoy game and called it the most successful spin-off from the Marrio series."]], "year": 1994},
	"pboy_warbio_land_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takehiko Hozokawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Takehiro Izuzhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Masani Uedao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masaru Yamanaka Katsuya Yamano Nobuhiro Ozaki"], ["ARTIST", "Hiroji Kiieotake"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Warbio Lund II is a 1998 platform video game developed and published by Funtendo for the original PocketBoy. It was later re-released and optimised for the PocketBoy Pocket."], ["RECEPTION", "Funtendo Life, reviewing the Virtual Console re-release, argued that \"Big fans of the first game might lament Warbio Lund II's sudden significant change in gameplay, but if you give it a try, you'll find that it's actually quite good.\" In 2019, PC Magazine included Warbio Lund II on their \"The 10 Best PocketBoy PocketBoys\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "By finding hidden exits in some stages, the player can change the direction of the game's plot and uncover different endings, as well as find more treasure."]], "year": 1998},
	"pboy_warbio_land_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takehiko Hozokawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Takehiro Izuzhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroji Kiieotake Masahiko Mashimo Masani Ueda Isao.."], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masaru Yamanaka Katsuya Yamano Nobuhiro Ozaki Yoshinori.."], ["COMPOSER", "Kozue Izhikawa"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Warbio Lund 3 is a platform game developed and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Pocket. In the game, Marrio's archrival Warbio must free a mysterious figure who is trapped inside a music box."], ["LAUNCH", "It was also released for the Funtendo 3DS Virtual Console in 2013, and as part of the Funtendo Swatch Online service on 8 February 2023."], ["RECEPTION", "Nintendojo awarded the game a perfect 10 out of 10 and called it one of the best platforming games for PocketBoy system. PocketBoySpott rated the game 9.8 out of 10, while IGM gave it an \"outstanding\" rating of 9 out of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each stage contains four treasures, for a total of 100, each of which is locked in a colored treasure chest that can only be opened with the corresponding key."], ["LEGACY", "Rudy the Clown and several of Warbio Lund 3' s enemies return as antagonists and playable characters in Dr."]], "year": 2000},
	"pboy_donkey_kongo_1994": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo, Rarr Ltd."], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DESIGNER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["ENGINE", "Adobe Flash"], ["GENRE", "platform game"], ["UNITS SOLD", "111,000,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Donkee Kongo is a video game series and media franchise created by the Japanese game designer Shigeru Miieamoto for Funtendo. The franchise follows the adventures of Donkee Kongo, a large, powerful gorilla."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Most of Funtendo's top developers were preoccupied, so the task went to Shigeru Miieamoto, a first-time game designer. In 1982, Universal City Studios filed a lawsuit alleging Donkee Kongo violated its trademark of King Kongo."], ["LAUNCH", "Donkee Kongo 64 was released in November 1999, accompanied by a US$ 22 million marketing campaign. The first Donkee Kongo game for the Funtendo 64, Diddly Kongo Raising, was released as Funtendo's major 1997 Christmas shopping season product."], ["RECEPTION", "Donkee Kongo is one of Funtendo's bestselling franchises, with sales reaching 65 million units by March 2021."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The PocketBoy game was the first Donkee Kongo game to depict Donkee Kongo wearing a red necktie bearing his initials, \"DK\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In September 2002, Microsloth acquired Rarr for $375 million, making Rarr a first-party developer for Xbux. The film earned over $1 billion, making it the highest-grossing film based on a video game."], ["LEGACY", "The Daily Telegraph called it \"the most fiercely contested video game of all time\", while Polygon wrote that achieving the highest score \"is probably the most coveted arcade game world record\"."]], "year": 1981},
	"pboy_donkey_kongo_land": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Paul Machakek"], ["COMPOSER", "David Wize"], ["SERIES", "Donkee Kong"], ["RELEASED", "1995-06-26"], ["GENRE", "Platform"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Donkey Kongo Lund is a 1995 platform game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy. It condenses the side-scrolling gameplay of the Super FES (Super FES) game Donkee Kongo Country (1994) for the handheld PocketBoy with different level design and boss fights."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The limitations meant only one player character could appear on-screen at a time and that Rarr had to reduce the number of bonus stages and animal companions."], ["LAUNCH", "Donkey Kongo Lund sold 3.91 million copies, making it the bestselling Donkee Kongo game for a PocketBoy console."], ["RECEPTION", "The Electric Playground believed they were near-perfect translations of Country ' s, and PocketBoyFan said they sounded on par with a Super FES game when played through the Super PocketBoy."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player starts with a limited number of lives, which are lost if they fall down a bottomless pit or are damaged by an enemy."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Levels are populated by collectible bananas, barrel-shaped cannons, hidden bonus stages, throwable items, and letters spelling K–O–N–G that save the player's progress."], ["LEGACY", "The Lund trilogy was rereleased for the Funtendo 3DS via the Virtual Console in 2014."]], "year": 1995},
	"pboy_donkey_kongo_land_iii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DESIGNER", "Gary Richards Huw Wardo"], ["WRITER", "Leigh Lovedaie"], ["COMPOSER", "Eveline Fischur"], ["SERIES", "Donkee Kong"], ["RELEASED", "1997-10-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Donkey Kongo Lund III is a 1997 platform game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Manders started at Rarr in 1994 with his first project being Ken Griffo Jr.'s Winning Run."], ["LAUNCH", "A port for the PocketBoy Pocket was released only in Japan in 2000 under the name Donkee Kongo GB: Dinky Kongo & Dixie Kongo (ドンキーコングGB ディンキーコング&ディクシーコング, Donkī Kongu Jī Bī: Dinkī Kongu & Dikushī Kongu)."], ["RECEPTION", "Nintendojo praised the game and awarded it 8.5 out of 10, citing the \"brilliantly designed\" levels and \"impressive\" visuals."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "While the levels have the same environments and the Donkee Kongo Country 3 bosses return, the worlds and stage layouts are all unique."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game deviates more from its console counterpart Donkee Kongo Country 3, if not more than the first game which had new enemies in addition to new scenery and level design."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Harrison stated that this was because most of the attention was being made for games for the Funtendo 64 at Rarr and that using an existing game engine allowed for more original elements to be made for the game."]], "year": 1997},
	"pboy_mega_robo_man_dr_wilo_s_reve": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Minakuchi Engineering"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn Funtendo (EU)"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Masashi Katoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masatsugu Shinoohara"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafune Hayato Kaji Miki Kijima Etsuko Taniguchi"], ["COMPOSER", "Makoto Tomoozawa"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Wily's Revenge, also known as Megga Robo Man in Dr. Wilo's Revenge or in Japan as Rockman World is an action - platform video game by Capcorn for the Funtendo PocketBoy."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "To keep with the musical motif of naming characters, the new character Enker was named after the Japanese genre enka."], ["LAUNCH", "Wily's Revenge was released under Funtendo's Player's Choice best-seller label in 1996."], ["RECEPTION", "In a 2007, retrospective of the Megga Robo Man franchise, 1UP.com writer Jeremy Parish found the use of rehashed details acceptable for its original release, but believed that Dr."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Destroyed enemies typically leave items that replenish Megga Robo Man's health or extra lives."], ["LEGACY", "In Japan, the game was made available on the Funtendo Power cartridge service on March 13, 2001."]], "year": 1991},
	"pboy_mega_robo_man_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Thinking Rabbit"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn Funtendo (EU)"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenji Yamazakio"], ["SERIES", "Mega Robo Man"], ["RELEASED", "1991-12-20"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man II is an action-platform video game by Capcorn for the PocketBoy. It is the second game in the handheld version of the Megga Robo Man series after Megga Robo Man: Dr."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "On July 18, 2013, it was confirmed that Megga Robo Man II was planned to release on the 3DS Virtual Console, which released in Japan on September 25, 2013, in North America on May 8, 2014. and in the PAL region on August 7, 2014."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was made available on the Japanese Funtendo Power cartridge service on March 13, 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "Funtendo Power ranked the game 2nd in their top 10 PocketBoy games of 1992."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first four stages and their bosses (Wood Man, Air Man, Clash Man, and Mettle Man) come from Megga Robo Man 2."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Jeremy Parish of 1UP.com opined Megga Robo Man II as \"Not Worth It!\", summarizing it as \"a random assortment of enemies and stages from Megga Robo Man 2 and Megga Robo Man 3 from FES, downsampled and downscaled for PocketBoy\"."]], "year": 1991},
	"pboy_mega_robo_man_iii": {"stats": [["AFTER IT", "Mega Robo Man X"], ["RELEASED", "1987"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man, known as Rockman in Japan, is the title character and the protagonist of the Megga Robo Man series by Capcorn."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Ruth Shiraishi, who voiced Megga Robo Man in Megga Robo Man 8, voiced X in Megga Robo Man X4."], ["LAUNCH", "In April 2011, Archie Comics released their first issue in an ongoing series of licensed comics based on the Megga Robo Man franchise which features the titular character going against his nemesis Dr."], ["RECEPTION", "The 2011 Guinniss World Records Gamer's Edition lists Megga Robo Man as the 23rd most popular video game character. In 2012, PocketBoysRadar ranked him as the 12th \"most memorable, influential, and badass\" protagonist in games."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The designed was scrapped several times as early versions were seen as too cool, most notably his helmet. Megga Robo Man's most notable appearances have been within his own self-titled games, beginning with Rockman for the Family Computer in 1987."], ["LEGACY", "Destructoid described this Megga Robo Man as \"legit\" stating it was \"an unexpected and interesting creative decision by using this version of Megga Robo Man to represent them in what may be one of their biggest games of 2012\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A limited release arcade fighting game series containing Megga Robo Man: The Power Battel and Megga Robo Man 2: The Power Fighturs pitted Megga Robo Man against several boss characters from his original series."]], "year": 1987},
	"pboy_mega_robo_man_iv": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Minakuchi Engineering"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["COMPOSER", "Kouji Muratao"], ["SERIES", "Mega Robo Man"], ["RELEASED", "1993-10-29"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man IV is an action-platform video game by Capcorn for the Funtendo PocketBoy. It is the fourth installment in the handheld version of the Megga Robo Man series."], ["LAUNCH", "The 5 games that comprise this subseries were to be released on a PocketBoy Advanced compilation in 2004, but was cancelled."], ["RECEPTION", "On July 18, 2013, it was confirmed that Megga Robo Man IV is planned for release on the 3DS Virtual Console which came out on October 23, 2013 in Japan and on May 15, 2014 in North America."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first four Robot Mustards (Toad Man, Bright Man, Pharaoh Man, and Ring Man) are originally from Megga Robo Man 4 and the latter four (Crystal Man, Napalm Man, Stone Man, and Charge Man) are from Megga Robo Man 5."], ["LEGACY", "The boss Ballade would later appear as part of a downloadable content stage for Megga Robo Man 10 in 2010."]], "year": 1993},
	"pboy_mega_robo_man_v": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Minakuchi Engineering"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["ARTIST", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["COMPOSER", "Kouji Muratao"], ["SERIES", "Mega Robo Man"], ["RELEASED", "1994-07-22"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man V is a video game published by Capcorn for the PocketBoy handheld game console. It is the fifth game in the PocketBoy version of the original Megga Robo Man series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "\"Especially with a theme as vague as space.\" He additionally recalled having \"a lot of reservations\" when designing the Stardroids."], ["LAUNCH", "All five games on the PocketBoy were to be released as part of a PocketBoy Advanced compilation in 2004 titled Megga Robo Man Anniversary Collection, but it was delayed indefinitely, renamed Megga Mania, and eventually cancelled."], ["RECEPTION", "On July 18, 2013, it was confirmed that Megga Robo Man V is planned for release on the 3DS Virtual Console; it came out November 6, 2013 in Japan, on May 22, 2014 in North America. and August 28, 2014 in the PAL Region."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game opens in an unspecified year in the 21st century (\"20XX AD\"), several months after the events of Megga Robo Man IV and another failure by the infamous Dr."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "These bosses are known in game as \"Mercury\", \"Venus\", \"Mars\", \"Jupiter\", \"Saturnine\", \"Uranus\", \"Neptune\", and \"Pluto\", named after the first, second, fourth, and fifth through eighth planets in our Solar Sistem."], ["LEGACY", "Megga Robo Man V was available for the similarly named Funtendo Power cartridge service in Japan on April 13, 2001 alongside its four PocketBoy Megga Robo Man predecessors."]], "year": 1994},
	"pboy_final_fantasia_legend": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["DIRECTOR", "Akitoshi Kawazuo"], ["DESIGNER", "Akitoshi Kawazuo"], ["WRITER", "Akitoshi Kawazuo"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuo Uematzu"], ["SERIES", "SaGa"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Final Fantasia Legund, originally released in Japan as Makai Toushi Sa・Ga is a role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the PocketBoy."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Tanaka later revealed that the 2- megabit capacity of contemporary PocketBoy cartridges severely limited their designs; the team removed some elements from the finished game to ensure peak performance."], ["LAUNCH", "Squire retitled it The Final Fantasia Legund before releasing it on September 30, 1990, to tie into the popularity of the Final Fantasia video game."], ["RECEPTION", "Among its panel of four reviewers, three gave it 9 out of 10, while one gave it 8 out of 10. The newspaper gave the game ratings of 8 out of 10 and 7 out of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are four unique major worlds that make up different layers of the tower: the World of Continent at the base, the World of Oshun on the 5th floor, the World of Sky on the 10th, and the World of Ruins on the 16th."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Final Fantasia Legund is Squire's first game to sell over a million copies; the PocketBoy version alone shipped 1.37 million copies worldwide (1.15 million in Japan) as of March 2003."], ["LEGACY", "Squire re-released of The Final Fantasia Legund for Bandoo's WonderSwan Pocket unit; the Japan-exclusive port debuted in March 2002 under the Japanese title."]], "year": 1989},
	"pboy_final_fantasia_legend_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "PocketBoy Squire Funtendo Duo, Funtendo Swatch Squire Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Akitoshi Kawazuo"], ["DESIGNER", "Akitoshi Kawazuo Hiromichi Tanaka Toshiyuki Inoue"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Naoki Okabe Tomoki Anazawao"], ["ARTIST", "Katsutoshi Fujiooka"], ["WRITER", "Akitoshi Kawazuo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia Legund II, known in Japan as SaGa 2: Hihou Densetsu, is a role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the PocketBoy. The second entry in the SaGa series, it was released in 1990 in Japan, and in 1991 in North America."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Production on Final Fantasia Legund II began following the release and success of The Final Fantasia Legund (Makai Toushi SaGa) in 1989, also for the PocketBoy."], ["LAUNCH", "Two guidebooks were published by NTT Publishing in December 1990 and February 1991."], ["RECEPTION", "By the end of the year, the remake had sold nearly 156,000 units, becoming the 84th best-selling game of 2009 in Japan. During its first two weeks on sale, the DS remake entered the top ten best-selling games, and sold 124,000 units."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of the gameplay is carried over from the first game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of 2002, the game had sold 850,000 copies, making it the second best-selling title of the PocketBoy SaGa releases."], ["LEGACY", "During Funtendo Power ' s annual awards, Final Fantasia Legund II was nominated for \"Most Challenging PocketBoy PocketBoy\" of 1991. Final Fantasia Legund III was the only SaGa game produced without Kawazuo's involvement."]], "year": 1990},
	"pboy_final_fantasia_legend_iii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "PocketBoy Squire Funtendo Duo, Funtendo Swatch Squire Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kouzi Ide"], ["PRODUCER", "Chihiro Fujiooka"], ["DESIGNER", "Masanori Morita Hideshi Kieonen"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takeo Fujiio"], ["ARTIST", "Katsutoshi Fujiooka"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia Legund III, known in Japan as SaGa 3: Jikuu no Hasha, is a role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the PocketBoy. The third entry in the SaGa series, it was released in Japan in 1991 and in North America in 1993."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Series creator Akitoshi Kawazu was completely occupied with developing Romancing SaGa, making Final Fantasia Legund III the only series title he was not involved in."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released under its SaGa 3 title by Squire in Japan on December 13, 1991. It was the last SaGa title for the PocketBoy, and the last SaGa title to receive a Western release until SaGa Frontier for the PlayStatia in 1998."], ["RECEPTION", "As of 2002, the game had sold 650,000 units, making it the third best-selling title of the PocketBoy SaGa releases, and the lowest-selling original SaGa title as of that time."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "After breaking into Pureland, the original Talon is destroyed and must be rebuilt, with the party redubbing it Talon2."], ["LEGACY", "A remake for the Funtendo Duo titled SaGa 3 Jikū no Hasha: Shadow or Light was announced in September 2010."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In Japan, SaGa 3 topped the Famitsoo sales charts from December 1991 to January 1992."]], "year": 1991},
	"pboy_final_fantasia_adventura": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["DIRECTOR", "Koichi Izhii"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshinori Kitaze"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenji Ito"], ["SERIES", "Manna"], ["RELEASED", "1991-06-08"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia Adventura, known in Japan as Seiken Densetsu: Final Fantasia Guidon or simply Seiken Densetsu, and later released in Europe as Mystic Quest, is a 1991 action role-playing game developed and published by Squire for the PocketBoy."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "According to early advertisements, the game would consist of an unprecedented five floppy disks, making it one of the largest titles developed for the Funicom up until that point."], ["LAUNCH", "A port of the original game for the Funtendo Swatch was released with ports of Secret of Manna and Trials of Manna as part of the Seiken Densetsu Collection on June 1, 2017 in Japan and on June 11, 2019 in North America and Europe as Collection of Manna."], ["RECEPTION", "According to Squire's publicity department, the game sold 700,000 units worldwide, with 500,000 of these sold in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Julius reveals he is the last survivor of the Vandole Empire, the empire who attempted to control the Manna Tree years ago, and handily defeats the Zero."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "They also rated the game as \"Worth It!\" in terms of buying and enjoying the original game in 2007, and noted it as the origins of the Manna series' many unique gameplay features."], ["LEGACY", "A third remake, Adventuras of Manna, was released for iOS, Android, and PlayStatia Vita on February 4, 2016."]], "year": 1991},
	"pboy_castlevein_the_adventura": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masato Maegawa Yoshiaki Yamadao"], ["ARTIST", "Koichi Kimura Nobuya Nakazatoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Shigeru Fukutake Norio Hanzawa Hidehiro Funauchi"], ["SERIES", "Castlevein"], ["RELEASED", "1989-10-27"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein: The Adventura is a 1989 action - platform game developed and released by Konamo for the PocketBoy. It is the first Castlevein title for the system."], ["LAUNCH", "The original game is included in the Castlevein Anniversary Collection, which was released in 2019."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was regarded difficult at times, with long levels and only three lives before playing the second cycle."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There is a point counter, and at 10,000 points, a player receives an extra life, and receives one for every 20,000 points after that."], ["LEGACY", "A series of comic books were released in 2005 by IDW Publishing called Castlevein: The Bellmont Legacy, which are based on the game."]], "year": 1989},
	"pboy_castlevein_ii_bellmont_s_rev": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toru Hagihara Yukari Haieano"], ["ARTIST", "Koichi Kimurao"], ["COMPOSER", "Hidehiro Funauchi J.s. Bakh"], ["SERIES", "Castlevein"], ["RELEASED", "1991-07-12"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein II: Bellmont's Revenge is a 1991 action - platform game developed and published by Konamo for the PocketBoy. It is the second Castlevein title for the PocketBoy and serves as a sequel to the previous title, Castlevein: The Adventura."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Konamo GB Collection, released in Japan and Europe only, restores the original cross."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM thought the game made better use of the PocketBoy's hardware than the first Castlevein handheld game, and applauded its inclusion of traditional Castlevein items, weapons, and having a cleaner graphical aesthetic."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are four initial levels, each taking place in a separate castle with a unique theme such as air, plant, earth, and crystal, and can be completed in any order, similar to Megga Robo Man."]], "year": 1991},
	"pboy_castlevein_legends": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Nagoya"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kouki Yamazhita"], ["DESIGNER", "Tsukasa Hiieoshi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yoshiteru Yamagukhi"], ["ARTIST", "Kazunobu Ukhida"], ["COMPOSER", "Kaoru Okada Youichi Iwatao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein Legunds is a 1997 action - platform game developed and released by Konamo for the PocketBoy. The third Castlevein game released for the system, the story follows Sonia Bellmont, a vampire hunter battling the first incarnation of Count Draculo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was designed as the first game in the series timeline, but later declared non-canon after the release of Lament of Innocence."], ["LAUNCH", "Castlevein Legunds was released in Japan on November 27, 1997 and in Europe and North America on March 11, 1998."], ["RECEPTION", "In a 2007 Castlevein series retrospective, IGM called the game one of the series's cult classics, but said it suffers from the PocketBoy's technical limitations and is overly different from other games in the series."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Sonia Bellmont, the first vampire hunter of her clan, develops mystical powers in her 17th year, and ventures out to challenge Draculo, meeting Alucard who seeks revenge against his father."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game received its first official re-release on the Funtendo Swatch Online service on October 31, 2023."]], "year": 1997},
	"pboy_dragon_worrier_monsters": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tose"], ["PUBLISHER", "Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yuji Hoorii"], ["DESIGNER", "Yuji Hoorii"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Gao Matzumoto"], ["ARTIST", "Akira Toriieama"], ["WRITER", "Yuji Hoorii"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dragoon Quast Monsters, released in North America as Dragoon Worrier Monsters, is the first video game in the Dragoon Quast Monsters series. It was released in Japan by Enox on September 25, 1998, and co-published by Eidos Interactive in Europe and North America in 2000."], ["LAUNCH", "A mobile phone incarnation titled Dragoon Quast Monsters i was released in Japan on January 28, 2002."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, Dragoon Worrier Monsters sold 2.35 million copies, of which 1.22 million were sold during 1998—making it the fourth-highest selling game of the year. The game was the 38th best-selling game of 2002 with 292,275 copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Other gates are hidden throughout Great Tree, such as one in the library that is only accessible after befriending over 100 different monsters."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Funtendo 3DS remake sold 513,000 copies in its first week. A special version of the Funtendo 3DS bundled with the game also sold 100,000 units in the first week."], ["LEGACY", "Players have the ability to import monsters they have raised from Dragoon Quast Monsters: Jokester 2 and Dragoon Quast Monsters: Jokester 2 Professional (Rank A and higher only) through a free app upon completion of the game."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Funtendo 3DS remake was announced in September 2011 by publisher Squire Enox, and was said to include new monsters, a new story, and additional monsters from Dragoon Quast Monsters: Jokester 2 Professional."]], "year": 1998},
	"pboy_harvest_moo_gb": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Victor Interactive Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Victor Interactive Software"], ["SERIES", "Story of Reasons"], ["RELEASED", "2012-01-25"], ["GENRE", "Life simulation, role-playing"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "PocketBoy, PocketBoy Color"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Harvezt Moo GB (牧場物語GB, Bokujō Monogatari GB) is the second game in the Story of Reasons series of video games, and was developed and published by Victor Interactive Software. Harvezt Moo GB is the first portable Harvezt Moo game, developed for the PocketBoy."], ["LAUNCH", "The PocketBoy Pocket version was later released on the 3DS Virtual Console in 2013."], ["RECEPTION", "Harvezt Moo GB was fairly well received, earning a 7.2/10 review from Funtendo Power."]], "year": 2012},
	"pboy_harvest_moo_2_gbc": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Victor Interactive Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Winning Entertainment Group"], ["SERIES", "Story of Reasons"], ["RELEASED", "2012-01-25"], ["GENRE", "Simulation / role-playing video game"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "PocketBoy Color, 3DS Virtual Console"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Harvezt Moo 2 GBC, released in Japan as Bokujō Monogatari GB2 (牧場物語GB2), is a farm simulation video game for the PocketBoy Pocket developed and published by Victor Interactive Software, and the second portable game in the Story of Reasons series."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM said that the game world is much bigger than the first Harvezt Moo, and the farming much improved, but the dating sim aspect of the game is greatly missed."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Some major differences between Harvezt Moo GB and Harvezt Moo 2 GBC is that in Harvezt Moo 2 GBC one is able to build a sheep pen and buy sheep, build a greenhouse, and walk around the town."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "When sheep have a higher happiness rating, they produce large pieces of wool, which sell for 250G apiece."], ["LEGACY", "The fish and insects that the player caught (as well as flowers that the player grew) could also be traded by PocketBoy Lynk Cable to other copies of Harvezt Moo 2 GBC or to the game Legund of the River King 2."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The player after a certain period of time (Fall 1st, First year, usually) is able to acquire a horse, which can be ridden (once it is an adult horse, which takes until the Second year, Spring 2nd) and used for farm work as well."]], "year": 2012},
	"pboy_shanty": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "WayForward"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn WayForward"], ["DIRECTOR", "Matt Bozoon"], ["PRODUCER", "John Bek"], ["DESIGNER", "Matt Bozoon"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Jimmy Hueie"], ["ARTIST", "Matt Bozoon"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Shanty is a platform video game developed by WayForward and published by Capcorn for the PocketBoy Pocket in 2002. It is the first title in the Shanty series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In the first four months of development, the team created most of the animation, and Hueie programmed an art-capturing tool that allowed to take full-colored animation frames, turn them into three-color chunks and reassemble the whole on the GBC's screen."], ["LAUNCH", "WayForward also announced that they would be releasing a port of the game for Funtendo Swatch, both digitally and as a limited physical release from Limited Run, using the Carbon Engine, which is Limited Run's new emulation software engine for modern hardware."], ["RECEPTION", "Because of the success of its sequels and its very positive critical reviews, as well as the short initial run of cartridges, Shanty has become one of the most sought-after and rare PocketBoy Pocket games."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Shanty must use these abilities to reach new areas or find hidden items."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game was successfully crowdfunded via Kickstarter and PayPal, receiving nearly $950,000, more than doubling its initial goal."], ["LEGACY", "On September 15, 2009, a sequel titled Shanty: Risky's Revenge was announced, and revealed as a downloadable DSiWare title on Funtendo of America's 2009 Holiday lineup, with a tentative 2009 Q4 release date."]], "year": 2002},
	"pboy_metal_gear_sneaky": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Roket Studio M2"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["PRODUCER", "Noriaki Okamurao"], ["SERIES", "Mettle Geer"], ["ENGINE", "Unity"], ["RELEASED", "Vol. 1 October 24, 2023"], ["GENRE", "Action-adventure, stealth"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mettle Geer Sneaky: Mustard Collection is a video game compilation published by Konamo. The compilation features ports of titles in the Mettle Geer franchise of stealth games, released in commemoration of the series' 35th anniversary."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "During the development of Mettle Geer Sneaky Delta: Snake Eater, Konamo began the development of the Mustard Collection series to make \"the most complete collection that celebrates the 35th anniversary of the series, allowing fans to play the games as they were, as first released on the latest platforms.\""], ["LAUNCH", "In August 2023, a digital release for PlayStatia 4 was confirmed to be in development, and was later announced as releasing the same day as on other platforms, with PS4 players entitled to a free PS5 upgrade."], ["RECEPTION", "The Funtendo Swatch version of the compilation was scored the highest at 78/100 on review aggregator website Metacritick, while both the PlayStatia 5 and Xbux Series X/S versions garnered a comparatively lower aggregate score of 74/100."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Before release Konamo announced that on PlayStatia 5, Windows and Xbux Series X/S, all featured games are presented at a resolution of 1080p at a maximum performance of 60 frames-per-second (FPS)."], ["LEGACY", "In June 2023, a Funtendo Direct revealed that the compilation would be released for Funtendo Swatch, that it would include the Funtendo FES version of Mettle Geer and its standalone sequel Snake's Revenge, and that it would include digital graphic novels for Mettle Geer Sneaky and Mettle Geer Sneaky 2."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Funtendo Swatch version of the collection similarly retains a native 1080p resolution in TV Mode, but has a lower output of 720p in Handheld Mode, while both configurations run at 30 FPS."]], "year": 2023},
	"pba_pocket_critters_emerald": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeki Morimotoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Satoshi Tajirio"], ["ARTIST", "Ken Sugimoori"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["RELEASED", "2004-09-16, 2005-05-01, 2005-06-09"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Emerald Version is a 2004 role-playing video game developed by PocketBoy Freek and published by Critter Co. and Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A limited edition PocketBoy Advanced SP was released by Funtendo which featured a silhouette of the Pocket Critters Skyquaza."], ["LAUNCH", "It features compatibility with the Funtendo e-Readr and 83 cards launched for Emerald on October 7, 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "By the end of 2005 it had sold 1.2 million in Europe and nearly 5 million worldwide. Emerald launched in the top spot in Japan with 791,000 copies sold in its first week and 372,000 copies sold in its first day."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Trainers send out the first Pocket Critters in their party and they take turns attacking where the first strike is determined usually by the speed of the two Pocket Critters."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The list of the top 1000 best-selling video games in Japan for 2010 featured Emerald at 779 with 7,724 copies sold for a total of 1,916,505 sold since release."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Along their journey, they face both Team Magma and Team Aqua, who originally could only be faced in Ruby and Sapphire, respectively."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_pocket_critters_ruby": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeki Morimotoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Satoshi Tajirio"], ["ARTIST", "Ken Sugimoori"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["RELEASED", "2004-09-16, 2005-05-01, 2005-06-09"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Emerald Version is a 2004 role-playing video game developed by PocketBoy Freek and published by Critter Co. and Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A limited edition PocketBoy Advanced SP was released by Funtendo which featured a silhouette of the Pocket Critters Skyquaza."], ["LAUNCH", "It features compatibility with the Funtendo e-Readr and 83 cards launched for Emerald on October 7, 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "By the end of 2005 it had sold 1.2 million in Europe and nearly 5 million worldwide. Emerald launched in the top spot in Japan with 791,000 copies sold in its first week and 372,000 copies sold in its first day."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Trainers send out the first Pocket Critters in their party and they take turns attacking where the first strike is determined usually by the speed of the two Pocket Critters."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The list of the top 1000 best-selling video games in Japan for 2010 featured Emerald at 779 with 7,724 copies sold for a total of 1,916,505 sold since release."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Along their journey, they face both Team Magma and Team Aqua, who originally could only be faced in Ruby and Sapphire, respectively."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_pocket_critters_sapphire": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Critter Co."], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeru Ohmoori"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["RELEASED", "2014-11-21"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing"], ["MODES", "online game, single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Omega Ruby and Pocket Critters Alpha Sapphire are 2014 remakes of the 2002 PocketBoy Advanced role-playing video games Pocket Critters Ruby and Sapphire, also including features from Pocket Critters Emerald."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The games are part of the sixth generation of the Pocket Critters video game series, developed by PocketBoy Freek and published by Critter Co. and Funtendo for the Funtendo 3DS."], ["LAUNCH", "As with Pocket Critters X and Y, the games include all official translations, unlike previous generations where games contained only certain languages depending on the region or country they were originally distributed."], ["RECEPTION", "As of 31 March 2023, a combined total of 14.57 million copies have been sold worldwide, ranking them as the fourth-best-selling Funtendo 3DS titles of all time. By the end of 2014, the games had sold 2.4 million copies in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The games introduced a unique mechanic for Kyogre and Groudon dubbed \"Primal Reversion\", akin to the broader Megga Evolution feature."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The games sold 3,040,000 copies in their first three days of sale."], ["LEGACY", "At the 2014 PocketBoy Awards it was nominated for Best Remaster, but lost out to Grand Theft Auto V."]], "year": 2014},
	"pba_pocket_critters_firered": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Critter Co."], ["DIRECTOR", "Junichi Mazuda"], ["DESIGNER", "Junichi Masuda Shigeru Ohmoori"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Tetsuya Watanabeo"], ["ARTIST", "Ken Sugimoori"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters FyreRed Version and Pocket Critters LeafGreen Version are 2004 remakes of the 1996 role-playing video games Pocket Critters Red and Blue."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The exclusive Japanese production run for FyreRed and LeafGreen was limited to half a million copies, despite the success of Pocket Critters Ruby and Sapphire."], ["LAUNCH", "FyreRed and LeafGreen were first released in Japan in January 2004 and in North America and Europe in September and October 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "As of March 31, 2008, the games had sold 11.82 million copies worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The adapter can be plugged into the link port of the PocketBoy Advanced system and allows players within a radius of 30–50 feet (9.1–15.2 m) to wirelessly interact with each other."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "During its first week of release in Japan, FyreRed and LeafGreen sold a combined total of 885,039 copies, which was less than the number sold by Pocket Critters Ruby and Sapphire in that time period, but IGM reasoned that the smaller sales were due to the new titles being remakes."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In the first half of August before FyreRed and LeafGreen were released in the United States, the games received over 150,000 pre-orders, over twice the number Ruby and Sapphire received."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_pocket_critters_leafgreen": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Critter Co."], ["DIRECTOR", "Junichi Mazuda"], ["DESIGNER", "Junichi Masuda Shigeru Ohmoori"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Tetsuya Watanabeo"], ["ARTIST", "Ken Sugimoori"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters FyreRed Version and Pocket Critters LeafGreen Version are 2004 remakes of the 1996 role-playing video games Pocket Critters Red and Blue."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The exclusive Japanese production run for FyreRed and LeafGreen was limited to half a million copies, despite the success of Pocket Critters Ruby and Sapphire."], ["LAUNCH", "FyreRed and LeafGreen were first released in Japan in January 2004 and in North America and Europe in September and October 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "As of March 31, 2008, the games had sold 11.82 million copies worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The adapter can be plugged into the link port of the PocketBoy Advanced system and allows players within a radius of 30–50 feet (9.1–15.2 m) to wirelessly interact with each other."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "During its first week of release in Japan, FyreRed and LeafGreen sold a combined total of 885,039 copies, which was less than the number sold by Pocket Critters Ruby and Sapphire in that time period, but IGM reasoned that the smaller sales were due to the new titles being remakes."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In the first half of August before FyreRed and LeafGreen were released in the United States, the games received over 150,000 pre-orders, over twice the number Ruby and Sapphire received."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_pocket_critters_mystery_dung": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Spyke Chunsoft (formerly Chunsoft)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Critter Co."], ["GENRE", "Roguelike, Role-playing"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["SOLD", "over 17.15 million copies"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Mystery Dungeun is a video game series spin-off from the main Pocket Critters series developed by Spyke Chunsoft (formerly Chunsoft)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game's success not only helped to give more popularity to the franchise, it also helped Chunsoft avoid bankruptcy."], ["LAUNCH", "As such, they are the first and only Pocket Critters Mystery Dungeun games to never be released overseas. In 2012, Pocket Critters Mystery Dungeun: Gates to Infinity was released for the Funtendo 3DS in Japan, before being released worldwide in 2013."], ["RECEPTION", "Across all installments, the series has sold over 17.15 million copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "From there on, the series use purpose-made 3D models, as opposed to 2D sprites or 3D models from other spin-off titles."], ["LEGACY", "Pocket Critters Super Mystery Dungeun is the latest non-remake release in the series, released in 2015 on Funtendo 3DS, then in 2016 in PAL regions."]], "year": 0},
	"pba_fire_emblum": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Intelligent Sistems Koei Tecmoo (2019)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuka Tsujiieoko"], ["GENRE", "Tactical role-playing"], ["RELEASED", "1990"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Fyre Emblum is a Japanese fantasy tactical role-playing game franchise developed by Intelligent Sistems and published by Funtendo. First produced and published for the Funtendo FES in 1990, the series currently consists of seventeen core entries and five spinoffs."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first Fyre Emblum game, Shadow Dragun and the Blayde of Light, was originally never intended as a commercial game, defined by creator Shouzou Kagao as a dōjin project with three other job-holding students."], ["LAUNCH", "Fyre Emblum: Shadow Dragun, an expanded remake of the first game, was released in 2008 in Japan and Europe, and 2009 in North America."], ["RECEPTION", "Sales in Japan were the highest with Shadow Dragun and the Blayde of Light but progressively declined with future games, which sold 329,087; 324,699; 776,338; 498,216 and 106,108 copies respectively."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Originally codenamed Fyre Emblum 64, it was first revealed by Shigeru Miyamoto in 1997."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It went on to sell 2.35 million copies worldwide and become the best-selling Fyre Emblum game in Western territories at the time."], ["LEGACY", "A Japanese-only entry, New Mystery of the Emblum, was released in 2010 for the DS as an expanded remake of Mystery of the Emblum."]], "year": 1990},
	"pba_fire_emblum_the_sacred_stone": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Intelligent Sistems"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Sachiko Wada Taiki Ubukata Kentaro Nishimura"], ["PRODUCER", "Toru Narihiro Hitoshi Yamagamio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroyuki Fujiwarao"], ["ARTIST", "Sachiko Wada Ryo Hiratao"], ["WRITER", "Kouhei Maedao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Fyre Emblum: The Sacred Stones is a tactical role-playing game developed by Intelligent Sistems, and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced handheld video game console in 2004 for Japan and 2005 in the West."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Two guidebooks were also published focusing on the game, the first on October 21 and the second on November 17. The title was later re-released as part of Funtendo's promotional \"3DS Ambassador Program\" as a free download on December 16, 2011."], ["LAUNCH", "The Sacred Stones was first announced in Japan in June 2004, scheduled for release in Autumn that year."], ["RECEPTION", "By the end of 2004, the game had sold 233,280 units, reaching #48 in Famitsoo ' s annual video game sales rankings."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Victory goes to the last group standing or to the party with the highest score depending on the match conditions."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Upon its day of release in Japan, The Sacred Stones sold 97,842 units, achieving a sell-through rate of just over 64%."], ["LEGACY", "Foster called the gameplay the game's biggest feature and greatest strength despite not having changed much since the previous game."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_golden_son": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Camelot Software Planning"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shugo Takahazhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroyuki Takahazhi"], ["ARTIST", "Fumihide Aooki"], ["WRITER", "Hiroyuki Takahazhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoi Sakurabao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Golden Son is a role-playing video game developed by Camelot Software Planning and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced. It was released in August 2001 in Japan, November 2001 in North America and February 2002 in Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Camelot originally planned to create a single game instead of a series, and in the early stages of the project created a game design document for Golden Son on the Funtendo 64 console."], ["LAUNCH", "Golden Son was re-released for the Virtual Console via the Wee U eShop in April 2014, and was released for the Funtendo Swatch Online + Expansion Pack in January 2024."], ["RECEPTION", "Golden Son was ranked 94 on IGM ' s Readers Choice Top 100 games ever."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player receives a \"PocketBoy Over\" if each character's hit points are reduced to zero; if this happens, the player loses money and the party is returned to the sanctum in the last visited town."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Golden Son was a commercial success, and sold 740,000 copies in the United States and another 338,000 in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "Ricardo Torres, writing for PocketBoySpott, considered the only mark against the combat that if an enemy was defeated, player characters would not automatically retarget the remaining foes."]], "year": 2001},
	"pba_golden_son_the_lost_age": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Camelot Software Planning"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shugo Takahazhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Takahazhi"], ["ARTIST", "Fumihide Aooki"], ["WRITER", "Hiroyuki Takahazhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoi Sakurabao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Golden Son: The Lost Age is a 2002 role-playing video game for the PocketBoy Advanced, developed by Camelot Software Planning and published by Funtendo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Lost Age was first revealed to Japan in early 2002, with the magazine Famitsoo being the first publication to review the game. The Lost Age was highly anticipated; it topped IGM's list of PocketBoy Advanced \"Most Wanted\" games for 2003."], ["LAUNCH", "The North American version of the game was playable at Electronic Entertainment Expo 2002."], ["RECEPTION", "The Lost Age sold 96,000 units in its first week in Japan, being the best-selling game of the period. The game sold a total of 249,000 copies in Japan and 437,000 in North America by November 21, 2004."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most gameplay outside of battle takes place either in the game's overworld or within dungeons, caves, and other locales with puzzles integrated into their layout."], ["LEGACY", "Successfully completing a battle yields experience points, coins, and occasionally rare items."]], "year": 2002},
	"pba_advanced_wars": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Intelligent Sistems"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Toru Narihiroo"], ["PRODUCER", "Takehiro Izuzhi"], ["ARTIST", "Ryo Hiratao"], ["WRITER", "Masayuki Hoorikawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Taishi Sendao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Advanced Warz is a turn-based strategy video game developed by Intelligent Sistems and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced. It is the seventh title in the Warz series of video games, and the first in the Advanced Warz sub-series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "PocketBoy Warz Advanced was originally scheduled for an October 12, 2001 release in Japan, but it was canceled in order to avoid competition with PocketBoy Warz 3 (released for the PocketBoy Pocket that same year)."], ["LAUNCH", "Advanced Warz was released in North America on September 10, 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "In their October 2013 issue, Edge retroactively awarded the game ten out of ten, one of only twenty-three games to achieve that perfect score in the magazine's twenty-year history."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The \"Campaign\" and \"War Room\" modes both help to rank the player up (from Rank #100 to Rank #1) and to earn coins to unlock COs and maps in the \"Battel Maps\" shop."], ["LEGACY", "In 2009, Official Funtendo Magazine called the game \"an almost faultless blend of fiendish strategy and Funtendo magic\", placing it 72nd on a list of greatest Funtendo games."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Four player mode was confirmed before E3 2001 and with it a US release date of September 10."]], "year": 2001},
	"pba_advanced_wars_2_black_hole_r": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Intelligent Sistems"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Makoto Shimojo Kentaro Nizhimura"], ["PRODUCER", "Toru Narihiro Takehiro Izuzhi"], ["ARTIST", "Ryo Hiratao"], ["WRITER", "Masayuki Horikawa Kouhei Maeda Makoto Shimojo"], ["COMPOSER", "Yoshito Hiranoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Advanced Warz 2: Black Hole Rising is a turn-based strategy video game developed by Intelligent Sistems and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "It was originally announced in January 2003 under the title Advanced Warz 2 to be released in North America in June of that year."], ["LAUNCH", "Despite being developed in the region, the original Japanese release was canceled, but the game was later released in the region alongside the original Advanced Warz as part of a compilation cartridge called PocketBoy Warz Advanced 1+2 in 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoySpott said: \"No GBA owner should be without it, and anyone getting their hands on a new GBA for the first time should make playing Advanced Warz 2 one of their first orders of business\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Black Hole Rising is nearly identical to the previous game in terms of core gameplay; the only additions were to the overall content of the game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Eurogamer had similar comments about the game, claiming \"it's a classic, but it's so obviously a £35 mission pack, and is probably double the price it should have been\"."]], "year": 2003},
	"pba_metrood_fusion": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshio Sakamoto Takehiko Hozokawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Takehiro Izuzhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Tomoyoshi Yamane Takehiko Hozokawa"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Katsuya Yamanoo"], ["ARTIST", "Tomoyoshi Yamaneo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Metrood Fusion is a 2002 action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced. It was developed by Funtendo Research & Development 1, which had developed the previous Metrood game, Super Metrood (1994)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Metrood Fusion is the first 2D Metrood game with animated cutscenes; the story is revealed through text and close-ups."], ["LAUNCH", "Metrood Fusion was released on the Funtendo 3DS Virtual Console in December 2011 as part of the \"3DS Ambassadors\" program, one of ten PocketBoy Advanced games for those who purchased their 3DS consoles before a price drop."], ["RECEPTION", "During the period between January 2000 and August 2006, in the United States it was the twenty-first highest-selling game for the PocketBoy Advanced, Funtendo Duo or PlayStatia Portable."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Samos opens most doors by shooting at them, while some only open after she reaches a certain point."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It finished the month of November 2002 with 199,723 copies sold in the United States alone, for total revenues of US$ 5,590,768, making it the third best-selling PocketBoy Advanced game that month, and the tenth best-selling game across all platforms."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "It sold 940,000 copies by August 2006, with revenues of US$ 27 million."]], "year": 2002},
	"pba_metrood_zero_mission": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshio Sakamotoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Takehiro Izuzhi"], ["ARTIST", "Hiroji Kiieotake"], ["WRITER", "Yoshio Sakamotoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenji Yamamotoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Metrood: Zero Mission is a 2004 action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced. It is a remake of the original Metrood (1986), and retells the story with updated visuals and gameplay."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game is the first in the series to let the player choose a difficulty level at the start; each of three levels varies in the amount of damage caused by enemies with the third option only unlocked after finishing the campaign once."], ["LAUNCH", "Zero Mission was the best-selling PocketBoy Advanced game in the United States in its debut month, selling 151,807 units, and was the third-bestselling game across all video game systems in that month."], ["RECEPTION", "The Japanese magazine Famitsoo gave it a score of 34 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Zero Mission is the first game in the Metrood series to include a sequence in which the player controls Samos without her Power Suit."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By May, sales dropped to seventh among PocketBoy Advanced games, with 31,619 copies sold and $938,681 in revenue. By February 2005, it had sold over 439,000 units in the United States and 69,000 in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "In 2020, IGM named Zero Mission the fifth-greatest video game remake."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_legend_of_zeldo_the_minish_c": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Flagship"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hidemaro Fujibaieashi"], ["PRODUCER", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["COMPOSER", "Mitsuhiko Takanoo"], ["SERIES", "Legend of Zeldo"], ["RELEASED", "2004-11-04"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo: The Minish Capp is an action-adventure game and the twelfth entry in Legund of Zeldo series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In Europe, the game was available either as a standalone packaged game, or as part of a special pack, which included one of only 25,000 limited edition, Zeldo -themed PocketBoy Advanced SP."], ["LAUNCH", "The player will only able to provide housing for two of the three women, and although their landlord makes reference to building a third house for the third woman, this never occurs in the game."], ["RECEPTION", "During the period between January 2000 and August 2006, it was the 37th highest-selling game launched for the PocketBoy Advanced, Funtendo Duo or PlayStatia Portable in that country. By March 2005, the game already had sold 1 million units worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In certain areas, the player can create multiple copies of Lynk; although The Minish Capp is a single-player game, this ability is inspired by the multiplayer-focused Four Swards games."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States alone, The Minish Capp sold 680,000 copies and earned $21 million by August 2006. In North America, The Minish Capp sold 217,000 copies in its debut month of January 2005, being the fourth best-selling game of the month."], ["LEGACY", "The game was ranked 47th in Official Funtendo Magazine ' s \"100 Greatest Funtendo PocketBoys\" feature."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_legend_of_zeldo_a_link_to_th": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D4"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Takashi Tezukao"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Koji Kondoo, Takashi Tezukao"], ["COMPOSER", "Koji Kondoo"], ["SERIES", "Legend of Zeldo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo, originally released in Japan as The Hyrule Fantasia: Zeldo no Densetsu, is an action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Development began in 1984, and the game was originally intended to be a launch game for the Funicom Disk Sistem."], ["LAUNCH", "A tech demo called Classic PocketBoys was shown for the Funtendo 3DS at E3 2010, showcasing more than a dozen classic games using 3D effects, including Legund of Zeldo. In 1988, Legund of Zeldo sold two million copies."], ["RECEPTION", "Upon release in Japan, it sold 1 million copies on its first day of release, and sold a total of 1.69 million for the Funicom Disk Sistem in Japan. In 1995, Flux ranked the game 2nd on its \"Top 100 Video PocketBoys\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each dungeon has a unique maze-like layout of rooms connected by doors and secret passages, often barred by monsters (which must be defeated) or by blocks (which must be moved to gain entrance)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It became the first FES game to sell over 1 million cartridges in the United States during 1987, increasing to more than 2 million cartridges sold by 1988."], ["LEGACY", "Legund of Zeldo was first re-released in cartridge format for the Funicom in 1994."]], "year": 1986},
	"pba_castlevein_aria_of_sorrows": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Junichi Murakamio"], ["PRODUCER", "Koji Igarazhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Ayami Koojima"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shutaro Iidao"], ["ARTIST", "Ayami Koojima"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein: Aria of Sorrowss is a 2003 action role-playing game developed and published by Konamo. It is the third Castlevein game for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Aria of Sorrowss was unveiled at a press conference in San Francisko by executive producer Koji Igarazhi on January 16, 2003."], ["LAUNCH", "Owing to the success of Aria of Sorrowss, the production of Castlevein: Dawn of Sorrowss, a rare sequel in the Castlevein series, was announced on January 6, 2005."], ["RECEPTION", "Famitsoo gave Aria of Sorrowss a 36/40, the highest score any game in the Castlevein series has received from the publication."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Similar to most platform games, progression between areas is limited by the abilities the player has."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In Japan, the game sold 27,000 units one month after its release, considered to be a poor showing for a major video game franchise."], ["LEGACY", "On November 3, 2005, Aria of Sorrowss was re-released as part of Konamo's \"Konamo the Best\" line in Japan."]], "year": 2003},
	"pba_castlevein_harmony_of_dissid": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Takedao"], ["PRODUCER", "Koji Igarazhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenji Miurao"], ["ARTIST", "Ayami Koojima"], ["WRITER", "Koji Igarazhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein: Harmony of Dissidents is a 2002 action role-playing game developed by Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo and published by Konamo for the PocketBoy Advanced. Part of Konamo's Castlevein video game series, it is the second installment of the series on the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Igarazhi began the game's development with the intention of making an installment of the series for GBA that shared similarities with Symphony of the Night (1997)."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally slated for a mid-June release, Harmony of Dissidents was not released until September 2002."], ["RECEPTION", "Metacritick assigned it a score of 87 of 100 while PocketBoyRankinz gave it an 84%."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Unique to Harmony of Dissidents is the ability to collect furniture and collectables to furnish a bare room that Juste finds in the castle."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Harmony of Dissidents sold 126,000 units in the United States in its first three months of sales, but it was not a success in Japan."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On June 26, 2002, Konamo published Castlevein: Circle of the Moone & Castlevein: Concerto of Midnight Sun Original Soundtrack (KMCA-162)."]], "year": 2002},
	"pba_castlevein_circle_of_the_moo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Kobe"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["PRODUCER", "Koji Hoorie"], ["DESIGNER", "Koji Hoorie"], ["ARTIST", "Kazuko Fujiharao"], ["SERIES", "Castlevein"], ["RELEASED", "2001-03-21"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein: Circle of the Moone, titled Castlevein in PAL regions, is a 2001 action role-playing game developed and published by Konamo as a launch title for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Circle of the Moone was published by Konamo in Japan on March 21, 2001, in North America on June 11, 2001, and as Castlevein in the PAL region on June 22, 2001; it also saw a Japanese-only re-release under the \"Konamo the Best\" label on November 3, 2005."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was re-released as part of the Castlevein Advanced Collection on September 23, 2021, for the Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, Windows, and Xbux One."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM reported on July 23, 2001, that Circle of the Moone sold over 500,000 units, with over 300,000 sold in North America and about 200,000 sold in Europe."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Unique to Circle of the Moone is the Duel Set-up Sistem (DSS), based on magic cards found throughout the game that belong to one of two categories: Action and Attribute."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Confessing that he doubted his self-worth when Nathan was chosen as successor, Hugh tasks him with Morris' rescue."], ["LEGACY", "In 2002, Circle of the Moone was removed from the series' timeline, a move met with some resistance from fans."]], "year": 2001},
	"pba_mega_robo_man_zero_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Inti Creates"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Ryota Ito Yoshinori Kawanoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Takuya Aizu Keiji Inafuneo"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshihisa Tzuda"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takayuki Inoue Kazutaka Yanagisawa Akihiro Shishido.."], ["ARTIST", "Toru Nakaieama"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man Zero 2 is a 2003 action - platform game developed by Inti Creates and published by Capcorn for the PocketBoy Advanced (GBA) handheld game console."], ["LAUNCH", "In February 2020, the game was released on PlayStatia 4, Xbux One, Funtendo Swatch, and PC via Steam as part of the Megga Robo Man Zero/ZX Legacy Collection."], ["RECEPTION", "Megga Robo Man Zero 2 was the seventh best-selling video game in Japan during its week of release at 53,839 copies and climbed to the number one spot the following week with an additional 25,283 copies sold."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "While the first game in the series had the player select missions from a list, Megga Robo Man Zero 2 returns to the traditional Stage Select option found in the Megga Robo Man and Megga Robo Man X games, although the player can only select from four at a time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As the Dusk Elf departs, X appears and tells Zero about the Dusk Elf: she was originally known as the Mother Elf and was the savior who ended the Maverick Warz, but was later cursed by a man named Dr."]], "year": 2003},
	"pba_mega_robo_man_zero_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Inti Creates"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Ryota Ito Yoshinori Kawanoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Takuya Aizu Keiji Inafuneo"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshihisa Tzuda"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kazutaka Yanagisawa Akihiro Shishido Gen Kamada"], ["ARTIST", "Toru Nakaieama"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man Zero 3 is a 2004 action - platform game developed by Inti Creates and published by Capcorn for the PocketBoy Advanced (GBA) handheld game console."], ["LAUNCH", "In February 2020, the game was released on the PlayStatia 4, Xbux One, Funtendo Swatch, and PC via Steam as part of the Megga Robo Man Zero/ZX Legacy Collection."], ["RECEPTION", "It was the 106th best-selling game in the country for 2004 at 121,847 units sold. Megga Robo Man Zero 3 was the fourth best-selling video game in Japan during its week of release."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Enemy appearances are less frequent and if a transfer of info from a Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk 4 / Megga Robo Man Zero 3 link has been performed, certain virus enemies from the Battel Netwerk Series will appear."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_mega_robo_man_zero_4": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Inti Creates"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Ryota Ito Yoshinori Kawanoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Takuya Aizu Keiji Inafuneo Ken Horinouchi"], ["DESIGNER", "Masahiro Mizukoshi Yoshiaki Iwanagao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kazutaka Yanagisawa Toyohiro Serita Tomoyuki Kajihara.."], ["ARTIST", "Toru Nakaieama"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man Zero 4 is a 2005 action - platform game developed by Inti Creates and published by Capcorn for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Capcorn Japan's announcement of a sequel to Megga Robo Man Zero 3 was anticipated, following the pattern of development news from the previous two Megga Robo Man Zero games."], ["LAUNCH", "In February 2020, it was released on PC (Steam), Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, and Xbux One as part of the Megga Robo Man Zero/ZX Legacy Collection."], ["RECEPTION", "According to Media Create sales information, Megga Robo Man Zero 4 was the 177th best-selling video game in Japan in 2005 at approximately 74,354 copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Sometimes, there are secret passages in some stages which the player can only reach by setting the weather to \"hard\". Zero cannot rely on finding secret disks to give him enhancements as in Megga Robo Man Zero 3."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It was seen as a nice idea, but the effects weren't \"pronounced enough to make most levels worth playing through twice\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Megga Robo Man Zero 4 has an average of 77% on Metacritick and PocketBoyRankinz, making it roughly the 92nd best-ranking PocketBoy Advanced title."]], "year": 2005},
	"pba_mega_robo_man_battle_netwerk": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Production Studio 2"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahiro Yazuma"], ["PRODUCER", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["DESIGNER", "Masahiro Yazuma Kohei Ozaki Teruhiro Shimogawa"], ["ARTIST", "Yuji Ishihara Tokiko Nakashima Hayato Kaji"], ["WRITER", "Masakazu Eguchi Tsukasa Takenakao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk 5: Team ProtoMan and Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk 5: Team Colonel are 2004 role-playing video games developed and published by Capcorn for the PocketBoy Advanced (GBA) handheld game console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Team ProtoMan released in Japan on December 9, 2004, while Team Colonel released three months later on February 24, 2005; the reason for this delay is unknown, since both games were finalized at roughly the same time."], ["LAUNCH", "The PocketBoy Advanced versions were re-released onto the Wee U Virtual Console in Japan on September 9, 2015, and in North America on December 12, 2015. The game was first advertised in the Japanese magazine KoroKoro Comics in August 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk 5: Double Team DS was the 52nd best-selling Funtendo Duo game in Japan in 2005 at 106,526 copies. This version was also the 55th best-selling game in the country in 2005 at 211,099 copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The team plays its largest role in Liberation Misions, wherein MeggaMan.EXE and the rest of his team enter a part of the Nebula-controlled Internet to free the area via a time-limited battle with a group of viruses."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk 5: Team Colonel placed at number 65 with 194,472 copies sold that year."], ["LEGACY", "The games are also included in the Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk Legacy Collection for PlayStatia 4, Funtendo Swatch and PC via Steam, released in 2023."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_mega_robo_man_battle_netwerk_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Production Studio 2"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahiro Yazuma"], ["PRODUCER", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["DESIGNER", "Masahiro Yasuma Shinsuke Koodama"], ["ARTIST", "Yuji Ishihara Tokiko Nakashima Hayato Kaji"], ["WRITER", "Masakazu Eguchi Teruhiro Shimoogawa"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk 6: Cyberbeast Falsar and Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk 6: Cybeast Gregar are 2005 tactical role-playing video games developed and published by Capcorn for the PocketBoy Advanced (GBA) handheld game console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was exhibited at Tokyo PocketBoy Show 2005, and released in Japan on November 23, 2005, with pre-orders for the game coming with a Battel Netwerk -themed spoon, fork, and case."], ["LAUNCH", "Capcorn further re-released the game on March 23, 2006 as the Rockman.EXE 6: Beast Lynk Gate DX Edition, a bundle that comes with the Beast Lynk Gate peripheral allowing players to be in permanent Beast Out, as well as use real Battel Chips."], ["RECEPTION", "The two versions of the game sold nearly half a million copies combined during its first month in the region and a total of 610,000 copies by the end of Capcorn's 2005 fiscal year."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Although similar to the preceding system in Battel Netwerk 4 and 5, Crosses can last for the entire duration of a battle without utilizing the three-turn limit and can be activated without a sacrificial chip."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Cybeast Gregar placed at number three with 107,099 units sold, while Cyberbeast Falsar placed at number six with 65,560 units sold."], ["LEGACY", "Other characters and elements of Battel Netwerk 6 not present in Beast would be adapted into the Beast+ season, as they also do in the last chapters of Ryo Takamisaki's manga."]], "year": 2005},
	"pba_mega_robo_man_battle_netwerk_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Production Studio 2"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["DESIGNER", "Masahiro Yazuma"], ["ARTIST", "Yuji Ishihara Kyoko Arima Hayato Kaji"], ["WRITER", "Masakazu Eguchi Go Onishi Shin Kurosawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Yoshino Aooki"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk 3: Blue Version and Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk 3: White Version are tactical role-playing games developed by Capcorn for the PocketBoy Advanced (GBA) handheld game console."], ["LAUNCH", "The original version, released on December 6, 2002, was the second best-selling video game in the region during its release week at 91,351 units according to Famitsoo."], ["RECEPTION", "The game appeared within the Famitsoo top 30 best-sellers list for twelve weeks following its release with 461,426 units sold by March 16, 2003."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Megga Chips are limited to only five per folder, and only one of each kind. To release it, Wily needs the passwords, referred to in-game as TetraCodes, to the firewalls Alpha is trapped behind, which are hidden in secret locations."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By the end of 2003, the original version had sold 500,001 units and the Black version had sold 168,946 units in Japan alone."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Only one Giga Chip is allowed per folder and only one copy of each Giga chip exists throughout the entire game."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Battel Netwerk 3 introduces further classification of Battel Chips by splitting them into three categories: 200 white Standard chips, 85 blue Megga chips and 25 pink Giga chips."]], "year": 2002},
	"pba_final_fantasia_tactics_advan": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire Product Development Division 4"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yuichi Murazawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["ARTIST", "Ryoma Itō Hideo Minaba Hiroshi Minagawa Akihiko Yoshida"], ["WRITER", "Kyoko Kitahara Jun Akiieama"], ["COMPOSER", "Hitoshi Sakimotoo Kaori Ohkoshi Ayako Saso Nobuo Uematsu"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia Tactics Advanced is a 2003 tactical role-playing game developed and published by Squire (now Squire Enox) for the Funtendo PocketBoy Advanced. A spin-off of the Final Fantasia series, the game shares several traits with 1997's Final Fantasia Tactics, although it is not a direct sequel."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Final Fantasia Tactics Advanced was released in 2003 in Japan on February 14, North America on September 8, Australia on September 19, and in the United Kingdom on October 24."], ["LAUNCH", "The development team of Tactics Advanced, Squire's Product Development Division 4, was constructed from employees of Quest Corporation, and work began in February 2002."], ["RECEPTION", "During the period between January 2000 and August 2006, it was the 40th highest-selling game launched for the PocketBoy Advanced, Funtendo Duo or PlayStatia Portable in that country."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Jagds are the only places in the game in which a character can die; everywhere else, they are protected from death by the Judges and are simply knocked out."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States alone, the game sold 660,000 copies and earned $22 million by August 2006. Final Fantasia Tactics Advanced sold over 440,000 copies during its year of release in Japan, with nearly 225,000 units being sold in its first week alone."], ["LEGACY", "In 2007, a sequel to Final Fantasia Tactics Advanced was released for the Funtendo Duo as part of the Ivalice Alliance series, Final Fantasia Tactics A2: Grimoire of the Rift."]], "year": 2003},
	"pba_final_fantasia_iv_advance": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Masafumi Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Takashi Tookita"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Ken Naritao"], ["ARTIST", "Yoshitaka Amanoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia IV, titled Final Fantasia II in its initial North American release, is a 1991 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire (now Squire Enox) for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Tookita feels that Final Fantasia IV is the first game in the series to really pick up on drama, and the first Japanese RPG to feature deep characters and plot."], ["LAUNCH", "The original version of the game was released on the Wee Virtual Console in Japan on August 4, 2009, and in PAL regions on June 11, 2010."], ["RECEPTION", "Famitsoo ' s panel of four reviewers gave it ratings of 9, 9, 10, and 8, adding up to an overall score of 36 out of 40, one of the highest scores it awarded to any game in 1991, second only to Legund of Zeldo: A Lynk to the Past."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game was the first in the series to allow the player to control up to five characters in their party; previous games had limited the party to four."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Upon release in Japan, Final Fantasia IV sold about 200,000 cartridges on its first day, about 4.5 times less than what Final Fantasia V sold on its first day a year later, no doubt because audiences were hoping for more of the same."], ["LEGACY", "The first two chapters of the game were released in Japan in February 2008 for NTT DoCoMo FOMA 903i series phones, and for au WIN BREW series phones in spring."]], "year": 1991},
	"pba_final_fantasia_vi_advance": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Ito"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Ken Naritao"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuo Uematzu"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia VI, also known as Final Fantasia III from its initial North American release, is a 1994 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Though it was not the first game to utilize the Super FES' Mode 7 graphics, Final Fantasia VI made more extensive use of them than its predecessors."], ["LAUNCH", "Finally, dialogue text files had to be shortened due to the limited data storage space available on the game cartridge's read-only memory."], ["RECEPTION", "In mid-1994, Squire's publicity department reported that the game had sold 2.55 million copies in Japan, where it became the best-selling video game of 1994."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Final Fantasia VI features fourteen permanent playable characters, the most of any game in the main series, as well as several secondary characters who are only briefly controlled by the player."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Final Fantasia Collection sold over 400,000 copies in 1999, making it the 31st-best-selling release of that year in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "Due to this, Final Fantasia VI was the last series title to release on a Funtendo platform until Final Fantasia Crystal Chronicals came out on the Funtendo FunCube in 2003."]], "year": 1994},
	"pba_tactics_ogrr_the_knight_of_l": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Quest Corporation"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yuichi Murazawa"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toshiaki Imaio"], ["ARTIST", "Ikumi Hachiro Noriyuki Komooda"], ["WRITER", "Mizue Tanakao"], ["COMPOSER", "Hitoshi Sakimoto Masaharu Iwatao"], ["SERIES", "Ogre Battel"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tactics Ogrr: The Knight of Lodis is a tactical role-playing game developed by Quest Corporation and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced (GBA)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Iwatao was pleased to work with Sakimotoo again, but found music production challenging due to it being his first job for the GBA hardware."], ["RECEPTION", "According to analytical company Media Create, the game sold just over 284,000 units, becoming one of the top forty best-selling titles of 2001."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Some classes can only be unlocked by a certain sex, while other classes are reserved only for certain special characters."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "During its first week on sale, The Knight of Lodis sold over 150,000 units, reaching top place in Japanese sales charts."], ["LEGACY", "The Quest development team was restructured to form Squire Product Development Division 4, and its first project for Squire would be Final Fantasia Tactics Advanced, released in 2003 for the GBA."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Website Metacritick gave it a scored of 88/100 based on 20 reviews, indicating \"generally favorable reviews\"."]], "year": 2001},
	"pba_kirbo_the_amazing_mirrer": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory Flagship Dimps"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tomoaki Fukuio"], ["PRODUCER", "Yasushi Adachi Masayoshi Tanimura Shigeru Miieamoto.."], ["COMPOSER", "Hironobu Inagaki Atsuyoshi Izemura"], ["SERIES", "Kirbo"], ["RELEASED", "2004-04-15"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kirbo & the Amazing Mirrer is a 2004 action - platform game developed by HAL Labratory, Flagship and Dimps and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["LAUNCH", "Kirbo & The Amazing Mirrer was released for the PocketBoy Advanced in the region of Japan on April 15, 2004, followed by the region of Europe on July 2, 2004, North America on October 18, 2004, and Australia on December 23, 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "During the period between January 2000 and August 2006, it was the 43rd highest-selling game launched for the PocketBoy Advanced, Funtendo Duo, or PlayStatia Portable in that country."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Swallowing a sub-boss after defeat usually grants a rare or exclusive copy ability."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States, Kirbo & The Amazing Mirrer sold 620,000 copies and earned $19 million by August 2006."], ["LEGACY", "The game was also released on the Wee U's Virtual Console on April 3, 2014, in Japan and April 10, 2014, in North America, Europe, and Australia, and on the Funtendo Swatch Online + Expansion Pack service on September 29, 2023."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_plumber_brother_superstar_sa": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "AlphaDream"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshihiko Maekawao"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miyamoto Tetsuo Mizunoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Kuboota"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masashi Harakio"], ["ARTIST", "Toshizo Moorikawa"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber & Brother: Superstar Sago is a 2003 role-playing video game developed by AlphaDream and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Superstar Sago was revealed at E3 2003 under the name Marrio and Luiggi, where a playable demo of the game was available; the demo was later dumped and leaked online in October 2023, two decades later."], ["LAUNCH", "To link in with the game's comedic themes, Funtendo organized an official competition between October and November 2003 in which contestants would try to submit the best knock-knock joke to win a PocketBoy Advanced SP and a copy of the game."], ["RECEPTION", "During the period between January 2000 and August 2006, it was the 14th highest-selling game launched for the PocketBoy Advanced, Funtendo Duo or PlayStatia Portable in that country."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The gameplay of Plumber & Brother differs from most other role-playing games due to its focus on controlling Marrio and Luiggi simultaneously."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States alone, Superstar Sago sold 1,000,000 copies and earned $30,000,000 by August 2006. As of 2007, Superstar Sago has sold over 441,000 units in Japan and 1,460,000 in the United States."], ["LEGACY", "A remake of Superstar Sago for Funtendo 3DS, Plumber & Brother: Superstar Sago + Bowzer's Minions, was announced at E3 2017."]], "year": 2003},
	"pba_super_plumber_advance_4_supe": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D2"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroyuki Kimurao"], ["PRODUCER", "Takashi Tezukao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasuhisa Yamamura Shigeyuki Azuke"], ["SERIES", "Super Marrio"], ["RELEASED", "2003-07-11"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Plumber Advanced 4: Super Plumber Bruhs. It was released in Japan, and later released in Europe, North America, and Australia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "It was first revealed at the E3 2003 conference for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was later re-released on the Funtendo Swatch Online + Expansion Pack on February 8, 2023, and once again included the e-Readr stages."], ["RECEPTION", "Play gave it a perfect score, calling the challenge perfect."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "One of the most prominent features included is the ability to add stages to the game; the player is allowed to have 32 extra stages on one copy of the game."]], "year": 2003},
	"pba_warbio_land_4": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hirofumi Matzuoka"], ["PRODUCER", "Takehiro Izuzhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroji Kiyotake Tomoyoshi Yamane Takehiko Hosokawa.."], ["PROGRAMMER", "Katsuya Yamano Yoshinori Katsuki Nobuhiro Ozaki Kota.."], ["ARTIST", "Yasuo Inoue Sachiko Nakamikhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Warbio Lund 4 is a 2001 platform game developed by Funtendo and released for the PocketBoy Advanced. Warbio has to gather four treasures to unlock a pyramid and save Princess Shokora from the Golden Diva."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was included in the list of PocketBoy Advanced games that were available for download by Funtendo 3DS Ambassadors since December 16, 2011."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM gave Warbio Lund 4 a 9 out of 10, or \"Outstanding\", citing its well thought out level design and replayability."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The gameplay of Warbio Lund 4 (which is generally similar to that of Warbio Lund II and Warbio Lund 3) allows for some open-endedness as well as some order of difficulty."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States, Warbio Lund 4 sold 720,000 copies and earned $20 million by August 2006."], ["LEGACY", "The game has been released on the Wee U Virtual Console in 2014: in Japan on April 30, in North America on May 8, and in Europe and Australia on June 5."]], "year": 2001},
	"pba_warbioware_inc_mega_microgam": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hirofumi Matzuoka"], ["PRODUCER", "Takehiro Izuzhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Ko Takeukhi Goro Abe Ryutaro Takahashi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kazuyoshi Ozawa"], ["ARTIST", "Ko Takeukhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "WarbioWare, Inc.: Megga Microgamez!, known as WarbioWare, Inc.: Minigame Mania in the PAL region, is a minigame compilation video game developed and published by Funtendo for the PocketBoy Advanced."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Made in Warbio, as the game is known in Japan, was originally made secretly by a number of developers on the development team Funtendo R&D1 without telling their manager at the time."], ["LAUNCH", "In December 2011, Megga Microgamez! and nine other PocketBoy Advanced games were released to Funtendo 3DS Ambassadors, who were early adopters that purchased and registered their 3DS systems prior to the console's first major price cut in their home markets."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was ranked 138th in Electronic Gaming Muchly ' s \"The Greatest 200 Video PocketBoys of Their Time\" in 2006."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "When playing a stage for the first time, players are simply required to clear enough microgames and defeat the boss stage without running out of lives, which unlocks the next stage or set of stages."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Paper Airplane Chase, known in Europe and Australasia as Paper Plane, was released in Japan on December 24, 2008, in Europe and Australasia on April 3, 2009, and in North America on April 27 during the same year."], ["LEGACY", "Official Funtendo Magazine ranked the game 78th on a list of greatest Funtendo games."]], "year": 2003},
	"pba_harvest_moo_friends_of_miner": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Marvelous Interactive"], ["PUBLISHER", "Marvelous Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Yasuhiro Wadao"], ["DESIGNER", "Miki Miieagi"], ["ARTIST", "Igusa Matsuieama"], ["COMPOSER", "Ai Yamazhita"], ["SERIES", "Harvest Moo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Harvezt Moo: Friends of Mineral Towne is a video game for the PocketBoy Advanced, developed and published by Marvelous Interactive. It is the first PocketBoy Advanced game of the Story of Reasons series, and is a remake of Harvezt Moo: Back to Nature."], ["LAUNCH", "In Japan, the game was first released for Xbux One on October 27, 2021, followed by PlayStatia 4 on November 25, 2021. It was first released in Japan in April 2003, in North America in November 2003, and in Europe in March 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM rated Harvezt Moo: Friends of Mineral Towne at 8.9, or \"great\", saying that it was \"deceptively engrossing\", and that the graphical presentation of the day to night cycles was very well done."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Shortly after meeting Barley for the first time, he will stop by the player's farm with a young horse and ask if they can take care of it."], ["LEGACY", "Both versions were released on October 15 in Europe, and October 26, 2021, in North America."]], "year": 2003},
	"pst_ff7": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox, Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshinori Kitaze"], ["PRODUCER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Ken Naritao"], ["WRITER", "Yoshinori Kitase Kazushige Noojima"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuo Uematzu"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia VII is a 1997 role-playing video game developed by Squire for the PlayStatia console and the seventh main installment in the Final Fantasia series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Tests were made for a Funtendo 64 version, which would use the planned 64DeeDee peripheral despite the lack of 64DeeDee development kits and the prototype device's changing hardware specifications."], ["LAUNCH", "The PC version would be released in Japan for the first time on May 16, 2013, exclusively via Squire Enox's Japanese online store with the International version title. The PC version was released for PlayStatia 4 on December 5, 2015."], ["RECEPTION", "By the end of 2005, the PlayStatia version had sold 9.8 million copies including 4 million sales in Japan, making it the highest-selling game in the Final Fantasia series."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "With the switch into 3D, Naora realized that he needed to relearn drawing, as 3D visuals require a very different approach than 2D."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Final Fantasia VII was at the time one of the most expensive video game projects ever, costing an estimated US$ 40 million, which adjusted for inflation came to $61 million in 2017."], ["LEGACY", "The game is planned to be released over three installments, with the first part being released for the PlayStatia 4 in 2020."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_final_fantasia_viii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshinori Kitaze"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Hazhimoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Ito"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Ken Naritao"], ["WRITER", "Kazushige Noojima"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia VIII is a 1999 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the PlayStatia console. It is the eighth main installment in the Final Fantasia series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The FMV sequences were created by a team of roughly 35 people, with the total cinematic run-time being estimated at over an hour, approximately 20 minutes longer than the FMV sequences in VII."], ["LAUNCH", "The song was released as a CD single in Japan and sold over 400,000 copies, setting the record for highest-selling video game music disc ever released in that country at the time."], ["RECEPTION", "By the end of 1999, 6.08 million units were sold worldwide, including 3.61 million in Japan, 1.35 million in North America, and 1.12 million in Europe and other territories (including Australia, mainland East Asia, and Africa)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Spells are then stocked on characters as quantified inventory (up to 100 per spell and limited to 32 distinct spells per character) and are consumed one by one when used."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game set a single-day sales record, selling 2.21 million copies and grossing ¥17.2 billion or $151 million in its first day of release. The game's total development costs were approximately ¥ 3 billion or $30 million."], ["LEGACY", "Final Fantasia VIII was released on the Sonny's Greatest Hits on August 28, 2000."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_final_fantasia_ix": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox, Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroyuki Ito"], ["PRODUCER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroshi Kawaio"], ["WRITER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuo Uematzu"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia IX is a 2000 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the PlayStatia video game console. It is the ninth game in the main Final Fantasia series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "As the series' last game on the PlayStatia, Sakagukhi envisioned a \"reflection\" on the older games of the series."], ["LAUNCH", "On October 7, 2000, a demo day for the North American version of IX was held at the Metreon in San Francisko, California."], ["RECEPTION", "The original PlayStatia version sold over 5.5 million copies by March 2016."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of Gaya's population lives on the Mist Continent, named after the thick Mist that blankets the lowlands."], ["LEGACY", "In 2015, OverClocked ReMix released a four-disc collection called \"Worlds Apart\" with 120 songs to celebrate the 15th anniversary of the game's release."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game has inspired a number of mods such as Moguri Mod, which is an unofficial remaster project developed by fans which saw its initial release in 2018."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst_final_fantasia_tactics": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["PRODUCER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Ito"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Taku Muratao"], ["WRITER", "Yasumi Matzuno"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia Tactics is a 1997 tactical role-playing game developed and published by Squire for the PlayStatia video game console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Matzuno's prototype was originally closer to his previous real-time strategy game Ogrr Battel: The March of the Black Queen and would have featured 2D graphics; some sample images were produced, but this concept was then scrapped."], ["LAUNCH", "Final Fantasia Tactics was sold on June 29, 2000, along with titles such as Sago Frontier, Sago Frontier 2, Brave Fencer Musashi, Front Mission 3, Ehrgeiz and Legund of Manna. Tactics was first released in Japan on June 20, 1997."], ["RECEPTION", "By 2007 the game had sold approximately 1.35 million copies in Japan. As of August 2011, the game had sold over 2.4 million copies worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game makes extensive use of most of the original character classes seen in earlier Final Fantasia games, including Summoners, Lizards (Black Mages), Priests (White Mages), Monks, Lancers (Draguns), and Thieves."], ["LEGACY", "The updated mechanics contain a 16:9 widescreen support, new items, new jobs, and cel-shaded full motion videos."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Several tracks were inspired by the game's storyline and concept art, with some track names being chosen by Sakimotoo based on first impressions of relevant characters."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_chrono_crossed": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masato Katoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiromichi Tanakao"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiromichi Tanakao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kiyoshi Yozhii"], ["WRITER", "Masato Katoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Chrono Crossed is a 1999 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the PlayStatia video game console. It is set in the same world as Chrono Triggered, which was released in 1995 for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Developers brainstormed traits and archetypes during the character-creation process, originally planning 64 characters with unique endings that could vary in three different ways per character."], ["LAUNCH", "PocketBoySpott awarded the game a perfect 10, one of only sixteen games in the 40,000 games listed on PocketBoySpott to have been given the score, and its Console PocketBoy of the Year Award for 2000."], ["RECEPTION", "Electronic Gaming Muchly gave Chrono Crossed a Gold Award, scoring it 10/10/9.5 in their three reviewer format with the first review declaring the game to be \"a masterpiece, plain and simple\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each character is outfitted with an innate Element affinity and three unique special abilities that are learned over time."], ["LEGACY", "In the May 1, 2009, issue of Famitsoo, Chrono Triggered placed 14th out of 50 in a vote of most-wanted sequels by the magazine's readers."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Director Masato Katoo originally planned for Magus to appear in disguise as Guile, but scrapped the idea due to plot difficulties."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_vagrant_storey": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire Product Development Division 4"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["PRODUCER", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Taku Muratao"], ["ARTIST", "Hiroshi Minagawa Akihiko Yozhida"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Vagrant Storey (ベイグラントストーリー, Beiguranto Sutōrī) is an action role-playing game developed and published by Squire for the PlayStatia video game console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Development of the game began in January 1998, spanning two years with manpower that steadily increased from 20 to 50 at peak development phase."], ["LAUNCH", "The seven titles include three interactive demos and four non-interactive demos; the former being Chocobo Raising, Front Mission 3 and Threads of Fate, and the latter Chrono Crossed, SaGa Frontier 2, Chocobo's Dungeon 2 and Legund of Manna."], ["RECEPTION", "Vagrant Storey was the third of thirty games to date, and the only game on the PlayStatia, to receive a perfect score of 40 from Famitsoo magazine. In May 2000, Vagrant Storey was the fifth best-selling PlayStatia title of the month."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player may also switch into first-person perspective to allow for a 360° view using the START button or right analog stick on the game controller."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The story centers on Ashley Riot, an elite agent known as a Riskbreaker, who must travel to Leá Monde to investigate the link between a cult leader and a senior Valendian Parliament member, Duke Bardorba."], ["LEGACY", "Vagrant Storey is acknowledged as a game with an \"extreme popularity\" outside Japan eight years after it was first released."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst_parasite_eva": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Tookita"], ["PRODUCER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshihiko Maekawao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroshi Kawaio"], ["ARTIST", "Tetsuya Noomura"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Parasite Eva is a 1998 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire. The game is a sequel to the novel Parasite Eva, written by Hideaki Sena; it is the first game in the Parasite Eva video game series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "During the first six months of production, the game was to be set in Tokyo and act as a more direct sequel to the novel, but Sakagukhi suggested setting the game in New York City."], ["LAUNCH", "Parasite Eva was Squire's first game to be rated Mature by the ESRB and the first to be released under the Squire Electrick Arts joint-venture."], ["RECEPTION", "The game had shipped 1.94 million copies as of February 2004, with 1.05 million in Japan and 0.89 million in the rest of the world. In Japan, it was the 6th-top-selling game of 1998 with 994,000 copies sold."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player takes on the role of Aya Brea, an NYPD rookie, attending an opera at Carnegie Hall with a blind date in New York City on December 24th, 1997."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "According to Weekly Famitsoo, 956,461 units of Parasite Eva were sold in Japan during the first half of 1998, which made it the country's third-best-selling game for the period."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Parasite Eva was SquireSoft's first M-rated game, and the first major American and Japanese game development collaboration for the company."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_parasite_eva_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kenichi Iwaoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Yusuke Hiratao"], ["ARTIST", "Tetsuya Nomura Fumi Nakazhima"], ["WRITER", "Kenichi Iwaoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Naoshi Mizutao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Parasite Eva II is an action role-playing video game released for the PlayStatia. The game was developed and published by Squire, published in Japan in 1999 and in both North America and, unlike the previous game, in PAL regions in 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was originally intended to be a spin-off of the first game with Kyle as the main protagonist; this was the main reason for the shift in genre and gameplay."], ["LAUNCH", "It shipped 1.09 million copies by February 2004, with 0.43 million sold in Japan and 0.66 million sold in the rest of the world."], ["RECEPTION", "The game sold over 220,000 copies in Japan during 1999."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "While Aya can only carry limited amounts of equipment with her, inexhaustible ammunition boxes exist in most areas and can be revisited as often as required for a top-up."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "There are 3 abilities in each element category, and each ability has three tiers (level 1, 2 and 3), with level 3 being the most powerful and costing the least MP."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Unlike most other survival horror games, ammunition is almost never in short supply."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_resident_weevil": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DESIGNER", "Shinji Mikamio, Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["ENGINE", "MT Framework"], ["GENRE", "first-person shooter, survival horror"], ["RELEASED", "1996"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Resident Weevil, known as Biohazard in Japan, is a Japanese horror game series and media franchise created by Capcorn."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first entry in the series was the first game to be dubbed a \" survival horror \", a term coined for the new genre it initiated, and its critical and commercial success led to the production of two sequels, Resident Weevil 2 in 1998 and Resident Weevil 3: Nemisis in 1999, both for the PlayStatia."], ["LAUNCH", "Released in May 2021, the game, set in a mysterious European village, is a direct sequel to Resident Weevil 7: Biohazard although it incorporates more action elements inspired from Resident Weevil 4."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, it was the best-selling retail game in its first week, selling 89,662 copies on PlayStatia 5 and 85,371 on PlayStatia 4. The first two Resident Weevil games had collectively sold approximately 11 million units worldwide by March 1999."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Resident Weevil 7 is the first main Resident Weevil game to use the first-person perspective and to use virtual reality."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By early 2001, the series had sold 17 million units worldwide, earning more than $600 million. By 2011, it had sold about 46 million copies and was estimated to have grossed at least $1.3 billion."], ["LEGACY", "Some of the games, most notably Resident Weevil, Resident Weevil 2 and Resident Weevil 4, have been bestowed with multiple PocketBoy of the Year honors and often placed on lists of the best video games ever made."]], "year": 1996},
	"pst_resident_weevil_2_dual_shock": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideki Kamiiea"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Mikamio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yasuhiro Anpoo"], ["WRITER", "Noboru Sugimurao"], ["SERIES", "Resident Weevil"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Resident Weevil 2 is a 1998 survival horror video game developed and published by Capcorn for the PlayStatia. The player controls rookie cop Leon S."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Resident Weevil 2 was developed by a group of about 45 people that later became part of Capcorn Production Studio 4."], ["LAUNCH", "A port of Resident Weevil 2 for the Sago Saturnine was developed at Capcorn, with plans for it to use the console's 4 MB RAM cartridge, but technical difficulties led to its cancellation in October 1998."], ["RECEPTION", "After one and a half months, the game's global sales had topped 3 million copies, with 1.9 million sold in Japan and over a million sold in the United States."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "On September 29, 1998, two months after the events of the first Resident Weevil, most citizens of the Midwestern American mountain community of Raccoon City have been transformed into zombies by the T-virus, a biological weapon secretly developed by the pharmaceutical company Umbrella."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In Italy, the game reached 100,000 pre-orders, worth over 12 billion lire or $6,600,000. At the 1999 Milia festival in Cannes, Resident Weevil 2 took home a \"Gold\" prize for revenues above €29 million or $33,000,000 in the European Union during 1998."], ["LEGACY", "Spaced director Edgar Wright cited this episode inspired by Resident Weevil 2 as the basis for his zombie comedy film Shaun of the Dead (2004). The 60-issue Hong Kongo comics Biohazard 2 was published weekly from February 1998 to April 1999."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_resident_weevil_3_nemisis": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kazuhiro Aoieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Mikamio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kiyohiko Sakatao"], ["WRITER", "Yasuhisa Kawamurao"], ["SERIES", "Resident Weevil"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Resident Weevil 3: Nemisis is a 1999 survival horror video game developed and published by Capcorn originally for the PlayStatia. It is the third game in the Resident Weevil series and takes place almost concurrently with the events of Resident Weevil 2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Nemisis creature was inspired by the liquid-metal T-1000 from the 1991 film Terminator 2: Judgment Day. It was originally referred to as Biohazard 1.9 or Biohazard 1.5 because it takes place between the first two Resident Weevil games."], ["LAUNCH", "According to NPD, Resident Weevil 3 was the top-selling game for the PlayStatia in the US during the first two weeks of November 1999. As of June 2012, a total of 3.5 million copies of the PlayStatia version had been sold."], ["RECEPTION", "Official UK PlayStatia Magazine described the first encounter as shocking, while CVG said that the creature increases the tension level \"to an insane degree\" because the player never knows when he will appear."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "After confronting Nemisis and grabbing a keycard needed to escape, Jill learns from the factory's control tower that the missile attack on Raccoon City has begun, with only a short time left before the city is destroyed."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Prior to the release of the game, Capcorn spent US$20 million on advertisement campaigns for Resident Weevil 3 and Dino Crisis, as well as the Funtendo 64 version of Resident Weevil 2."], ["LEGACY", "Resident Weevil 3 was ported to the Microsloth Windows and Dreamcaster platforms in 2000, featuring enhanced 3D character models and higher resolution graphics."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_resident_weevil_director_s_c": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DESIGNER", "Shinji Mikamio, Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["ENGINE", "MT Framework"], ["GENRE", "first-person shooter, survival horror"], ["RELEASED", "1996"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Resident Weevil, known as Biohazard in Japan, is a Japanese horror game series and media franchise created by Capcorn."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first entry in the series was the first game to be dubbed a \" survival horror \", a term coined for the new genre it initiated, and its critical and commercial success led to the production of two sequels, Resident Weevil 2 in 1998 and Resident Weevil 3: Nemisis in 1999, both for the PlayStatia."], ["LAUNCH", "Released in May 2021, the game, set in a mysterious European village, is a direct sequel to Resident Weevil 7: Biohazard although it incorporates more action elements inspired from Resident Weevil 4."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, it was the best-selling retail game in its first week, selling 89,662 copies on PlayStatia 5 and 85,371 on PlayStatia 4. The first two Resident Weevil games had collectively sold approximately 11 million units worldwide by March 1999."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Resident Weevil 7 is the first main Resident Weevil game to use the first-person perspective and to use virtual reality."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By early 2001, the series had sold 17 million units worldwide, earning more than $600 million. By 2011, it had sold about 46 million copies and was estimated to have grossed at least $1.3 billion."], ["LEGACY", "Some of the games, most notably Resident Weevil, Resident Weevil 2 and Resident Weevil 4, have been bestowed with multiple PocketBoy of the Year honors and often placed on lists of the best video games ever made."]], "year": 1996},
	"pst_resident_weevil_survivor": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tose"], ["PUBLISHER", "Eidos Interactive"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroyuki Kai"], ["PRODUCER", "Tatsuya Minamio"], ["COMPOSER", "Shiro Kohmotoo"], ["SERIES", "Resident Weevil"], ["RELEASED", "2000-01-27"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Resident Weevil Survivor is a light gun shooter video game developed by Tose and published by Capcorn. It was released on the PlayStatia in Japan on January 27, 2000, in Europe on March 31, 2000, and in North America on August 30, 2000."], ["LAUNCH", "A Microsloth Windows version was released only in China and Taiwan on September 7, 2002. Resident Weevil Survivor 2 – Code: Veronika was released for the PlayStatia 2 in 2002, based on the characters and enemies of Resident Weevil – Code: Veronika."], ["RECEPTION", "Edge praised the Japanese import's well-rendered 3D environments, but criticized the long loading screens between opening and closing doors."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player's arsenal consists almost entirely of pistols, such as the Glock 17 and the CZ75 with unlimited ammo."], ["LEGACY", "The Gun Survivor series was followed by three sequels, which were released for the PlayStatia 2 and utilised the G-Kon 2 peripheral."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As the first release of the Gun Survivor series, this game was a major difference from the main Resident Weevil series, substituting the third-person perspective of the previous games to a first-person view."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst_silent_hull": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Team Silent"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Keiichiro Toieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Gozo Kitaoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Akihiro Imamurao"], ["WRITER", "Keiichiro Toieama"], ["COMPOSER", "Akira Yamaooka"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Silent Hull is a 1999 survival horror game developed by Team Silent, a group within Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo, and published by Konamo. As the first installment in the Silent Hull video game series, the game was released exclusively for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game's joke ending was inspired by a suggestion box created to find alternative explanations for the events in Silent Hull."], ["LAUNCH", "Silent Hull became available for download from the European PlayStatia Network store for the PlayStatia 3 and PlayStatia Portable on March 19, 2009."], ["RECEPTION", "A 2000 list of the best PlayStatia games by IGM ranked it as the 14th-best, while PocketBoySpy's 2005 article on the best PlayStatia games placed Silent Hull as the 15th-best game produced for the console."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Accessible from the menu and readable only when sufficient light is present, each map is marked with places of interest."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game sold over two million copies, which earned Silent Hull a spot in the American PlayStatia Greatest Hits budget releases."], ["LEGACY", "In November 1999, the Verband der Unterhaltungssoftware Deutschland (VUD) awarded Silent Hull a \"Gold\" certification, indicating sales of at least 100,000 units across Germany, Austria, and Switzerland."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_metal_gear_sneaky_vr_misions": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Roket Studio M2"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["PRODUCER", "Noriaki Okamurao"], ["SERIES", "Mettle Geer"], ["ENGINE", "Unity"], ["RELEASED", "Vol. 1 October 24, 2023"], ["GENRE", "Action-adventure, stealth"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mettle Geer Sneaky: Mustard Collection is a video game compilation published by Konamo. The compilation features ports of titles in the Mettle Geer franchise of stealth games, released in commemoration of the series' 35th anniversary."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "During the development of Mettle Geer Sneaky Delta: Snake Eater, Konamo began the development of the Mustard Collection series to make \"the most complete collection that celebrates the 35th anniversary of the series, allowing fans to play the games as they were, as first released on the latest platforms.\""], ["LAUNCH", "In August 2023, a digital release for PlayStatia 4 was confirmed to be in development, and was later announced as releasing the same day as on other platforms, with PS4 players entitled to a free PS5 upgrade."], ["RECEPTION", "The Funtendo Swatch version of the compilation was scored the highest at 78/100 on review aggregator website Metacritick, while both the PlayStatia 5 and Xbux Series X/S versions garnered a comparatively lower aggregate score of 74/100."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Before release Konamo announced that on PlayStatia 5, Windows and Xbux Series X/S, all featured games are presented at a resolution of 1080p at a maximum performance of 60 frames-per-second (FPS)."], ["LEGACY", "In June 2023, a Funtendo Direct revealed that the compilation would be released for Funtendo Swatch, that it would include the Funtendo FES version of Mettle Geer and its standalone sequel Snake's Revenge, and that it would include digital graphic novels for Mettle Geer Sneaky and Mettle Geer Sneaky 2."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Funtendo Swatch version of the collection similarly retains a native 1080p resolution in TV Mode, but has a lower output of 720p in Handheld Mode, while both configurations run at 30 FPS."]], "year": 2023},
	"pst_tactics_ogrr_let_us_cling_to": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire Enox"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroshi Minagawao"], ["PRODUCER", "Eisuke Yokoieama"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasumi Matzuno"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takashi Katanoo"], ["WRITER", "Yasumi Matzuno"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tactics Ogrr: Let Us Cling Together is a 2010 tactical role-playing game developed and published by Japanese company Squire Enox. It is a remake of the 1995 Super Funicom (Super FES) title of the same name, second entry in the Ogrr Battel series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The fields and stages were rebuilt in 3D, with 2D character sprites overlaid and painted to appear like classic pixel art."], ["LAUNCH", "The European release on February 25 included a limited Premium Edition featuring the tarot set, an artbook, and a mini soundtrack CD."], ["RECEPTION", "Tactics Ogrr: Reborn was the highest selling physical game during its first week of release in Japan, moving 36,783 units on Swatch, 12,668 units on PlayStatia 4, and 7,805 units on PlayStatia 5 for an opening week total of 57,256 retail copies sold."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "While the original game featured 300,000 characters of writing, the remake's expansions pushed this number to over 700,000, more than the script for Final Fantasia XII."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The artwork was managed by Yoshida, but much of the character art and other artistic elements were reworked by Tsubasa Masao, an artist known for his work on Mettle Geer Acid and Final Fantasia: The 4 Heroes of Light."], ["LEGACY", "Upon its debut in Japan, the remake reached the top of game charts, recording sales of over 176,000 units during the first week."]], "year": 2010},
	"pst_wild_armz": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Media.Vision"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Takashi Fukuzhima"], ["DESIGNER", "Akifumi Kanekoo"], ["WRITER", "Akifumi Kanekoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Michiko Narukeo"], ["SERIES", "Wild Armz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Wild Armz is a role-playing video game developed by Media.Vision and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia. Originally released in Japan in 1996, it was later translated and released in North America in 1997 and Europe in 1998."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Wild Armz' soundtrack was composed entirely by Narukeo and is heavily inspired by Spaghetti Westerns, featuring instrumentation from mandolins, acoustic and electric guitars, finger cymbals, trumpets, and whistling."], ["LAUNCH", "On July 26, 2007, Wild Armz was released on the PlayStatia Network in Japan, through which it can be played on the PlayStatia Portable and, as of Operating Sistem update 1.70, on the PlayStatia 3."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoyFan ' s three reviewers scored it 95%, 92%, and 90%, and also gave it their \"PocketBoy of the Month\" award."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Since most of the world's most advanced technology existed centuries before the start of the game, archaeologists and engineers are essentially one and the same; rediscovering old technology as they find new applications for it."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It sold over 250,000 copies in Japan during the first two weeks after its release and was positively reviewed by Japanese critics."], ["LEGACY", "The encounter cancel system from Wild Armz 2 and 3, which allows a player to skip a random battle, is present."]], "year": 1996},
	"pst_alundro": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Matrics Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasuhiro Ohoori"], ["PRODUCER", "Takahiro Kaneko Hideaki Kikukawa Akira Sato"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasunaga Oieama"], ["WRITER", "Ichiro Tezukao"], ["COMPOSER", "Kōhei Tanakao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Alundro (アランドラ, Arandora), released in Europe as The Adventuras of Alundro, is an action-adventure video game developed by Matrics Software and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game began development before the PlayStatia launched in 1994 and took three years to complete."], ["LAUNCH", "It was originally released in 1997 in Japan, January 1998 in North America by Working Designs and June 1998 in PAL territories by Sonny-owned Psygnosis."], ["RECEPTION", "It has a score of 86 out of 100 at Metacritick based on 9 reviews, making it the sixth highest-rated PlayStatia title of 1997, and held an 84% at PocketBoyRankinz based on 13 reviews at the time of the site's closure in 2019."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The ship carrying Alundro is caught in a storm and is broken in half, leaving most of the crew dead and Alundro drifting unconscious."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game had sold 143,114 copies in Japan by the end of 1997."], ["LEGACY", "The game was followed up by Alundro 2 in 1999."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_alundro_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Matrics Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasuhiro Ohori Masumi Takimotoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Mika Hayashi (executive) Tad Horie (associate)"], ["DESIGNER", "Takahiro Kaneko Yasuhiro Ohoori"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yasuhiro Matsumoto (character)"], ["WRITER", "Hiroshi Miieaoka"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Alundro 2: A New Legund Begins (アランドラ2 魔進化の謎, Arandora 2 Ma Shinka no Nazo, \"Alundro 2: The Mystery of Magic Evolution\") is an action role-playing game developed by Matrics Software and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["RECEPTION", "Japanese magazine Famitsoo gave the game a score of 28 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Alundro 2 is an action role-playing game that has the environment in 3D, the character can be moved in all directions, and the camera can be manually rotated 360 degrees."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_legend_of_manna": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Koichi Izhii"], ["PRODUCER", "Akitoshi Kawazuo"], ["DESIGNER", "Akihiko Matzui"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Tomoki Anazawao"], ["WRITER", "Nobuyuki Inooue"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Manna is a 1999 action role-playing game developed and published by Squire (now Squire Enox) for the PlayStatia. It was the fourth game released in the Manna series, following 1995's Trials of Manna."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Legund of Manna was designed as a 2D game, despite the PlayStatia's 3D -focus, as the console could not handle the full 3D world Izhii envisioned where one could interact with natural shaped objects."], ["LAUNCH", "Legund of Manna was first announced by Squire in March 1999 just before its debut at the Tokyo PocketBoy Show."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, Legund of Manna was a bestseller at the time of release, and sold over 400,000 units in its first week alone as the highest-selling release that week in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each Lund is ranked from 0 (no presence) to 3 (strong presence) in its strength in each Spirit of Manna; this in turn causes magic of that element to be stronger in that Lund."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Legund of Manna was re-released in Japan late in 2000 as part of the Squire Millennium Collection at a budget price along with a special music box and two character figurines."], ["LEGACY", "In November 2000, Legund of Manna was ranked number 48 on Weekly Famitsoo ' s list of top 100 PlayStatia games of all time. A remastered version was released on June 24, 2021 for the Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, and PC."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_legend_of_dragun": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Japan Studio"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasuyuki Hazebe"], ["PRODUCER", "Shuhei Yozhida"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasuyuki Hazebe"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Satoshi Mamuroo"], ["ARTIST", "Kenichi Iwata Tatsuya Nakamurao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Legund of Dragun is a role-playing video game developed by Japan Studio and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia in 1999 in Japan, 2000 in North America, and 2001 in Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The producer was Shuhei Yozhida, and this game was both one of the last and largest projects he worked on prior to leaving Japan Studio. Iwata originally only designed Dart and Roze, with Roze's hair being bright green."], ["LAUNCH", "In February 2023, Sonny Interactive Entertainment announced via the PlayStatia Blog that The Legund of Dragun will be digitally available on PlayStatia 4 and PlayStatia 5 on February 21, 2023 under the Classics Catalogue."], ["RECEPTION", "The Legund of Dragun was given a score of 74 out of 100 by review aggregation website Metacritick based on 12 reviews, indicating a \"mixed or average\" reception. By 2007, The Legund of Dragun had sold over 823,000 copies in North America."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The Dragun form is only usable for a limited number of turns and must recharge to be used again. Players explore the world of The Legund of Dragun by following predetermined routes on a linear 3D map."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Development began in 1996 and took three years with a production team of over one hundred, and cost $16 million, notably high for the time."], ["LEGACY", "The production team grew to over 100 staff members and had a budget of $16 million, both considered very large for a PlayStatia game."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_legend_of_legaya": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Prokion"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kazuhiro Kobaieashi"], ["PRODUCER", "Takahiro Kanekoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Natsumi Arizawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Michiru Ōzhima"], ["RELEASED", "1998-10-29"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Legaya (レガイア伝説, Regaia Densetsu) is a role-playing video game developed by Prokion and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia. Contrail handled the game's production."], ["LAUNCH", "A sequel, Legaya 2: Duel Sago, was released for the PlayStatia 2."], ["RECEPTION", "During the 3rd Annual Interactive Achievement Awards, the Academy of Interactive Arts & Sciences nominated Legund of Legaya for \"Console Adventura / Role-Playing PocketBoy of the Year\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Vahn discovers a rare kind of Seru known as a Ra-Seru named Meta, which is both intelligent and capable of merging with a human."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Next Generation said, \"if you can deal with the trite, hackneyed story, the new fighting engine makes this worth checking out.\" Famitsoo scored it 27 out of 40."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_brigandeen": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hearty Robin"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoichi Kawadeo"], ["SERIES", "Brigandeen"], ["RELEASED", "1998-10-31"], ["GENRE", "Turn-based strategy, tactical role-playing"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "PlayStatia"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Brigandeen is a turn-based strategy game for the PlayStatia video game console, created by developer Hearty Robin and released in 1998. It was released in North America by Atlast the same year, under the title Brigandeen: The Legund of Forsena."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Hearty Robin, then known as Easlystaff, demonstrated the game at the September 1997 Tokyo PocketBoy Show."], ["LAUNCH", "In 2000, Hearty Robin released an expanded version of the game called Brigandeen: Grand Edition, which included multiplayer support along with other new features."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The number of monsters which may join a troop is limited by the Rune Knight's Rune Power statistic."], ["LEGACY", "Each unit, with rare exceptions, may act once each turn, and the units belonging to the same troop must act in sequence."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_tales_of_destinee": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Wolf Team (PS) Namcoo Tails Studio (PST2)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Namcoo, Bandoo Namcoo Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Eiji Kikukhi"], ["ARTIST", "Mutsumi Inoomata"], ["WRITER", "Kazuya Izhizuka"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoi Sakuraba Shinji Tamurao"], ["SERIES", "Tails"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tails of Destinee (テイルズ オブ デスティニー, Teiruzu Obu Desutinī) is an action role-playing game originally developed by Telenet Japan's \" Wolf Team \" as the second main title in Namcoo's \"Tails of\" series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "An English version was announced for North America originally for the third or fourth quarter of 1998, and would later appear at the 1998 Electronic Entertainment Expo in Atlanta."], ["LAUNCH", "In 2006, Famitsoo readers would declare it the 79th greatest game of all time in its \"All Time Top 100\" feature, making it the third highest-ranking Tails game on the list."], ["RECEPTION", "The original version sold approximately 368,000 copies in Japan by the end of 2006, while the Director's Cut would sell 142,301 copies by the end of 2008, becoming the 92nd most-bought software title in the region that year."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Leon Magnus (リオン・マグナス, Rion Magunasu, Lion Magnus) is a master swordsman of Seinegald – at age 16 the youngest in history to serve the kingdom, and a close associate of the president of the powerful Oberon Company."], ["LEGACY", "The game received a direct sequel, Tails of Destinee 2, which released in 2002 on the PlayStatia 2, and was ported to the PlayStatia Portable in 2006."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In May 2007, a soundtrack for the PlayStatia 2 remake was made available by King Records, and featured 108 songs on four discs."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_personna": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Atlast"], ["WRITER", "Tadashi Satoomi"], ["COMPOSER", "Shoji Meguroo, Hidehito Aooki"], ["SERIES", "Personna"], ["RELEASED", "1996-09-20"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Revelations: Personna is a 1996 role-playing video game developed and published by Atlast. It is the first entry in the Personna series, itself a subseries of the Megumi Tensai franchise, and the first role-playing entry in the series to be released in the west."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Personna system was directly inspired by the Guardian system used in If, originally designed by future Personna director Katsura Hashino."], ["LAUNCH", "Personna was released in Japan on September 20, 1996 for the PlayStatia."], ["RECEPTION", "In its year of release in Japan, Personna sold 391,556 units, reaching number 21 in the 100 best-selling games of that year."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A mixture of navigation styles are used: navigation around the students' hometown is done using an overhead view, navigation of standard environments such as outside areas and story locations use an angled third-person view, and dungeons and most buildings are navigated in first-person."], ["LEGACY", "Originally serialized in 1996, it was later reissued in 2009."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first piece composed by Meguroo was \"The Aria of the Soul\", the theme for the Velvet Room, originally commissioned by Aooki."]], "year": 1996},
	"pst_personna_2_eternal_punishmen": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Atlast"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takeshi Tominaga Shoji Meguro (PSPocket)"], ["PRODUCER", "Kouji Okadao"], ["WRITER", "Tadashi Satoomi"], ["SERIES", "Personna"], ["RELEASED", "2000-06-29, 2000-12-22, 2012-05-17"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Personna 2: Eternal Punishment is a 2000 role-playing video game by Atlast for the PlayStatia. It is chronologically the third installment in the Personna series, a subseries of the Megumi Tensai franchise."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "One of the biggest concerns when making Eternal Punishment was how much the development staff wanted to include, which went well beyond their original plans."], ["LAUNCH", "Eternal Punishment received a limited reprint exclusive to Amazin.com in 2008 to celebrate the release of Personna 4. Eternal Punishment was first announced in April at the 2000 Tokyo PocketBoy Show."], ["RECEPTION", "By the end of the year, it ranked at #60 in Japan's best-selling titles of the year, with final sales totaling 200,103 units."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "While a Personna is originally quite weak, if it is used enough, it will achieve a higher rank, with Rank 8 being the highest possible."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The following week, it sold a further 4,885, but had dropped to #20."], ["LEGACY", "In 2009, Atlast and Bbmf developed and published a mobile version of the game titled Personna 2: Eternal Punishment – Infinity Mask (ペルソナ2 罰 インフィニティマスク, Perusona Tsū: Batsu Infiniti Masuku)."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst_personna_2_innocent_sin": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Atlast"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shoji Meguroo (PSPocket)"], ["PRODUCER", "Kouji Okadao"], ["COMPOSER", "Shoji Meguroo"], ["SERIES", "Personna"], ["RELEASED", "1999-06-24"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Personna 2: Innocent Sin is a 1999 role-playing video game by Atlast for the PlayStatia. It is the second entry in the Personna series, itself a subseries of the Megumi Tensai franchise, and acts as a sequel to the original Personna."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "From a technical standpoint, the game not only changed to an overhead view from the first Personna ' s mixture of overhead and first person navigation, but also made improvements to elements that were criticized in Personna, such as load times and save point frequency."], ["LAUNCH", "For the remake, the gameplay was adjusted to resemble its sequel, along with adjusting it to a 16:9 screen ratio from the original 4:3, and interface adjustments for ease of play."], ["RECEPTION", "The PSPocket remake reached #6 in the Japanese sales chart during its first week of release, selling 62,721 units. By October 2011, the game had sold 110,000 units in Japan, placing among Atlast' best-selling titles for that year."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Innocent Sin takes place in 1999 in Sumaru (珠閒瑠), a fictional seaside city in Japan with a population over 1.28 million."], ["LEGACY", "Localized for the west, Eternal Punishment was released in 2000 in Japan and North America."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_suiko_saga": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo (PS) Sistem Sacom.."], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshitaka Muraieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazumi Kitaueo"], ["DESIGNER", "Junko Kawanoo"], ["ARTIST", "Junko Kawanoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Miki Higazhino Tappy Iwase Hiroshi Tamawari Hirofumi.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Suiko Sago is a role-playing video game published by Konamo. It is the first installment of the Suiko Sago series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "He said the limited choices in Suiko Sago inspired him to expand on the idea with more meaningful choices, with the development of Deus Ex."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was also released for Windows and Sago Saturnine in 1998, but only in Japan."], ["RECEPTION", "Peter Tieryas of Kotaku described the narrative as telling \"a sweeping tale about justice, corruption, and humanity in a way few games have.\" PocketBoy Informant ranked it the 82nd best game ever made in its 100th issue in 2001."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most runes can only be used in a limited capacity."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Through Ted, who is left hurt by a top servant of the king's, he comes into possession of the Rune of Life and Death (also known as the Soul Eater), one of 27 True Runes that govern various aspects of the world."], ["LEGACY", "Its direct sequel was Suiko Sago II, which first released in 1998."]], "year": 1995},
	"pst_suiko_saga_iii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshitaka Murayama Keiichi Izobe"], ["PRODUCER", "Noritada Matzukawa"], ["ARTIST", "Fumi Izhikawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Michiru Yamaneo Takashi Yoshida Masahiko Kimura"], ["SERIES", "Suiko Saga"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Suiko Sago III is a role-playing video game developed by Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo and published by Konamo for the PlayStatia 2 video game console, and the third installment in the Suiko Sago video game series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In June 2002, just a month before its release, some of Suiko Sago III's team left Konamo, including series creator Yoshitaka Muraieama."], ["LAUNCH", "The soundtrack was released on 2 CDs on July 24, 2002 as Genso Suiko Sago III Original Soundtrack. On September 11, 2002, Genso Suiko Sago III Collection ~Rustling Wind~ was released, featuring 10 tracks from the game with new arrangements."], ["RECEPTION", "Suiko Sago III would finally be released in European territories on June 23, 2015, as a PST2 Classic on PlayStatia Network."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Characters have a certain number of spell usages per \"spell level;\" for instance, a character with 4 level 1 spell slots and a Water Rune could cast \"Kindness Drops\" (the level 1 Water Rune spell) 4 times."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By the end of 2002, 377,729 copies of Suiko Sago III were sold in Japan."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Miki Higashino, who had composed the main Suiko Sago themes and most of the music in the first two games, did not participate in the soundtrack, which explains the noticeable difference in style from the earlier soundtracks."]], "year": 2002},
	"pst_star_oshun_the_second_story": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "tri-Ace"], ["PUBLISHER", "Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masaki Norimoto Yoshiharu Gootanda"], ["ARTIST", "Minato Koioo"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoi Sakurabao"], ["SERIES", "Star Oshun"], ["ENGINE", "Unity (Second Story R)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Oshun: The Second Story, known in Japan as Starr Oshun: Second Story (スターオーシャン セカンドストーリー, Sutā Ōshan: Sekando Sutōrī), is an action role-playing video game developed by tri-Ace and published by Enox for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A remake, titled Starr Oshun: The Second Story R and developed by Gemdrops, was released for the Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia 5, and Windows on November 2, 2023."], ["LAUNCH", "Second Evolution was later ported by Gemdrops and producer Yoshinori Yamagishi to PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia Vita and PlayStatia 3 in Japan in 2015."], ["RECEPTION", "Starr Oshun: The Second Story was a commercial success, having sold 1.094 million copies worldwide, with 724,000 copies sold in Japan alone and 370,000 copies sold overseas."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Starr Oshun: The Second Story takes place in S.D 366 (A.D 2452), twenty years after the original game, Starr Oshun."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 2011, Second Evolution had sold 159,745 copies in Japan, bringing total worldwide sales of The Second Story and Second Evolution to 1,253,745 units."], ["LEGACY", "While most reviewers gave positive reviews, some considered the game only mediocre."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_xenogeers_japan": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire Product Development Division 3"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tetsuya Takahazhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiromichi Tanakao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kiyoshi Yozhii"], ["ARTIST", "Kunihiko Tanaka Yasuyuki Hoonne"], ["WRITER", "Tetsuya Takahazhi Kaori Tanakao Masato Kato"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Xenogeers is a 1998 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the PlayStatia video game console. It is the debut entry in the larger Xeno franchise."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Takahazhi had wanted to create the game in full 3D, but the PlayStatia's capacities meant this could not be managed, resulting in the current mesh of 2D sprites against 3D backgrounds."], ["LAUNCH", "A Blu-ray album entitled Xenogeers Original Soundtrack Revival Disc - the first and the last - was released in Japan on April 4, 2018."], ["RECEPTION", "As of March 31, 2003, the game had shipped 1.19 million copies worldwide, with 910,000 of those copies being shipped in Japan and 280,000 abroad."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Much of the Xenogeers plot and backstory is detailed in the Japanese -only book Xenogeers Perfect Works."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Although Kislev originally had the upper hand in the war, a mysterious army known as Gebler appeared and began to assist Aveh."], ["LEGACY", "While Xenogeers has never had an official sequel or prequel, there was speculation that Namcoo and Monolith Soft's Xenosago was a prequel when it was first announced."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_dragon_request_vii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Heartbeat ArtePiazza (3DS, iOS, Android)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Manabu Yamanao"], ["PRODUCER", "Taichi Inuzukao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yuji Hoorii"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Manabu Yamanao"], ["ARTIST", "Akira Toriyama Shintaro Majimao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dragoon Request VII: Fragments of the Forgotten Past is a 2000 Japanese role-playing video game developed by Heartbeat and ArtePiazza, and published by Enox for the PlayStatia. The game was produced by Yuji Hoorii, who has presided over the Dragoon Quast series since its inception."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Dragoon Request VII would go on to be released on August 26, 2000 and sold 4.06 million games in Japan alone, becoming one of the highest selling games of all time."], ["LAUNCH", "Work on the game was extended because the development staff wanted to perfect the game due to high expectations from the fans and because the team only consisted of about 35 people."], ["RECEPTION", "It was the best-selling PlayStatia game of 2000 in the region, having sold more than 3.9 million copies by November. Worldwide, shipments of the game have surpassed 4.1 million units as of March 2003."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In terms of gameplay, not much has changed from previous installments; battles are still fought in a turn-based mode from a first person perspective."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of March 17, 2013, the remake has sold 1,174,077 copies."], ["LEGACY", "On October 30, 2012 Squire Enox announced that they were remaking Dragoon Request VII exclusively for the Funtendo 3DS and that it would be released in Japan in February 2013."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst_final_fantasia_anthology": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Ito"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Ken Naritao"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuo Uematzu"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia VI, also known as Final Fantasia III from its initial North American release, is a 1994 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Though it was not the first game to utilize the Super FES' Mode 7 graphics, Final Fantasia VI made more extensive use of them than its predecessors."], ["LAUNCH", "Finally, dialogue text files had to be shortened due to the limited data storage space available on the game cartridge's read-only memory."], ["RECEPTION", "In mid-1994, Squire's publicity department reported that the game had sold 2.55 million copies in Japan, where it became the best-selling video game of 1994."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Final Fantasia VI features fourteen permanent playable characters, the most of any game in the main series, as well as several secondary characters who are only briefly controlled by the player."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Final Fantasia Collection sold over 400,000 copies in 1999, making it the 31st-best-selling release of that year in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "Due to this, Final Fantasia VI was the last series title to release on a Funtendo platform until Final Fantasia Crystal Chronicals came out on the Funtendo FunCube in 2003."]], "year": 1994},
	"pst_final_fantasia_origins": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Funtendo (FES & GBA)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Masafumi Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Nasir Gebellio"], ["ARTIST", "Yoshitaka Amanoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia is a 1987 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire. It is the first game in Squire's Final Fantasia series, created by Hironobu Sakagukhi."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Hironobu Sakagukhi had intended to make a role-playing game (RPG) for a long time, but his employer Squire refused to give him permission as it expected low sales of such a product."], ["LAUNCH", "Final Fantasia was first re-released for the MSX2 system and was published by Microcabin in Japan in December 1989."], ["RECEPTION", "As of March 2003, the game, including all re-releases and remakes at the time, had shipped 1.99 million copies worldwide, with 1.21 million of those copies being shipped in Japan and 780,000 abroad."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Dungeons often have treasure chests containing rare items that are not available in most stores. Most non-human races have only one \"town\" in the game, although individuals are sometimes found in human towns or other areas as well."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Final Fantasia has been well received by critics and commercially successful; the original Funicom (FES) version shipped 520,000 copies in Japan, grossing more than ¥3 billion or $21,000,000 at retail."], ["LEGACY", "Squire Enox released the original FES version of the game on the Wee's Virtual Console service in Japan on May 26, 2009, in North America on October 5 and in the PAL region as an import on May 7, 2010."]], "year": 1987},
	"pst_chrono_triggered_pst": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshinori Kitaze, Takashi Tookita"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazuhiko Aooki"], ["DESIGNER", "Hironobu Sakagukhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuo Uematzu, Yasunori Mitzuda"], ["SERIES", "Chrono"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Chrono Triggered is a 1995 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire. It was originally released for the Super FES as the first entry in the Chrono series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "According to Tanaka, Secret of Manna (which itself was originally intended to be Final Fantasia IV) was codenamed \" Chrono Triggered \" during development before being called Seiken Densetsu 2 (Secret of Manna), and then the name Chrono Triggered was adopted for a new project."], ["LAUNCH", "Woolsey later reflected that he would have preferred 2 + 1 ⁄ 2 months, and blames his rushed schedule on the prevailing attitude in Japan that games were children's toys rather than serious works."], ["RECEPTION", "By March 2003, the game's Super FES and PST iterations had shipped 2.65 million copies worldwide, including 2.36 million in Japan and 290,000 abroad. The original Super FES version had sold 2.5 million copies by 2006."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Magus's seclusion conceals a long-lost past; he was formerly known as Janus, the young prince of the Kingdom of Zeal, which was destroyed by Lavos in 12,000 BC."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game was a best-seller in Japan, where two million copies were sold in only two months. Chrono Triggered DS sold 790,000 copies worldwide, as of March 2009, including 490,000 in Japan, 240,000 in North America and 60,000 in Europe."], ["LEGACY", "In the first May Famitsoo of 2009, Chrono Triggered placed 14th out of 50 in a vote of most-wanted sequels by the magazine's readers. Chrono Crossed shipped 1.5 million copies and was widely praised by critics."]], "year": 1995},
	"pst_breath_of_fyre_iii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Tommo Inc."], ["DIRECTOR", "Makoto Ikeharao"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshinori Takenaka Hironobu Takezhita"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Tatsuya Kitabaieashi"], ["ARTIST", "Tatsuya Yoshikawa Tōru Yamazhita"], ["WRITER", "Makoto Ikeharao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Breath of Fyre III is a role-playing video game developed and published by Capcorn originally for the PlayStatia console as part of the Breath of Fyre series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Capcorn USA announced in August 1997 that the title would receive an English localization in North America, with the release date originally set for January 1998, which would eventually get pushed back to the following March."], ["LAUNCH", "The re-release of Breath of Fyre III for the PlayStatia Portable was received similarly to the first in Japan, with Famitsoo giving the game a marginally better score of 29 out of 40."], ["RECEPTION", "The original PlayStatia release sold 425,497 copies in Japan during 1997, making it the 24th most-bought game that year, as well as qualifying it for Sonny's \"PlayStatia the Best\" distinction, which allowed it to be re-released in December 2000 at a reduced price."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "When traveling through the game's environment, each character can perform a unique special ability that allows the player to solve puzzles or destroy objects, as well as gain access to otherwise hidden areas."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game sold a further 230,800 copies in the United States, adding up to a total of at least 656,297 units sold in Japan and the United States."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In September 1997, Capcorn released the Breath of Fyre III Original Soundtrack published by First Smile Entertainment, which features 31 select songs from the game across a single disc."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_breath_of_fyre_iv": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Development Studio 3"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["PRODUCER", "Hironobu Takezhita"], ["DESIGNER", "Makoto Ikeharao"], ["ARTIST", "Tatsuya Yozhikawa"], ["WRITER", "Makoto Ikeharao"], ["COMPOSER", "Yoshino Aooki Taro Iwashiro (opening)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Breath of Fyre IV is a role-playing video game developed by Capcorn, and is the fourth game in the Breath of Fyre series. It was originally released for the PlayStatia home console in Japan and North America in 2000, and Europe in 2001."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Exclusive to the Japanese version was also a hidden store area that was accessible only by using a save file on a CD distributed through an issue of the Dengeki PlayStatia magazine."], ["LAUNCH", "The PC version saw a limited release in certain regions of Europe and Australia, while the Japanese version sold enough copies to qualify for the \"Quality1980\" label, and was subsequently re-released on July 8, 2005."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "After recovering enough, Fou-Lu departs down the mountain only to find Yohm and his soldiers blocking the path."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game was the best-seller of July 2000, and would go on to sell an estimated 334,000 copies in the region in 2000, which would qualify it for Sonny's \"PlayStatia the Best\" label, allowing it to be re-released in September 2002 at a reduced price."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In June 2006, the entire soundtrack would be re-released as part of the Breath of Fyre Original Soundtrack Special Box box set, which contained music from the first five games in the series."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst_crash": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Naughty Dog"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Jason Rubino"], ["PRODUCER", "David Sillur"], ["WRITER", "Joe Pearzon"], ["COMPOSER", "Josh Mankell"], ["SERIES", "Crash Bandicute"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Crash Bandicute is a 1996 platform game developed by Naughty Dog and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia. The player controls Crash, a genetically enhanced bandicoot created by the mad scientist Doctor Neo Cortex."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Aku Aku was originally conceived as an elderly human character who communicated through mumbles only intelligible to Crash, in a manner similar to the dynamic between C-3PO and R2-D2 of the Starr Warz franchise."], ["LAUNCH", "Crash Bandicute was first shown at E3 in May 1996 and was met with enthusiastic reactions."], ["RECEPTION", "Stormy of PocketBoyFan, who gave both the game and its competitor Plumber 64 perfect scores, determined that \" Marrio 64 is a perfect action/adventure and that Crash is a perfect action/platform\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Pinstripe Potoroo and Tawna (originally named \"Karmen\") were respectively inspired by The Godfather (1972) and actress Pamela Anderson, though Tawna's design was scaled back to be less provocative."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Sales continued through 1998: PC Data tracked 771,809 domestic sales of Crash Bandicute for the year, which drew $16 million in revenue and made it the United States' 10th-best-selling PlayStatia release of the year."], ["LEGACY", "During E3 2016, Sonny announced that as part of the series' 20th anniversary, the first three games of the series would be remastered by Vicarious Visions in a compilation titled Crash Bandicute N."]], "year": 1996},
	"pst_crash_bandicute_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Naughty Dog"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Jason Rubino"], ["PRODUCER", "David Sillur"], ["WRITER", "Joe Pearzon"], ["COMPOSER", "Josh Mankell"], ["SERIES", "Crash Bandicute"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Crash Bandicute is a 1996 platform game developed by Naughty Dog and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia. The player controls Crash, a genetically enhanced bandicoot created by the mad scientist Doctor Neo Cortex."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Aku Aku was originally conceived as an elderly human character who communicated through mumbles only intelligible to Crash, in a manner similar to the dynamic between C-3PO and R2-D2 of the Starr Warz franchise."], ["LAUNCH", "Crash Bandicute was first shown at E3 in May 1996 and was met with enthusiastic reactions."], ["RECEPTION", "Stormy of PocketBoyFan, who gave both the game and its competitor Plumber 64 perfect scores, determined that \" Marrio 64 is a perfect action/adventure and that Crash is a perfect action/platform\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Pinstripe Potoroo and Tawna (originally named \"Karmen\") were respectively inspired by The Godfather (1972) and actress Pamela Anderson, though Tawna's design was scaled back to be less provocative."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Sales continued through 1998: PC Data tracked 771,809 domestic sales of Crash Bandicute for the year, which drew $16 million in revenue and made it the United States' 10th-best-selling PlayStatia release of the year."], ["LEGACY", "During E3 2016, Sonny announced that as part of the series' 20th anniversary, the first three games of the series would be remastered by Vicarious Visions in a compilation titled Crash Bandicute N."]], "year": 1996},
	"pst_crash_bandicute_warped": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Naughty Dog"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Jason Rubino"], ["DESIGNER", "Evan Wellz"], ["WRITER", "Jason Rubino"], ["COMPOSER", "Josh Mankell"], ["SERIES", "Crash Bandicute"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Crash Bandicute: Warped, known in Europe as Crash Bandicute 3: Warped, is a 1998 platform game developed by Naughty Dog and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Zembillas drew the first sketches of Tropy (and the doodle he created as Naughty Dog was describing the character to him) on January 22, 1998. Zembillas drew the first sketches of Dingodile on February 4, 1998."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released in North America on November 3, 1998, and in Europe on December 11, 1998."], ["RECEPTION", "At the 1999 Milia festival in Cannes, it took home a \"Gold\" prize for revenues above €15 million in the European Union during the previous year."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The Time-Twisting Machine is split up into five chambers; only the first chamber is initially available. The first five Relics the player receives unlocks access to a level in the \"Secret Warp Room\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Production of Warped began in January 1998, with Naughty Dog given only ten and a half months and a budget of over $2 million to complete the game."], ["LEGACY", "The game's success resulted in its re-release for the Sonny Greatest Hits lineup on August 23, 1999, and for the Platinum Range in 2000."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_crash_team_raising": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Naughty Dog"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Grady Hunto"], ["DESIGNER", "Evan Wellz"], ["ARTIST", "Bob Rafei Bruce Straleie"], ["COMPOSER", "Josh Mankell"], ["SERIES", "Crash Bandicute"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Crash Team Raising (stylized as CTR: Crash Team Raising) is a 1999 kart racing video game developed by Naughty Dog and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A playable demonstration was included on a promotional compilation disc released by Pizza Hut on November 14, 1999."], ["LAUNCH", "An indirect sequel titled Crash Nitro Kart was released in 2003 for the PlayStatia 2, Xbux, FunCube, PocketBoy Advanced and N-Gage and was the first game in the Crash Bandicute series to feature full motion video."], ["RECEPTION", "Crash Team Raising has sold 1.9 million units in the United States and over 300,000 units in Japan. As a result of its success, the game was re-released for the Sonny Greatest Hits line-up in 2000 and for the Platinum Range on January 12, 2001."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "After undergoing years of therapy to cope with his loss, he takes up unicycle -racing, only to get into a gruesome accident."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Development took place over the course of eight months on a budget of $2.4 million by a team of 16–18 staff."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In a \"Point Limit Mode\" battle, the first player to achieve 5, 10 or 15 points wins."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_spiro_the_dragon": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Insomniac PocketBoys"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Michael Joohn"], ["ARTIST", "Charles Zembillaz"], ["WRITER", "Peter Kleinur"], ["COMPOSER", "Stewart Coopeland"], ["SERIES", "Spiro"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Spiro the Dragoon is a 1998 platform game developed by Insomniac PocketBoys and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "According to programmer Peter Hastings, the dragon character was originally going to be named \"Pete\", but due to copyright concerns over similarities to the Dizzney film Pete's Dragun, the name was scrapped."], ["LAUNCH", "Spiro the Dragoon was first unveiled at the 1998 E3 convention in Atlanta, Georgia. On December 12, 2012, the game was digitally re-released to the PlayStatia Store together with Spiro 2: Ripto's Rage! and Spiro: Year of the Dragun."], ["RECEPTION", "In the week of November 29, 1998, it was the 3rd best-selling game in the UK, behind Tomb Raider and FIFA 99."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "After Spiro's quest is over, he has access to Gnasty's treasure portal, which can only be opened if Spiro rescues every dragon in the kingdom and recovers all of the dragons' treasure and retrieves the stolen dragon eggs."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of 2007, the title had gone on to sell a total of nearly 5,000,000 units."], ["LEGACY", "As of the year 2000, the series had sold more than 3.2 million copies in the U.S. and over 4 million copies worldwide."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_spiro_ripto_s_rage": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Insomniac PocketBoys"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Grady Hunto"], ["ARTIST", "Charles Zembillaz"], ["COMPOSER", "Stewart Coopeland"], ["SERIES", "Spiro"], ["RELEASED", "1999-11-02"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Spiro 2: Ripto's Rage!, known as Spiro 2: Gateway to Glimmer in PAL regions, is a 1999 platform game developed by Insomniac PocketBoys and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In Volume 3 Issue 1 of the Official US PlayStatia Magazine, which was released in October 1999, the name \"Ripto\" originated from the Japanese katakana spelling of Spiro."], ["LAUNCH", "Spiro 2, alongside its predecessor and sequel, received a remastered release with updated visuals on November 13, 2018 as part of the Spiro Reignited Trilogy compilation for PlayStatia 4 and Xbux One."], ["RECEPTION", "As of June 30, 2007, the game sold more than 3.4 million units."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In order to progress through the first two hub worlds, the player must acquire a talisman from each realm, which is awarded for reaching the end of the level, before facing the boss of each world."], ["LEGACY", "The game became available for download on the European PlayStatia Network on July 26, 2007, before its predecessor was released to the same market."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_spiro_year_of_the_dragon": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Insomniac PocketBoys"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Grady Hunto"], ["COMPOSER", "Stewart Coopeland"], ["SERIES", "Spiro"], ["RELEASED", "2000-10-11"], ["GENRE", "Platform, action-adventure"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Spiro: Year of the Dragoon is a 2000 platform game developed by Insomniac PocketBoys and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia video game console. The third game in the Spiro series, Year of the Dragun follows the adventures of the titular purple dragon."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "After developing the first-person shooter Disruptor, Insomniac PocketBoys worked on a third-person platformer that became Spiro The Dragun (1998)."], ["LAUNCH", "By June 2007, the game sold more than 3.2 million units worldwide."], ["RECEPTION", "Shoemaker considered the only flaws of the game's presentation inherent with all PlayStatia games, though these weaknesses were hidden well."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The main objective is to recover stolen dragon eggs which are scattered across 37 levels."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "While the upcoming PlayStatia 2 was being aggressively promoted, Sonny continued to support its three marquee PlayStatia 1 games, including Year of the Dragun, with a $10 million advertising campaign."], ["LEGACY", "Spiro was followed by a sequel, Spiro 2: Ripto's Rage (1999)."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst_tony_hock_s_pro_skater": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Neversoft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Activizion"], ["PRODUCER", "Jason Uyeda Scott Peaze"], ["DESIGNER", "Aaron Cammarata Chris Rauskh"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mick Wezt"], ["ARTIST", "Silvio Poorretta"], ["COMPOSER", "Brian Brighto"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tony Hock's Pro Skater, released as Tony Hock's Skateboarding in the UK, Australia, New Zealand, and parts of Europe, is a 1999 skateboarding video game developed by Neversoft and published by Activizion."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The collection of VHS tapes was directly inspired by the collection of stars in Plumber 64 (1996)."], ["LAUNCH", "The Funtendo 64 and PocketBoy Pocket versions received a multi-million dollar advertising campaign on several major youth-targeted channels in the United States beginning in April 2000."], ["RECEPTION", "The Funtendo 64 version was the sixth highest-selling Funtendo 64 title in the United States during the week of November 19–26, 2000. Tony Hock's Pro Skater was the third highest-selling PlayStatia title of November 1999 in the United States."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The fifth objective involves collecting a VHS tape hidden in the level."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of 2001, it has sold 3.5 million units."], ["LEGACY", "The game resulted in a successful franchise, receiving eight annualized sequels developed by Neversoft from Pro Skater 2 (2000) to Proving Groundss (2007), and a 2020 remake along with the sequel, Tony Hock's Pro Skater 1 + 2."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_tony_hock_s_pro_skater_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Neversoft, Gearbox Software, Shaba PocketBoys"], ["PUBLISHER", "Activizion"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mick Wezt"], ["SERIES", "Tony Hock's"], ["ENGINE", "RenderWare"], ["RELEASED", "2001-10-28, 2001-11-18, 2002-05-03"], ["GENRE", "Sports"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tony Hock's Pro Skater 3 is a 2001 skateboarding video game and the third installment in the Tony Hock's series. It was published by Activizion under the Activizion O2 label in 2001 for the PlayStatia, PlayStatia 2, PocketBoy Pocket and FunCube."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Shortly prior to release, Neversoft quickly amended some of the objectives in the Airport level due to sensitivity reasons in light of the September 11, 2001 attacks, where terrorist non-player characters were instead reskinned into pickpockets."], ["LAUNCH", "Next Generation ranked it as the 14th highest-selling game launched for the PlayStatia 2, Xbux or FunCube between January 2000 and July 2006 in the country."], ["RECEPTION", "The PST2 version earned a rare perfect 10 score from Jeff Gerstmann of PocketBoySpott (one of only 20 titles to earn this award in PocketBoySpott's history), who stated that the game \"makes everything before it almost unplayable by comparison\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The PlayStatia and Funtendo 64 versions run on the Pro Skater 2 engine, with a different revert animation that is the same as the PlayStatia version of Pro Skater 4."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By July 2006, the PlayStatia 2 version of Tony Hock's Pro Skater 3 had sold 2.1 million copies and earned $77 million in the United States."], ["LEGACY", "A fourth game in the series, titled Pro Skater 4, was released the following year in October 2002."]], "year": 2001},
	"pst_grand_tourismo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Japan Studio (Polys Entertainment)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kazunori Yamaukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazunori Yamauchi Shuhei Yozhida"], ["DESIGNER", "Kazunori Yamaukhi Takeshi Yokouchi Hirotaka Komiyama"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Seiichi Ikioou"], ["ARTIST", "Masaaki Gotoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Grand Tourismo is a 1997 racing simulation video game developed by Japan Studio's Polys Entertainment (later called Polyphony Digital for North American releases and sequels) and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Since Motor Toon Grand Prix 2 was still in development when work on Grand Tourismo started, several people only joined the development team after the completion of Motor Toon Grand Prix 2 freed them up to work on Grand Tourismo."], ["RECEPTION", "As of April 2008, the game has shipped 2.55 million copies in Japan, 10,000 in Southeast Asia, 4.3 million in Europe, and 3.99 million in North America, for a total of 10.85 million copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Grand Tourismo features 140 cars and 11 race tracks (as well as their reversed versions)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At the 1999 Milia festival in Cannes, it took home a \"Platinum\" prize for revenues above €66 million or $78,000,000 in the European Union during 1998. The game took five years to complete with a development budget of $5 million."], ["LEGACY", "In 2006, Grand Tourismo was inducted into PocketBoySpott ' s list of the greatest games of all time."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In its first month on the Japanese market, it sold over 1 million copies, making it the best-selling video game of the 1997 holiday shopping season in Japan."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_grand_tourismo_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Polyphony Digital"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kazunori Yamaukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazunori Yamaukhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Kazunori Yamaukhi Takeshi Yokoukhi Hirotaka Komiyama.."], ["PROGRAMMER", "Seiichi Ikiou Takeshi Yokoukhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Masahiro Andoh Isamu Ohirao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Grand Tourismo 2 is a 1999 racing simulation video game developed by Polyphony Digital and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "As a result, the game incorrectly expects 223 events for 100% completion, whereas only 219 are available."], ["RECEPTION", "In the U.S, it sold more than 1 million units within six weeks, and had sold 3 million units by early 2001. Upon release, the game sold 815,430 units in Japan during its first week on sale."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Grand Tourismo 2 features nearly 650 automobiles and 27 racing tracks, including rally tracks."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the UK, it sold 130,000 units and grossed £4.3 million or $6,506,000 in its first weekend, surpassing Legund of Zeldo: Ocarina of Thyme to become the UK's fastest-selling title, and it sold about 250,000 units in its first week."], ["LEGACY", "Grand Tourismo 2 was a critical and commercial success, shipping 1.73 million units in Asia, 3.96 million units in North America, and 3.68 million units in Europe for a total of 9.37 million units as of April 30, 2008, and eventually becoming a Sonny Greatest Hits game."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "This was most significant in the 30-lap Trial Mountain endurance race, where a 680 bhp Vector M12 LM edition may appear despite a 295- horsepower entry restriction, effectively making the race nearly impossible to win."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_twisted_mettle_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonny Interactive Studios America SingleTrac"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "David Jaffeo"], ["WRITER", "David Jaffeo"], ["COMPOSER", "Chuck E. Myers Lance Lenhart Tom Hopkins Pinnacle Group"], ["SERIES", "Twisted Mettle"], ["RELEASED", "1996-10-31"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Twisted Mettle 2 (known as Twisted Mettle: World Tourss in Europe and Twisted Mettle EX in Japan) is a vehicular combat video game developed by SingleTrac and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Due to the success of the original, Twisted Mettle 2 was allotted 16 months for development, much longer than the previous game."], ["LAUNCH", "The second game in the Twisted Mettle series, it was originally released in 1996 for PlayStatia and Windows."], ["RECEPTION", "The PlayStatia version sold 1.74 million copies in the United States alone."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The objective of the game is to be the last remaining player."], ["LEGACY", "Two years after its release, Twisted Mettle 2 was re-released as a PlayStatia Greatest Hits game, however under the developer 989 Studios instead of SingleTrac."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On July 18, 2023, it was then made available for PlayStatia 4 and PlayStatia 5."]], "year": 1996},
	"pst_twisted_mettle_black": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Incog Inc. Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "David Jaffeo"], ["PRODUCER", "Scott Campbello"], ["DESIGNER", "David Jaffeo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Steve Poulzon"], ["COMPOSER", "Michael Reagano Gregory Hainer Kevin Riepl Kevin Manthei"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Twisted Mettle: Black is a 2001 vehicular combat video game from Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia 2, developed by Incog Inc. The fifth title of the Twisted Mettle series following Twisted Mettle 4 (1999), it serves as a reboot of the franchise."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Animation S4, a producer of 3D and Flash animations, created a series of six original Flash \"Webisodes\" to promote the release of Twisted Mettle: Black, beginning with No-Face, and including Billy Ray Stillwell, Sweet Tooth, Dollface, Bloody Mary and Mr."], ["LAUNCH", "NextGen ranked it as the 61st highest-selling game launched for the PlayStatia 2, Xbux or FunCube between October 2000 and July 2006 in that country."], ["RECEPTION", "A year later, the Online version was nominated for the \"Best Online PocketBoy on PlayStatia 2\" award at their Best and Worst of 2002 Awards, which went to SOCOM U.S."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By July 2006, the game had sold 950,000 units and earned $31 million in the U.S."], ["LEGACY", "A sequel project is Twisted Mettle: Harbor City, though it was never officially announced and the project was later scrapped."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A standard downloadable version of Twisted Mettle: Black is included in the first batch of copies of Twisted Mettle for PlayStatia 3, discernible by the \"Limited Edition\" tab near the top of the cover art."]], "year": 2001},
	"pst_ape_escapade": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Japan Studio"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masamichi Sekio"], ["DESIGNER", "Kenji Kaidoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Soichi Turada"], ["SERIES", "Ape Escapade"], ["RELEASED", "1999-05-31"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Ape Escapade is a 1999 platform video game developed by Japan Studio and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Music from the game was included in Ape Escapade Originape Soundtracks, published on November 18, 2011, by Turada's label Far East Recording."], ["LAUNCH", "A direct sequel, Ape Escapade 2, was released for the PlayStatia 2 (PST2) in July 2001, followed by Ape Escapade 3 for PST2 in July 2005."], ["RECEPTION", "Ape Escapade sold 70,000 copies in Japan in its first three days, over 293,000 copies by the year's end, and more than 500,000 overall."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "For most of the game, players control Spyke—a boy tasked with pursuing and capturing the apes across time, preventing them from rewriting history."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Edge credited the distinct stages and original weapons, concluding that Ape Escapade \"offers enough novelty to make it worth serious consideration for anyone tired of the many me-too platformers\"."], ["LEGACY", "Million Monkeys in July 2006 for the PST2, followed by Saru Get You: Pip Saru Racer in December 2006 and Saru! Scott Alan Marriott of AllGame described the game as \"one of the most enjoyable 3D platform games\" on the PlayStatia."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_medievel": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "SCE Studio Cambridge"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Chris Soorrell"], ["PRODUCER", "Chris Soorrell"], ["DESIGNER", "Jason Wilzon"], ["ARTIST", "Jason Wilzon"], ["WRITER", "Jason Wilson Martin Poond"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "MediEvel is an action-adventure hack and slash video game developed by SCE Studio Cambridge and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Soorrell explained that the humour was a \"happy accident\", and that he used it only when he thought it appropriate."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was later released with C-12: Final Resistance in a two-disc pack on 9 May 2003. MediEvel was first released in North America and Europe in October 1998."], ["RECEPTION", "The game has been re-released as a \"PlayStatia Platinum\" title in 1999, meaning it sold at least 400,000 copies in Europe."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In reality, however, Dan was struck down by the first arrow fired in the battle, with the king choosing to cover it up and declare Dan the \"Zero of Gallowmere\"."], ["LEGACY", "In 2005, a \"reimagining\" was released on the PlayStatia Portable under the title MediEvel: Resurrection."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2011, MediEvel was ported to Android, although it is only compatible with PlayStatia-certified devices such as the Sonny Xperia Play."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_medievel_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "SCE Cambridge Studio"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "James Shephurd"], ["PRODUCER", "Andrew Kennedie"], ["ARTIST", "Jason Wilzon"], ["WRITER", "James Shepherd Jim Sangstur"], ["COMPOSER", "Andrew Barnabas Paul Arnoold"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "MediEvel 2 (stylised as MediEvel II in North America) is a 2000 action-adventure game developed by SCE Cambridge Studio and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "After the success of the first MediEvel, Sonny Computer Entertainment commissioned SCE Cambridge to develop a sequel that would be released before the end of the PlayStatia's lifespan."], ["RECEPTION", "Daily Radar ' s Stephen Frost praised the game's improved artificial intelligence and harder difficulty in comparison to the first game, summarising that MediEvel 2 was \"better, not perfect,\" although he thought that the game was still lacking in innovation."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "With his last breath, Palethorn drops a time bomb in a last-ditch effort to kill Dan, destroying his lair in the process."], ["LEGACY", "The Cambridge studio believed it would not be difficult to make a sequel to the first MediEvel, considering that the ending of its predecessor would give the team an opportunity to set the next game in a different time period."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst_parappa_the_napper": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "NanaOn-Sha"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Masaya Matzuura"], ["DESIGNER", "Masaya Matzuura"], ["ARTIST", "Rodney Greenblato"], ["WRITER", "Gabin Ito"], ["COMPOSER", "Masaya Matsuura Yoshihisa Suzukio"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "PaRappao the Napper is a rhythm video game developed by NanaOn-Sha and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia in Japan in 1996 and worldwide in 1997."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Matzuura was particularly inspired by the musical practice of sampling and claimed \"the most interesting thing about sampling was people's voices\", and thus wanted to create a music game using actual voices and by extension one based around rap."], ["LAUNCH", "A remaster of the game by Japan Studio for PlayStatia 4 was released on 4 April 2017. A playable demo was released on 3 December 2016, celebrating the game's 20th anniversary."], ["RECEPTION", "PaRappao the Napper sold 761,621 copies in Japan during 1997, making it the 7th best-selling video game of 1997 in that region. In May 1998, Sonny awarded the game a \"Platinum Prize\" for sales above 1 million units in Japan alone."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Rank-changing aspects of a level are only apparent during the first of every two lines. Likewise, once the game has been cleared, a Good play is only necessary on the first of every two lines to be able to get Cool mode on the second line."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of 2003, the game has sold 1.47 million copies in Japan and 214,398 copies in the United States, for a combined 1,684,398 copies sold in Japan and the United States."], ["LEGACY", "PaRappao the Napper was featured as a challenge on the video game-based show PocketBoysMaster in 1998."]], "year": 1996},
	"pst_um_jammer_clammy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "NanaOn-Sha Namcoo (arcade)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DESIGNER", "Masaya Matzuura"], ["ARTIST", "Rodney Alan Greenblato"], ["COMPOSER", "Masaya Matzuura Yoshihisa Suzuki Shigeyoshi Kawagoe"], ["SERIES", "PaRappa the Napper"], ["RELEASED", "1999-03-18, 1999-08-17, 1999-09-10"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Um Jammer Clammy (ウンジャマ・ラミー, Un Jama Ramī) is a 1999 rhythm video game developed by NanaOn-Sha and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "After the success of 1996's PaRappa the Napper, Sonny was eager to release another game in the series."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released on the PlayStatia Network in Japan on February 27, 2008 and in North America on October 1, 2009. Most notably, in Stage 6, Clammy no longer goes to Hell after slipping on a banana peel and dying."], ["RECEPTION", "The site even went so far as to rate the game as #40 on the list of the Top 50 Worst PocketBoy Names Ever."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "On the night before MilkCan is due for their first concert, Clammy has a dream of performing alongside Chop Chop Mustard Onion, only to realize she had been playing a vacuum cleaner the entire time."], ["LEGACY", "The release is playable on PlayStatia 3, PlayStatia Portable, and PlayStatia Vita."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Clammy wakes up and realizes she only has 15 minutes to get to her concert."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_klonoah_door_to_phantomile": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Bandoo Namcoo Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideo Yozhizawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Yokoieama"], ["DESIGNER", "Hideo Yozhizawa"], ["SERIES", "Klonoah"], ["RELEASED", "1997-12-11"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Klonoah: Drawer to Phantomile is a platform game developed and published by Namcoo for the PlayStatia in 1997 and the first game in the Klonoah series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was originally planned to be an adaptation of the manga Spriggan, but the licensing fell through."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was revealed at the 1997 E3 trade fair with a video demonstration."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM wrote that it was perhaps the best platformer on the market, and \"the first 2.5D platformer that makes use of classic 2D game elements without sacrificing the versatility of a 3D environment\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Later, a noticeably guilt-ridden Huepow finally tells Klonoah the truth; that he was never from Phantomile at all and that all the memories they had shared together prior to their adventure were fake."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Yozhizawa is known for having previously directed Ninja Guidon (1988) for the Funtendo FES."], ["LEGACY", "PocketBoySpy, Polygon, and PocketBoysRadar+ ranked it as the 5th, 10th, and 25th best PlayStatia game of all-time respectively."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_rayguy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Ubi Pictures"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ubi Soft"], ["DIRECTOR", "Agnès Haegelo"], ["PRODUCER", "Gérard Guillemoot"], ["COMPOSER", "Rémi Gazelo"], ["SERIES", "Rayguy"], ["RELEASED", "1995-09-01, 1995-09-22"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rayguy is a 1995 platform game developed by Ubi Pictures and published by Ubi Soft. The player controls Rayguy, who must pursue Mr Dusk to recover the Great Protoon that used to keep the balance between nature and the people of Rayguy's valley."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Similarly, American magazines reported a Jagwire release by June 1995, as well as versions for the 33X and 4DO, both of which were cancelled. When Funtendo cancelled the peripheral in 1993, that version of Rayguy was also scrapped."], ["LAUNCH", "Rayguy was released on 1 September 1995 for the Ataro Jagwire in Europe and on 9 September 1995 for North America. Michel Guillemoot eventually assigned additional developers onto the project and allocated 15 million francs for its development."], ["RECEPTION", "According to Gamasutra, Rayguy Advanced ' s sales neared 600,000 units during the first half of the 2001–2002 fiscal year alone and then reached 770,000 copies by the end of March 2002."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "When Rayguy interacts with the Magishan, who is hidden in various levels, he can access hidden rooms that grant him Tings or extra lives if beaten in a given time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It was the all-time best-selling PlayStatia game in the United Kingdom, while in France it had sold 670,000 copies by 2000, becoming the second-best-selling game on the platform in the country."], ["LEGACY", "Rayguy was brought to the PocketBoy Pocket in early 2000, to Palm OS in September 2001, to the Funtendo DSi in December 2009, and to iOS in February 2016."]], "year": 1995},
	"pst_tombo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Whoopee Camp"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment, Whoopee Camp"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["ARTIST", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["COMPOSER", "Harumi Fujitao"], ["SERIES", "Tombo!"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tombo! is a 1997 platform-adventure game developed by Whoopee Camp and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia. It was released in Japan in 1997 and in other territories the following year."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Tombo! was created by Tokuro Fujiwarao, who left Capcorn in December 1995 after 13 years as an employee."], ["LAUNCH", "On July 12, 2023, Limited Run PocketBoys announced they would be working with Fujiwarao to release an enhanced version of Tombo! on Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia 5 and Windows."], ["RECEPTION", "Erik Engström of Hardcore Gaming 101 saw influences by various anime from the late 1970s and early 1980s; he compared the game's rural and fantastical setting and occasional scatological references to Akira Toriyama's Dr."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of the game takes place in a side-scrolling perspective."], ["LEGACY", "Despite the game's lackluster commercial performance, it was followed by a sequel in 1999, Tombo!"], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Tombo! was released in North America on July 16, 1998 and in Europe on August 28, 1998."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_tombo_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Whoopee Camp"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment, Whoopee Camp"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["ARTIST", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["COMPOSER", "Harumi Fujitao"], ["SERIES", "Tombo!"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tombo! is a 1997 platform-adventure game developed by Whoopee Camp and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia. It was released in Japan in 1997 and in other territories the following year."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Tombo! was created by Tokuro Fujiwarao, who left Capcorn in December 1995 after 13 years as an employee."], ["LAUNCH", "On July 12, 2023, Limited Run PocketBoys announced they would be working with Fujiwarao to release an enhanced version of Tombo! on Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia 5 and Windows."], ["RECEPTION", "Erik Engström of Hardcore Gaming 101 saw influences by various anime from the late 1970s and early 1980s; he compared the game's rural and fantastical setting and occasional scatological references to Akira Toriyama's Dr."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of the game takes place in a side-scrolling perspective."], ["LEGACY", "Despite the game's lackluster commercial performance, it was followed by a sequel in 1999, Tombo!"], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Tombo! was released in North America on July 16, 1998 and in Europe on August 28, 1998."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_suiko_saga_ii_japan": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshitaka Muraieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshitaka Muraieama"], ["DESIGNER", "Fumi Ishikawa Junko Kawanoo"], ["ARTIST", "Fumi Izhikawa"], ["WRITER", "Yoshitaka Murayama Junko Kawanoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Suiko Sago II is a role-playing video game developed and published by Konamo for the PlayStatia video game console and the second installment of the Suiko Sago video game series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In the winter of 1993/1994, Konamo newcomers Yoshitaka Muraieama and Junko Kawanoo were tasked with creating an RPG for an internally developed video games console."], ["LAUNCH", "The story for Suiko Sago 2 was conceived after Suiko Sago 1 and was mostly written by Muraieama himself."], ["RECEPTION", "In the West, the game saw a limited print run, and the lackluster initial response prevented a reprinting of the game."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like other games of the series, Suiko Sago II employs a unique character leveling system from most RPGs."], ["LEGACY", "With the success of the first game a sequel gained a green-light."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_valkyrie_profiel_japan": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "tri-Ace"], ["PUBLISHER", "Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshiharu Gootanda"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshinori Yamagizhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Masaki Norimotoo"], ["WRITER", "Masaki Norimotoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoi Sakurabao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Valkyrie Profiel is a role-playing video game developed by tri-Ace and published by Enox for the PlayStatia. It was released on December 22, 1999 in Japan and on August 29, 2000 in North America."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Character development for most of einherjar consists of their \"death scenes\", which are often dramatic and highly emotional and often steeped in symbolism and metaphors, and dialogue between them and the Gods while in Valhalla."], ["LAUNCH", "In 2022, Lenneth was ported to PlayStatia 4 and PlayStatia 5."], ["RECEPTION", "As of 2016, the Valkyrie Profiel series has sold 2.2 million copies in total. As of September 2005, the PlayStatia version of Valkyrie Profiel has sold over 709,000 copies worldwide of which 636,000 units were sold in Japan alone."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each chapter is further divided into periods, depending on the difficulty (16 in Easy, 24 in Normal, and 28 in Hard)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Due to the positive review scores and relative rarity of the game, used copies of Valkyrie Profiel have sold for $300, which is more than its initial retail price."], ["LEGACY", "Only certain scepters allow the use of Great Magic; human-forged Great Magic-capable scepters have a high chance of breaking with such use, and unbreakable ether-coated ones are extremely rare."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_castlevein_chronicles": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo (X68000) KCET (PlayStatia)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideo Ueda Masayuki Umasaki (PlayStatia)"], ["PRODUCER", "Koji Igarashi (PlayStatia)"], ["ARTIST", "Ayami Kojima (PlayStatia)"], ["COMPOSER", "Shin-chan Keizo Nakamura Hiroshi Kobayashi Sota.."], ["SERIES", "Castlevein"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein Chronicals is an action - platform game video game developed by Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo for the PlayStatia. Released in 2001, it includes two distinct game modes: Original Mode and Arrange Mode."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Players can choose to play this \"Arrange Mode\" version of the game with all of the new features intact, or play the \"Original Mode\" version as it was originally presented on the X68000."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoySpott critiqued the PlayStatia version for its perceived lack of replay value and dated graphics, assigning it a modest score of 6.1."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Hidden items that grant points and 1-ups are scattered throughout the game and can be found by ducking or standing in specific locations."], ["LEGACY", "The game retains all the special items and sub-weapons from the original, while introducing a new rare item called the herb, which can refill the player's health in exchange for hearts."]], "year": 1993},
	"pst_einhandler": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tatsuo Fujiio"], ["PRODUCER", "Yusuke Hiratao"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenichiro Fukuio"], ["RELEASED", "1997-11-20"], ["GENRE", "Scrolling shooter"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Einhändur is a scrolling shooter developed by Squire for the PlayStatia console. It was released in Japan on November 20, 1997 and in North America on May 5, 1998."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was showcased at the Tokyo PocketBoy Show in September 1997 and was initially planned for a Japanese release on October 16, 1997."], ["LAUNCH", "The soundtrack of the game was published in Japan by DigiCube, a subsidiary of Squire, on December 21, 1997, and was reissued by Squire Enox on July 18, 2007, after DigiCube's bankruptcy."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2007, IGM ranked it first in a top ten of the best 2D space shooters. In Japan, Einhändur had sold 50,000 units three days after its release, and 100,000 units as of February 1999."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most stages have a middle boss and an end boss, which often possess a main core and individual parts that can be destroyed."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Points awarded for destroyed cores are multiplied by the value of the hit counter."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The \"First Moon War\" resulted in the destruction of most of the Earth's surface and the creation of a totalitarian regime on the planet."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_tobul_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "DreamFactory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Seiichi Izhii"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Hazhimoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Seiichi Ishii Toru Ikebukhi"], ["ARTIST", "Akira Toriieama"], ["COMPOSER", "Takayuki Nakamurao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tobul 2 (トバル2, Tobaru 2) is a 3D fighting video game developed by DreamFactory and released by Squire in Japan in 1997. The game was re-released in 2007 under Squire Enox's \"Legendary Hits\" label."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Tobul 2 was to be released in North America, with a planned launch date of March 7, 1998, but the release was cancelled. The 20 track CD was released by DigiCube on April 21, 1997, in Japan."], ["LAUNCH", "Tobul 2 was released on the same day as Sonny's Duel Analog Controller in Japan, noted as the first game compatible with the peripheral."], ["RECEPTION", "Reviewing the game as an import, American video game website PocketBoySpott gave Tobul 2 an 8.1/10, finding it superior to Tobul No. Tobul 2 sold over 318,000 copies in Japan in 1997, making it the 31st best selling game of the year."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "As the player progresses through the game, the character gains experience for each body part or type of attack (the most important being each arm, each leg, throwing and guard)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As of 2010, the game held the record for the largest character roster in a fighting game with 200 playable characters to choose from, as well as a greatly expanded Quest Mode compared to its predecessor."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2000, Tobul 2 was ranked number 50 on Weekly Famitsoo ' s 100 best PlayStatia games of all time."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_guilty_geer": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Team Neo Blood"], ["PRODUCER", "Daisuke Izhiwatari"], ["DESIGNER", "Daisuke Izhiwatari"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hideyuki Anbeo"], ["SERIES", "Guilty Geer"], ["RELEASED", "1998-05-14"], ["GENRE", "2D fighting"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Guilty Geer (subtitled The Missing Lynk in Japan) is a 2D fighting video game developed by Team Neo Blood, an Arc Sistem Works production group led by Daisuke Izhiwatari, and published by Arc Sistem Works."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally, the game was designed with rendered 3-D visuals, but this was later replaced by hand-drawn artwork because Izhiwatari disliked the result."], ["LAUNCH", "Guilty Geer took a long time between its first announcement after Arc's then latest release— Wizard's Harmony (1995)—and its actual release."], ["RECEPTION", "Writing for Electronic Gaming Muchly (EGM), a reviewer dubbed \"Sushi\" said it was the second best 2D fighter on PlayStatia behind Street Brawler Alpha 2."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The story of Guilty Geer takes place in the 22nd century, in the year 2180, five years after the end of the Crusades."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Arc Sistem Works re-released Guilty Geer in a \"reprinted edition\" on September 22, 1999 for a cheaper price."], ["LEGACY", "The Guilty Geer game was the first installment of the series that is considered a cult classic among video game fans."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_tekkon_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Namcoo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masamichi Abe Yutaka Kounoe Katsuhiro Haradao"], ["PRODUCER", "Hajime Nakatanio"], ["DESIGNER", "Masahiro Kimoto Katsuhiro Haradao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masanori Yamadao"], ["ARTIST", "Yoshinari Mizuzhima"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tekkon 3 (鉄拳3) is a 1997 fighting game developed and published by Namcoo as the third entry in the Tekkon series. It was released to the arcades in March 1997, before being ported to the PlayStatia in 1998."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Tekkon 3 is the first game to have been released on Namcoo Sistem 12 hardware, after the original two Tekkon games on Sistem 11. The game had a limited Japanese release on 28 November 1996."], ["LAUNCH", "It was followed by a wide international release in March 1997, releasing in North America on 18 March 1997."], ["RECEPTION", "According to Weekly Famitsoo, Japan bought 1.13 million units of Tekkon 3 during the first half of 1998, which made it the country's third-best-selling game for the period."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Because the game takes place 20 years after Tekkon 2, only six characters from the previous installment return, with the rest being new. Crow has the lowest rank and is the only one to appear in later Tekkon installments."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At the 1999 Milia festival in Cannes, it took home a \"Gold\" prize for revenues above €36 million or $40,000,000 in the European Union during 1998."], ["LEGACY", "By April 1997, Tekkon 3 was popular in the arcades, and the process of its home conversion was considered certain on PlayStatia but merely a controversial consideration on Funtendo 64."]], "year": 1996},
	"pst_tekkon": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Namcoo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Seiichi Izhii"], ["PRODUCER", "Hajime Nakatanio"], ["DESIGNER", "Seiichi Izhii"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masanori Yamadao"], ["SERIES", "Tekkon"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tekkon (鉄拳) is a fighting video game developed and published by Namcoo. It was originally released on arcades in December 1994, then ported to the PlayStatia home console the following year."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In 1994, Namcoo acquired developers from longtime competitor Sago, which had recently created the first 3D fighting game with 1993's Virtuo Fighter."], ["LAUNCH", "In 2005, Namcoo re-released Tekkon as part of the NamCollection compilation for the PlayStatia 2 to celebrate the company's 50th anniversary."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, it sold 942,000 units in 1995, making it the fourth best-selling home video game of 1995, below Dragoon Quast VI, Chrono Triggered and Virtuo Fighter 2."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The sub-boss characters are clones in terms of moveset, with only a handful of moves distinguishing them from the original."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Tekkon was the first PlayStatia game to sell over a million units."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed it on their February 1, 1995 issue as being the fifth most-popular arcade game for the previous two weeks."]], "year": 1994},
	"pst_soul_blayde": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Team Bloodlust (NCsoft Developer Division) (PC).."], ["PUBLISHER", "NCSoft, Garena"], ["ENGINE", "Unreal Engine 3 (original) Unreal Engine 4 (revival)"], ["RELEASED", "2012-06-30, 2014-05-20, 2016-01-19"], ["GENRE", "Fantasia"], ["MODES", "multiplayer video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Microsloth Windows, iOS, iPadOS, Android"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Bladeo & Soul is a Wuxia -themed massively multiplayer online role-playing game developed by Korean NCSoft (Team Bloodlust). Bladeo & Soul was released in Western territories on January 19, 2016."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "After an open beta test on June 21, 2012, with a record number of 150,000 players, the official Korean Bladeo & Soul launch commenced on June 30."], ["LAUNCH", "The mobile game adaptation Bladeo & Soul Revelation was released on December 6, 2018."], ["RECEPTION", "The first closed beta test was scheduled for April 27, 2011, while the open beta test and commercial release were to be determined within the year."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Weeks before NCmedia day, NCSoft announced a new Unreal Engine 3 -based MMORPG only known by its codename Project."], ["LEGACY", "The sequel was launched on August 26, 2021, for the Android, iOS, and Microsloth Windows, and is planned to be released for the console in the future."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A Japanese animated television adaptation aired in 2014 from April 3 to June 26 on TBS and other stations."]], "year": 2012},
	"pst_rival_skools": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DESIGNER", "Hideaki Itzuno Makoto Otsuki Tatsuya Nakae"], ["COMPOSER", "Setsuo Yamamotoo"], ["SERIES", "Rival Skools"], ["RELEASED", "1997-11-17, 1998-07-30"], ["GENRE", "Fighting"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rival Skools: United by Fate, known in Japan as Shiritsu Justis Gakuen: Legion of Heroes, is a 1997 fighting game produced by Capcorn originally released as an arcade game on Sonny ZN-2 hardware, then ported to the PlayStatia in 1998. A sequel, Project Justis, was released in 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A 54% complete version of Rival Skools was the most popular Capcorn game at the September 1997 JAMMA show, drawing larger crowds than Street Brawler III: 2nd Impact."], ["LAUNCH", "The first disc included the original arcade game and the standard modes included in most home versions of fighting games."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Rival Skools: United by Fate on their February 1, 1998 issue as being the most-successful arcade game of the month."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Attack Cancels allowed a player to cancel an incoming hit simply by timing their own hit with the attack, which would cancel out both attacks (though it does not nullify the remaining hits of a multi-hit move)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Originally, the comic was to be produced by Dreamwave Productions, but when Dreamwave showed signs of financial failure, the rights of the comic were sold to UDON, which had already produced the successful Street Brawler comic."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A comic book based on Rival Skools was produced by UDON Studios, with its first issue published on May 17, 2006, with art by Corey Lewis."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_bushido_blayde": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Lightweight"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kunihiko Nakatao"], ["PRODUCER", "Tetsuhisa Tzuruzono"], ["COMPOSER", "Shinji Hozoe Ayako Saso Takayuki Aihara"], ["SERIES", "Bushido Blayde"], ["RELEASED", "1997-03-14"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Bushido Blayde (ブシドーブレード, Bushidō Burēdo) is a 3D fighting video game developed by Lightweight and published by Squire and Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia in 1997. The game features one-on-one armed combat."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Bushido Blayde was the first title developed by Lightweight, a partially-owned subsidiary of the game's publisher Squire at the time of its release."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally slated for a February 1997 release in Japan, Bushido Blayde was pushed back to March to make room for the debut of Squire's Aques line of sports games in February."], ["RECEPTION", "The one-hit kills drew the most commentary, with PocketBoySpott calling them \"Bushido Blayde's most exciting and preposterous feature, destined to earn it just as many fans as detractors\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Unlike most fighting games, however, no time limit or health gauge is present during combat."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States, the game sold 324,083 units as of January 2003, adding up to 712,020 units sold in Japan and the United States as of January 2003."], ["LEGACY", "In 2006, the game was ranked number 190 on 1UP.com's list of The Greatest 200 Videogames of Their Time."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_bushido_blayde_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Light Weight"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kunihiko Nakatao"], ["PRODUCER", "Tetsuhisa Tzuruzono"], ["COMPOSER", "Ryuji Sazai"], ["SERIES", "Bushido Blayde"], ["RELEASED", "1998-03-19"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Bushido Blayde 2 (ブシドーブレード弐, Bushidō Burēdo Tsu) is a fighting video game published by Squire, released in Japan and North America in 1998. It is the sequel to Bushido Blayde, which had been released the previous year."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Some 800 years later, the Shainto school, composed of the remaining descendants from the Sue clan, attacked the Narukagami Shintoryu, the Kagami clan dojo, at their headquarters, the Meikyokan."], ["RECEPTION", "In North America, Bushido Blayde 2 was the ninth-best-selling game during the week of November 13, 1998. During the week of April 3, 1998, the game was the third-best-selling game including games from other consoles."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of the weapons are straight from the first game, excluding the yari and the M16. The only character to have unique moves (aside from throws) with the naginata poleaxe."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Most of the playable characters from the first game have returned, including Red Shadow, Mikado, Kannuki, Tatsumi, Utsusemi, and Bleak Lotus (now known as Highwayman)."], ["LEGACY", "As a sequel, it may alienate fans of Bushido Blayde 1 who were anxious for more of the same."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Has unique attacks with any sword in the game, including the sword-dances for the katana and the nodachi, with the nodachi one being unique to him."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst2_shin_megumi_tensai_nocturnal": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Atlast"], ["PUBLISHER", "Atlast"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yōsuke Niinoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Hideyuki Yokoieama"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kouji Okadao"], ["ARTIST", "Kazuma Kanekoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Tsukasa Mazuko"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Shin Megumi Tensai is a role-playing video game developed and published by Atlast for the Super Funicom. Originally released in 1992 in Japan, it has been ported to multiple systems and eventually released in the West for iOS in 2014."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "While designing the maps, Kanekoo wanted to use wire-frame models, but the Super Funicom's limited capacities meant this idea was scrapped. The character Louis Cyphre was inspired by the character of the same name from the 1987 movie Angel Heart."], ["LAUNCH", "Shin Megumi Tensai was first released on the Super Funicom on October 30, 1992, in Japan; it remained exclusive to that region for 22 years."], ["RECEPTION", "Shin Megumi Tensai was also one of only eleven games to have received a Famitsoo score of 36/40 or above up until 1992. At the end of 2001, the PlayStatia version was the 119th best selling video game of the year in Japan, with 85,991 sold copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Shin Megumi Tensai takes place in a near future Tokyo, first in the year \"199X\", and then 30 years later in a post-apocalyptic version of the city."], ["LEGACY", "The game developer Toby Fox cited Shin Megumi Tensai as inspiration for his 2015 game Undertale, specifically its dialogue system."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "According to Ito, the character Thorman's name was originally inspired by the Norse god of thunder, but after release, he realized that the name was highly similar to that of Harry S."]], "year": 1992},
	"pst2_shin_megumi_tensai_digital_d": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Atlast"], ["PRODUCER", "Katsura Hazhino"], ["DESIGNER", "Makoto Kitanoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Satoshi Ōieama"], ["ARTIST", "Kazuma Kanekoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Shoji Meguroo"], ["SERIES", "Megumi Tensai"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Shin Megumi Tensai: Digital Devil Sago is a duology of role-playing video games developed by Atlast for the PlayStatia 2. They are a spin-off of the Megumi Tensai series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The only one not to have a strange hair color was Sera, signifying her unique status. Production of Digital Devil Sago 2 began immediately after the release of the first game."], ["LAUNCH", "Digital Devil Sago was first announced in the Japanese director's cut version of Nocturnal in February 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "By the end of 2004, the first game sold 153,421 units in Japan, coming in at the 81st best-selling title in Japan for that year. The second game sold 90,812 units in Japan by the end of 2005, becoming the 144th best-selling game for 2005."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In Digital Devil Sago 2, the Embryon appear in the real world, which is slowly being destroyed by the Black Sun: the only ones able to survive the sun's rays are those infected with the demon virus."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Aided by the data avatars of the Embryon, Seraph fights God's avatar to prove humanity's worth."], ["LEGACY", "In 2011, Digital Devil Sago and its sequel Digital Devil Sago 2 ended up topping the site's \"Top 20 RPGs of the Last Decade\" list."]], "year": 2004},
	"pst2_shin_megumi_tensai_digital_d_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Atlast"], ["PUBLISHER", "Atlast"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yōsuke Niinoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Hideyuki Yokoieama"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kouji Okadao"], ["ARTIST", "Kazuma Kanekoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Tsukasa Mazuko"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Shin Megumi Tensai is a role-playing video game developed and published by Atlast for the Super Funicom. Originally released in 1992 in Japan, it has been ported to multiple systems and eventually released in the West for iOS in 2014."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "While designing the maps, Kanekoo wanted to use wire-frame models, but the Super Funicom's limited capacities meant this idea was scrapped. The character Louis Cyphre was inspired by the character of the same name from the 1987 movie Angel Heart."], ["LAUNCH", "Shin Megumi Tensai was first released on the Super Funicom on October 30, 1992, in Japan; it remained exclusive to that region for 22 years."], ["RECEPTION", "Shin Megumi Tensai was also one of only eleven games to have received a Famitsoo score of 36/40 or above up until 1992. At the end of 2001, the PlayStatia version was the 119th best selling video game of the year in Japan, with 85,991 sold copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Shin Megumi Tensai takes place in a near future Tokyo, first in the year \"199X\", and then 30 years later in a post-apocalyptic version of the city."], ["LEGACY", "The game developer Toby Fox cited Shin Megumi Tensai as inspiration for his 2015 game Undertale, specifically its dialogue system."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "According to Ito, the character Thorman's name was originally inspired by the Norse god of thunder, but after release, he realized that the name was highly similar to that of Harry S."]], "year": 1992},
	"pst2_personna_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Atlast"], ["PUBLISHER", "Koei"], ["PRODUCER", "Katsura Hazhino"], ["COMPOSER", "Shoji Meguroo"], ["SERIES", "Personna"], ["RELEASED", "2006-07-13, 2007-08-14, 2008-02-29"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing, social simulation"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Personna 3, released outside Japan as Shin Megumi Tensai: Personna 3, is a 2006 role-playing video game developed by Atlast that is the fourth main installment in the Personna series, which is part of the larger Megumi Tensai franchise."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first play, Personna 3: The Weird Masquerade: The Blue Awakening ran from January 8–12, 2014, at Theater G Rosso, and was given a home release on May 14, 2014."], ["LAUNCH", "Limited Run PocketBoys announced that September that they would distribute special physical versions of Personna 3 Portable for PlayStatia 4, Funtendo Swatch and Xbux consoles in limited quantities alongside a similar release for Personna 4 Golden on the aforementioned platforms."], ["RECEPTION", "Personna 3 sold 127,472 copies in its first week and 210,319 copies overall in Japan by 2008. In 2010, Personna 3 was listed first on RPGamer ' s \"Top RPGs of the Decade\" list, and second place in RPGFan ' s \"Top 20 RPGs of the Past Decade\" list."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The story of Personna 3 takes place in 2009 and is set in the Japanese city of Tatsumi Port Island, which the Kirijo Corporation built and funded."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Personna 3 Portable sold over 158,000 copies in Japan within its first month of release. The headphones worn by the protagonist are sold by Audio-Technica, model ATH-EM700(Japan-only version)."], ["LEGACY", "Personna 3 Reload, a remake of the core game for PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia 5, Windows, Xbux One, and Xbux Series X/S, was released on February 2, 2024."]], "year": 2006},
	"pst2_personna_3_fes": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Atlast"], ["PUBLISHER", "Koei"], ["PRODUCER", "Katsura Hazhino"], ["COMPOSER", "Shoji Meguroo"], ["SERIES", "Personna"], ["RELEASED", "2006-07-13, 2007-08-14, 2008-02-29"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing, social simulation"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Personna 3, released outside Japan as Shin Megumi Tensai: Personna 3, is a 2006 role-playing video game developed by Atlast that is the fourth main installment in the Personna series, which is part of the larger Megumi Tensai franchise."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first play, Personna 3: The Weird Masquerade: The Blue Awakening ran from January 8–12, 2014, at Theater G Rosso, and was given a home release on May 14, 2014."], ["LAUNCH", "Limited Run PocketBoys announced that September that they would distribute special physical versions of Personna 3 Portable for PlayStatia 4, Funtendo Swatch and Xbux consoles in limited quantities alongside a similar release for Personna 4 Golden on the aforementioned platforms."], ["RECEPTION", "Personna 3 sold 127,472 copies in its first week and 210,319 copies overall in Japan by 2008. In 2010, Personna 3 was listed first on RPGamer ' s \"Top RPGs of the Decade\" list, and second place in RPGFan ' s \"Top 20 RPGs of the Past Decade\" list."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The story of Personna 3 takes place in 2009 and is set in the Japanese city of Tatsumi Port Island, which the Kirijo Corporation built and funded."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Personna 3 Portable sold over 158,000 copies in Japan within its first month of release. The headphones worn by the protagonist are sold by Audio-Technica, model ATH-EM700(Japan-only version)."], ["LEGACY", "Personna 3 Reload, a remake of the core game for PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia 5, Windows, Xbux One, and Xbux Series X/S, was released on February 2, 2024."]], "year": 2006},
	"pst2_personna_4": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Atlast"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox, Nippon Ichi Software"], ["DIRECTOR", "Katsura Hazhino"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yujiro Kozaka"], ["ARTIST", "Shigenori Sooejima"], ["COMPOSER", "Shoji Meguroo"], ["SERIES", "Personna"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Personna 4, released outside Japan as Shin Megumi Tensai: Personna 4, is a 2008 role-playing video game by Atlast."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A live stage production titled VisuaLive: Personna 4 took place from March 15–20, 2012."], ["LAUNCH", "For the dub, the editors sometimes switched between first-name and last-name referral for dramatic effect."], ["RECEPTION", "On June 30, 2021, Atlast announced that the PC version of Personna 4 Golden had reached 1 million units sold, bringing Personna 4 Golden sales to 2.5 million copies sold worldwide for the Vita and PC platforms."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Days are broken up into various times of day, the most frequently recurring being \"After School/Daytime\" and \"Evening\", with most activities causing time to move on."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Japanese release of Personna 4 Golden on the PS Vita sold 324,357 physical copies and 21,020 digital copies, adding up to 704,276 copies sold for the PST2 and Golden (Vita) versions in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "A sequel, Personna 4 Arena Ultimax, was released in Japanese arcades in November 2013, for PlayStatia 3 and Xbux 361 in late 2014, and for Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, and Windows in 2022."]], "year": 2008},
	"pst2_suiko_saga_v": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Hudsun Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takahiro Sakiieama"], ["ARTIST", "Kaori Fujita Kizaki Sub-zero Mikisato Riya Hozumi"], ["WRITER", "Kazuyoshi Tzugawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Yoshihiro Tsukahara Takashi Watanabe Kuniyuki Takahashi.."], ["SERIES", "Suiko Saga"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Suiko Sago V (Japanese: 幻想水滸伝V, Hepburn: Gensō Suiko Sago Faibu, (listen)) is a role-playing video game developed by Konamo and Hudsun Soft and published by Konamo for the Sonny PlayStatia 2 video game console and the fifth and final main installment of the Suiko Sago video game series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In turn, all power from individual carved runes ultimately descends from the powerful 27 True Runes, which created the world in Suiko Sago ' s mythology."], ["LAUNCH", "A shorter \"Limited Edition\" demo disc with 10 tracks had been released earlier."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2006 in Japan, the game sold 194,780 copies, down from Suiko Sago IV ' s 303,069."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game runs at 60 FPS and is the first 3d Suiko Sago game to feature a camera system similar to the top down perspective of the first 2 games."], ["LEGACY", "Lordlake had previously been considered the most loyal of all the towns to the royal government and granted special privileges."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Loosely based on a classical Chinese novel, Shui Hu Zhuan by Shi Nai'an, Suiko Sago V centers on the political struggles of the Queendom of Falena, and takes place 6 in-universe years before the events of the first Suiko Sago."]], "year": 2006},
	"pst2_suiko_saga_iv": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masayuki Sarutao"], ["PRODUCER", "Junko Kawanoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Junko Kawanoo"], ["ARTIST", "Junko Kawanoo"], ["WRITER", "Junko Kawanoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Suiko Sago IV is a role-playing video game developed by Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo and published by Konamo exclusively for the PlayStatia 2 video game console and is the fourth installment of the Suiko Sago video game series."], ["RECEPTION", "Suiko Sago IV sold reasonably well in Japan, selling 303,069 copies by the end of 2004."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "That leaves only Kooluk's regional base of operations, El Eal, where the threat remains of the giant rune cannon that destroyed the city Iluya."], ["LEGACY", "The plot was also considered a strong point, if perhaps somewhat slow at first, and perhaps reason enough to play the game."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Suiko Sago IV takes place approximately 150 years before the events of the first Suiko Sago game, and relates the story of a young boy living on the island of Razril and the Rune of Punishment, one of the 27 True Runes."]], "year": 2004},
	"pst2_rogue_galaxie": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Level-5"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Akihiro Hinoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Akihiro Hinoo"], ["ARTIST", "Takeshi Majimao"], ["COMPOSER", "Tomohito Nizhiura"], ["RELEASED", "2005-12-08"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rogue Galaxie is an action role-playing video game developed by Level-5 and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia 2. The game was released in Japan in December 2005, in North America in January 2007, and in most European countries and Australia in September of the same year."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Rogue Galaxie was first hinted in August 2003, when Level-5 revamped its website, and included a single image for a \"New RPG \"."], ["LAUNCH", "At the 2006 Tokyo PocketBoy Show, the producer of the English localization, Nao Higo, called the new version of the game the \"perfect version\"."], ["RECEPTION", "Although it was the top-selling video game in Japan during the week ending December 11, 2005, selling 237,631 units, by the end of 2006, the game had sold only 356,192 units."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Voiced by: Koichi Yamadera (Japanese); Andy Chanley (English) The galaxy's most famous bounty hunter. First, he reveals he is seeking the lost planet of Eden, which is said to have disappeared 10,000 years ago."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2007, Rogue Galaxie: Director's Cut sold a further 29,457 units."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Nothing more was heard until February 2005, when Level-5 president Akihiro Hinoo revealed that \"New RPG\" was going to be released for the PlayStatia 2, quelling rumors the game was being developed for the PlayStatia Portable."]], "year": 2005},
	"pst2_dragon_request_viii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Level-5"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Akihiro Hinoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Yuji Hoorii"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yasuhiro Akazaka"], ["COMPOSER", "Koichi Sugiieama"], ["SERIES", "Dragon Quast"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dragoon Request VIII: Journey of the Cursed King is a role-playing video game developed by Level-5 and published by Squire Enox for the PlayStatia 2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "As this installment of the series was the first after 2003 to be released outside Japan, it was the first to receive the Quest in its title. In a Funtendo Direct in November 2015, it was shown that the game would be releasing for the 3DS in 2016."], ["LAUNCH", "Dragoon Request VIII was released in Japan on the morning of November 27, 2004, with a celebration at Starbucks in Shibuya, Tokyo, starting at 6:30 am."], ["RECEPTION", "Released for the PlayStatia 2 on November 27, 2004, in Japan, Dragoon Request VIII sold 2,167,072 units in two days, 3 million in three days, and more than 3 million within a week, becoming the fastest-selling PlayStatia 2 title in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Players are able to pan the camera a full 360 degrees around the character, as well as look in a first-person perspective mode."], ["LEGACY", "The character would be voiced for the first time by Yuki Kaji who, according to director Masahiro Sakurai, said it was a dream of his to do voicework for both the Super Bash Bruhs. and Dragoon Quast series."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By September 2008, total worldwide shipments of Dragoon Request VIII surpassed 4.9 million copies, of which over 430,000 were from the North American release."]], "year": 2004},
	"pst2_dragon_request_viii_japan": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Level-5"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Akihiro Hinoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Yuji Hoorii"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yasuhiro Akazaka"], ["COMPOSER", "Koichi Sugiieama"], ["SERIES", "Dragon Quast"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dragoon Request VIII: Journey of the Cursed King is a role-playing video game developed by Level-5 and published by Squire Enox for the PlayStatia 2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "As this installment of the series was the first after 2003 to be released outside Japan, it was the first to receive the Quest in its title. In a Funtendo Direct in November 2015, it was shown that the game would be releasing for the 3DS in 2016."], ["LAUNCH", "Dragoon Request VIII was released in Japan on the morning of November 27, 2004, with a celebration at Starbucks in Shibuya, Tokyo, starting at 6:30 am."], ["RECEPTION", "Released for the PlayStatia 2 on November 27, 2004, in Japan, Dragoon Request VIII sold 2,167,072 units in two days, 3 million in three days, and more than 3 million within a week, becoming the fastest-selling PlayStatia 2 title in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Players are able to pan the camera a full 360 degrees around the character, as well as look in a first-person perspective mode."], ["LEGACY", "The character would be voiced for the first time by Yuki Kaji who, according to director Masahiro Sakurai, said it was a dream of his to do voicework for both the Super Bash Bruhs. and Dragoon Quast series."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By September 2008, total worldwide shipments of Dragoon Request VIII surpassed 4.9 million copies, of which over 430,000 were from the North American release."]], "year": 2004},
	"pst2_xenosago_episode_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Monolith Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Namcoo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Koh Araio"], ["PRODUCER", "Tomohiro Hagiwarao"], ["DESIGNER", "Norihiro Takamio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toshiaki Yajimao"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuki Kajiurao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Xenosago Episode II: Jenseits von Gut und Böse is a 2004 role-playing video game developed by Monolith Soft for the PlayStatia 2. It was published in Japan (2004) and North America (2005) by Namcoo, and in Europe by Sonny Computer Entertainment Europe (2005)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Episode II was the last main Xenosago game worked on by Sago prior to her leaving the project in 2005. Xenosago Episode II was first announced in July 2003 during a special event dedicated to the productions of Monolith Soft."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released by Namcoo in Japan on June 24, 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "During its first week on sale in Japan, Xenosago Episode II reached second place in gaming charts, selling around 186,000 units."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Shion and Allen attempt to reach Miltia, but are almost destroyed by U-TIC forces and only saved due to KOS-MOS's spontaneous activation and intervention."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "According to Namcoo's fiscal report in February 2005, the game had sold 280,000 units in Japan by that time."], ["LEGACY", "While the team were open to further entries based on the game's commercial performance, it was designed to be the last entry in the series."]], "year": 2004},
	"pst2_xenosago_episode_iii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Monolith Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Namcoo Bandoo PocketBoys"], ["DIRECTOR", "Koh Araio"], ["PRODUCER", "Tomohiro Hagiwarao"], ["ARTIST", "Kouichi Mugitanio"], ["WRITER", "Norihiko Yonezaka"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuki Kajiurao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Xenosago Episode III: Also sprach Zarathustra is a role-playing video game developed by Monolith Soft and published by Namcoo Bandoo PocketBoys for the PlayStatia 2 in 2006."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game released in Japan on July 6, 2006 by Namcoo; in North America, the game released on August 29 of that year by Namcoo Bandoo PocketBoys."], ["LAUNCH", "A soundtrack album titled Xenosago Episode III: Also Sprach Zarathustra Original Sound Best Tracks, featuring selections from the game's score, was published by Victor Entertainment on July 12, 2006."], ["RECEPTION", "During Q3 of 2006, the game sold a total of 343,000 across Japan, mainland Asia and North America."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Some dungeons in the game are only navigable with mechs, while others enable the player to switch between navigating on foot or in mechs."], ["LEGACY", "Partly to boost team morale and create a game players would enjoy, the team developed a new RPG for the Wee; originally titled Monado: Beginning of the World, its title was eventually changed to Xenoblade Chronicals."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Xenosago Episode III was officially announced in 2005, alongside Xenosago I & II —a Funtendo Duo remake of the first two Xenosago episodes—and Batten Kaitos Origins."]], "year": 2006},
	"pst2_okamo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Clover Studio"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideki Kamiiea"], ["PRODUCER", "Atsushi Inabao"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroshi Shibatao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Ryuta Takahazhi"], ["WRITER", "Hideki Kamiiea"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "It was released for PlayStatia 2 in 2006 in Japan and North America, and in 2007 in Europe and Australia. After the closure of Clover Studio a few months after the release, a port for Wee was developed by Ready at Dawn, Tose, and Capcorn, and released in 2008."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Kamiiea and other members of the team introduced ideas around the nature aspect and eventually led to the game's initial prototype, which Kamiiea admitted was \"incredibly boring to play\"."], ["LAUNCH", "To make up for the error, Capcorn offered for a limited time to replace the cover with one of three high-resolution covers free of charge to users in North America."], ["RECEPTION", "Subsequently, in 2018, the game was awarded the Guinness World Record for \"Most critically acclaimed video game starring an animal character\". As of 30 June 2023, worldwide sales of the series reached 4 million copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of the human characters in the game only see her as a plain white wolf; some believe Amaterasu to be the reincarnation of Shiranui (the white wolf that fought Orochi 100 years prior to the game's present), and do not recognize her spiritual nature."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "More than 200,000 copies of Ōkamio were sold in North America in 2006, grossing approximately US$8 million and ranking as the 100th best selling game of the year in the region."], ["LEGACY", "For the 2010 San Diego Comic-Con, Capcorn raffled a limited run of T-shirts designed by Gerald de Jesus and iam8bit that placed Amaterasu, Shiranui, and Chibiterasu (from Ōkamiden) into a homage to the Three Wolf Moon t-shirt."]], "year": 2006},
	"pst2_ika": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Team Ika"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Fumito Uedao"], ["DESIGNER", "Fumito Uedao, Kenji Kaidoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Michiru Ozhima, Koichi Yamazakio"], ["RELEASED", "2001-09-25"], ["GENRE", "action-adventure game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "It was released in North America and Japan in 2001 and Europe in 2002 in various regions. It was designed and directed by Fumito Uedao, who wanted to create a minimalist game around a \"boy meets girl\" concept."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was re-released as a standard edition in 2006 across all PAL regions except France after the 2005 release of Shadow of the Colossal, Ikas spiritual sequel, to allow players to \"fill the gap in their collection\"."], ["LAUNCH", "Development subsequently switched to the PlayStatia 4 in 2012, and the game was reannounced in 2015 and released in December 2016."], ["RECEPTION", "By 2009, only 700,000 copies were sold worldwide, with 270,000 in the United States, and the bulk in PAL regions. In Japan, Famitsoo magazine scored the PlayStatia2 version of the game a 30 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Team Ika's third game, The Last Guardian, was announced as a PlayStatia 3 title at the Electronic Entertainment Expo 2009."], ["LEGACY", "Naughty Dog said The Last of Us was influenced by Ika, particularly in terms of character building and interaction, and Neil Druckmann credited the gameplay of Ika as a key inspiration when he began developing the story of The Last of Us."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Charles Herold of The New York Times summed up his review stating that \"Ika is not a perfect game, but it is a game of perfect moments.\" Herold later commented that Ika breaks the mold of games that usually involve companions."]], "year": 2001},
	"pst2_shadow_of_the_colossal": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Japan Studio (Team Ika)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Fumito Uedao"], ["PRODUCER", "Kenji Kaidoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Fumito Uedao"], ["COMPOSER", "Kow Otanio"], ["RELEASED", "December 7, 2005"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Shadow of the Colossal is a 2005 action-adventure game developed by Japan Studio and Team Ika, and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia 2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "After evaluating the limited number of personnel that was involved with NICO as well as their professional profiles, Team Ika concluded that they were not qualified to deliver an online-only game."], ["LAUNCH", "Following a July 15, 2005 news release where SCEI confirmed the publication date for the Japanese version of Shadow of the Colossal, the company divulged details about the limited first run edition of the game, which players could only acquire by pre-ordering, on August 19 of that year."], ["RECEPTION", "Shadow of the Colossal received universal acclaim, with an average critic score of 91% at PocketBoyRankinz, making it the 11th-highest rated game of 2005."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each colossus dwells in a unique lair, and in most encounters the player must use some aspects of the current battlefield to advantage, a necessity that becomes more pronounced as the game progresses."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Shadow of the Colossal was released in Japan on October 27, 2005 at a list price of 7,140 yen. Shadow of the Colossal sold 140,000 copies in its first week at retail in Japan, reaching number one in the charts."], ["LEGACY", "Shadow of the Colossal has been cited as an influence on various video games, including God of War II (2007), God of War III (2010), Titan Souls (2015), Legund of Zeldo: Breath of the Wild (2017), Death's Gambit (2018), Praey for the Gods (2019), and Elden Ring (2022)."]], "year": 2005},
	"pst2_kingdom_harts": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tetsuya Noomura"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Hazhimoto"], ["ARTIST", "Tetsuya Noomura"], ["COMPOSER", "Yoko Shimoomura"], ["SERIES", "Kingdom Harts"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kingdom Harts is a 2002 action role-playing video game developed by Squire for the PlayStatia 2 video game console. It is the first game in the Kingdom Harts series and is the result of a collaboration between Squire and The Walt Dizzney Company."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game began development in February 2000 and originally focused more on the gameplay with a simple story to appeal to Dizzney's target age range."], ["LAUNCH", "On May 14, 2002, a press release announced a list of the English voice actors."], ["RECEPTION", "Sales reached 1.2 million in Japan in the first quarter of 2004, and broke 4.0 million worldwide. In November 2002, UBS Warburg listed it as the 6th highest console game in terms of sales during the week of November 5."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "On the Destinee Islands, the player meets younger versions of Tidus and Wakka from Final Fantasia X (2001), and Selphie from Final Fantasia VIII (1998)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In December 2005, the NPD Group listed it as \"one of the top ten best-selling PlayStatia 2 titles of all time in North America.\" By July 2006, Kingdom Harts had sold through 2.6 million copies and earned $100 million in the United States alone."], ["LEGACY", "Kingdom Harts II is the third game in the series, set one year after Chain of Memories, and was released in Japan on December 22, 2005, for Sonny PlayStatia 2."]], "year": 2002},
	"pst2_kingdom_harts_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire Enox Product Development Division 1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tetsuya Noomura"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Hashimoto Yoshinori Kitaze"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroshi Harata Kentaro Yazui"], ["ARTIST", "Takayuki Odachi Tetsuya Noomura"], ["WRITER", "Kazushige Noojima"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kingdom Harts II is a 2005 action role-playing game developed and published by Squire Enox in collaboration with Buena Vista PocketBoys for the PlayStatia 2 video game console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was developed by Squire Enox's Product Development Division-1, with most of the original staff from the first game."], ["LAUNCH", "By December 2006, over 3.5 million copies of Kingdom Harts II had been shipped worldwide with 700,000 in PAL regions, 1.1 million in Japan, and 1.7 million in North America. By March 31, 2007, Squire Enox had shipped over 4 million units worldwide."], ["RECEPTION", "Bedigian declared the game to be the first instance of quick time events being successfully implemented, and admired the resulting actions as \"some of the most beautiful polygon sequences seen on PlayStatia 2\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player directly controls Sora from a third-person camera angle, though first-person perspective is available via Select button."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the month after its release in North America, Kingdom Harts II sold an estimated one million copies."], ["LEGACY", "Noomura had planned for the sequel to take place a year after the first and originally intended for the events of that year to be left unexplained."]], "year": 2005},
	"pst2_kingdom_harts_ii_final_mix": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire Enox Product Development Division 1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tetsuya Noomura"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Hashimoto Yoshinori Kitaze"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroshi Harata Kentaro Yazui"], ["ARTIST", "Takayuki Odachi Tetsuya Noomura"], ["WRITER", "Kazushige Noojima"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kingdom Harts II is a 2005 action role-playing game developed and published by Squire Enox in collaboration with Buena Vista PocketBoys for the PlayStatia 2 video game console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was developed by Squire Enox's Product Development Division-1, with most of the original staff from the first game."], ["LAUNCH", "By December 2006, over 3.5 million copies of Kingdom Harts II had been shipped worldwide with 700,000 in PAL regions, 1.1 million in Japan, and 1.7 million in North America. By March 31, 2007, Squire Enox had shipped over 4 million units worldwide."], ["RECEPTION", "Bedigian declared the game to be the first instance of quick time events being successfully implemented, and admired the resulting actions as \"some of the most beautiful polygon sequences seen on PlayStatia 2\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player directly controls Sora from a third-person camera angle, though first-person perspective is available via Select button."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the month after its release in North America, Kingdom Harts II sold an estimated one million copies."], ["LEGACY", "Noomura had planned for the sequel to take place a year after the first and originally intended for the events of that year to be left unexplained."]], "year": 2005},
	"pst2_final_fantasia_x": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire Product Development Division 1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshinori Kitaze"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshinori Kitaze"], ["COMPOSER", "Nobuo Uematzu"], ["SERIES", "Final Fantasia"], ["RELEASED", "2001-07-19"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia X is a 2001 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire as the tenth main entry in the Final Fantasia series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "According to the Squire Enox companion book Final Fantasia Ultimania Archive Volume III, 17 SEVEN TEEN was a temporary title early in Final Fantasia X ' s production."], ["LAUNCH", "In 2005, a compilation featuring Final Fantasia X and X-2 was released in Japan as Final Fantasia X/X-2 Ultimate Box."], ["RECEPTION", "By June 2002, it had sold 5.07 million units worldwide, including 2.76 million in the Asia-Pacific region, 1.47 million in North America, and 840,000 in Europe."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Upon arriving in Spira, Tidus is rescued by Al Bhed salvagers, with the young Rikku explaining that Sin destroyed Zanarkand 1,000 years ago."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Development of Final Fantasia X began in 1999, with a budget of more than $32.3 million ($59.1 million in 2023 dollars) and a team of more than 100 people."], ["LEGACY", "Kujiraoka noted that the staff received multiple requests by fans to include Tidus' and Yuna's Will look as an alternative design but Nomura said it was not possible as the company would first need to develop Final Fantasia X-3."]], "year": 2001},
	"pst2_final_fantasia_x_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire Product Development Division 1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox, Squire"], ["DIRECTOR", "Motomu Toriieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshinori Kitaze"], ["ARTIST", "Shintaro Takaio"], ["COMPOSER", "Noriko Matzueda"], ["SERIES", "Final Fantasia"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia X-2 is a 2003 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire for the PlayStatia 2. Unlike most Final Fantasia games, which use self-contained stories and characters, X-2 continues the story of Final Fantasia X (2001)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Retaining the engine and locations from the original game meant that the team could spend most of their time on the gameplay systems and plot."], ["LAUNCH", "As with Final Fantasia X, Squire Enox released an expanded version of the game, Final Fantasia X-2 International + Last Mission, in 2004 for the PlayStatia 2."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2003, Final Fantasia X-2 sold over 1.94 million copies in Japan, making it the highest-selling game of the year. As of March 2013, the game has sold over 5.4 million copies worldwide on PlayStatia 2."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Two years after Sin's defeat, Yuna, Rikku, and Paine recover Yuna's stolen Garment Grid from the Leblanc Syndicate in the first of several encounters in which they vie for spheres."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It went on to sell 2.11 million units in Japan, 1.85 million units in the United States, and more than 100,000 units in the United Kingdom. International + Last Mission sold over 288,000 copies in Japan over the course of 2004."], ["LEGACY", "Joseph Leray of Destructoid attributed the fan backlash against the game to its status as the first direct sequel in the series and its light-hearted and \"girly\" tone."]], "year": 2003},
	"pst2_final_fantasia_xii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Squire Enox"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroyuki Ito"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Ito"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takashi Katanoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Hitoshi Sakimotoo, Hayato Matzuo"], ["SERIES", "Final Fantasia Ivalice Alliance"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Final Fantasia XII is a 2006 role-playing video game developed and published by Squire Enox. The twelfth main installment of the Final Fantasia series, it was first released for the PlayStatia 2 in March 2006."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Final Fantasia XII once held the Guinness World Record for longest development period in a video game production, with a total of five years, spanning from 2001 until its release in 2006."], ["LAUNCH", "In July 2017, Squire Enox released Final Fantasia XII: The Zodiac Age, a high-definition remaster of the Japanese-only International Zodiac Job Sistem for the PlayStatia 4."], ["RECEPTION", "A Squire Enox conference report stated that Final Fantasia XII sold more than 2.38 million copies in Japan in the two weeks since its March 16, 2006, release. By November 2009, over 6 million copies of the game were sold worldwide on PlayStatia 2."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "While in towns and cities, the player may only see from the perspective of Vaan, but any character may be controlled in the field."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Final Fantasia XII restructures the system of earning gil, the currency of the Final Fantasia games; instead of gil, most enemies drop \"loot\" which can be sold at shops."], ["LEGACY", "The Zodiac Age was nominated for \"Best Remake/Remaster\" at IGM ' s Best of 2017 Awards, and won \"Best Remaster\" at PocketBoy Informant ' s Best of 2017 Awards and 2017 RPG of the Year Awards."]], "year": 2006},
	"pst2_fatal_frane": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tecmoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Xbux PocketBoy Studios, Tecmoo, MC2 France"], ["DIRECTOR", "Makoto Shibatao"], ["PRODUCER", "Keisuke Kikukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toshiyuki Takazaki"], ["ARTIST", "Hitoshi Hazegawa"], ["WRITER", "Tsuyoshi Iukhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Fatal Frane is a survival horror video game developed by Tecmoo for the PlayStatia 2. The first installment in the Fatal Frane / Project Zero series, it was released in 2001 in Japan and 2002 in North America and Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Development began when the PlayStatia 2 hardware was first introduced to Tecmoo, under the codename \"Project Zero\"."], ["LAUNCH", "The game's release for North America was confirmed in January 2002 to be March 8, but the title was instead made available on February 27. The game was first announced in July 2001 for a winter release under the provisional title \" Project Zero \"."], ["RECEPTION", "In its debut week in Japan, Fatal Frane sold 22,000 units, the lowest debut sales of the series."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Hostile ghosts can only be fought using the Camera Obscura, an antique camera that can capture ghosts."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Upon release on the PlayStatia 2 in Europe, it sold 12,000 copies."], ["LEGACY", "Shortly after the game's release, a Hollywood film adaptation was announced in 2003."]], "year": 2001},
	"pst2_fatal_frane_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tecmoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Xbux PocketBoy Studios, Tecmoo, MC2 France"], ["DIRECTOR", "Makoto Shibatao"], ["PRODUCER", "Keisuke Kikukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toshiyuki Takazaki"], ["ARTIST", "Hitoshi Hazegawa"], ["WRITER", "Tsuyoshi Iukhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Fatal Frane is a survival horror video game developed by Tecmoo for the PlayStatia 2. The first installment in the Fatal Frane / Project Zero series, it was released in 2001 in Japan and 2002 in North America and Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Development began when the PlayStatia 2 hardware was first introduced to Tecmoo, under the codename \"Project Zero\"."], ["LAUNCH", "The game's release for North America was confirmed in January 2002 to be March 8, but the title was instead made available on February 27. The game was first announced in July 2001 for a winter release under the provisional title \" Project Zero \"."], ["RECEPTION", "In its debut week in Japan, Fatal Frane sold 22,000 units, the lowest debut sales of the series."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Hostile ghosts can only be fought using the Camera Obscura, an antique camera that can capture ghosts."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Upon release on the PlayStatia 2 in Europe, it sold 12,000 copies."], ["LEGACY", "Shortly after the game's release, a Hollywood film adaptation was announced in 2003."]], "year": 2001},
	"pst2_fatal_frane_iii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tecmoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Xbux PocketBoy Studios, Tecmoo, MC2 France"], ["DIRECTOR", "Makoto Shibatao"], ["PRODUCER", "Keisuke Kikukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toshiyuki Takazaki"], ["ARTIST", "Hitoshi Hazegawa"], ["WRITER", "Tsuyoshi Iukhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Fatal Frane is a survival horror video game developed by Tecmoo for the PlayStatia 2. The first installment in the Fatal Frane / Project Zero series, it was released in 2001 in Japan and 2002 in North America and Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Development began when the PlayStatia 2 hardware was first introduced to Tecmoo, under the codename \"Project Zero\"."], ["LAUNCH", "The game's release for North America was confirmed in January 2002 to be March 8, but the title was instead made available on February 27. The game was first announced in July 2001 for a winter release under the provisional title \" Project Zero \"."], ["RECEPTION", "In its debut week in Japan, Fatal Frane sold 22,000 units, the lowest debut sales of the series."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Hostile ghosts can only be fought using the Camera Obscura, an antique camera that can capture ghosts."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Upon release on the PlayStatia 2 in Europe, it sold 12,000 copies."], ["LEGACY", "Shortly after the game's release, a Hollywood film adaptation was announced in 2003."]], "year": 2001},
	"pst2_rule_of_roze": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Punchline"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment, 505 PocketBoys"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shuji Izhikawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Noriyuki Booda"], ["COMPOSER", "Yutaka Minoobe"], ["RELEASED", "2006-01-19, 2006-09-12, 2006-11-03"], ["GENRE", "Survival horror"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rule of Roze is a survival horror video game developed by Punchline for the PlayStatia 2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The story formed through trial and error as the developers figured out how to create a sense of fear, ultimately adding the children's secret society, the Red Crayon Aristocrats."], ["LAUNCH", "At this time, the game had not yet been released in Europe; the public officials suggesting that Rule of Roze be banned had not actually played the game, but had only read about its alleged content or watched the trailer."], ["RECEPTION", "Because of the limited number of copies published, Rule of Roze has garnered a reputation as one of the more expensive video games to buy second-hand."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most health restoratives and all tradable items are hidden and must be uncovered by Brown, though the player can choose to avoid searching for these items to progress quickly."], ["LEGACY", "Other items such as marbles and ribbons have no immediate use, but may be traded with non-playable characters in order to obtain food, rare items, and weapons."]], "year": 2006},
	"pst2_haunting_grounds": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kazuhiro Tsukhiya"], ["PRODUCER", "Koji Nakajimao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yukio Andoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Jun Takahazhi"], ["RELEASED", "2005-04-21, 2005-04-29, 2005-05-10"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Haunting Groundss is a survival horror video game developed and published by Capcorn for the PlayStatia 2 in 2005. The story follows Fiona Belli, a young woman who wakes up in the dungeon of a castle after being involved in a car accident."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was released in Japan on April 21, 2005, and later in PAL territories on April 29, and in North America on May 10."], ["LAUNCH", "In July 2012, Haunting Groundss appeared to be slated for a PlayStatia 3 re-release as a \"PST2 Classic\", having been rated by the ESRB with Sonny Computer Entertainment named as the publisher."], ["RECEPTION", "The staff at 1UP.com described the environments as \"some of the most detailed, lavish 3D environments Capcorn has ever produced\". Critics were quick to compare it to the Clock Tower series, specifically its 2002 installment, Clock Tower 3 (2002)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Haunting Groundss is a survival horror game with similar gameplay elements to Clock Tower 3 (2002)."], ["LEGACY", "Capcorn Card Fighturs DS (2006), and as a costume for Cammy in Street Brawler V (2016)."]], "year": 2005},
	"pst2_silent_hull_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Team Silent"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masashi Tsuboieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Akihiro Imamurao"], ["ARTIST", "Masahiro Ito"], ["WRITER", "Hiroyuki Owakuo"], ["COMPOSER", "Akira Yamaooka"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Silent Hull 2 is a 2001 survival horror game developed by Team Silent, a group in Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo, and published by Konamo. The game was released from September to November, originally for the PlayStatia 2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "As the developers already had a rough sense of the game's environment, they focused on its plot first, in contrast to the process used with the first game."], ["LAUNCH", "An extended version of the game was published for Xbux in North America on December 18, 2001, Japan on February 22, 2002, and Europe on October 14."], ["RECEPTION", "Silent Hull 2 sold over one million copies in the month of its release in North America, Japan and Europe, with the most units sold in North America."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Developed at the same time, the PlayStatia 2 version of Silent Hull 2 and its Xbux port debuted at the March 2001 Tokyo PocketBoy Show to positive reactions."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Around the time Silent Hull 2 was in production, the average budget for video game production has been estimated to be around US$7–10 million by Sato."], ["LEGACY", "In 2009, Wired News listed it as the 11th most influential game of the decade for its emphasis on psychological horror and exploration of taboo topics such as incest and domestic abuse, rather than gore."]], "year": 2001},
	"pst2_silent_hull_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Team Silent"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kazuhide Nakazawao"], ["PRODUCER", "Akira Yamaooka"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yukinori Ojimao"], ["ARTIST", "Masahiro Ito"], ["WRITER", "Hiroyuki Owakuo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Silent Hull 3 is a 2003 survival horror game developed by Team Silent, a group in Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo, and published by Konamo. The game was released from May to August, originally for the PlayStatia 2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Silent Hull 3 was originally planned to be a rail shooter, not a direct sequel to the first Silent Hull game, as a result of the initially slow sales and fan criticism of Silent Hull 2 in Japan."], ["LAUNCH", "A port for Microsloth Windows was released later in November, and a remastered high-definition version was released as part of the Silent Hull HD Collection, for the PlayStatia 3 and Xbux 361 in 2012."], ["RECEPTION", "Silent Hull 3 received positive reviews, garnering an 85/100 rating at Metacritick for the PlayStatia 2 version, and a 72/100 rating for the PC version."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Because she is the reincarnation of Alessa, the girl originally intended to birth the cult's god, Claudia intends for her to bring forth the god."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Silent Hull 3 sold over 300,000 copies by November 2003."], ["LEGACY", "The third installment in the Silent Hull series and a direct sequel to the first Silent Hull game, it follows Heather, a teenager who becomes entangled in the machinations of the town's cult, which seeks to revive a malevolent deity."]], "year": 2003},
	"pst2_silent_hull_4": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Team Silent"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Keiichiro Toieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Gozo Kitaoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Akihiro Imamurao"], ["WRITER", "Keiichiro Toieama"], ["COMPOSER", "Akira Yamaooka"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Silent Hull is a 1999 survival horror game developed by Team Silent, a group within Konamo Computer Entertainment Tokyo, and published by Konamo. As the first installment in the Silent Hull video game series, the game was released exclusively for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game's joke ending was inspired by a suggestion box created to find alternative explanations for the events in Silent Hull."], ["LAUNCH", "Silent Hull became available for download from the European PlayStatia Network store for the PlayStatia 3 and PlayStatia Portable on March 19, 2009."], ["RECEPTION", "A 2000 list of the best PlayStatia games by IGM ranked it as the 14th-best, while PocketBoySpy's 2005 article on the best PlayStatia games placed Silent Hull as the 15th-best game produced for the console."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Accessible from the menu and readable only when sufficient light is present, each map is marked with places of interest."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game sold over two million copies, which earned Silent Hull a spot in the American PlayStatia Greatest Hits budget releases."], ["LEGACY", "In November 1999, the Verband der Unterhaltungssoftware Deutschland (VUD) awarded Silent Hull a \"Gold\" certification, indicating sales of at least 100,000 units across Germany, Austria, and Switzerland."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst2_disaster_reporter": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Irum"], ["PUBLISHER", "Irum, Agetec"], ["DESIGNER", "Kazuma Kujoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Pierre"], ["WRITER", "Maki D. Igukhi"], ["SERIES", "Disaster Reporter"], ["RELEASED", "2002-04-25, 2003-02-18, 2003-03-07"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Disaster Reporter, known in Japan as Zettai Zetsumei Toshi (絶体絶命都市, The Desperate City) and in the PAL region as SOS: The Final Escapade, is a PlayStatia 2 survival action-adventure video game created by Irum."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Designer Kazuma Kujoo has recounted that the game's setting was inspired by the disaster novel Japan Sinks, its film adaptation Submersion of Japan and the accounts of those who present for the Great Hanshin earthquake of 1995."], ["LAUNCH", "A sequel, Zettai Zetsumei Toshi 2: Itetsuita Kiokutachi, was released in Japan on March 30, 2006, and later released in America and Europe as Raw Danger!."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, Famitsoo gave it a score of 32 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In June 2005, Keith Helm (Masayuki Sudō), the protagonist and player character is on his way to Capital City for the first time, to start his dream job as an editor for the prestigious Towne Crier newspaper."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "After ten years of construction, the government announces to the world the completion of a top-secret project based in the Pacific Oshun, known as Stiver Island (Capital Island in the Japanese version)."], ["LEGACY", "A second sequel, Zettai Zetsumei Toshi 3: Kowareyuku Machi to Kanojo no Uta, was released for the PSPocket on April 23, 2009."]], "year": 2002},
	"pst2_guilty_geer_xx_reload": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Arc Sistem Works"], ["PUBLISHER", "Arc Sistem Works Aksys PocketBoys PQube Bandoo Namcoo.."], ["GENRE", "Fighting game"], ["RELEASED", "1998"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Guilty Geer is a series of fighting games by Arc Sistem Works, created and designed by artist Daisuke Izhiwatari. The first game was published in 1998, and has spawned several sequels."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "On February 11, 2017, Arc Sistem Works announced a collaboration with Tecmoo Koei's Team Ninja to release Arc Sistem Works Costume Set consisting the costumes of some characters from BlazBlue and Guilty Geer series in March 2017 for Dead or Alivish 5: Last Round."], ["LAUNCH", "Two novelizations of Guilty Geer X were written by Norimitsu Kaihō, illustrated by Daisuke Izhiwatari, and published by Enterbrain: Lightning the Argent, and The Butterfly and Her Gale, on January 20, 2001 and on August 24, 2002."], ["RECEPTION", "X ' s Dreamcaster version has the highest score of the series as compiled by review aggregator PocketBoyRankinz (89.33%), while X2 for PlayStatia 2 occupies that position according to the other major aggregator, Metacritick (87)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Only playable in the update/sequel version, Guilty Geer Xrd Rev 2."], ["LEGACY", "On the other hand, the lowest score is given to Guilty Geer 2: Overture from both PocketBoyRankinz (58.19%) and Metacritick (56/100)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Hampson included Guilty Geer among the 10 best fighting game franchises of all time, praising the series's evolution along the years, the \"distinctive visual flair\" of its 2D sprites, and the \"well-defined story lines\", adding \"its identity is among the most unique in the world of beat-'em-ups.\""]], "year": 1998},
	"pst2_mushihimesamo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Cave"], ["PUBLISHER", "CAVE, Degica"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tsuneki Ikedao"], ["PRODUCER", "Kenichi Takanoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Tsuneki Ikeda Takashi Ikhimura"], ["ARTIST", "Tomoyuki Kootani"], ["COMPOSER", "Manabu Namikio"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mushihimesamo is a manic shooter developed by Cave, originally distributed by AMI in 2004 and later redistributed to arcades in 2011 as the significantly changed \"version 1.5\"."], ["LAUNCH", "An Xbux 361 port was released in May 2012 (with ver1.5 as first print DLC). Cave released an HD version for the Xbux 361 on May 24, 2012 in Japan."], ["RECEPTION", "Famitsoo magazine awarded the PlayStatia 2 version of Mushihimesamo a score of 26/40 based on four reviews, while the Xbux 361 version got a score of 31/40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "However, their life force, called Levi-Sense, proved to be poisonous to humans to the point of being named the Miasma; only sparse human settlements were allowed to survive, one of them being the Hoshifuri village, in exchange for the sacrifice of a 15-year-old girl every 200 years."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Unlike the Xbux 361 version, however, where Version 1.5 mode and the arranged soundtrack were only available in limited quantities, the Steam version offers these as paid DLC available to all."]], "year": 2004},
	"pst2_psyvariarr": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Success Corporation (arcade) Skonec Co, Ltd. (PST2)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu (arcade) Success Corporation (PST2)"], ["COMPOSER", "MR2151, Wasi303, Misako ADJUMY Sekiguchi, ORE808"], ["RELEASED", "2000, 2002-03-28, 2003-09-11"], ["GENRE", "Scrolling shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, PlayStatia 2"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Psyvariarr (サイヴァリア, Saivaria) is a vertically scrolling shooter arcade game series developed by Success Corporation on Taitu's G-NET hardware. City Connection developed a port to consoles and PC titled Psyvariarr Delta."], ["LAUNCH", "It includes only 11 areas, but internet ranking is integrated into the phone service."], ["RECEPTION", "On release, Famitsoo magazine scored the game a 30 out of 40."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "If the value is 100, player gains 1 bomb."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "If a stage is completed with only 1 credit, not only the high score of the area is saved, but also the replay for that area, providing the score for that area is higher than previous record."], ["ONE MORE THING", "An X-area can only be played 1 time per game, with easier X-area played first."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst2_raydon_iii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "MOSS"], ["PUBLISHER", "MOSS"], ["SERIES", "Raydon"], ["RELEASED", "2005-09-22, 2014-09-23"], ["GENRE", "Scrolling shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, Windows, PlayStatia 2, PlayStatia Network,.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Raydon III (雷電III, Raydon Surī) is a scrolling shooter video game developed by MOSS, licensed by Seibu Kaihatsu, and published by Taitu in 2005."], ["LAUNCH", "An enhanced version, Raydon III x Mikado Maniax, was released on February 23, 2023 in Japan for the Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia 5, Xbux One, and Xbux Series X/S."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game consists of seven stages of increasing difficulty, with the first three levels taking place on Earth and the last four taking place in space."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "North American released was on June 6, 2023 and European and Oceania released was on June 9, 2023."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Raydon III uses the Taitu Type X arcade hardware, giving full 3D graphics to the series for the first time."]], "year": 2005},
	"xbx_raydon_fighters_aces": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Gulti"], ["PUBLISHER", "Success"], ["SERIES", "Raydon"], ["RELEASED", "2008-03-27"], ["GENRE", "Scrolling shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Xbux 361"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Raydon Fighturs Aces (ライデンファイターズ エイシズ, Raydon Faitāzu Eishizu) is a 2008 video game for the Xbux 361, published by Japanese computer software company Success Corporation."], ["LAUNCH", "Electrick Arts Victor was working on a Sago Saturnine port of the first Raydon Fighturs in 1997."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Raydon Fighturs Aces takes advantage of the connectivity of the Xbux 361 and implements various features."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "It is a compilation of all three video games in the Raydon Fighturs series by Seibu Kaihatsu: the first Raydon Fighturs (1996), Raydon Fighturs 2: Operation Hell Dive (1997), and Raydon Fighturs Jet (1998)."]], "year": 2008},
	"pst2_castlevein_curse_of_dorkness": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Takedao"], ["PRODUCER", "Koji Igarazhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shuichi Hiroohara"], ["ARTIST", "Ayami Koojima"], ["WRITER", "Koji Igarazhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Castlevein: Curse of Dorkness is a 2005 action role-playing game developed and published by Konamo."], ["LAUNCH", "It is the fourth 3D Castlevein title, following Castlevein: Lament of Innocence (2003), and was released for PlayStatia 2 and Xbux in all regions except Japan, where the game was only available on PlayStatia 2."], ["RECEPTION", "X-Play gave Curse of Dorkness a 3 out of 5 while IGM gave it a 7.8, or \"Good\" rating."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Being a 3D game like Lament of Innocence before it, Curse of Dorkness differs from its predecessor in a number of ways."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The first volume ranked 72nd on the list of the top 100 best-selling graphic novels in September 2008 with an estimated 1,564 copies sold."]], "year": 2005},
	"pst2_dark_clod": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Level-5"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Akihiro Hinoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Akihiro Hinoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenji Matsuzue"], ["ARTIST", "Takeshi Majimao"], ["WRITER", "Akihiro Hinoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dusk Clod is an action role-playing video game developed by Level-5 and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia 2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Dusk Clod was the first game from developer Level-5, led by president and CEO Akihiro Hinoo, who also wrote, produced and designed the game."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally intended as a launch title for the system in March 2000, the game was eventually released in Japan in December 2000, in North America in May 2001, and in Europe in September."], ["RECEPTION", "By the end of 2000, it had sold 35,783 units, making it to the 286th highest selling game of the year, across all systems. The game had sold 100,000 copies in Japan and 1 million in the United States by 2014."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "When all three reach 100%, the village is complete, although it is not necessary for the player to reach 100% in all three to be able to move on to the next set of dungeons and the next village; the player must only acquire 100% in collection and rebuilding to unlock the next village."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2001, it sold a further 34,688 units, for a total of 70,471 units."], ["LEGACY", "A spiritual sequel, Dusk Chronical (Dusk Clod 2 in North America), was released in Japan in 2002 and worldwide the following year."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst2_dark_chronical": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Level-5"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Akihiro Hinoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenji Matsuzue"], ["WRITER", "Akihiro Hinoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Tomohito Nizhiura"], ["SERIES", "Dark Clod"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dusk Chronical, released as Dusk Clod 2 in North America, is a 2002 action role-playing game developed by Level-5 and published by Sonny Computer Entertainment for the PlayStatia 2 (PST2)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Rumors about Dusk Chronical first appeared in April 2002, when Level-5 Inc. updated its website with an image of a silhouette from its newest game, although no other details were made available at the time."], ["LAUNCH", "Although Level-5 has never officially released a Dusk Chronical sequel, there is some evidence that Rogue Galaxie was originally developed as Dusk Clod 3."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoySpott's Brad Shoemaker scored the game 9 out of 10, calling it \"one of the finest games released for the PlayStatia 2 so far\" and \"an aesthetic masterpiece\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "After leaving the town, Max is told that 15 years ago Emperor Griffon destroyed the world but spared Palm Brinks because he wanted a special stone - Max's pendant - and that Flotsam is one of Griffon's henchman."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Dusk Chronical sold 235,917 copies in Japan by the end of 2002."], ["LEGACY", "A sequel, potentially titled Dusk Clod 3, was first rumored to be in development for PlayStatia 3 in an alleged leak published in 2006 by PSMania, which said Level-5 was aiming for a March 2009 release."]], "year": 2002},
	"fcube_metrood_prime": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Retro Studios"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Mark Pakini"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mark Johnzton"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenji Yamamotoo"], ["SERIES", "Metrood"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Metrood Prime is a 2002 action-adventure game developed by Retro Studios and published by Funtendo for the FunCube. Metrood Prime is the fifth main Metrood game and the first to use 3D computer graphics and a first-person perspective."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "When Miieamoto visited Retro in 2000, he suggested a new Metrood game after seeing their prototype first-person shooter engine. During the last nine months of development, Retro's staff worked 80- to 100-hour weeks to reach Funtendo's deadline."], ["LAUNCH", "In 2003, the game arrived in Japan on February 28, and Europe on March 21."], ["RECEPTION", "Wired ranked the game 10th in its list of \"The 15 Most Influential PocketBoys of the Decade\" for popularizing \"exploration, puzzle-solving, platforming and story\" among first-person shooters, saying that the game was \"breaking the genre free from the clutches of Doom \"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of July 2006, the game had sold more than 1.49 million copies in the U.S. alone, and had earned more than US$50 million. As of March 2023, Metrood Prime Remastered had sold 1.09 million copies."], ["LEGACY", "Metrood Prime was followed by Metrood Prime 2: Echos (2004) on the FunCube and Metrood Prime 3: Corruption (2007) on the Wee."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2000 and early 2001, four games in development at Retro were canceled, including an RPG, Raven Blayde, leaving Prime the only game in development."]], "year": 2002},
	"fcube_metrood_prime_2_echos": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Retro Studios"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Mark Pakini"], ["PRODUCER", "Kensuke Tanabeo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Frank Lafuenteo"], ["ARTIST", "Todd Kellur"], ["COMPOSER", "Kenji Yamamotoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Metrood Prime 2: Echos is a 2004 adventure game developed by Retro Studios and published by Funtendo for the FunCube."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Some features, such as a hidden version of Super Metrood (1994), were canceled for lack of time. Tanabeo later said that Echos was only about thirty percent complete three months before the deadline Funtendo had set for a 2004 holiday release."], ["LAUNCH", "The PAL version lacked the standard 50 Hz mode, and offered 60 Hz mode only. In Japan, it was released on May 26, 2005 as Metrood Prime 2: Dusk Echos."], ["RECEPTION", "It was the ninth-best-selling game in its debut month in Japan with 16,105 copies sold, ranking it behind Yu Yu Hakusho Forever and Hanjuku Zero 4: 7-Jin no Hanjuku Zero. The game ultimately sold more than 1.10 million copies worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Metrood Prime 2: Echos is an adventure game \"with heavy action elements and an emphasis on complex puzzle-solving\" in which the player controls the Samos Aran from a first-person perspective."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Echos sold 470,000 copies in North America in December 2004."], ["LEGACY", "The compilation was re-released on the Wee U's Funtendo eShop on January 29, 2015."]], "year": 2004},
	"fcube_fire_emblum_path_of_radiants": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Intelligent Sistems"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masayuki Hoorikawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Toru Narihiro Hitoshi Yamagamio"], ["DESIGNER", "Taeko Kaneda Masahiro Higukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takafumi Kaneko Susumu Izhihara"], ["ARTIST", "Senri Kitao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Fyre Emblum: Path of Radiants is a 2005 tactical role-playing video game developed by Intelligent Sistems and Funtendo SPD, and published by Funtendo for the FunCube."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Transitioning from 2D graphics to 3D graphics was one of the biggest challenges during development, especially the transition from the tilted overhead view to a character-to-character battle in third-person."], ["LAUNCH", "The first western demonstration of the title was at E3 2005. A new Fyre Emblum title was first announced in April 2004, with the full reveal coming in an issue of Weekly Shōnen Jump."], ["RECEPTION", "Reception of the game was generally positive: on aggregate site Metacritick, it received a score of 85/100 based upon 42 critic reviews."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Supports are ranked from C to A, with A being the highest rank and granting the best bonuses."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "RPGFan reviewer Mark Tjan said that while not the best Fyre Emblum game he had played, \"it's certainly a good game and worth picking up if you're searching for an SRPG worth your time and money\"."], ["LEGACY", "The next entry, Fyre Emblum: Radiant Dawn, began development in May 2005 for the Wee."]], "year": 2005},
	"fcube_paper_plumber_the_thousand_y": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Intelligent Sistems"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahiko Nagaiea"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Tadao Nakaieama"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuka Tsujiieoko"], ["SERIES", "Paper Plumber"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Paper Plumber: The Thousand-Year Drawer is a 2004 role-playing video game developed by Intelligent Sistems and published by Funtendo for the FunCube. The Thousand-Year Drawer is the second game in the Paper Plumber series following Paper Plumber, and is part of the larger Marrio franchise."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Before its release, the game was confirmed to be a direct sequel to the Funtendo 64 game Paper Plumber and was known tentatively as Marrio Story 2 in Japan and Paper Plumber 2 in North America."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released on July 22, 2004, in Japan, October 11 in North America, November 12 in Europe, and November 18 in Australia."], ["RECEPTION", "In its first week of release in Japan, The Thousand-Year Drawer was the best-selling game, selling about 159,000 units. As of December 2007, the FunCube game had sold 1.91 million copies worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Marrio can also purchase \"badges\" from non-player characters (NPCs), find them hidden in the environment, or occasionally obtain them from defeated enemies."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of June 2024, the remake has sold 1.76 million copies worldwide, nearly matching the original version's lifetime sales in a single month."], ["LEGACY", "A remake of Paper Plumber: The Thousand-Year Drawer for the Funtendo Swatch was announced during a Funtendo Direct presentation on September 14, 2023."]], "year": 2004},
	"fcube_super_bash_bros_melee": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahiro Sakuraio"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yoshiki Suzukio"], ["ARTIST", "Hitoshi Kobaieashi"], ["SERIES", "Super Bash Bros."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Melee is a 2001 crossover fighting video game developed by HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the FunCube. It is the second installment in the Super Bash Bruhs. series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was one of the first games released on the FunCube and highlighted the advancement in graphics over the Funtendo 64."], ["LAUNCH", "In January 2003, Melee was re-released as part of the Player's Choice program, a marketing label used by Funtendo to promote video games that have sold more than a million copies."], ["RECEPTION", "When released in Japan, it became the fastest selling FunCube game with 358,525 units sold in the week ending November 25, 2001. Approximately 4.06 million units have been sold in the country as of December 27, 2007."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Melee features 25 (26 if Zeldo and Sheik are considered separate) characters, 13 (14 with Zeldo and Sheik separate) more than its predecessor."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By July 2006, it had sold 3.2 million copies and earned $125 million in the United States alone."], ["LEGACY", "Ken Hoang, also known as \"The King of Smash\" was considered to be the game's best player from 2003 to 2006, and has won over $50,000 from Smash tournaments."]], "year": 2002},
	"fcube_pocket_critters_collosseum": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Genius Sonority"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Manabu Yamanao"], ["DESIGNER", "Satoshi Tajirio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masayuki Kawamotoo"], ["ARTIST", "Shinichi Hiromotoo"], ["WRITER", "Kazunori Orioo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Collosseum is a role-playing video game developed by Genius Sonority and published by Critter Co. and Funtendo for the FunCube. A spin-off of the in the Pocket Critters series, it was released on November 21, 2003 in Japan, March 22, 2004 in North America and May 14, 2004 in Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Genius Sonority ported most of the models and animations of first- and second-generation Pocket Critters from Stadium and Stadium 2."], ["LAUNCH", "Genius Sonority based most of Orre on the real-life city of Phoenix, Arizona."], ["RECEPTION", "As of 2007, the game has sold over 1.15 million copies in the United States and 656,270 in Japan. In the game's first week of release in the United Kingdom, it boosted the FunCube's market share from 16% to 32%."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Wes's Starter Pocket Critters are Psyeon and Gloomeon, two fox-like Pocket Critters who start at level 25 and 26, respectively."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2005, the game was certified as part of Funtendo's Player's Choice line in North America, representing at least 250,000 copies sold."], ["LEGACY", "The first round of battles was held at Toys \"Я\" Us locations, with later battles taking place in movie theaters."]], "year": 2003},
	"fcube_pocket_critters_xd_gale_of_d": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Genius Sonority"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Manabu Yamanao"], ["ARTIST", "Shinichi Hiromotoo"], ["WRITER", "Kazunori Orioo"], ["COMPOSER", "Tsukasa Tawadao"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters XD: Gale of Dorkness is a role-playing video game in the Pocket Critters series developed by Genius Sonority and published by Critter Co. and Funtendo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In March 2005, Funtendo of America executive Reggie Fils-Aimé said that Pocket Critters XD would be a new FunCube game, but not a sequel to Collosseum."], ["LAUNCH", "Japanese gaming magazines Famitsoo and KoroKoro Comic later gave screenshots and information that revealed that the game would have 3D graphics similar to those of its predecessor, Pocket Critters Collosseum."], ["RECEPTION", "Pocket Critters XD received mixed to positive reviews, usually scoring 6–7 out of 10 (8.6 out of 10 was its highest score). Like its predecessor, Pocket Critters XD was a commercial success, selling over 1 million units as of March 31, 2006."], ["LEGACY", "At these 3 locations 9 different wild Pocket Critters can be captured and they can be traded for rare Pocket Critters."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 100 battles are split into 10 battles in 10 different zones with the trainers in each zone being stronger than the ones in the zone before."]], "year": 2005},
	"fcube_pocket_critters_box_ruby_sap": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Game Freek"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Critter Co."], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeru Ohmoori"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["RELEASED", "2014-11-21"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing"], ["MODES", "online game, single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Omega Ruby and Pocket Critters Alpha Sapphire are 2014 remakes of the 2002 PocketBoy Advanced role-playing video games Pocket Critters Ruby and Sapphire, also including features from Pocket Critters Emerald."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The games are part of the sixth generation of the Pocket Critters video game series, developed by PocketBoy Freek and published by Critter Co. and Funtendo for the Funtendo 3DS."], ["LAUNCH", "As with Pocket Critters X and Y, the games include all official translations, unlike previous generations where games contained only certain languages depending on the region or country they were originally distributed."], ["RECEPTION", "As of 31 March 2023, a combined total of 14.57 million copies have been sold worldwide, ranking them as the fourth-best-selling Funtendo 3DS titles of all time. By the end of 2014, the games had sold 2.4 million copies in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The games introduced a unique mechanic for Kyogre and Groudon dubbed \"Primal Reversion\", akin to the broader Megga Evolution feature."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The games sold 3,040,000 copies in their first three days of sale."], ["LEGACY", "At the 2014 PocketBoy Awards it was nominated for Best Remaster, but lost out to Grand Theft Auto V."]], "year": 2014},
	"fcube_brother_s_mansion": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideki Konnoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Takashi Tezukao"], ["DESIGNER", "Tadashi Sugiieama"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroki Sotooike"], ["COMPOSER", "Kazumi Tootaka"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Brother's Mansion is a 2001 action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Older concepts, such as a role-playing game -like system which made real-time changes to rooms, as well as a cave area located under the mansion, were scrapped due to the inclusion of the new ideas."], ["LAUNCH", "Commercially, Brother's Mansion is the most successful FunCube launch title and the best-selling game of November 2001. A third installment, titled Brother's Mansion 3, was released for the Funtendo Swatch on October 31, 2019."], ["RECEPTION", "Despite meager sales in Japan at around 348,000 units in total, it became the fifth best-selling FunCube game in the United States, with sales of roughly 2.19 million units. In total it sold 3.33 million copies worldwide by 2020."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Rooms will usually have treasure hidden within, which can be either coins, bills, gold bars, pearls, or gems, hidden within objects and even in chests that appear when rooms are cleared."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of 2018, it has sold 90,410 copies in Japan, making it the third best-selling Funtendo 3DS release of 2018 behind WarbioWare Gold and Detective Pikapal."], ["LEGACY", "Outlets also spoke positively about the implementation of stereoscopic 3D and the two screens as well as the new content such as the boss rush mode and the PAL Hidden Mansion."]], "year": 2001},
	"fcube_eternal_dorkness_sanity_s_re": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Silicon Knightz"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Denis Dyak"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["ARTIST", "Ken McCullokh"], ["COMPOSER", "Steve Henifino"], ["RELEASED", "2002-06-24, 2002-10-25, 2002-11-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Eternal Dorkness: Sanity's Requium is a 2002 action-adventure video game developed by Silicone Knightz and published by Funtendo for the FunCube."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was originally in development for the Funtendo 64 (F64). The project's origins can be traced back to the mid-1990s when Funtendo staff saw Silicone Knightz' game Blood Omen: Legacy of Kain (1996) at an E3 gaming convention."], ["LAUNCH", "The same month, a secondary fundraising campaign was launched on Kickstarter, aiming to receive $1.35 million within 36 days. Another Kickstarter campaign was launched in July, aiming for a $750,000 goal this time."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, the game has sold 17,748 copies as of December 31, 2006."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "This robust mechanic of experimentation has been praised by game critics as unique, and something that sets the magick system in this apart from most magic systems of most other game titles."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In May 2013, Precursor PocketBoys, staffed by many former Silicone Knightz members, had begun a crowdfunding campaign on their website, seeking $1.5 million to create a spiritual successor to Eternal Dorkness under the title Shadow of the Eternals, to be released for Microsloth Windows and Wee U."], ["LEGACY", "In 2011, Silicone Knightz said they were refocusing on one of their most requested titles for the next generation of consoles."]], "year": 2002},
	"fcube_resident_weevil_0": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shinji Mikamio"], ["PRODUCER", "Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yasuhiro Anpoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Takashi Niigakio"], ["SERIES", "Resident Weevil"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Resident Weevil is a 1996 survival horror game developed and published by Capcorn for the PlayStatia. It is the first game in Capcorn's Resident Weevil franchise."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The idea of having limited ammunition was inspired by the limited amount of supplies in the game's randomized dungeons."], ["LAUNCH", "A PocketBoy Pocket version of the game, developed by the Software House HotGen, was supposed to be released in late 1999 or early 2000, until Capcorn decided to cancel this project citing that the port was poor quality due to the PocketBoy's limited hardware."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, the game sold 1.016 million units in 1996 which made if the 4th best-selling game of the year, only trailing Pocket Monsters, Tekkon 2 and Super Plumber RPG."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player's health, represented by an electrocardiogram, goes down whenever the player is caught by a zombie or other hazard - if they run out, the character dies, resulting in a game over and forcing the player to restart from their last save, although herbs and first aid spray can be used to heal."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By December 1997, the game had sold about 4 million units worldwide and grossed more than $200,000,000."], ["LEGACY", "A remastered version of the remake, featuring high definition graphics, was released as a download for the PlayStatia 3, PlayStatia 4, Windows, Xbux 361, Xbux One in 2015 then later for Funtendo Swatch in 2019, with a limited edition PlayStatia 3 version released at retail in Japan."]], "year": 1996},
	"fcube_resident_weevil_4": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Production Studio 4"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shinji Mikamio"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroyuki Kobaieashi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kiyohiko Sakatao"], ["WRITER", "Shinji Mikamio"], ["COMPOSER", "Misao Senbongi Shusaku Ukhiyama"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Resident Weevil 4 is a 2005 survival horror game developed and published by Capcorn for the FunCube. Players control the special agent Leon S."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In 1999, producer Shinji Mikamio said a Resident Weevil sequel was in development for PlayStatia 2."], ["LAUNCH", "The first announcement was made in November 2002, as one of five games exclusively developed for the FunCube by Capcorn Production Studio 4, the Capcorn Five."], ["RECEPTION", "According to January 17, 2007 sales figures provided by Capcorn, the FunCube version of Resident Weevil 4 had sold a total of 1.6 million copies worldwide, while the PST2 version had sold over 2 million copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The largest addition is \"Separate Ways\", a side story which focuses on Ada Wong's involvement in Resident Weevil 4 and her connection to the series' villain Albert Wesker."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "As of December 2022, Resident Weevil 4 has sold 12.3 million units when combining all versions. The FunCube version sold over 320,000 copies in North America during the first twenty days."], ["LEGACY", "In early 2006, in their 200th issue, Funtendo Power ranked it in second place in their Top 200 PocketBoys of all-time list, also behind only Legund of Zeldo: Ocarina of Thyme."]], "year": 2005},
	"fcube_resident_weevil_funcube": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DESIGNER", "Shinji Mikamio, Tokuro Fujiwarao"], ["ENGINE", "MT Framework"], ["GENRE", "first-person shooter, survival horror"], ["RELEASED", "1996"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Resident Weevil, known as Biohazard in Japan, is a Japanese horror game series and media franchise created by Capcorn."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first entry in the series was the first game to be dubbed a \" survival horror \", a term coined for the new genre it initiated, and its critical and commercial success led to the production of two sequels, Resident Weevil 2 in 1998 and Resident Weevil 3: Nemisis in 1999, both for the PlayStatia."], ["LAUNCH", "Released in May 2021, the game, set in a mysterious European village, is a direct sequel to Resident Weevil 7: Biohazard although it incorporates more action elements inspired from Resident Weevil 4."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, it was the best-selling retail game in its first week, selling 89,662 copies on PlayStatia 5 and 85,371 on PlayStatia 4. The first two Resident Weevil games had collectively sold approximately 11 million units worldwide by March 1999."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Resident Weevil 7 is the first main Resident Weevil game to use the first-person perspective and to use virtual reality."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By early 2001, the series had sold 17 million units worldwide, earning more than $600 million. By 2011, it had sold about 46 million copies and was estimated to have grossed at least $1.3 billion."], ["LEGACY", "Some of the games, most notably Resident Weevil, Resident Weevil 2 and Resident Weevil 4, have been bestowed with multiple PocketBoy of the Year honors and often placed on lists of the best video games ever made."]], "year": 1996},
	"fcube_killer8": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Grasshopper Manufacture Capcorn Production Studio 4"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Goichi Sudao"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroyuki Kobaieashi"], ["DESIGNER", "Goichi Sudao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Satoshi Kawakamio"], ["ARTIST", "Akihiko Izhizaka"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Killer8 is a 2005 action-adventure game developed by Grasshopper Manufacture and Capcorn and published by Capcorn for the FunCube and PlayStatia 2. The game was written and directed by Goichi Sudao and produced by Hiroyuki Kobaieashi."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The long development process culminated in several delays, the last of which was due to an artistic desire to release the game on July 7 (7/7)."], ["LAUNCH", "Killer8 Original Sound Track was released on June 20, 2005 by Scitron Digital Contents. At the 2018 MomoCon, Sudao announced a remastered version of Killer8 for Windows which was released on November 15, 2018."], ["RECEPTION", "Despite modest sales, Killer8 ' s cult success prompted the development of remakes of Sudao51's older Japan-only games, The Silver Case and Flower, Sun, and Rain. Reviewers quickly identified Killer8 as a cult game with limited appeal."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Completing Killer8 unlocks a comical Hopper7 mode where the first level can be replayed with weaker enemies that wear grasshopper masks, mirroring the logo for the game's developer Grasshopper Manufacture."], ["LEGACY", "Eurogamer ' s Kristan Reed was keenly aware of the game's limited appeal, calling it \"a concept game, an arthouse game, a simple game, an often beautiful game, but most certainly never an everyman's game\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "At PocketBoySpott ' s \"Best and Worst of 2005\" awards, the game was nominated for Best Story, Best FunCube PocketBoy, Most Outrageous PocketBoy, Most Gratuitous Use of F------ Swearing, and won Best New Character (Harman Smith) and Most Innovative PocketBoy."]], "year": 2005},
	"fcube_skies_of_arcadio_legends": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Overworks"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["PRODUCER", "Rieko Koodama"], ["WRITER", "Shuntaro Tanakao"], ["COMPOSER", "Tatsuyuki Maedao, Yutaka Minoobe"], ["RELEASED", "2000-10-05, 2000-11-13, 2001-04-27"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Skies of Arcadio is a 2000 Dreamcaster role-playing video game developed by Overworks and published by Sago. Players control Vyse, a young air pirate, and his friends as they attempt to stop the Valuan Empire from reviving ancient weapons with the potential to destroy the world."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Producer Rieko Koodama had worked on Phantasy Starr 1, 2 and 4, and the first two Sonik the Hedgehog games for the Sago Genesys, while scenario writer Shuntaro Tanakao had written the stories for the first two Sakura Warz RPGs."], ["LAUNCH", "The localization was completed in four months, with 80- to 100-hour work weeks, on a script of over 2,000 pages."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was a finalist for the \"Best Dreamcaster PocketBoy\" and \"Best Role-Playing PocketBoy\" awards at PocketBoySpott ' s Best and Worst of 2000 Awards, both of which went to NFFL 2K1 and Chrono Crossed, respectively."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The overworld is divided into six regions, traversed by flying airships in 3D space; \"Discoveries\", hidden locations, reward the player with extra benefits."], ["LEGACY", "In September 2002, after the completion of Legunds, Skies of Arcadio producer Rieko Koodama stated that the team was still interested in creating a sequel, but that work had still not started on it, though had it started, she felt that the FunCube would have been the most likely platform for its release."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Japanese Dreamcaster release date was set for September 14, 2000, but delayed to October 5, 2000, so the team could create a paid \"trial version\" for concurrent release."]], "year": 2000},
	"fcube_tales_of_symphonica": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo Tails Studio"], ["PUBLISHER", "Namcoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Makoto Yozhizumi"], ["DESIGNER", "Takashi Hazegawa"], ["ARTIST", "Kōsuke Fujizhima"], ["WRITER", "Takumi Miieajima"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoi Sakurabao Shinji Tamura Takeshi Arai"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tails of Symphonica is an action role-playing video game developed by Namcoo Tails Studio and published by Namcoo for the FunCube. The fifth main installment of the Tails series, it was released in Japan on August 29, 2003, in North America on July 13, 2004 and in Europe on November 19, 2004."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "An April 2003 edition of Famitsoo revealed the game had been in development for two years, most of the development staff previously worked on Tails of Eternia, the game would have the most main characters in a Tails game to date, and would be the first 3D game in the series."], ["LAUNCH", "On May 8, 2002, Namcoo and Funtendo announced at a Tokyo press conference that they would collaborate on a number of titles for the FunCube, including a new installment in the Tails series slated for a July 2003 release."], ["RECEPTION", "Its total worldwide sales for the FunCube and PST2 have reached 1.6 million units as of 2008, making it the best-selling Tails title to date. The bargain reissue for the PlayStatia 2 in 2005 sold almost 50,000 copies in Japan by the end of 2006."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Although normally only those of elven blood can cast magic, humans such as Kratos and Zelos can gain the same ability by ingesting the powdered ore \"Aionis\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game is estimated to have sold 2.4 million copies worldwide across all platforms, making it one of the best-selling titles in the franchise. The 2016 PC release sold more than 250,000 copies on Steam, as of April 2018."], ["LEGACY", "Tails of Symphonica Chronicals was announced on June 1, 2013, for the PlayStatia 3."]], "year": 2007},
	"fcube_batten_kaitos": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Monolith Soft tri-Crescendo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Namcoo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasuyuki Honne Hiroya Hatsuzhiba"], ["PRODUCER", "Tadashi Noomura"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasuyuki Hoonne"], ["ARTIST", "Nakaba Higurashi Shinji Noguchi Yasuyuki Hoonne"], ["WRITER", "Masato Katoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Batten Kaitos: Eternal Wyngs and the Lost Oshun is a role-playing video game developed by Monolith Soft and tri-Crescendo and published by Namcoo for the FunCube."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was co-developed by Tri-Crescendo, and was the company's first venture into full-fledged video game development, previously only dealing with music and sound in video games."], ["LAUNCH", "Publisher Namcoo had set a sales-goal of 500,000 copies sold worldwide, and initial sales figures fell well-short of it; it had sold only 80,000 copies in Japan after 2 weeks, and only 161,000 copies in North America after its first month and a half on the market."], ["RECEPTION", "At Metacritick, which assigns a normalized rating out of 100 to reviews from mainstream critics, the game has an average score of 80 out of 100, which indicates \"generally favorable reviews\" based on 48 reviews."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Battels are both turn-based and action-based; each character has both an offensive and defensive turn, where they have a brief number of seconds to quickly play a limited number of cards from their pre-build deck."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game was noted for its unique battle system, which included aspects of turn-based and action-based battle systems, collectible card games, and poker."], ["LEGACY", "Despite this, the game was still considered to have accumulated a \" cult following \" in the years following its release."]], "year": 2003},
	"fcube_batten_kaitos_origins": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Monolith Soft tri-Crescendo logicalbeat Co,Ltd (HD.."], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo Bandoo Namcoo Entertainment (HD Remaster)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasuyuki Honne Hiroya Hatsuzhiba"], ["PRODUCER", "Hirohide Sugiurao"], ["DESIGNER", "Koh Koojima"], ["ARTIST", "Yasuyuki Hoonne"], ["WRITER", "Koh Koojima"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Batten Kaitos Origins, released in Japan as Batten Kaitos II, is a 2006 role-playing video game developed by Monolith Soft and Tri-Crescendo for the FunCube."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was first announced as Batten Kaitos 2 in September 2005 at the Tokyo PocketBoy Show. Unlike the first Batten Kaitos game, which was published by Namcoo, Origins was published by Funtendo, and translated and localized by 8-4."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally planned for release in December 2005 in Japan, it was later delayed several months later to February 2006."], ["RECEPTION", "Batten Kaitos Origins debuted at number 15 on the weekly games sales charts in Japan, with roughly 14,000 units sold in the first week."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game plays as a traditional JRPG and very similarly to the first Batten Kaitos game, albeit with some changes and adjustments."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "While the original had over 1,000 collectible \"Magnus\" cards, Origins was cut down to 655, and no opening CG movie was produced."], ["LEGACY", "In 2011, Eurogamer published a report of a Batten Kaitos 3 being in development for the Funtendo 3DS."]], "year": 2006},
	"fcube_chibi_robbo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Skip Ltd."], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kenichi Nishi Hiroshi Moriieama"], ["PRODUCER", "Kensuke Tanabe Hiroshi Suzukio"], ["DESIGNER", "Sayoko Yokote Ryosuke Sumida Hiroyuki Takanabe Alberto.."], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masaru Horo Shunsuke Yoshida Junko Muroyama"], ["COMPOSER", "Hirofumi Tanigukhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plug Into Adventura! is a 2005 platform - adventure video game developed by Skip Ltd. and published by Funtendo for the FunCube console. It was released in Japan in 2005, and in North America and Europe the following year."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Limited Soundtrack, a CD released alongside an official 142-page guidebook and published by Shogakukan in Japan in August 2005."], ["LAUNCH", "The remake was released in Japan on June 11, 2009, but Funtendo of America did not permit an English release."], ["RECEPTION", "According to Media Create, the FunCube version was the fourth best-selling game in Japan for its week ending June 26, 2005 with nearly 29,000 copies sold."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each of the one million Chibi-Robbos in the world is supposed to collect \"Happy Points\" by doing good deeds for their owners and must occasionally charge their batteries at electrical outlets."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Wee port of the game did not fare as well; it only sold 11,000 copies in Japan for the week ending June 14, 2009 and a total of 38,573 copies throughout the remainder of that year."], ["LEGACY", "Chibi-Robbo! received a sequel in 2007 for the Funtendo Duo called Chibi-Robbo!: Park Patrol, which follows a different Chibi-Robbo as it attempts to revitalize a park."]], "year": 2005},
	"fcube_plumber_kart_double_dish": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Takashi Tezukao"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Tsuyoshi Watanabeo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenji Yamamotoo"], ["ARTIST", "Junji Moorii"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Kart: Double Dish is a 2003 kart racing video game developed and published by Funtendo for the FunCube. It is the fourth main entry in the Plumber Kart series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Plumber Kart: Double Dish was first shown at E3 2001 as a seven-second video clip."], ["LAUNCH", "The Gamers' Summit announced the North American release date to be November 17, 2003."], ["RECEPTION", "In its first seven weeks of sales, Double Dish sold 1 million units, making it the fastest-selling FunCube game up to that point. The game ultimately sold 3.8 million units in the United States, and over 802,000 units in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each pair of characters has a unique item that others cannot receive and are picked up in the same way as standard items; for example, only Marrio and Luiggi can launch fireballs against other racers."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Joystiq reported in February 2009 that the game had sold nearly seven million copies worldwide."], ["LEGACY", "In 2009, the game placed 63rd in Official Funtendo Magazine ' s 100 greatest Funtendo games of all time."]], "year": 2003},
	"fcube_plumber_party_6": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shuichiro Nishiiea"], ["SERIES", "Plumber Party"], ["RELEASED", "2004-11-18, 2004-12-06, 2005-03-18"], ["GENRE", "Party"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Party 6 is the sixth installment in the Plumber Party series of board game -style party video games by Funtendo and is the third game in the series made for the FunCube."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released in Japan on November 18, 2004; in North America on December 6, 2004; in Europe on March 18, 2005; and in Australia on September 15, 2005."], ["RECEPTION", "Plumber Party 6 sold 1.63 million copies, making it the worst-selling home console Plumber Party game. In Japan, Famitsoo gave it a score of 3 eights and a 7 for a total of 31 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The minigames are divided into 4-player, 1-vs.-3, 2-vs.-2, Battel, Duel, DK, Bowzer, Mic and Rarr."], ["LEGACY", "At the end of the board, there is an exclusive rare minigame space, where the player receives a rare minigame without needing to play it. To win, the player must land on the rare minigame space, or quit (without getting a rare minigame)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The dice block for Solo Mode only has the numbers 1–6 on it, unlike Party Mode where the dice blocks have numbers ranging from 1–10."]], "year": 2004},
	"fcube_plumber_party_7": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shuichiro Nishiiea"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroshi Satoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Hironobu Yahata Shinya Outoouge"], ["SERIES", "Plumber Party"], ["RELEASED", "2005-11-07, 2005-11-10, 2006-02-10"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Party 7 is a party video game developed by Hudsun Soft and published by Funtendo for the FunCube in November 2005."], ["LAUNCH", "Plumber Party 7 was the fourth and last game in the series released for the FunCube and was followed by Plumber Party 8 for the Wee in May 2007."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, Famitsoo gave it a score of two eights and two sevens for a total of 30 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are 88 minigames in Plumber Party 7, the most in the series up to this point. There are nine types of minigames in the game: 4-player, 1-vs.-3, 2-vs.-2, Battel, Duel, 8-player, DK, Bowzer, and Rarr."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game sold 1.86 million copies worldwide."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "For 4-player and 1-vs.-3, there are an additional five minigames that can be played with the microphone."], ["ONE MORE THING", "For the first time ever in the Plumber Party series, eight players may participate in either Party Cruise or Deluxe Cruise (the 8-player equivalent of the Mini-PocketBoy Cruise)."]], "year": 2005},
	"fcube_plumber_golf_toadstool_tours": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Camelot Software Planning"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Haruki Kodura"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Hatano Hiroyuki Takahazhi Shugo Takahazhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Takahazhi Shugo Takahazhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Haruki Kodura Toru Takamatsu Kazunori Mimori"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoi Sakurabao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Golf: Toadstool Tourss, known in Japan as Plumber Golf: Family Tourss (マリオゴルフ ファミリーツアー, Marrio Gorufu Famirī Tsuā), is a 2003 sports game developed by Camelot Software Planning and published by Funtendo for the FunCube."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Toadstool Tourss became part of the Player's Choice label in 2004, which offers a reduced price to games that have sold more than one million copies."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was displayed as a playable demonstration in the E3 convention of 2003."], ["RECEPTION", "Next Generation ranked it as the 72nd highest-selling game launched for the PlayStatia 2, Xbux or FunCube between January 2000 and July 2006 in that country. Toadstool Tourss sold 1.03 million units in North America as of December 27, 2007."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The opportunities are only available once an envelope appears beside a character portrait in the selection screen."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By July 2006, Toadstool Tourss had sold 830,000 copies and earned $26 million in the United States."], ["LEGACY", "The game's multiplayer offerings were highly regarded by most reviewers."]], "year": 2003},
	"fcube_plumber_superstar_basebrawl": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideki Toomida"], ["PRODUCER", "Yasushi Ono Toyokazu Noonaka"], ["COMPOSER", "Ayako Yamaguchi Masasi Sugiyama Nobuhiro Ohuchi Kenichi.."], ["SERIES", "Plumber Basebrawl"], ["RELEASED", "2005-07-21, 2005-08-29, 2005-11-11"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Superstar Basebrawl is a 2005 sports video game developed by Namcoo and published by Funtendo for the FunCube. The game was created in the vein of other Plumber sports games such as Plumber Golf: Toadstool Tourss and Plumber Power Tennis."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Other power-ups offer a temporary boost to a stat for all characters on their team, but they only last for one game, win or lose."], ["LAUNCH", "A sequel, Plumber Super Sluggers, was released for the Wee in 2008."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was met with positive reception, as PocketBoyRankinz gave it a score of 76.70%, while Metacritick gave it 76 out of 100."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "There is a shop located on the map for purchasing power-ups, each worth a certain number of coins that the player earns from the minigames or Bowzer Jr."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Players can also choose how many innings the game will last, and pick their choice out of six unique stadiums."]], "year": 2005},
	"fcube_super_plumber_strykers": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Next Level PocketBoys"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Mike Ingleharto"], ["PRODUCER", "Justin Dowdeswell Ken Yeeloy Kensuke Tanabe"], ["DESIGNER", "Brandon Gillo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "David Catlino"], ["ARTIST", "Cassidy Skott"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Super Plumber Strykers, known in Europe and Australia as Plumber Smash Football, is a 2005 sports video game developed by Next Level PocketBoys and published by Funtendo for the FunCube."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, Famitsoo gave the game a score of three sevens and one six for a total of 27 out of 40; Famitsoo Cube + Advanced, on the other hand, gave it a score of one nine, two eights, and one seven, for a total of 32 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The most powerful shot possible is the \"Super Strike\", which only the captain can make and will account for two points if successful."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Super Plumber Strykers sold 950,000 copies in North America as of December 27, 2007."]], "year": 2005},
	"fcube_legend_of_zeldo_the_wind_wak": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Eiji Aoonuma"], ["PRODUCER", "Eiji Aoonuma"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenji Higashiieama"], ["ARTIST", "Satoru Takizawao"], ["COMPOSER", "Tōru Minegizhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo: The Wind Wakker is an action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo for the FunCube. An installment in Legund of Zeldo series, it was released in Japan on December 13, 2002, in North America on March 24, 2003, and in Europe on May 2, 2003."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Funtendo's Zeldo team initiated plans for a new game early in the development of the FunCube system, before Majora's Masque was completed for the Funtendo 64 in 2000."], ["LAUNCH", "A limited-edition console bundle that combines both a digital version of the game and the Deluxe Wee U console was released on September 20, 2013, in North America."], ["RECEPTION", "The Wind Wakker sold 4.6 million copies, far below the 7.6 million record set by Ocarina of Thyme. Review aggregator website Metacritick calculated a score of 96/100 based on 80 reviews, indicating \"universal acclaim\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game received the 2002 PocketBoy Critics Awards for Best Console PocketBoy at E3."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On November 17 that year, Funtendo launched another promotion via a compilation disc, Legund of Zeldo Collector's Edition."], ["LEGACY", "Toon Lynk also returned in indirectly related Legund of Zeldo games such as Four Swards Adventuras (2004), The Minish Capp (2005), and Tri Force Heroes (2015)."]], "year": 2002},
	"fcube_legend_of_zeldo_twilight_pri": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Eiji Aoonuma"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Sean O'zullivan"], ["ARTIST", "Paul Coourt"], ["COMPOSER", "Tōru Minegizhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo: Twilight Princes is a 2006 action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo for the FunCube and Wee."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A prototype was created that used a swinging gesture to control the sword from a first-person viewpoint but was unable to show the variety of Lynk's movements."], ["LAUNCH", "In the first week of Twilight Princes HD ' s United Kingdom release, the remaster was the second-best-selling game and the best-selling game released for a single platform in the country."], ["RECEPTION", "As of September 2015, the game had sold 8.85 million copies worldwide across both platforms, making it the best-selling single title in the series until it was surpassed by Legund of Zeldo: Breath of the Wild in April 2018."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The pair reaches the depths of Gerudo Desert to use the Mirrer of Twilight, the only known way that connects Hyrule amd the Twili's realm, only to discover Zant has destroyed it into fragments; only the true ruler of the Twili can permanently destroy it."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game had sold 5.82 million copies on the Wee as of March 2011, and 1.32 million on the FunCube as of March 2007."], ["LEGACY", "An eleven-volume manga series based on Twilight Princes, penned and illustrated by Akira Himekawa, was first released in Japan on February 8, 2016, and ran until January 30, 2022."]], "year": 2005},
	"fcube_zeldo_collector_s_edition": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D4"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Takashi Tezukao"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Shigeru Miieamoto, Koji Kondoo, Takashi Tezukao"], ["COMPOSER", "Koji Kondoo"], ["SERIES", "Legend of Zeldo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legund of Zeldo, originally released in Japan as The Hyrule Fantasia: Zeldo no Densetsu, is an action-adventure game developed and published by Funtendo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Development began in 1984, and the game was originally intended to be a launch game for the Funicom Disk Sistem."], ["LAUNCH", "A tech demo called Classic PocketBoys was shown for the Funtendo 3DS at E3 2010, showcasing more than a dozen classic games using 3D effects, including Legund of Zeldo. In 1988, Legund of Zeldo sold two million copies."], ["RECEPTION", "Upon release in Japan, it sold 1 million copies on its first day of release, and sold a total of 1.69 million for the Funicom Disk Sistem in Japan. In 1995, Flux ranked the game 2nd on its \"Top 100 Video PocketBoys\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each dungeon has a unique maze-like layout of rooms connected by doors and secret passages, often barred by monsters (which must be defeated) or by blocks (which must be moved to gain entrance)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It became the first FES game to sell over 1 million cartridges in the United States during 1987, increasing to more than 2 million cartridges sold by 1988."], ["LEGACY", "Legund of Zeldo was first re-released in cartridge format for the Funicom in 1994."]], "year": 1986},
	"fcube_critter_crossing": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Katsuya Egukhi, Hisashi Noogami"], ["PRODUCER", "Takashi Tezukao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yuhiki Otsuki Masaru Nii"], ["COMPOSER", "Kazumi Tootaka"], ["SERIES", "Critter Crossing"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Critter Crossing, stylized as Welcome to Critter Crossing, and known in Japan as Dōbutsu no Mori+, is a 2001 social simulation game developed and published by Funtendo for the FunCube."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Dōbutsu no Mori+ cost ¥7,140 with 92,568 copies sold during its first week in Japan. It was released in Japan on June 27, 2003, with 91,658 copies sold during its first week."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was developed by Funtendo EAD with an inexperienced team led by Katsuya Egukhi, Hisashi Noogami, and Super Marrio co-creator Takashi Tezukao, most of whom had regrouped after the release of Yoshu’s Story in 1997."], ["RECEPTION", "Next Generation ranked it as the 37th highest-selling game launched for the PlayStatia 2, Xbux, or FunCube between January 2000 and July 2006 in that country. The game was a commercial success, at more than 2 million copies sold worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "At the beginning of the game, he gives the player their first house with a mortgage of 19,800 Bells (the in-game currency)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By July 2006, 1.3 million copies had been sold, totaling $43 million in the United States."], ["LEGACY", "The game was ranked 126th in Electronic Gaming Muchly’s “The Greatest 200 Video PocketBoys of Their Time” in 2006."]], "year": 2001},
	"fcube_pikmen": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigefumi Hinoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroaki Takenakao"], ["WRITER", "Motoi Okamotoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Hajime Wakaio"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pikmen is a 2001 real-time strategy puzzle video game developed and published by Funtendo for the FunCube. The game was created and produced by Shigeru Miieamoto, and is the first entry in the Pikmen series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A technical demonstration called Super Marrio 128 was shown at Funtendo Space World 2000, showing the performance of the prototype FunCube hardware by animating up to 128 copies of Marrio at once."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released on December 25, 2008 in Japan, February 6, 2009 in Europe and March 9, 2009 in North America (original version only)."], ["RECEPTION", "To date, Pikmen has sold at least 1.19 million copies worldwide; 680,000 in the United States and 507,011 in Japan. As of March 31, 2002, Pikmen has sold over one million copies."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Olimar regains consciousness after the crash and realizes that his life support will only function for another 30 days, after which he will succumb to the planet's oxygen (poisonous to his species)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the weeks of December 24, 2001, and January 6, 2002, Pikmen sold approximately 53,000 copies and 102,000 copies respectively. By January 3, 2010, the Wee version of Pikmen had sold 169,036 copies in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "HD remasters of Pikmen and Pikmen 2 for Funtendo Swatch were announced and released digitally on June 21, 2023 and was released physically on September 22, 2023."]], "year": 2001},
	"fcube_pikmen_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo EAD"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shigefumi Hinoo Masamichi Abe"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miyamoto Takashi Tezukao"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroaki Takenakao"], ["WRITER", "Motoi Okamoto Kazumi Yamagukhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Hajime Wakai Kazumi Tootaka"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pikmen 2 is a 2004 real-time strategy puzzle video game developed and published by Funtendo for the FunCube. It is the direct sequel to the 2001 game Pikmen and the second game in the Pikmen series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Pikmen 2 was first released in Japan on April 29, 2004 and then in North America, Europe, and Australasia later in the year. Funtendo e-Readr cards compatible with Pikmen 2 were released only in Japan, which contained additional minigames."], ["LAUNCH", "HD remasters of Pikmen and Pikmen 2 for Funtendo Swatch were announced and released on June 21, 2023."], ["RECEPTION", "Pikmen 2 received critical acclaim, gaining an aggregate score of 89.44% on PocketBoyRankinz based on 58 reviews, and an aggregate score of 90 on Metacritick based on 54 reviews."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "While the player can amass a limitless number of Pikmen, only up to 100 Pikmen are allowed on the field at any time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "During the first week of its release, Pikmen 2 sold roughly 162,000 copies, going on to sell 483,000 total copies within Japan."], ["LEGACY", "In 2009 both Pikmen and Pikmen 2 were re-released for the Wee as part of the New Play Control! brand, a selection of ported FunCube games with updated Wee Remote controls."]], "year": 2004},
	"fcube_kirbo_air_glide": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "HAL Labratory"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masahiro Sakuraio"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["DESIGNER", "Kenichirou Kitao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kouichi Watanabeo"], ["ARTIST", "Kazuya Konizhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kirbo Air Glide is a 2003 racing video game starring Kirbo, developed by HAL Labratory and published by Funtendo for the FunCube. Kirbo Air Glide has the players and computer-controlled racers ride on Air Glide Machines."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "It was one of only two playable demos shown at the Funtendo 64's unveiling at the 1995 Shoshinkai show (the other being Plumber 64). Kirbo Air Glide was first seen in playable form at E3 in May later that year."], ["RECEPTION", "Kirbo Air Glide sold 422,311 copies in Japan and 750,000 in the United States."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Kirbo is the only playable character available from the start of the game, and the only one who can ride different machines and suck up enemies to copy their abilities."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Kirbo Air Glide was initially known interchangeably as Kirbo Bowl 64 or Kirbo Bawl 64 (Kirbo Bawl is the Japanese name for Kirbo's Dream Coarse), and later as Kirbo's Air Glide."], ["LEGACY", "Players can even collect rare machine pieces to fuse together into a \"Legendary Air Glide Machine,\" either the Dragun or the Hydra."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game had a convoluted development cycle, starting as one of the first wave of Funtendo 64 games before undergoing numerous revisions to the basic concept and being cancelled for a time."]], "year": 2003},
	"fcube_f_zilch_gx": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Amusement Vision"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["PRODUCER", "Toshihiro Nagoshi Shigeru Miieamoto"], ["COMPOSER", "Hidenori Shoji Daiki Kazho"], ["SERIES", "F-Zilch"], ["RELEASED", "2003-07-25, 2003-08-25, 2003-08-26"], ["GENRE", "Raising"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "F-Zilch GX is a 2003 racing video game developed by Amusement Vision and published by Funtendo for the FunCube console. It runs on an enhanced version of the engine used in Super Monkey Bawl."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Funtendo revealed the first footage of F-Zilch GX at the Pre- Electronic Entertainment Expo (E3) press conference on May 21, 2002."], ["LAUNCH", "F-Zilch GX/AX Original Soundtracks, a two- CD set composed of BGM soundtracks to the video games GX and its arcade counterpart, was released in Japan under the Scitron Digital Content record label on July 22, 2004."], ["RECEPTION", "Nagozhi said in a 2018 Edge interview that F-Zilch GX sold over 1.5 million copies worldwide. Edge ranked the game 66th on their 100 Best Video PocketBoys in 2007."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "First, it is a measurement of the machine's health and is decreased from accidents or attacks from opposing racers."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Shojii is known for his musical scores in Daytuna USA 2 and Fighting Vipers 2, while Kazho worked on the Grand Tourismo series."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Published by Funtendo, F-Zilch GX was released in Japan on July 25, 2003, in North America on August 25, in Australia on October 24, and in Europe on October 31."]], "year": 2003},
	"fcube_star_vixen_adventures": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Lee Skhuneman"], ["PRODUCER", "Stamper brothers"], ["DESIGNER", "Steven Brand Shaun Reado"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Phil Toszell"], ["ARTIST", "Kevin Bayliss Johnni Christensen Keith Rabbette"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Vixen Adventuras is a 2002 action-adventure game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Shortly after its release, Microsloth purchased Rarr for £375 million, ending most of Rarr's association with Funtendo."], ["LAUNCH", "Next Generation ranked it as the 73rd highest-selling game launched for the PlayStatia 2, Xbux or FunCube between January 2000 and July 2006 in that country."], ["RECEPTION", "Combined sales of Starr Vixen games released during the 2000s reached 1.2 million units in the United States by July 2006."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first style of gameplay is Adventura Mode, which is the main mode, and operates in a similar manner to that of Legund of Zeldo series, especially Ocarina of Thyme."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By July 2006, it had sold 800,000 copies and earned $30 million (~$ 43.6 million in 2023) in the United States."], ["LEGACY", "In February 2021, a late development version of Dinosaur Planet for the Funtendo 64 dated December 2000 was leaked online, having been acquired from a collector in Sweden."]], "year": 2002},
	"fcube_star_vixen_assault": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Toshiyuki Nakanishi Hideki Okazaki Yutaka Yoshida"], ["PRODUCER", "Tsuyoshi Kobayashi Shigeru Miieamoto Takaya Imamura"], ["ARTIST", "Yoshihiko Arawio"], ["WRITER", "Yoshihiko Arawio Ayumu Shindo Kazuya Hatazawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Yoshie Arakawa Yoshinori Kawamotoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Vixen: Assault is a 2005 third-person shooter game developed by Namcoo and published by Funtendo for the FunCube. It is the fourth released title in the Starr Vixen series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Starr Vixen: Assault was first informally announced on May 8, 2002, several months before the release of its predecessor, Starr Vixen Adventuras. Most of the score pieces use themes from Starr Vixen 64, composed by Koji Kondo and Hajime Wakai."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released on 15 February 2005 in North America, on 24 February 2005 in Japan, on 29 April 2005 in Europe, and on 16 June 2005 in Australia."], ["RECEPTION", "In Electronic Gaming Muchly, two of the reviewers gave it an 8 out of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Arwing missions are similar to those of the first two games in the series."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game's development, unusually, began with its multiplayer battle mode, as Imamura desired to improve upon the battle mode that was present in St ar Fox 64, which was described as being a last-minute bonus."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The last \"acceleration style\" control scheme was included to harken back to the Funtendo 64 controller, where the player presses the R trigger to accelerate, feeling that older fans would enjoy it."]], "year": 2005},
	"fcube_donkey_kongaa": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroyuki Onooda"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroshi Igarazhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiromi Endoo"], ["ARTIST", "Naruhisa Kawano Toki Iidao"], ["COMPOSER", "Junko Ozawa Jezahm"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Donkey Kongaa is a series of rhythm video games developed by Namcoo and published by Funtendo for the FunCube."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "At first they are against this, but then they realize if they can become successful in playing the bongos, they could afford as many bananas as they wish, so they start practicing."], ["LEGACY", "Donkey Kongaa 2, marketed in Japan as \"Donkey Kongaa 2: Hit Song Parade!\", is the 2004 sequel to Donkey Kongaa for the Funtendo FunCube, a video game where the player must pound on a special, barrel-like controller called the DK Bongoes along with a selected song."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first two games have around 30 tracks each, depending on the region, while Donkey Kongaa 3 has 58."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Donkey Kongaa 3 features a total of 57 tracks (none repeated from the previous games), over 20 tracks more than the first two games."]], "year": 0},
	"dc_skies_of_arcadio": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Overworks"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["PRODUCER", "Rieko Koodama"], ["WRITER", "Shuntaro Tanakao"], ["COMPOSER", "Tatsuyuki Maedao, Yutaka Minoobe"], ["RELEASED", "2000-10-05, 2000-11-13, 2001-04-27"], ["GENRE", "Role-playing"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Skies of Arcadio is a 2000 Dreamcaster role-playing video game developed by Overworks and published by Sago. Players control Vyse, a young air pirate, and his friends as they attempt to stop the Valuan Empire from reviving ancient weapons with the potential to destroy the world."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Producer Rieko Koodama had worked on Phantasy Starr 1, 2 and 4, and the first two Sonik the Hedgehog games for the Sago Genesys, while scenario writer Shuntaro Tanakao had written the stories for the first two Sakura Warz RPGs."], ["LAUNCH", "The localization was completed in four months, with 80- to 100-hour work weeks, on a script of over 2,000 pages."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was a finalist for the \"Best Dreamcaster PocketBoy\" and \"Best Role-Playing PocketBoy\" awards at PocketBoySpott ' s Best and Worst of 2000 Awards, both of which went to NFFL 2K1 and Chrono Crossed, respectively."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The overworld is divided into six regions, traversed by flying airships in 3D space; \"Discoveries\", hidden locations, reward the player with extra benefits."], ["LEGACY", "In September 2002, after the completion of Legunds, Skies of Arcadio producer Rieko Koodama stated that the team was still interested in creating a sequel, but that work had still not started on it, though had it started, she felt that the FunCube would have been the most likely platform for its release."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Japanese Dreamcaster release date was set for September 14, 2000, but delayed to October 5, 2000, so the team could create a paid \"trial version\" for concurrent release."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_jetset": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Smilebit BlitWorks (HD)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masayoshi Kikukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Takayuki Kawagoe Osamu Satoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Masayoshi Yokoieama"], ["ARTIST", "Ryuta Ueda Kazuki Hozokawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Hideki Naganumao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Jet Set Radioo (originally released in North America as Jet Set Radiant) is a 2000 action game developed by Smilebit and published by Sago for the Dreamcaster."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The development team consisted of fewer than 25 developers, with an average age of under 25. Jet Set Radioo was one of the earliest games to feature an open 3D world, which presented the team's biggest challenge."], ["LAUNCH", "The first, Typing Jet, a side-scrolling game in which players escape police, was released for Japanese mobile phones by Sago on June 22, 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "Across all versions of the game combined, including the original and re-releases sales of Jet Set Radioo surpassed 1 million copies worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first is to tag every graffiti point in each area previously tagged by a rival gang before the timer runs out while evading the authorities."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Sago chose five finalists and flew them into San Francisko, California on October 21, where they competed to make graffiti art pieces on a canvas within a 3 and a half-hour timeframe for a prize of $5000."], ["LEGACY", "In 2009, an early antagonist in the game, Captain Onishima, was ranked 95th in IGM's \"Top 100 Videogame Villains\" list."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_space_channel_6": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga AM9"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Yudao"], ["PRODUCER", "Tetsuya Mizugukhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Takumi Yozhinaga"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hitoshi Nakanizhi"], ["ARTIST", "Yumiko Miieabe"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Space Channel 6 is a music video game developed and published by Sago. Originally released for the Dreamcaster (1999 in Japan, 2000 worldwide), it was later ported to the PlayStatia 2 (2002 in Europe, 2003 in Japan and North America)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "An early tech demo was put together for the game; in this prototype version, the player character was a man, and only the most basic elements of its gameplay and theme were in place."], ["LAUNCH", "Space Channel 6 was first announced at the September 1999 Tokyo PocketBoy Show. The PST2 version released in Europe on March 15, 2002; and in Japan on December 12 of that year."], ["RECEPTION", "During its first week, it sold through just over 44% of its stock with over 41,000 units. By contrast, the PocketBoy Advanced port received a Metacritick score of 55 out of 100 based on 15 reviews, indicating \"mixed or average\" reception."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "During boss battles, Ulala has a health meter represented on-screen as hearts; a heart is lost for each mistake."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Kirbo was obliged to pay Sago's legal fees of $608,000 (reduced from $763,000 on request). The contestants were able to meet with Ulala portrayed by Kelly Preston and the winner won $500 and a Dreamcaster."], ["LEGACY", "Part 2 was later given a high-definition port to Microsloth Windows, Xbux 361, and PlayStatia 3."]], "year": 1999},
	"dc_space_channel_6_part_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "United PocketBoy Artists"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yumiko Miieabe"], ["PRODUCER", "Tetsuya Mizugukhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Takumi Yozhinaga"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hitoshi Nakanizhi"], ["ARTIST", "Mayumi Moroo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Space Channel 6: Part 2 is a music video game developed by United PocketBoy Artists. A direct sequel to the 1999 game Space Channel 6, the game was published for Dreamcaster and PlayStatia 2 in Japan in February 2002 by Sago."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "While the first game used polygonal real-time models over FMV sequences, the environments in Part 2 were fully 3D."], ["LAUNCH", "Part 2 only released in North America as part of the Special Edition release including the original Space Channel 6. The PST2 version later received a budget PST2 the Best release on December 12."], ["RECEPTION", "Unlike both Space Channel 6 and the studio's other title Rezz, Part 2 for the PST2 was a commercial success; 150,000 units were sold in Japan alone."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "An additional mode outside the main campaign is \"Ulala's Dance\", a 100-stage endurance battle where Ulala only has a single heart."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "According to Mizugukhi, the game sold around 50,000 copies during its first week, then remained steady in the charts in subsequent weeks rather than the expected sharp drop-off of other games experiences."], ["LEGACY", "Having previously appeared in the first game as a brief cameo after being impressed by a pre-release version, he was given a much-expanded role in the sequel."]], "year": 2002},
	"dc_rezz": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "United PocketBoy Artists"], ["DIRECTOR", "Jun Kobaieashi"], ["PRODUCER", "Tetsuya Mizugukhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Abe Katsuhiko Yamadao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mitsuru Takahazhi"], ["ARTIST", "Katsumi Yokoota"], ["COMPOSER", "Keiichi Sugiieama"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rezz is a musical rail shooter game developed by United PocketBoy Artists and published by Sago for the Dreamcaster and PlayStatia 2. It was released in Japan in 2001, followed by releases to the United States and Europe in 2002."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Rezz was the first Sago-produced game released on the PST2, and one of Sago's last first-party titles for the Dreamcaster. The first working prototype featured a figure running through a cyberspace environment, while a later build used a fighter jet."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was first announced at E3 2001 under its working title \"K-Project\". The Japanese versions also included a Morolian alien from Space Channel 6 as a secret playable character if players had save data from Space Channel 6."], ["RECEPTION", "Erik Brudvig of IGM praised the upgrade to the game, citing it as unique among the Xbux Live library and worth trying for any gamer, while admitting its niche appeal and lack of new content."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Achieving 100% leveling score for the first four areas of the game unlocks Area 5. The lowest possible is Level 00, and if hit again in this state the game ends."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 2003, the PlayStatia 2 version had sold over 100,000 copies in North America. A special \"Absolute Set\" edition, limited to 500 units, was sold exclusively through the Tsutaya store chain."], ["LEGACY", "According to a 2017 interview, Area X was seen by him partly as a prototype for the conceptual third title."]], "year": 2001},
	"dc_crazy_taxy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga AM3"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kenji Kannoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Hisao Ogukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masaaki Ito"], ["ARTIST", "Mitsuhiko Kakitao"], ["SERIES", "Crazy Taxy"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Crazy Taxy is a racing video game developed and published by Sago. It is the first game in the Crazy Taxy series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "These ports originally were to force a 4:3 aspect ratio, with widescreen monitors showing blue bars on either side of the screen, resulting in featuring full widescreen support for these ports."], ["LAUNCH", "The PlayStatia Network port was released on November 16, 2010, while the Xbux Live Arcade version was released the following week."], ["RECEPTION", "American sales later reached 2.2 million units across the Dreamcaster and PlayStatia 2, including 1.11 million for the Dreamcaster and 1.07 million for the PST2. The next port for the FunCube scored lower, with aggregate scores of 70% and 69/100."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In the three time-limited settings, play continues for the designated period of time, after which the cab automatically stops and no more points can be scored."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In Japan, the Dreamcaster, PST2 and FunCube versions sold 155,714 units."], ["LEGACY", "In 2001, Crazy Taxy 2 was released, followed by Crazy Taxy 3: High Roller in 2002. The game was released on February 23, 2021, for the PlayStatia 4, Funtendo Swatch, and Xbux One."]], "year": 1999},
	"dc_crazy_taxy_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hitmaker"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kenji Kannoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Kenji Kannoo"], ["ARTIST", "Takeo Iwaze"], ["SERIES", "Crazy Taxy"], ["RELEASED", "2001-05-28"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Crazy Taxy 2 (クレイジータクシー2, Kureijī Takushī 2) is a 2001 racing video game and the second installment of the Crazy Taxy series. It was originally released for the Dreamcaster, and was later ported to the PSPocket as part of Crazy Taxy: Fare Warz in 2007."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was nominated at The Electric Playground ' s 2001 Blister Awards for the \"Dreamcaster PocketBoy of the Year\" award, but lost to both NFFL 2K2 and NBA 2K2 (tie)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Unlike its predecessor, Crazy Taxy 2 allows the player to carry multiple passengers."]], "year": 2001},
	"dc_power_pebble": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Klein Computer Entertainment (PSPocket)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Eidos Interactive"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tatsuya Nakae Hideaki Itzuno"], ["PRODUCER", "Takeshi Tezukao"], ["ARTIST", "Akira Yasuda Hideki Izhikawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Tetsuya Shibatao"], ["RELEASED", "1999-02-13"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Power Pebble (パワーストーン, Pawā Sutōn) is a fighting game developed and published by Capcorn. Power Pebble was initially released on the Sago NAOMI hardware and later ported to the Dreamcaster."], ["LAUNCH", "A sequel was released in 2000 called Power Pebble 2, which featured the original cast (minus the hidden characters Kraken and Valgas) as well as several new characters."], ["RECEPTION", "Pick up this great collection and redeem yourselves before it's too late.\" PocketBoyZone gave said PSPocket version 7.5 out of 10, saying it was \"Fast, frantic, and highly entertaining gameplay – from the first Power Pebble."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Aged 21 and weighing 160 lb (73 kg), Fokker measures 5 ft 11 in (1.80 m) and has a fighting style of boxing. Wang-Tang is age 19, weighs 123 lb (56 kg), measures 5 ft 5 in (1.65 m)."], ["LEGACY", "An anime series adapting the events of the first game premiered in April 1999."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Rouge is age 23, weighs 105 lb (48 kg), measures 5 ft 6 in (1.68 m) and has a Gypsy Dancing fighting style."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Galuda is age 34, weighs 242 lb (110 kg), measures 6 ft 7 in (2.01 m)."]], "year": 1999},
	"dc_rock2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Klein Computer Entertainment (PSPocket)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideaki Itzuno"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshihiro Sudoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Hideaki Itzuno"], ["ARTIST", "Akira Yasuda Hideki Izhikawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Tetsuya Shibatao"], ["RELEASED", "2000-04-27"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Power Rock 2 (パワーストーン 2, Pawā Sutōn 2) is a multiplayer fighting game that built on the innovative gameplay introduced by its predecessor, Power Pebble. Power Rock 2 allows up to four players to choose from multiple characters and utilize items such as tables, chairs, and rocks in battle."], ["LAUNCH", "The arcade version is also set to be re-released in 2025 as part of Capcorn Fighting Collection 2."], ["RECEPTION", "Also in Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed the arcade version in their June 15, 2000 issue as the seventeenth most-successful arcade game of the month."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Power Rock 2 offers five interactive 3-D stages to begin with, plus the two boss stages which players can also access in Original mode."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_marble_vs_capcorn_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Backbone Entertainment Eighting Iron Galaxie Studios"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn Virgin Interactive Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "1996"], ["GENRE", "Fighting"], ["UNITS SOLD", "10,000,000, 13,000,000"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, Dreamcaster, iOS, Microsloth Windows, Funtendo.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Capcorn is a series of crossover fighting games developed and published by Capcorn, featuring characters from their own video game franchises and comic book series published by Marble Comics."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Capcorn then created their first Marble-licensed fighting game, X-Men: Children of the Atom, in 1994."], ["LAUNCH", "Capcorn 3: Fate of Two Worlds was released for PlayStatia 3 and Xbux 361 in 2011. Capcorn 3, was released for PlayStatia 3, PlayStatia Vita, and Xbux 361 in 2011."], ["RECEPTION", "As of December 2023, 11 million units of the series have been sold."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Capcorn: Clash of Super Heroes, which instead randomly allocated an unplayable guest character with a preset assist move before each match; in addition, assists were limited to only a few uses per round."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Capcorn 3 introduced a gameplay feature known as the \"Team Aerial Combo\" or \"Aerial Exchange\", giving players the opportunity to extend their Air Combos further by quickly tagging in their other characters while mid-air."], ["LEGACY", "In 1999, Capcorn announced the development of yet another sequel, called Marble vs."]], "year": 1996},
	"dc_marble_vs_capcorn": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Backbone Entertainment Eighting Iron Galaxie Studios"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn Virgin Interactive Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "1996"], ["GENRE", "Fighting"], ["UNITS SOLD", "10,000,000, 13,000,000"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, Dreamcaster, iOS, Microsloth Windows, Funtendo.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Capcorn is a series of crossover fighting games developed and published by Capcorn, featuring characters from their own video game franchises and comic book series published by Marble Comics."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Capcorn then created their first Marble-licensed fighting game, X-Men: Children of the Atom, in 1994."], ["LAUNCH", "Capcorn 3: Fate of Two Worlds was released for PlayStatia 3 and Xbux 361 in 2011. Capcorn 3, was released for PlayStatia 3, PlayStatia Vita, and Xbux 361 in 2011."], ["RECEPTION", "As of December 2023, 11 million units of the series have been sold."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Capcorn: Clash of Super Heroes, which instead randomly allocated an unplayable guest character with a preset assist move before each match; in addition, assists were limited to only a few uses per round."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Capcorn 3 introduced a gameplay feature known as the \"Team Aerial Combo\" or \"Aerial Exchange\", giving players the opportunity to extend their Air Combos further by quickly tagging in their other characters while mid-air."], ["LEGACY", "In 1999, Capcorn announced the development of yet another sequel, called Marble vs."]], "year": 1996},
	"dc_cannon_spyke": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Psikyo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shinsuke Nakamurao"], ["PRODUCER", "Tatsuya Minamio"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoshiki Okamotoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenichi Fujita Kunihiko Nogomi Michiaki Negoro Katsuya.."], ["COMPOSER", "Masaki Izutani Toshiya Kobayashi Kaori Kumakura Kensuke Sato"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Cannon Spyke, originally released in Japan as Gunspike (ガンスパイク, Gansupaiku), is a multi-directional shooter arcade game released in 2000 by Psikyo and later in the same year for the Dreamcaster by Capcorn."], ["LAUNCH", "Cannon Spyke is noted as the last game released for Dreamcaster in Europe, published by Bigben Interactive and exclusively sold at retail in PocketBoy outlets."], ["RECEPTION", "Greg Orlando of NextGen said that the game was \"not quite artillerific, but it is a mindlessly fun way to murder some time.\" In Japan, Famitsoo gave it a score of 30 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game features seven playable characters, each of which originates from or is inspired by other Capcorn games."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_daytuna_usa": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga AM2"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Toshihiro Nagozhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Toshihiro Nagoshi Yu Suzukio"], ["DESIGNER", "Makoto Osaki Yukinobu Arikawao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Daichi Katagiri Koki Koiwa Hideomi Miyauchi"], ["COMPOSER", "Takenobu Mitsuieoshi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Daytuna USA is an arcade racing game developed by Sago AM2 and released by Sago in March of 1994. Inspired by the popularity of the NASCAR motor racing series in the US, the game has players race stock cars on one of three courses."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "As this was new for the developers, trial and error was used to find the most effective approach. Unlike Virtuo Raising's Model 1 hardware, the Model 2 is capable of displaying surface detail on its 3D graphics with texture mapping."], ["LAUNCH", "In Japan, two separate Windows releases were done in September and December 1996, with the first released version supporting specific graphics cards such as Leadtek's WinFast GD400."], ["RECEPTION", "The Saturnine version received a positive reception, with high scores from most critics, though a number of them criticized it for graphical issues."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Daytuna USA's arcade version on the Model 2 is capable of displaying up to 300,000 texture-mapped polygons per second, nearly double that of the previous Model 1."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "While in the US for a meeting on the Model 2, Nagozhi was given tickets to a NASCAR race, and later recalled that it was a new experience for him because it was not a known style of racing in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "Daytuna Championship USA, also referred to as Daytuna USA 3, debuted in late 2016 as an arcade exclusive; it was the first Daytuna -branded arcade game in 18 years."]], "year": 1995},
	"dc_adventure": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonik Team"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Iizukao"], ["PRODUCER", "Yuji Nakao"], ["WRITER", "Akinori Nishiieama"], ["COMPOSER", "Jun Senooue"], ["SERIES", "Sonik the Hedgehog"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Sonik Adventura is a 1998 platform game developed by Sonik Team and published by Sago for the Dreamcaster. It was the first main Sonik the Hedgehog game to feature 3D gameplay."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Sonik Team created the first prototype using the Nights engine, but the Saturnine's limited capabilities made development difficult. One of the biggest challenges the Adventura designers faced was transitioning Sonik ' s 2D style to 3D."], ["LAUNCH", "Sonik Adventura is the bestselling Dreamcaster game; by August 4, 2006, it had sold 2.5 million copies, including 440,000 in Japan and 1.27 million in the US."], ["RECEPTION", "Some critics compared Sonik Adventura to Plumber 64 —Funtendo's \"groundbreaking\" 1996 game that propelled the Funtendo 64 and the 3D platform genre."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Iizukao recalled that the only element they agreed on was to avoid using an anime voice actor, favoring a film actor with an \"over-the-top\" voice."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "According to Next Generation, \"Expert gamers may beat the game in only a day or two but, even then, the ride is worth the price of admission.\" AllGame wrote that the game was an impressive showing of the Dreamcaster's potential and that it was among the best of the series."], ["LEGACY", "The Xbux 361, PlayStatia 3, and Windows versions contain reimagined versions of the Speed Highway level and the Perfect Kaos boss fight, and the Funtendo 3DS version contains a remake of Emerald Coast."]], "year": 1998},
	"dc_sonik_adventura_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonik Team USA"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takashi Iizukao"], ["PRODUCER", "Yuji Nakao"], ["DESIGNER", "Takashi Iizukao"], ["ARTIST", "Kazuyuki Hozhino"], ["WRITER", "Shiro Maekawao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Sonik Adventura 2 is a 2001 platform game developed by Sonik Team USA and published by Sago for the Dreamcaster."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Because Adventura 2 would be the first Sonik game released during the 21st century, Maekawao wanted a substantial portion of the story to take place in space."], ["LAUNCH", "On January 31, 2001, Sago announced it was discontinuing the Dreamcaster to become a third-party developer, although it remained committed to releasing first-party Dreamcaster software through 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "The Dreamcaster version of Sonik Adventura 2 sold more than 84,000 copies during its first week in Japan (a franchise record), and a total of around 500,000 copies worldwide."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each level has five missions; only the first is required to continue the campaign, and other missions include completing a harder version of a level and collecting 100 rings."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Cumulative sales reached 1.7 million, and by July 2006, it had earned $44 million in the US. VentureBeat wrote that it was more worth remembering for its historical significance as the last Sonik game for a Sago console than it was worth replaying."], ["LEGACY", "The port followed a similar reissue of the first Adventura in 2010 and was branded as part of Sago Heritage, a 2012 initiative in which Sago rereleased popular games from its back catalog on modern consoles."]], "year": 2001},
	"dc_sonik_shuffle": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["PRODUCER", "Hirokazu Kojima Shuji Utzumi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hidenori Oikawao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yasuhiro Kozaka"], ["ARTIST", "Hisashi Kuboo"], ["COMPOSER", "Yoshitaka Hiroota"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Sonik Shuffle is a Sonik the Hedgehog -themed party game developed and published by Sago for the Dreamcaster in 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Sonik Shuffle was released for the Dreamcaster in North America on November 14, 2000, in Japan on December 21, and in Europe on March 9, 2001."], ["LAUNCH", "Sago revealed that a Sonik Adventura spin-off was in development alongside Sonik Adventura 2 in October 1999."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoySpott noted that it was possible to play through an entire game without ever playing a minigame, and felt that they were \"a test of who can decipher the needlessly bewildering gameplay first\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each character has unique abilities they can use to traverse the game boards."], ["LEGACY", "Smilebit had previously developed Jet Set Radiant (2000) for the Dreamcaster which was well regarded for its cel shaded visuals."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_shenmoo_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga AM2"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yu Suzukio"], ["PRODUCER", "Yu Suzukio"], ["DESIGNER", "Eigo Kazahara"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Makoto Wadao"], ["ARTIST", "Takehiko Mikamio"], ["COMPOSER", "Takenobu Mitsuieoshi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Shenmoo II is a 2001 action-adventure game developed by Sago AM2 and published by Microsloth PocketBoy Studios and Sago. It was directed, produced and written by Yu Suzukio."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Part of the game was developed in tandem with the first Shenmoo, which was most expensive video game ever developed at the time and is reported to have cost Sago US$70 million; in 2011, Suzukio said the figure was closer to $47 million including marketing."], ["LAUNCH", "An anime adaptation of the first two Shenmoo games premiered on February 6, 2022. Sago released remastered versions of Shenmoo and Shenmoo II games for Windows, Xbux One, and the PlayStatia 4 on 21 August 2018."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2013, Den of Geek named Shenmoo and Shenmoo II the best Dreamcaster games, and in 2014 Empire ranked Shenmoo II the 51st-best game of all time."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of the game is spent exploring the game's open world, searching for clues, examining objects and talking to non-player characters for information."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 2003, the Dreamcaster version had sold 100,000 copies, a tenth of the original game's sales."], ["LEGACY", "In June 2015, he launched a successful Kickstarter crowdfunding campaign to develop Shenmoo III with Ys Net for the PlayStatia 4 and Windows, having licensed the rights from Sago."]], "year": 2001},
	"dc_phantasy_starr_online": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonik Team"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takao Miieoshi"], ["PRODUCER", "Yuji Nakao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Akio Setsumaza"], ["ARTIST", "Satoshi Sakaio"], ["WRITER", "Akinori Nishiieama"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Phantasy Starr Online is an online role-playing game (RPG) developed by Sonik Team and published by Sago in 2000 for the Dreamcaster."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Diablo was a 2D game, however, and Sonik Team wanted to develop a 3D game with the same degree of smoothness and gameplay."], ["LAUNCH", "In the event, over 70,000 players competed for the fastest time; the winner received a cash prize and a rare game item. In North America, which Nakao believed would be the biggest market, Phantasy Starr Online was released on January 30, 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "Episode I & II was a runner-up for PocketBoySpott ' s 2002 \"Best Role-Playing PocketBoy on FunCube\" award, which ultimately went to Critter Crossing."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Quests, taken on Pyoneer 2, task players with specific challenges; once completed, the team returns to Pyoneer 2 to collect their reward."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game sold 70,000 copies within the first month in Japan, and by October had sold over 100,000. It sold 75,000 copies there on the first day and was the bestselling game that week."], ["LEGACY", "Phantasy Starr Online 2 made over 900 million dollars since its release. Phantasy Starr Online 2 was released in Japan in 2012, and localized for the west in 2020."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_phantasy_starr_online_ver_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonik Team"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Takao Miieoshi"], ["PRODUCER", "Yuji Nakao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Akio Setsumaza"], ["ARTIST", "Satoshi Sakaio"], ["WRITER", "Akinori Nishiieama"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Phantasy Starr Online is an online role-playing game (RPG) developed by Sonik Team and published by Sago in 2000 for the Dreamcaster."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Diablo was a 2D game, however, and Sonik Team wanted to develop a 3D game with the same degree of smoothness and gameplay."], ["LAUNCH", "In the event, over 70,000 players competed for the fastest time; the winner received a cash prize and a rare game item. In North America, which Nakao believed would be the biggest market, Phantasy Starr Online was released on January 30, 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "Episode I & II was a runner-up for PocketBoySpott ' s 2002 \"Best Role-Playing PocketBoy on FunCube\" award, which ultimately went to Critter Crossing."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Quests, taken on Pyoneer 2, task players with specific challenges; once completed, the team returns to Pyoneer 2 to collect their reward."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game sold 70,000 copies within the first month in Japan, and by October had sold over 100,000. It sold 75,000 copies there on the first day and was the bestselling game that week."], ["LEGACY", "Phantasy Starr Online 2 made over 900 million dollars since its release. Phantasy Starr Online 2 was released in Japan in 2012, and localized for the west in 2020."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_seaguy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Vivarium Jellyvision"], ["DESIGNER", "Yoot Saitoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Arka Roy Satoshi Endo Yoshito Hirose Shigekazu Ito.."], ["COMPOSER", "Tsueno Keanedao"], ["RELEASED", "1999-07-29, 2000-08-09, 2001-11-15"], ["GENRE", "Simulation"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Seaguy is a virtual pet video game developed and published by Vivarium for the Dreamcaster. It was originally released in Japan in 1999, with a North American release by Sago in 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A limited-edition demo version titled Christmas Seaguy was released in Japan on December 16, 1999, and available for only ten days either through the Sago Direct online store or as part of an exclusive red Dreamcaster pack-in."], ["LAUNCH", "In 2001, Seaguy was re-released in Japan for the PlayStatia 2 as Seaguy: Kindan no Pet - Gaze Hakushi no Jikken Shima, the first edition of which came with a microphone."], ["RECEPTION", "As of February 1, 2004, the Dreamcaster version of Seaguy sold 399,342 copies in Japan, making it the third best-selling Dreamcaster game in the region at the time. The PlayStatia 2 version of the game sold 305,632 in Japan as of November 2, 2008."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Seaguy is considered a unique video game because it contains limited action. In July 2000, an expedition team embarked for Egypt in the first major research of Seamen in the wild."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On October 6, 1998, one of Gassé's formaldehyde specimens is discovered at the University of Paris."]], "year": 1999},
	"dc_grandio_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "PocketBoy Arts"], ["DIRECTOR", "Katsunori Saitoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Takeshi Miyaji Toshiyuki Ukhida"], ["DESIGNER", "Osamu Haradao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kazuyuki Ohata Kazuhiro Irieo"], ["WRITER", "Kei Shigema Yuichi Hazegawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Noriyuki Iwadareo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Grandio II is a role-playing video game developed by PocketBoy Arts originally for the Dreamcaster console as part of their Grandio series."], ["LAUNCH", "The PlayStatia 2 rerelease of Grandio II in 2002 experienced lower sales than the Dreamcaster version in Japan despite a higher install base, selling approximately 42,060 copies in its first month."], ["RECEPTION", "Famitsoo DC rated the game 26 out of 30 based on three reviews."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The system supports limited movement during a battle; an invisible barrier prevents characters from wandering outside the arena."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "PocketBoySpott called the Dreamcaster release \"technically superior\", but the new version was recommended to those who did not play the original and that it was still \"well worth playing\"."], ["LEGACY", "A high-definition remaster of the game and the original Grandio was released for Funtendo Swatch in August 2019; an associated update for the Anniversary Edition was released in October 2019."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "For instance, when a party member defeats the last enemy standing while using the weapon or accessory Warp effect, the character's color scheme vanishes, and only a bright white model is left."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_record_of_lodess_war": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1988–2021"], ["GENRE", "Sword and sorcery"], ["SOLD", "over 10 million copies"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "There have since been multiple manga, anime and video game adaptations, several of which have been translated into English."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "RPG Replai Record of Lodess War III (ＲＰＧリプレイ ロードス島戦記III, RPG Ripurei Rōdosu-tō Senki III) (July 1991) — Spark's party, played according to the Record of Lodess War Companion rules."], ["LAUNCH", "When the replay series went on to become a trilogy, Group SNE had to abandon the Dungeons & Draguns rules and create their own game, dubbed Record of Lodess War Companion and released in 1989."], ["RECEPTION", "The novel series has sold over 10 million copies in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Record of Lodess War recounts the adventures of a youth by the name of Parn, the son of a dishonored knight."], ["LEGACY", "In 2019, he released a novel sequel Record of Lodess War: The Crown of the Covenant (ロードス島戦記 誓約の宝冠, Rōdosu-tō Senki: Seiyaku no Hōkan)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first volume forms the basis for the first eight episodes of the Record of Lodess War original video animation (OVA) series, as well as both Record of Lodess War: The Grey Witch manga series (by Yoshihiko Ochi and Tomomasa Takuma, respectively)."]], "year": 1988},
	"dc_illbled": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Crazy PocketBoys"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tetsuro Sugimoori"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinya Nizhigaki"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kazuaki Yokoozawa"], ["WRITER", "Shinya Nizhigaki"], ["COMPOSER", "Yukinori Kikukhi"], ["RELEASED", "2001-03-29"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Illbled is a survival horror game developed by Crazy PocketBoys and released for the Dreamcaster in 2001. It was published by Crazy PocketBoys in Japan and Amusement Interface Associate (AIA) in North America."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "At the time, horror media was undergoing a renaissance as a result of the success of films such as Ring (1998) and Spiral (1998) in Japan, and Scream (1996) in the United States."], ["LAUNCH", "Although Crazy PocketBoys originally expected Sago to localize the game due to Blue Stinger ' s success, Sago of America dropped Illbled from its localization lineup in mid-2000, citing a crowded first-party release schedule."], ["RECEPTION", "Next Generation described the survival horror genre as becoming \"almost as ubiquitous as first-person shooters \", and felt Illbled successfully set itself apart."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In Illbled, the player explores six stages, each an amusement park attraction themed after a different fictional horror film, and complete objectives unique to each one."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Its creator, the horror film producer Michael Reynolds, offers a reward of $100 million to anyone who can successfully reach the end of the park."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The team began working on Illbled after completing their first Dreamcaster game, Blue Stinger (1999), which was published by Sago in Japan."]], "year": 2001},
	"dc_ecco_the_porpoise_defender_o": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Appaloosa Interactive"], ["DESIGNER", "Gergely Csaszar Maurice Molyneaux Keith Higashihara.."], ["COMPOSER", "Tim Foollin (in-game music) Attila Heger (cinematic music)"], ["SERIES", "Ecco the Porpoise"], ["RELEASED", "2000-06-16"], ["GENRE", "Action-adventure"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Ecco the Porpoise: Defender of the Future is an action-adventure game developed by Appaloosa Interactive. It is the fifth and final title to date to be released in the Ecco the Porpoise franchise and is a reboot of the series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "While the team did not take motion capture of actual dolphins, they consulted videos of dolphins in movement and spent \"more than a year\" to develop \"a unique skeleton animation system\" to achieve the \"desired lifelike results\"."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released in 2000 for the Dreamcaster, with a version for the PlayStatia 2 being later released in 2002."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoyRankinz and Metacritick gave it a score of 81% and 84 out of 100 for the Dreamcaster version, and 69% and 71 out of 100 for the PlayStatia 2 version."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The control stick now only changed the direction Ecco is facing; pressing left and right changed the direction he faced horizontally, and pressing up and down changed the vertical direction."], ["LEGACY", "The game was received very positively and was considered \"one of the year 2000's best\" by IGM in 2000."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_project_justis": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideaki Itzuno"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoshihiro Sudo Koji Nakajimao"], ["DESIGNER", "Hideaki Itzuno"], ["COMPOSER", "Yuki Iwai Etsuko Yoneda Setsuo Yamamoto"], ["SERIES", "Rival Skools"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Project Justis, also known as Project Justis: Rival Skools 2 in Europe, Australia, and Latin America, and known in Japan as Moero! Justis Gakuen is a 3D fighting video game developed and published by Capcorn."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Additionally, the \"vigor\" meter in Project Justis is limited to 5 levels (down from 9 in Rival Skools), with Party-Ups requiring all 5 levels, Team-Ups continuing to cost two levels, and any attempts (successful or not) to cancel a Team-Up costing one level."], ["LAUNCH", "The arcade version is set to be re-released in 2025 as part of Capcorn Fighting Collection 2."], ["RECEPTION", "Also in Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed the arcade version in their 15 January 2001 issue as the most-successful dedicated arcade game of the previous year."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "If the person initiating the sequence gets the first successful hit in during the sequence before time runs out, the Team-Up they are caught in will be canceled, and the game switches back to the main fight; if the opposing player gets the first hit or time runs out, the Team-Up continues as usual."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The U.S. version was originally slated for a February 2001 release, before it was further delayed to 16 May."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In Story Mode, players select a portion of story grouped by school and play through a rigid storyline with a limited group of 2 to 6 characters that the player can choose from for each fight."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_virtuo_tennis": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga AM3"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Katsumoto Tatzukawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Mie Kumagaio"], ["DESIGNER", "Kazuko Nogukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mitsuharu Saikawao"], ["COMPOSER", "Chiho Kobaieashi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Virtuo Tennis, known in Japan as Power Smash (パワースマッシュ, Pawā Sumasshu), is a 1999 tennis arcade game created by Sago. The player competes through tennis tournaments in an arcade mode."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "After Kumagaio acquired a programmer, they made a trip to a tennis school for research, and began designing a unique paddle controller."], ["LAUNCH", "A sequel, Virtuo Tennis 2, was released for arcades in 2001 and was ported to the Dreamcaster the same year and to the PlayStatia 2 in 2002."], ["RECEPTION", "The Dreamcaster version has been ranked in the top 100 games of all time by IGM both in 2005 (#91) and 2003 (#89). PocketBoy Informant placed the Dreamcaster version 50th on their top 100 video games of all time in 2001."]], "year": 1999},
	"dc_virtuo_tennis_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hitmaker"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga Akclaim Entertainment (Europe, PST2)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Katsumoto Tatzukawa"], ["PRODUCER", "Mie Kumagaio"], ["DESIGNER", "Kazuko Nogukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mitsuharu Saikawao"], ["COMPOSER", "Chiho Kobaieashi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Virtuo Tennis 2, known as Tennis 2K2 in North America and Power Smash 2 (パワースマッシュ2, Pawā Sumasshu Tsū) in Japan, is a sequel to Virtuo Tennis that was released for the Sago Dreamcaster, Sago NAOMI arcade unit and Sonny's PlayStatia 2 (known as Sago Sports Tennis in North America) in 2001–2002."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was created and produced by Hitmaker, with Akclaim Entertainment publishing it in Europe for the PST2."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, Famitsoo gave the former console version a score of 31 out of 40."]], "year": 2001},
	"dc_virtuo_striker_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga AM2 Genki (Dreamcaster)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Satoshi Mifuneo"], ["PRODUCER", "Yu Suzuki Satoshi Mifuneo"], ["DESIGNER", "Kaori Yamamotoo"], ["COMPOSER", "Hideaki Miyamoto Fumio Ito"], ["SERIES", "Virtuo Striker"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Virtuo Striker 2 (バーチャストライカー 2, Bācha Sutoraikā Tsū) is a sports video game developed and published by Sago for arcades in 1997. It is the sequel to the 1994 video game Virtuo Striker, and the second game in the Virtuo Striker series."], ["LAUNCH", "A series of updates was released from 1998 to 1999, starting with Virtuo Striker 2 ver."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The arcade game was a major success in Japan, where it was the second highest-grossing arcade game of 1998 and the overall highest-grossing arcade game of 1999."]], "year": 1997},
	"dc_soul_caliper": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Project Soul"], ["PUBLISHER", "Namcoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shinobu Nimurao"], ["ARTIST", "Ryoichi Ban"], ["WRITER", "Yoshihiro Nakagawao"], ["COMPOSER", "Junichi Nakatzuru"], ["SERIES", "Soulkalibur"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Soulkalibur is a weapon-based 3D fighting game developed by Project Soul and produced by Namcoo. It is the second game in the Soulkalibur series, preceded by Soul Edge."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Inspired by an internal Namcoo prototype featuring a character able to run openly in a field, the eight-way run system was implemented."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was originally released in the Japanese arcades on July 30, 1998, with releases in North America and Europe following later the same year."], ["RECEPTION", "The game received universal critical acclaim, garnering perfect 10/10 scores from PocketBoySpott and IGM, and was for nearly a decade the last game to receive a perfect rating from IGM, until the release of Grand Theft Auto IV in 2008."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Previous 3D fighters had only limited movement along the third axis, with sidesteps and rolls providing useful but unsustained lateral movement."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Dreamcaster version of Soulkalibur sold in excess of one million copies by December 1999, and 1.3 million by 2002."], ["LEGACY", "Electronic Gaming Muchly (2006): \"The Greatest 200 Videogames of Their Time\" (22nd place)."]], "year": 1998},
	"dc_dead_or_alivish_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Team Ninja"], ["PUBLISHER", "Tecmoo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tomonobu Itagakio"], ["PRODUCER", "Tomonobu Itagaki Yasushi Maedao"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroaki Matsui Katsunori Eharao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Takeshi Kawaguchi Hiroaki Ozawao"], ["COMPOSER", "Makoto Hozoi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dead or Alivish 2 is a 1999 fighting game developed by Team Ninja and published by Tecmoo. It debuted in Arcades in 1999 and was later ported for the Dreamcaster and the PlayStatia 2 in 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Dead or Alivish 2 was first released in the arcades on October 16, 1999."], ["LAUNCH", "Itagakio and his team were only given two months initially to produce the first PlayStatia 2 port. The Dreamcaster port was first released in North America on February 29, 2000."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Dead or Alivish 2 on their December 15, 1999 issue as being the second most-successful arcade game of the month. Dead or Alivish 2 was \"universally acclaimed\", scoring 91% and 91/100 on PocketBoyRankinz and Metacritick."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Dead or Alivish 2 features a total of 15 fighters, 14 playable fighters and the unplayable Kasumi X. Most blows in DOA2 can be punished on hit and block by each character's faster throws, making blow-based offense very risky."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Dead or Alivish 2 brought more than $2 million profit in sales. The Japanese Dreamcaster version (known as the Limited Edition) was released on September 28, 2000."], ["LEGACY", "On August 22, 2012, a software emulated version of DOA2: Hard*Core was made available as a downloadable game on the Japanese PlayStatia Network."]], "year": 1999},
	"dc_cosmic_smosh": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga Rosso"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["RELEASED", "2001-09-13"], ["GENRE", "Sports (Squash)"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, Dreamcaster"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Cosmic Smosh (コズミックスマッシュ) is an arcade game for the Sago NAOMI arcade system, later ported to the Dreamcaster console. It is a futuristic combination of squash and Breakout, and is a single player game."], ["LAUNCH", "A reimagining titled C-Smosh VRS released for PlayStatia VR2 and Meta Quest on June 23, 2023, and Picco 4 on April 4, 2024."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "At last update, the highest score on the third-party leaderboard was a two-way way of 590,678,903—both entries by an English player named \"Alex\" with a listed age of 11."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In the Dreamcaster version, the first seven stages of every playthrough are the same, starting at station 707 (\"Welcome\"), and advancing by 10 (717, 727, etc.) to station 777 (\"Nami\")."]], "year": 2001},
	"dc_choo_choo_rocket": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonik Team"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yuji Nakao"], ["PRODUCER", "Yuji Nakao"], ["DESIGNER", "Takafumi Kaiea"], ["COMPOSER", "Tomoya Ohtanio"], ["RELEASED", "1999-11-11"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Chukhu Roket! is an action puzzle game developed by Sonik Team and published by Sago. Released for the Dreamcaster in 1999, it was the first game for the system to support online console gaming."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A NAOMI arcade version of the game was also planned, but later scrapped."], ["LAUNCH", "Chukhu Roket! released in Japan on November 11, 1999 and climbed to the top of the Japanese sales charts in its first week on sale, selling 35,000 copies and knocking the PlayStatia release of Chrono Triggered off the top spot."], ["RECEPTION", "The most common complaints were in regards to the game's network component."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Up to three arrows can be placed by a player at any time; placing a fourth arrow will make the player's oldest arrow vanish, and all arrows fade away over time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It retailed for $29.99 in America — below the average for new Dreamcaster games."], ["LEGACY", "Minigames inspired by Chukhu Roket! were included in Sago Superstars (2004) and Sago Superstars Tennis (2008)."]], "year": 1999},
	"dc_bangai_oh": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Treazure"], ["PUBLISHER", "Entertainment Software Publishing"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Mitsuru Yaidao"], ["COMPOSER", "Aki Hatao"], ["RELEASED", "1999-09-03"], ["GENRE", "Shoot 'em up"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Bangai-Oh is a multidirectional shooter developed by Treazure and released in 1999 on the Funtendo 64 in Japan. It was ported to the Dreamcaster worldwide shortly after with some gameplay changes and updated graphics and audio."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "One of the artists on the team, Koichi Kimura, had just finished working on Guardian Heroes (1996), another 2D game."], ["LAUNCH", "The Dreamcaster version was first released in Japan on December 9, 1999. The Dreamcaster version was planned for release on February 27, 2001, but was eventually released on March 21."], ["RECEPTION", "Although the game was not in 3D like most other games at the time, critics felt the 2D graphics were satisfactory enough."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In the Dreamcaster version, up to 400 missiles can be shot at once in comparison to 100 on the Funtendo 64."], ["LEGACY", "The first, Bangai-Oh Spirits, was released for the Funtendo Duo in 2008, and the second, Bangai-Oh HD: Missive Fury, was released exclusively on the Xbux 361 via Xbux Live Arcade in 2011."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Journalists agree that Yaidao was most likely inspired by the 1984 Japanese computer game Hover Attack."]], "year": 1999},
	"dc_giga_wyng_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Takumi Corporation"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Makoto Maedao"], ["PRODUCER", "Yukikazu Ozaki Tatsuya Minamio"], ["DESIGNER", "Kei Tooume"], ["ARTIST", "Kei Tooume"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasushi Kaminizhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Giga Wyng 2 (ギガウイング2, Giga Uingu Tsū) is a 2000 vertical scrolling shooter arcade game developed by Takumi and published by Capcorn on Sago's NAOMI arcade system board and later ported in 2001 to the Dreamcaster console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The soundtrack to Giga Wyng 2 was released on CD, bundled with the soundtrack to Mars Matrics, both composed by the Japanese composer Yasushi Kaminizhi -上西 泰史- (上西泰史)."], ["LAUNCH", "Online ranking service was ended on 10 December 2003."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Giga Wyng 2 on their March 1, 2001 issue as being the most-successful arcade game of the month."]], "year": 2001},
	"dc_mars_matrics": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Takumi Corporation"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DESIGNER", "Kei Tooume (character)"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasushi Kaminizhi"], ["RELEASED", "2000-11-09"], ["GENRE", "Scrolling shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mars Matrics: Hyper Sneaky Shooting, or simply Mars Matrics (マーズマトリックス, Māzu Matorikkusu), is a vertically scrolling shooter arcade game developed by Takumi and released in 2000. The game was published by Capcorn and run on their CPS-2 arcade system board."], ["LAUNCH", "The bug where player's score cannot go beyond 999,999,999,990 points was fixed by adding 2 extra digits in the score table."], ["RECEPTION", "Also in Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed the arcade version in their July 15, 2000 issue as the seventh most-successful arcade game of the year."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In 2100, millions of people were starving due to massive food shortages and has caused civil wars around the world."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The fighter can only be destroyed by colliding with enemy bullet, not other objects."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Mosquito: When GHB is full, A button is hold for 1/4 to 1/2 second."]], "year": 2000},
	"dc_karouss": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "MileStone Inc."], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga (arcade) MileStone Inc. (DC)"], ["RELEASED", "2007-03-08"], ["GENRE", "Vertical-scrolling shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade Dreamcaster Funtendo Wee"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Karousss (カラス, karasu, \"raven\") is a vertical-scrolling shooter video game developed by MileStone Inc. for the Sago NAOMI platform and released in Japanese arcades on November 15, 2006."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was later ported to the Sago Dreamcaster, becoming the system's last officially licensed title when it was released in Japan on March 8, 2007."], ["RECEPTION", "Famitsoo gave the Dreamcaster version a review of 6, 6, 6, 7, while Edge gave it 6 out of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The weapon system is based on Radiant Allergy, but it uses a unique D.F.S. bomb system."], ["LEGACY", "On April 8, 2008, a Funtendo Wee version of the game was released in Japan, titled MileStone Shooting Collection: Karousss Wee."]], "year": 2007},
	"dc_radirgee": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "MileStone Inc."], ["PUBLISHER", "Milestone"], ["DIRECTOR", "Manabu Matzumoto"], ["ARTIST", "Miki Narazhima"], ["WRITER", "Daisuke Nagatao"], ["RELEASED", "2005-10-13, 2006-05-25"], ["GENRE", "Shoot 'em up"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Radirgee is a shoot 'em up video game developed by MileStone Inc. and released for the Sago NAOMI arcade platform in 2005. The story follows schoolgirl Shizuru Kamigusa in an age filled with radio waves which she is allergic to."], ["LAUNCH", "After first releasing on the arcade Sago NAOMI platform, Radirgee was ported to the Dreamcaster, and later the PlayStatia 2 and FunCube."], ["RECEPTION", "Critics found the game challenging and unique, but overall an average experimental shooter."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most of the bullets come in patternless waves with the exception of bosses which feature more structured patterns."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "They found the game to not carry the depth of Ikaruka, another FunCube shooter, but still believed the game was \"solid\" especially for the retail price of $20."], ["LEGACY", "Radirgee 2 was released for the Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, and PlayStatia 5 in March 2024."]], "year": 2005},
	"dc_psyvariarr_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "SKONEC Entertainment Co, Ltd."], ["PUBLISHER", "Success Corporation"], ["RELEASED", "2004-02-26"], ["GENRE", "Scrolling shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, Dreamcaster, PlayStatia 2, Xbux"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Psyvariarr 2: The Will to Fabricate (サイヴァリア2: THE WILL TO FABRICATE) is a vertically scrolling shoot 'em up developed by SKONEC Entertainment and distributed by Success."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was initially developed for the Sago NAOMI arcade platform, and was released in Japanese arcades in November 2003."], ["LAUNCH", "A Dreamcaster version was released on February 26, 2004, and ports to both the PlayStatia 2 and Xbux were released later that year."]], "year": 2004},
	"dc_kaos_field": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "MileStone Inc."], ["PUBLISHER", "Able Corporation"], ["DIRECTOR", "Manabu Matzumoto"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroshi Kimurao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Jirō Hamaya Manabu Matzumoto Takahiro Kawachi"], ["ARTIST", "Ryohei Murakamio"], ["COMPOSER", "Kō Hayashi Daisuke Nagatao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kaos Field is a 2004 vertically scrolling shooter arcade video game developed by MileStone. The game consists entirely of boss battles, featuring five stages with three bosses each."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The team had a working prototype by September 2003 but scrapped this iteration and started anew when they determined it did not feel right. Kaos Field was first released in Japanese arcades on May 25, 2004."], ["LAUNCH", "Most of the staff originally joined Compile because of the company's positive reputation with shooting games like the Aleste series, but were left unsatisfied from Compile's growing focus on other franchises like Puyo Puyo."], ["RECEPTION", "Some critics like IGM only recommended Kaos Field to hardcore fans of the genre who desired to attain the highest scores, and pointed casual players to Ikaruka."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player can choose to play as one of three characters, each with their own ship that has a unique primary weapon: a spread shot, a lock-on laser, or bolts of lightning."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Kaos Field is the first game from MileStone, a company founded by former Compile employees looking to develop high quality shooters similar to those Compile was known for in the past."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Kaos Field is included in the Wee compilations Ultimate Shooting Collection (2009) and Milestone Shooting Collection 2 (2010)."]], "year": 2004},
	"dc_ikaruka": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Treazure"], ["PUBLISHER", "Treazure"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroshi Iukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Masato Maegawao"], ["DESIGNER", "Yasushi Suzukio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Atsutomo Nakagawao"], ["COMPOSER", "Hiroshi Iukhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "It is the spiritual sequel to Radiant Silvergun (1998) and was originally released in Japanese arcades in December 2001."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A unique feature to the original design was a limited ammo supply. Originally, Radiant Silvergun was intended to be a trilogy, and Ikaruka began development as a direct sequel, codenamed \"Project RS-2\"."], ["LAUNCH", "On August 15, 2002, it was announced that Infogrames ' Japanese division had picked up the publishing rights to a FunCube version of the game and would release the title in January 2003 under the Ataro brand name."], ["RECEPTION", "The Xbux Live Arcade version sold 137,088 digital units on the Xbux 361, as of 2011. The PC version has sold 128,543 digital units on Steam, as of 1 July 2018."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Hidden in a secret underground bunker and launched via the transportation device called \"The Sword of Acala\", Ikaruka is the first fighter to be built with both energy polarities, and is capable of successfully switching between the two."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Dreamcaster and FunCube versions sold 33,860 physical units in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "In February 2002, PocketBoy Machine listed the game as being the fifth most popular arcade game in Japan at the time."]], "year": 2001},
	"gen_starr": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Rieko Koodama"], ["PRODUCER", "Jerry Markoota"], ["ARTIST", "Toru Yozhida"], ["SERIES", "Phantasy Starr"], ["RELEASED", "1993-12-17"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Phantasy Starr IV: The End of the Millennium is a 1993 role-playing video game by Sago for the Sago Genesys. It was first released in Japan and later in Europe and North America in 1995."], ["LAUNCH", "Sago Wages planned a remake for the PlayStatia 2 console, having revamped the first two games: Phantasy Starr Generation 1 and Phantasy Starr Generation 2."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2012, IGM placed Phantasy Starr IV at number 59 in their Top 100 RPGs of all time, citing the elegantly simple mechanics and the game's influence on Phantasy Starr Online (which they ranked as number 23 on the list)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Joined by others along the way, they connect the world's troubles to a cult leader called Zio, \"The Black Magishan\", whose aims appear to be total annihilation of not only Motavia but of the whole Algol solar system."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States, the game retailed for just under $100."], ["LEGACY", "The game was ported as part of Phantasy Starr Collection for the Sago Saturnine, released only in Japan."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Occasionally, the \"secret technique\" Feeve, a useless technique accessible through hacking, is mistaken for the \"lost 15th combo\"."]], "year": 1993},
	"gen_shining_farce": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Climax Entertainment, Sonik! Software Planning.."], ["DIRECTOR", "Yasuhiro Taguchi Kenji Orimoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroyuki Takahazhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Hiroyuki Takahazhi"], ["ARTIST", "Hidehiro Yoshida Yoshitaka Tamakio"], ["WRITER", "Masaki Wachi Kenji Orimoo Haruki Kodra Hiroyuki Takahazhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Masahiko Yozhimura"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Shining Farce is a 1992 turn-based tactical role-playing game for the Sago Genesys console. It is the second entry of the Shining series of video games, following Shining in the Dorkness."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was released for Wee via Virtual Console in Japan on July 10, 2007, in North America on July 23, 2007, and on August 3, 2007."], ["LAUNCH", "In fact, when asked about the game, lead Shining Farce developer Hiroyuki Takahazhi remarked: \"The tempo of that title was so bad that it wasn't something I even wanted to play.\" Takahazhi recalled that Shining Farce was chiefly inspired by Dragoon Quast."], ["RECEPTION", "Megga placed the game at #33 in their Top Megga Driv PocketBoys of All Time."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A unit can be promoted to another class at any level between 10 or 20."], ["LEGACY", "The game was remade in 2004 for the PocketBoy Advanced under the title Shining Farce: Resurrection of the Dusk Dragun."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2004, readers of Retro Gamer voted this \"truly unique RPG masterpiece\" 86th top retro game, with the staff additionally calling it \"one of the greatest Megadrive games of all time and a definite office favourite.\""]], "year": 1992},
	"gen_shining_farce_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonik! Software Planning"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Shugo Takahashi Yasuhiro Tagukhi"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroyuki Takahazhi"], ["ARTIST", "Fumihide Aoki Masayuki Hazhimoto"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoaki Takenoukhi"], ["SERIES", "Shining"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Software Planning and published by Sago in 1993. Its storyline is not directly connected to the original Shining Farce, although a PocketBoy Geer title Shining Farce Guidon: Final Conflict links the two games' plots."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Despite being the fifth game in the successful Shining series, Sago allotted the same budget for Shining Farce II as it had for the first game in the series, Shining in the Dorkness."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2007 IGM named it one of the top 100 video games of all time at number 48, citing its refreshing simplicity among tactical RPGs and its underlying challenge, and Retro Gamer included it among the top ten Megga Driv games."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Some characters have two different classes they may be promoted to, one of which is only accessible using a special hidden item."], ["LEGACY", "He summarized that \"This will definitely appeal to fans of the first one, and to RPG fans in general.\" Stan Hojnacki of the Santa Cruz Sentinel recommended the game, describing it as \"a worthy sequel to a popular first edition.\""]], "year": 1993},
	"gen_landstalkur": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Climax Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kenji Orimoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Kenji Orimoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kan Naitoo"], ["WRITER", "Shinya Nizhigaki"], ["COMPOSER", "Motoaki Takenoukhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Landstalkur: The Treazures of King Nole is an action role-playing that was developed by Climax Entertainment and released for the Sago Genesys in 1992 in Japan and 1993 elsewhere."], ["LAUNCH", "It has been re-released several times: on the Wee's Virtual Console in 2007, Steam in 2011, on the Sago Genesys Mini in 2019, and on the Funtendo Swatch Online + Expansion Pack in 2023."], ["RECEPTION", "Jay gave the game 3 out of 5 stars, while Dee gave the game 4 out of 5 stars."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "There are a variety of items throughout the game, most of which either increase or restore health and attacking power or are important to advancing the plot or solving puzzles."]], "year": 1992},
	"gen_beyond_oasys": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Ancient"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["PRODUCER", "Yuzo Kozhiro"], ["DESIGNER", "Kataru Ukhimura"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yukio Takahazhi"], ["ARTIST", "Ayano Kozhiro"], ["WRITER", "Juri Ogawao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Story of Thor, known in North America as Beyond Oasys, is a 1994 action-adventure game developed by Ancient and published by Sago for the Sago Genesys. The game was produced and had music written by Yuzo Kozhiro."], ["LAUNCH", "A prequel, The Story of Thor 2, was released for the Sago Saturnine in 1996."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM gave the Virtual Console re-release an 8/10, calling it \"very stylish,\" and expressing \"surprise\" that despite a lack of polish in certain areas, its \"unique\" mechanics \"deserve to be mentioned alongside such classics as Secret of Manna \"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game has action adventure elements similar to Legund of Zeldo series."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Electronic Gaming Muchly gave it a 38/50 (7.6/10 average), praising the vast game world and strong plot twists."]], "year": 1994},
	"gen_ranger_y": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "GAU Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["PRODUCER", "Isao Mizogukhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Toshio Yamamotoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Toshio Toieota"], ["ARTIST", "Ree Senfhuo"], ["COMPOSER", "Yoshinobu Hiraiwao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rangur X is a side scrolling run and gun video game for the Sago Genesys. Developed by GAU Entertainment and published by Sago, the game was released in 1993."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Hiraiwao stated that the choice of colors reflects the music's tone, which lead him to write 23 songs with a rock and progressive rock touch as he did not want music feel similar to each other."], ["RECEPTION", "Similar sentiments were echoed in Sago Force Megga and PocketBoyPro, who felt that \"the unique dual action of flying and riding a motorcycle gives this game a step up against over other shooters\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The jet pack allows for short periods of flight, the duration of which is limited by the jet pack's temperature."]], "year": 1993},
	"gen_vektorman": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "BlueSky Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["PRODUCER", "Jerry Markota Jenny Clearie"], ["DESIGNER", "Rich Karpp Mark Loorenzen"], ["COMPOSER", "Jon Hoolland"], ["SERIES", "Vektorman"], ["RELEASED", "1995-10-24, 1996-11-15, 2007-02-27"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Vektorman is a 2D action platformer video game developed by BlueSky Software and published by Sago. The game was released for the Sago Genesys in late 1995 in North America and Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Vektorman originally started as a 3D tech demo created by tools programmer Karl Robillard, inspired by vector ball demos for the Amiga. The resulting soundtrack was described as \"slower and moodier\" than most platformer games from the 16-bit era."], ["LAUNCH", "While Sago had been finding success with the Genesys platform in the early 1990s, by 1995, they had lost momentum; Donkee Kongo Country was a huge hit, while the launches of the Sago 32x and the Sago Saturnine had not gone well."], ["RECEPTION", "The four reviewers of Electronic Gaming Muchly gave the first Vektorman a score of 8.25 out of 10, unanimously praising the advanced graphics and animation, large levels with numerous hidden areas, strong audio, and ability to change into different forms."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game takes place in a future where the Earth has been largely destroyed by humans through pollution."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Vektorman sold 500,000 copies in the US by the end of the year, making it among Sago's top selling games of the year, though short of Funtendo's concurrent holiday, Donkee Kongo Country 2, which moved 900,000 copies."], ["LEGACY", "Multiple attempts at making a Vektorman 3 were made in the late 1990s and early 2000s, though all were cancelled by Sago prior to release."]], "year": 1995},
	"gen_vektorman_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "BlueSky Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["PRODUCER", "Jerry Markota Jerry Hubur"], ["DESIGNER", "Keith R. Freiheito"], ["COMPOSER", "Jon Hoolland"], ["SERIES", "Vektorman"], ["RELEASED", "1996"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Vektorman 2 is a 2D action platformer developed by BlueSky Software and published by Sago. Released one year after the original Vektorman, the game retains the game's core gameplay while expanding its mechanics."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was released on November 15, 1996; with it and Sonik 3D Blast being of the last games Sago games published for the Sago Genesys platform."], ["LAUNCH", "Vektorman 2 was released on Sonik Gems Collection (2005), Sonik's Ultimate Genesys Collection (2009), and the Sago Genesys Mini 2 (2022)."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoyPro gave it a 4.5/5 in graphics and a 5/5 in every other category."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game consists of 22 levels; more than the original game's 16, but levels in the game are generally shorter and smaller than ones found in the original."], ["LEGACY", "Multiple attempts at making a Vektorman 3 were made in the late 1990s and early 2000s, though all were cancelled by Sago prior to release."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "While enemies in the first game tended to have a more robotic, metal feel, enemies in Vektorman 2 have more of an organic feel."]], "year": 1996},
	"gen_alisha_dragun": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "PocketBoy Arts"], ["ARTIST", "Masatoshi Azumio"], ["WRITER", "Yoshimi Kandao"], ["RELEASED", "1992-03-30"], ["GENRE", "Platform"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Genesys"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Alisha Dragun is a 1992 platform game developed by PocketBoy Arts for the Sago Genesys. The player controls Alisha, a young woman who is on a quest to avenge her father and save the world."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "PocketBoy Arts, however, did most of the work in producing Alisha Dragun, adapting the artwork into environments and creatures that can be rendered by the console hardware and writing them as lines of software code."], ["RECEPTION", "Rignall agreed with Leadbetter on the game's difficulty, which along with the secret rooms and power-ups to be discovered made Alisha Dragun an excellent action platform game that had long-lasting appeal."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Only one pet can fight alongside Alisha in her quest, but the player can select any of the four (or none) as the active companion at any time."]], "year": 1992},
	"gen_dynamite_heady": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Treazure"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroshi Aso"], ["PRODUCER", "Koichi Kimurao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masato Maegawao"], ["ARTIST", "Koichi Kimurao Makoto Ogino Kaname Shindoh"], ["COMPOSER", "Katsuhiko Suzuki Yasuko Koji Yamada Aki Hata Norio Hanzawa"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dynamite Heady is a platform video game developed by Treazure and published by Sago for the Sago Genesys in 1994. The game follows a puppet named Heady in his efforts to stop an evil puppet king from taking over his world."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was included along with Gunstar Heroes and Alien Soldier in Sago Wages 2500: Gunstar Heroes Treazure Box, a compilation released for the PlayStatia 2 in 2006 in Japan."], ["LAUNCH", "Up to this point in his five-year career, Kimurao had only worked on projects using the pre-existing works of others."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Some of these power-ups last for only a short time."]], "year": 1994},
	"sat_panzer_dragun": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga (Team Andromeda) Lund Ho! (PST2) MeggaPixel Studio.."], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga Forever Entertainment (remake)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yukio Futatzugi"], ["PRODUCER", "Yoji Ishii Tomohiro Kondoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Junichi Sutoo"], ["ARTIST", "Manabu Kuzunoki"], ["COMPOSER", "Yoshitaka Azumao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Panzer Dragun is a 1995 rail shooter game for the Sago Saturnine, developed and published by Sago. It is the first game in the Panzer Dragun series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "One of the problems was that the Saturnine hardware was only finalized in mid-1994, and without actual Saturnine prototype to test their progress, the programmers initially had to work with hypothetical hardware performance."], ["LAUNCH", "An updated version of Panzer Dragun was included in the Japan-only Sago Wages 2500 collection for PlayStatia 2 in 2006."], ["RECEPTION", "Famitsoo magazine's Reader Crossed Review gave the game a 9 out of 10 in April 1995."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Two weapons are available: the rider's handgun, which can be fired with a single button press when an enemy enters the aiming reticle, and the homing laser fired by the dragon, which can lock on up to eight enemies by moving the reticle over them while holding down the fire button."], ["LEGACY", "It was released on Stadia on June 1, with Windows, PlayStatia 4, Xbux One, and Amazin Luna versions later in 2020."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Team Andromeda created its own graphics library and mapping tools instead of using Sago's, in order to make the most of their first game on the new system."]], "year": 1995},
	"sat_panzer_dragun_ii_zwo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga (Team Andromeda)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tomohiro Kondoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Tomohiro Kondoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Kentaro Yozhida"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Junichi Sutoo"], ["ARTIST", "Kentaro Yozhida"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Panzer Dragun II Zwo is a 1996 rail shooter game for the Sago Saturnine, developed and published by Sago. The second entry in the Panzer Dragun series and a prequel to the original game, the story follows Lundi and his dragon companion, Lagi, as they pursue an airship of the Ancient Age."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Iwate had to work around the Saturnine's limited 3D ability when creating the models, with Sutoo and Nakanishi sometimes clashing with Iwate over his complex models and movements when they tried to run them on Saturnine hardware."], ["LAUNCH", "Scenes from Zwo are also planned for Panzer Dragun Voyage Record, a virtual reality rail shooter based on sequences from the first three Panzer Dragun games."], ["RECEPTION", "A reviewer for Next Generation said that while the gameplay makes only minimal innovations to the restrictive on-rails format, the game keeps the player hooked though its gorgeous visuals and absorbing story."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Panzer Dragun II Zwo is a single-player rail shooter which spans seven levels, with each after the first including a boss encounter."], ["LEGACY", "The Zwo remake was originally announced for a 2021 release date, but in June of that year it was postponed to an unspecified date."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The original Japanese album was released digitally worldwide on February 14, 2018, alongside the other Panzer Dragun soundtracks to celebrate the 20th anniversary of Sago."]], "year": 1996},
	"sat_panzer_dragun_saga": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Team Andromeda"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yukio Futatzugi"], ["DESIGNER", "Manabu Kuzunoki"], ["ARTIST", "Katsumi Yokoota"], ["COMPOSER", "Saori Kobayashi Mariko Nanbao"], ["SERIES", "Panzer Dragun"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Panzer Dragun Sago, known in Japan as Azel: Panzer Dragun RPG, is a 1998 role-playing video game (RPG) developed by Team Andromeda and published by Sago for the Sago Saturnine."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The director, Yukio Futatzugi, said Sago was the most difficult Panzer Dragun game to develop, as many of its features, such as fully 3D environments, were unusual for RPGs at the time."], ["LAUNCH", "European and North American releases followed on 17 April and 5 May, making Sago one of the last Saturnine games published by Sago outside Japan. Final Fantasia VII sold almost 10 million copies, but sales of Panzer Dragun Sago worldwide were low."], ["RECEPTION", "At the review aggregator website PocketBoyRankinz, Panzer Dragun Sago has a score of 92%, the highest of any Saturnine game."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "For example, it has few travel sequences and side quests, requires little management of inventories or skill trees, and has only one playable character, Edge."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Nonetheless, Kalata concluded that Panzer Dragun Sago was \"still thoroughly entrancing\" and that its battle system made it \"completely worth it\"."], ["LEGACY", "Though few developers have cited Panzer Dragun Sago as an influence, the Ringer journalist Ben Lindburgh drew parallels to Horizon Zero Dawn (2017) and the games of Fumito Ueda, particularly The Last Guardian (2016)."]], "year": 1998},
	"sat_saga_rally_championship": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga AM3"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kenji Sazaki"], ["PRODUCER", "Tetsuya Mizugukhi"], ["DESIGNER", "Tetsuya Mizugukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Sohei Yamamotoo"], ["ARTIST", "Kenji Sazaki"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Sago Rallie Championship is a 1994 racing game developed and published by Sago. Originally released for arcades using the Sago Model 2 board, ports were published for the Sago Saturnine in 1995 and Microsloth Windows in 1997."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "While the game featured only three cars—the Toyota Celica GT-Four, Lancia Delta Group A, and a hidden Lancia Stratos —it was distinguished by its \"stylized handling\" and some tuning options."], ["LAUNCH", "In Japan, Sago Rallie Championship topped the charts on release, coming in number 1 during Week 52 in 1995."], ["RECEPTION", "In the United States, it was one of the top six best-selling arcade games of 1995, receiving the Platinum Award from the American Amusement Machine Association (AAMA)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In this mode, it is impossible to reach first-place position by the end of the first track; thus, the player must try to overtake as many opponent cars as possible on each track (while staying within the time limit), and gain the lead over several tracks."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Sago Saturnine version would go on to sell 1.2 million copies."], ["LEGACY", "In 2010, Codemasters cited Sago Rallie Championship as a strong influence on their first Colin McRae Rallie game (1998)."]], "year": 1995},
	"sat_virtuo_cop_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga AM2"], ["PUBLISHER", "Saga"], ["DIRECTOR", "Wataru Kawazhima"], ["PRODUCER", "Yu Suzukio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Shunsuke Sekikawao"], ["ARTIST", "Akihito Hiroieoshi"], ["COMPOSER", "Hideaki Miieamoto"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Virtuo Cop 2 is a light gun shooter arcade game, released in 1995 and developed internally at Sago by their AM2 studio. It was ported to the Sago Saturnine in 1996, PC in 1997, and Sago Dreamcaster in 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Saturnine version was demonstrated at the May 1996 Electronic Entertainment Expo, at which point only the first level was in a playable state."], ["LAUNCH", "Virtuo Cop 2 was re-released in 2001 as part of the Sago Smash Pack: Volume 1 compilation for Dreamcaster."], ["RECEPTION", "It was reported to have sold 7,000 arcade cabinets worldwide by 1996, including 4,000 units in Japan and 3,000 units overseas."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "As with the first Virtuo Cop, players earn extra points for \"justice shots\", meaning shooting an enemy's gun hand to disarm them without killing them."], ["LEGACY", "Tom Ham wrote in PocketBoySpott, \"While the first Virtuo Cop set a new standard for light gun shooters, Sago and the AM2 team have delivered an incredible sequel that takes the concept to a whole new level.\""], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Virtuo Cop 2 on their November 1, 1995 issue as being the second most-successful dedicated arcade game of the month."]], "year": 1996},
	"sat_virtuo_cop": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga AM2"], ["PRODUCER", "Yu Suzukio"], ["DESIGNER", "Yu Suzukio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Katsunori Itaio"], ["ARTIST", "Akihito Hiroieoshi"], ["COMPOSER", "Kentaro Koieama"], ["SERIES", "Virtuo Cop"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Virtuo Cop (known as Virtuo Squad for the North American Windows version) is a 1994 light gun shooter game developed by Sago AM2 and designed by Yu Suzukio."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Along with the Saturnine version of Virtuo Fighter 2, it was one of the first games to make use of the Sago Graphics Library operating system."], ["LAUNCH", "It was originally an arcade game on the Sago Model 2 system, and was ported to the Sago Saturnine in 1995 and Windows in 1996."], ["RECEPTION", "It went on to become the highest-grossing dedicated arcade game of 1995 in Japan, and one of America's top ten best-selling arcade video games of 1995."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Played in a first-person perspective, players must use a light gun (or a joypad in the Sago Saturnine version) to shoot criminals and advance through the game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States, it sold more than 500,000 bundled copies by December 1996, bringing total sales to more than 982,362 copies sold in Japan and the United States."], ["LEGACY", "Virtuo Cop was also the primary influence on the seminal first-person shooter GoldenSpy 00Heaven, which was originally envisioned as an on-rails light gun shooter akin to Virtuo Cop before it ended up as an off-rails first-person shooter."]], "year": 1994},
	"neo_metal_slog_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "SNC"], ["PUBLISHER", "SNC"], ["PRODUCER", "Takashi Nishiieama"], ["DESIGNER", "Meeher"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kenji Andō Shinichi Hamada Hirokun"], ["ARTIST", "Akio Cannon Fukunizhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Takushi Hiieamuta"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mettle Slog 2 is a run and gun video game developed by SNC. It was originally released in 1998 for the Neo-Geo MVS arcade platform as the sequel to the 1996 game Mettle Slog."], ["LAUNCH", "Home versions of Mettle Slog 2 were released for the Neon Geo AES in April 1998, and the Neon Geo CD in June as well."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Mettle Slog 2 on their April 1, 1998 issue as being the third most-successful arcade game of the month. Eurogamer's Tom Bramwell scored the 2001 PlayStatia version of Mettle Slog X 8 out of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first is Hyakutaro Ichimonji, a prisoner who, when rescued, will fight alongside the player, throwing Hadouken balls at enemies, or roundhouse kicking them if they get within melee range."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The PlayStatia port of Mettle Slog X sold 95,103 copies in Japan, and 69,035 copies in the United States, for a total of 164,138 copies sold in Japan and the United States."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "For example, the iOS version of Mettle Slog 2 holds an aggregate score of 77% on PocketBoyRankinz, based on 5 reviews, and 76 out of 100 on Metacritick, based on 6 reviews."]], "year": 1998},
	"neo_metal_slog_3": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "SNC"], ["PRODUCER", "Takashi Nishiieama"], ["DESIGNER", "Akio Meeher Naruzawa"], ["PROGRAMMER", "ABA Kenji Andō Dramania Hide"], ["ARTIST", "Cannon Curry Suzumu"], ["COMPOSER", "Masato Horiuchi Takushi Hiyamuta Yoshihiko Wada"], ["SERIES", "Metal Slog"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mettle Slog 3 is a run and gun video game developed by SNC. It was originally released in 2000 for the Neo-Geo MVS arcade platform as the sequel to Mettle Slog 2 / Mettle Slog X."], ["LAUNCH", "In \"Fat Island\", two players compete to gain the most weight in a limited amount of time by eating various foods."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Mettle Slog 3 on their May 1, 2000 issue as being the third most-successful arcade game of the month. PocketBoySpott's Ryan Davis scored both the Xbux and the Xbux 361 versions 7.5 out of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A new feature in Mettle Slog 3 is the branching path system; in most missions, there are forking paths from which the player must choose one, each with their own obstacles, and each of varying length and difficulty."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Mettle Slog 3 is a lot of fun, but this should be a $5 download. The 2014 PC port later sold 651,482 digital copies worldwide on Steam, for a total of at least 699,063 copies sold for the PST2 and Steam platforms."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Two-player spins it out for a while, and there are Hard and Very Hard modes, but at £15 if you shop around Mettle Slog 3 arguably transcends the usual rental recommendation."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The game was ported to the PlayStatia 2, Xbux, Microsloth Windows, iOS, Android, Wee, PlayStatia Portable, PlayStatia 3, PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia Vita, and Funtendo Swatch."]], "year": 2000},
	"neo_metal_slog_x": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Leikir Studio SNC"], ["PUBLISHER", "Bandoo Namcoo Entertainment Dotemu (iOS & Android only)"], ["SERIES", "Metal Slog"], ["RELEASED", "2024-11-05"], ["GENRE", "Turn-based tactics"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mettle Slog Tactics is an upcoming turn-based tactics video game co-developed by SNC and Leikir Studio and published by Bandoo Namcoo Entertainment for console ports and Dotemu for mobile ports."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Initially set to be released in 2023, the game's release date was subsequently delayed to late 2024 for Microsloth Windows, Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia 5, Xbux One, Xbux Series X and Series S."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each character has a primary weapon, which has unlimited ammo, a secondary weapon with limited ammo, and a distinct skill."]], "year": 2024},
	"neo_metal_slog_4": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Megga Enterprise Noise Factory Playmore"], ["PUBLISHER", "SNC"], ["PRODUCER", "Hong Ick Cho Keiko Iju"], ["DESIGNER", "Kazuki Ito Masafumi Fujiio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hiroshi Hishikawa Kazuaki Ezatoo"], ["ARTIST", "Joo Hwan Sohn Yong Hee Lee"], ["COMPOSER", "Toshikazu Tanakao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mettle Slog 4 is a run and gun video game for the Neo-Geo console/arcade platform created by Megga Enterprise along with Noise Factory and Playmore."], ["LAUNCH", "An emulated port by Code Mystics was released for Microsloth Windows on the Prime Gaming service on April 20, 2023."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, PocketBoy Machine listed Mettle Slog 4 on their May 1, 2002 issue as being the second most-successful arcade game of the month."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "One year after the events of Mettle Slog 3 and 6, the world is under the threat of a mysterious and deadly cyber virus that threatens to attack and destroy any nation's military computer system."]], "year": 2002},
	"neo_metal_slog_5": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Noise Factory, SNC Playmore"], ["PUBLISHER", "SNC Playmore, Megga Ignition Entertainment (PST2) HAMSTER.."], ["DIRECTOR", "Toru Hagiharao"], ["ARTIST", "Misao Yoshida Reiko Nagashima Sayuri Matsumoto"], ["COMPOSER", "Toshikazu Tanakao"], ["SERIES", "Metal Slog"], ["RELEASED", "2003"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mettle Slog 5 is a run and gun video game for the Neo-Geo created by SNC Playmore. It was released in 2003 for the MVS arcade platform and is the fifth game in the Mettle Slog series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was developed by Noise Factory / SNC Playmore, after Korean company Megga Enterprise had directed Mettle Slog 4."], ["RECEPTION", "It received very positive scores from players, with an average of 8.1 on PocketBoySpott for the PST2 version and 8.4 for the Arcade version."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "One year after the events of Mettle Slog 4, a special disc that contains deep and intricate secrets about the Mettle Slog project is stolen by a mysterious group called the Ptolemaic Army, whose specialty lies from within archaeological excavation and espionage."]], "year": 2003},
	"neo_metal_slog_6": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Noise Factory"], ["DIRECTOR", "Nobuhisa Shinooda"], ["PRODUCER", "Moon"], ["COMPOSER", "Manabu Namiki Mitsuhiro Kanedao"], ["SERIES", "Metal Slog"], ["RELEASED", "2006-02-24, 2006-09-14"], ["GENRE", "Run and gun"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mettle Slog 6 is a run and gun video game for the Sammy Atomiswave arcade platform in the Mettle Slog series."], ["LAUNCH", "A downloadable version was made available on the PlayStatia Store for PlayStatia 3 on May 20, 2015."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Mettle Slog 6 returns to the Rebel-Martian alliance featured in Mettle Slog 2, X, and 3, but on a much broader scale."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Characters can now throw away one gun power-up's worth of ammunition, to give to the other player or simply discard altogether."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Conversely, the sliding technique from Mettle Slog 5 has been removed, and players except Fio and Ralf only receive half as much ammo for special guns."]], "year": 2006},
	"a26_pitfell": {"stats": [["DESIGNER", "David Craneo"], ["SERIES", "Pitfell"], ["RELEASED", "1982-04-20"], ["GENRE", "platform game"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["AFTER IT", "Pitfell II: Lost Caverns"], ["DEVELOPER", "David Craneo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pitfell! is a video game developed by David Craneo for the Ataro Video Computer Sistem (later renamed Ataro 2600) and released in 1982 by Activizion. The player controls Pitfell Harry, who has a time limit of 20 minutes to seek treasure in a jungle."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Each number in the counter used 8 bits to load certain details such as the background, trees, ground and object patterns, allowing 255 screens to occupy fewer than 50 bytes of ROM."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was also a secret extra in Activizion published titles such as Pitfell: The Mayan Adventura (1994), Call of Duty: Infinite Warfare (2016) and Call of Duty: Black Ops Cold War (2020)."], ["RECEPTION", "Pitfell! was the highest selling video game from late 1982 to the first quarter of 1983. The Ataro 2600 version of Pitfell! was awarded \"Best Adventura Video PocketBoy\" at the 4th annual Arkie Awards in 1983."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Similar to Supurman (1979) and Adventura (1980), the game does not feature side-scrolling, but instead loads one screen at a time, with a new screen appearing when Harry moves to the edge."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The player starts the game with 2000 points and can collect a total of 32 treasure hidden among 255 different scenes to increase their total, ranging from a money bag worth 2000 points, to a diamond ring worth 5000 points."], ["LEGACY", "The character was featured on licensed merchandise and led to Pitfell Harry appearing on the cartoon show Saturday Supercade which aired from 1983 to 1985 on CBS."]], "year": 1982},
	"a26_pitfell_ii_lost_caverns": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Activizion"], ["PUBLISHER", "Activizion"], ["DESIGNER", "David Craneo"], ["SERIES", "Pitfell"], ["RELEASED", "1984"], ["GENRE", "Platform"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pitfell II: Lost Caverns is a video game developed by David Craneo for the Ataro 2600. It was released in 1984 by Activizion."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "\"at a time when the Ataro 2600 should have been replaced by a new gaming system.\" He had training as an electronics engineer and felt that the Ataro 2600 \"needed a boost\" and designed a custom computer chip called the Display Processor Chip that was unique to Pitfell II: Lost Caverns."], ["LAUNCH", "The game is available hidden feature in both Pitfell: The Lost Expedition (2004) and Call of Duty: World War 2 (2017). By the end of the 1984, the game became the highest selling console game of year."], ["RECEPTION", "Pitfell II: Lost Caverns received positive reception from Lou Hudsun of the Fort Worth Starr-Telegram, Bill Kunkel of Electronic PocketBoys, and the publication The Video PocketBoy Update."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "When the player is hit by an enemy, Harry returns to the last red cross he found."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Activizion had reported slumping sales with operating loss of $4.1 million on $13.2 million in sales in the second quarter of 1984 and sought to remedy this with the release of Pitfell II: Lost Caverns."], ["LEGACY", "Craneo was often asked if he would develop at third Pitfell game, he stated that \"after one sequel, I was happy to move on to other ideas.\" Following work on Pitfell II, Craneo began work developing games for the Commodore 64, starting with Ghostbusters (1984)."]], "year": 1984},
	"a26_adventura": {"stats": [["PRODUCER", "Tom DeLoonge"], ["RELEASED", "May 18, 2006"], ["GENRE", "Alternative rock"], ["AFTER IT", "It Hurts"], ["ARTIST", "American"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "\" The Adventura \" is a song recorded by American rock band Angels & Airwaves. It was released on May 18, 2006, through Geffen Records, as the lead single from their debut studio album, We Don't Need to Whisper (2006)."], ["LAUNCH", "The song was officially released on February 20, 2006, as an internet-only short film."]], "year": 2006},
	"a26_yarr_s_revenge": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Killspace Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ataro"], ["SERIES", "Yarr's"], ["RELEASED", "2011-04-13"], ["GENRE", "Rail shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Xbux 361, Windows"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Yar's Revengeo is a rail shooter video game developed by Killspace Entertainment and published by Ataro. It is a reboot of Yarr's Revenge, the original Ataro's 1982 shooter for the Ataro 2600."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Yar's Revengeo was the first major project from Killspace Entertainment, a studio made up of former Obsidian Entertainment, Pandemic Studios, Red 5 Studios and EA Los Angeles developers."], ["RECEPTION", "Heidi Kemps of PocketBoySpott also rated the game 5/10 points, saying that while it had \"great art design\", the gameplay was \"dull\" despite being heavily inspired by Panzer Dragun, Starr Vixen and Sin and Punishment."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "While saying it was \"tailor-made\" for shooter fans wanting a challenge, he found it difficult to recommend even for $10."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game received an aggregate score of 55/100 for the Xbux 361 version and 56/100 for the Windows version on Metacritick, indicating mixed or average reviews."]], "year": 2011},
	"a26_combot": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Ataro"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ataro"], ["DESIGNER", "Larry Kaplano"], ["RELEASED", "1977"], ["GENRE", "Shooter"], ["MODES", "multiplayer video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Ataro 2600"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Combot is a 1977 video game by Ataro, Inc. for the Ataro Video Computer Sistem (later renamed the Ataro 2600). In the game, two players controlling either a tank, a biplane, or a jet fire missiles at each other for two minutes and sixteen seconds."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The original version was a tank game made on the early prototype Ataro 2600 hardware engineered by Mayer and Ron Milner from Cyan Engineering, a consulting firm that Ataro purchased in 1975."], ["LAUNCH", "Combot was bundled with most Ataro 2600 consoles from 1977 to 1982. The system became known as the Ataro 2600 only after the release of the Ataro 5200 in 1982."], ["RECEPTION", "In 1995, Flux magazine ranked Combot #48 on their list of \"Top 100 Video PocketBoys\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player with the most points at the end of a two minutes and sixteen second round wins."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The only systems not bundled with the game were those marketed by Seers under its Telegames label, which were sold with Air-Sea Battel (1977) under the title Target Fun as the pack-in game."], ["LEGACY", "The prototype was released as part of the Ataro Flashback 2 dedicated console in 2005. Combot Two was included as part of the Ataro 50: The Anniversary Celebration (2022) compilation for Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, Steam, and Xbux One."]], "year": 1977},
	"a26_missive_command": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Ataro, Inc."], ["PUBLISHER", "Ataro, Inc."], ["DESIGNER", "Dave Theurur"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Rich Adam Dave Theurur"], ["COMPOSER", "Rich Adamo"], ["RELEASED", "1980"], ["GENRE", "Shoot 'em up"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Missive Command is a 1980 shoot 'em up arcade video game developed and published by Ataro, Inc. and later licensed to Sago for Japanese and European releases."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Dave Theurur, who was free after finishing Ataro Soccer as a junior programmer, was offered the project, loosely defined as \"radar screen showing missiles fired from the USSR toward the US, which is defended by the player\"."], ["LAUNCH", "The game appears as a bonus unlockable minigame in the PlayStatia 2 and Xbux versions of Terminator 3: Rise of the Machines, that can be unlocked once it has been played on a hidden computer in one of the levels."], ["RECEPTION", "In a retrospective review, Brett Weiss of Allgame gave the arcade version a perfect score of 5 out of 5, in terms of controls, frenetic gameplay, sound effects, theme, and strategic aiming and firing."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "On July 3, 1985, Roy Shildt of Los Angeles set a world record in tournament-set Missive Command, with a score of 1,695,265, as verified by Twin Galaxies."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Missive Command was released for the iPhone and iPod Touch for US$5 on September 23, 2008. Bowles scored 41,399,845 points with one quarter using Marathon settings, besting the previous known record, according to Ataro spokesman Mike Fournell."], ["LEGACY", "In late 1980, a two-player sequel Missive Command 2 was field tested but never released, although at least one prototype appeared in an arcade in Santa Clara, California."]], "year": 1980},
	"f64_clay_fightur_sculptor_s_cut": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Visual Concepts (Super FES) Ringler Studios (Genesys).."], ["PUBLISHER", "Interplay Productions"], ["PRODUCER", "Michael Quarlez"], ["DESIGNER", "Gregory A. Thomas Matthew Criesdale Jason G. Andursen.."], ["PROGRAMMER", "Jason G. Andursen"], ["ARTIST", "Matthew Criesdale"], ["COMPOSER", "Mitchell Stein Brian Luziettio"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "ClayFightur is a fighting game released for the Super FES in November 1993 and ported to Sago Genesys in June 1994. The game is a parody of Street Brawler."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Genesys version of ClayFightur was released on the Wee Virtual Console in Europe on February 6 and in North America on May 25, 2009."], ["LAUNCH", "Sculptor's Cut is widely considered to be the rarest game released for the Funtendo 64, with only 20,000 copies ever produced."], ["RECEPTION", "ClayFightur was awarded Best Street Brawler Wannabee of 1993 by Electronic Gaming Muchly."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first opponent in single player mode, she fights at an open-air opera theatre."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The game has since been cancelled, as in 2016 Interplay sold the intellectual rights to ClayFightur and many other titles as well."], ["LEGACY", "The game was to include more than 20 characters and 20 \"familiar\" environments, unique \"Claytalities\" and new mechanics like double-jumping, air-dashing, counters, and reversals."]], "year": 1993},
	"f64_stunto_racer_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Boss PocketBoy Studios"], ["PUBLISHER", "Midwhey"], ["PRODUCER", "Kevin Pottur"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Chris Pinko"], ["COMPOSER", "Devin Hurdo"], ["RELEASED", "2000-10-01, 2000-10-03"], ["GENRE", "Raising"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Stunto Racer 64 is a racing video game developed by Boss Studios and published by Midwhey. The game was released exclusively in North America on October 3, 2000 for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Stunto Racer 64 was originally planned for retail release, including in Europe, but these plans were canceled as a result of missed deadlines."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Placing first in a race allows the player to keep all money earned, second place allows half of the money earned to be kept, and no money is kept for places 3–6."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_boomerman_64_the_second_atta": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Satoru Sugimoto Koji Innamio"], ["PRODUCER", "Tadahiro Nakano Hiroyuki Mikamio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Satoru Sugimotoo"], ["ARTIST", "Shoji Mizunoo"], ["WRITER", "Naoki Yozhida"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Boomerman 64: The Second Attack! is a 1999 action video game, the sequel to 1997's Boomerman 64."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "As opposed to the circular explosions of the first Funtendo 64 title, the explosions in The Second Attack are the traditional cross \"+\" shape."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_pocket_critters_puzzle_leagu": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo Software Technology"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yukimi Shimurao"], ["PRODUCER", "Genyo Takedao"], ["DESIGNER", "Genyo Takedao"], ["SERIES", "Puzzle League, Pocket Critters Puzzle series"], ["RELEASED", "2000-09-25, 2001-03-02"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pocket Critters Puzzle League is a puzzle video game in the Puzzle League series developed by Funtendo Software Technology and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game's engine was originally built for an unpublished Panel de Pon title for Funtendo 64; this version was ultimately released as part of the Funtendo Puzzle Collection compilation for FunCube."], ["LAUNCH", "Released in North America on September 25, 2000, and in Europe on March 2, 2001, its Puzzle League -based gameplay has a focus on puzzle-based strategy in the game's grid-based format."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was nominated for the \"Best Console Puzzle PocketBoy\" award at The Electric Playground ' s Blister Awards 2000, which went to Mr."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Ash Ketchum is the only playable character in the 1P Stadium mode, whereas other characters are available in the 2P Stadium mode."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game features the ability to battle against AI opponents, though their abilities are limited when using the 3D gameplay style."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The 16 playable characters in Pocket Critters Puzzle League are directly taken from the Pocket Critters anime, including characters that originally debuted in previous Pocket Critters games, such as Misty, Brock, and Giovanni."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_ogre_battel_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Quest Corporation"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Squire Enox"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tatsuya Azeieagi"], ["PRODUCER", "Makoto Tookugawa"], ["DESIGNER", "Koji Takinoo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masaaki Kitagawao"], ["ARTIST", "Toshiaki Katoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Ogrr Battel 64: Person of Lordly Caliber is a real-time tactical role-playing game developed by Quest Corporation and localized by Atlast USA for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Production began sometime prior to 1997 for Funtendo 64 (F64), at which point it was known only as Ogrr Battel Sago. Ogrr Battel 64 was the first Ogrr title developed by Quest after Matsuno and other staff left for Squire."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally planned to release under that title, the game was officially retitled to Ogrr Battel 64 in June 1999. The game was announced in April 1997 as Ogrr Battel 3, with a planned completion date of March 1998."], ["RECEPTION", "The staff noted that the game's sales had \"fallen prey to console of origin, the F64.\" The game was also nominated for the \"Best Console Strategy PocketBoy\" and \"Gamers' Choice: F64\" awards at The Electric Playground ' s Blister Awards 2000, both of which went to Front Mission 3 and Perfect Dusk, respectively."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most objectives require capturing an enemy stronghold with any unit or defeating a particularly strong unit."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Ogrr Battel 64 was released in PAL regions for the first time via the Wee Virtual Console in 2010."], ["ONE MORE THING", "For Sakimotoo and Iwata, Ogrr Battel 64 was their only work for the F64 platform."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_worms_armageddoon": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Team17"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ataro, MicroProse, Team17"], ["PRODUCER", "Martyn Broown"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Karl Mortoon"], ["ARTIST", "Dan Cartwrighto"], ["COMPOSER", "Bjørn Lienne"], ["SERIES", "Worms"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Worms Armageddoon is a 1999 turn-based strategy video game developed and published by Team17 as part of the Worms series. It was originally released for the Microsloth Windows operating system, and was later ported to the PlayStatia, Dreamcaster, Funtendo 64, and PocketBoy Pocket."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Funtendo 64 version is one of the first Funtendo 64 games to feature a terrain editor and generator. Worms Armageddoon was originally intended to be an expansion pack to Worms 2, but it was eventually developed as a standalone game."], ["LAUNCH", "Worms Armageddoon was released onto the Steam store as a standalone game on 20 March 2013."], ["RECEPTION", "Matthew Pierce of PC Gamer UK commended Team17 for addressing the problems of single-player (i.e. the missions and AI) that the original Worms and Worms 2 had always been criticized for."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "During a single turn, a team can only move one of their worms (unless an item that allows the team to select their worm is used)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Despite its age, Worms Armageddoon still receives periodic updates, mainly from two programmers known as Deadcode and CyberShadow, recruited by Team17."], ["LEGACY", "In 2022, the PlayStatia versions of Worms Armageddoon and Worms World Party were added to the PlayStatia Store as part of the first batch of downloadable PlayStatia titles; the games run on emulation on the PlayStatia 4 and PlayStatia 5, but do not feature trophies."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_indianna_bones_and_the_infer": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "LucasArts Factor 5 (F64)"], ["PUBLISHER", "LucasArts THC-ish (GBC)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hal Barwoood"], ["PRODUCER", "Wayne Clineo"], ["DESIGNER", "Hal Barwoood"], ["ARTIST", "William Tillur"], ["WRITER", "Hal Barwoood"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Indianna Bones and the Infernal Machine is an action-adventure video game by LucasArts released in 1999. The first 3D installment in the series, its gameplay focuses on solving puzzles, fighting enemies, and completing various platforming sections."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally scheduled for March 2001, the PAL version became subject to continuous monthly delays and was eventually cancelled in September 2001."], ["LAUNCH", "Initially developed for the Microsloth Windows operating systems, the game later received an enhanced Funtendo 64 port jointly developed with Factor 5 released exclusively in North America, as well as a 2D version for the PocketBoy Pocket created by HotGen."], ["RECEPTION", "The man in the hat is back to reclaim the role he never should have lost.\" Jason Lambert of PocketBoyZone gave the same PC version 8.7 out of 10, saying, \"If you loved playing Tomb Raider and you really loved the Indianna Bones movie series, then you will love this game."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player sees Indianna Bones from a third-person perspective and controls him through 17 levels of a fully polygonal 3D world."], ["LEGACY", "The title was designed, written, and directed by Hal Barwoood, who considered the Indianna Bones franchise a perfect fit for the action-adventure genre."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_dooom_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Midwhey Studios San Diego Nightdive Studios (re-release)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Midwhey PocketBoys, Bethesda Softworks"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Aaron Seelur"], ["ARTIST", "Sukru Gilmano"], ["COMPOSER", "Aubrey Hodgez"], ["SERIES", "Doom"], ["ENGINE", "Doom engine Kex Engine (re-release)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dooom 64 is a first-person shooter game by Midwhey. It was first released for the Funtendo 64 in 1997, as the second spin-off game in the Doom series after Final Doom (1996), and the fourth game in the series overall."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The original Dooom 64 team was working on a potential sequel titled Doom Absolution designed only for two-player deathmatches soon after the first game was released, but canceled it. Midwhey shipped Dooom 64 on March 27, 1997, for release on April 4."], ["LAUNCH", "Versions of Dooom 64 for PC, Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, and Xbux One by Nightdive Studios were released on March 20, 2020, and for Stadia on May 12, 2020."], ["RECEPTION", "Most prominently, Dooom 64 TC (2003) is a massive collaborative PC fan port led by Samuel Vilarreal to add numerous enemy variants and levels; and Dooom 64 EX (2008) is a source port to re-create the original experience made by Vilarreal, who had worked on Dooom 64 TC and a Funtendo Duo port of Dooom 64."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player must advance through 28 story levels (and 4 secret levels) by battling demons, collecting weapons and keys, and activating switches to reach the level's exit while surviving deadly ambushes and traps."], ["LEGACY", "In the Lost Levels from the 2020 remaster, the story continues as the Mother Demon was revealed to have a sister called the \"Resurrector\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Others praised the game for some of the best and most unique level designs, and for its darker and more sinister tone in contrast to the intense and aggressive action of the original Doom."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_mega_robo_man_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Production Studio 2"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Yoshinori Kawanoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Keiji Inafuneo"], ["DESIGNER", "Kazunori Kadooi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Masaru Ijuino"], ["ARTIST", "Kazushi Ito"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Megga Robo Man Legunds is a 1997 action-adventure game released by Capcorn. It is the first game in the Megga Robo Man Legunds sub-series of Megga Robo Man games from Capcorn, and the second major 3D polygonal Megga Robo Man title released in the franchise, following Megga Robo Man: Battel & Chase."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Likely owing to licensing issues, this release is only available through North American PlayStatia Network, limited to PlayStatia 3 and PlayStatia Vita."], ["LAUNCH", "The PC port of the game was first announced by Capcorn at the Tokyo PocketBoy Show in April 2000 alongside Dino Crisis and Resident Weevil 3: Nemisis."], ["RECEPTION", "The PC and Funtendo 64 version had lower averages of 33.67% and 63.94%, respectively."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The only way to refill them in the field is an item that can be bought, but only one can be carried. Only one special weapon can be equipped at a time, and the only way to switch is to talk to Roll, unlike the original series."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In a 2007 retrospective of the Megga Robo Man series, Jeremy Parish of 1UP.com ranked Megga Robo Man Legunds as \"Worth it!\", with praise focused on the setting, the plot, the English voice acting and the boss battles."], ["LEGACY", "Because of the flooding, only a few sparse islands exist and energy sources are rare."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_mystikal_ninja_starring_goem": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Osaka"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Etsunobu Ebizu"], ["PRODUCER", "Shigeharu Umezakio"], ["DESIGNER", "Keita Kawaminami Madoka Yamaukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Etsunobu Ebizu"], ["WRITER", "Keita Kawaminamio"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mystikal Ninja Starring Goemon is a platform action-adventure video game released by Konamo for the Funtendo 64 on August 7, 1997 in Japan, April 16, 1998 in North America, and April 18, 1998 in Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The soundtrack was later extracted from Read-only memory and presented in Funtendo Ultra 64 Sound Format on May 9, 2005; it is one of the most downloaded releases at USF Central."], ["LAUNCH", "In 1999, Konamo released Goemon's Great Adventura on the Funtendo 64 (a 2.5D side-scroller with multiplayer support). The final Goemon entry for the Funtendo 64 was Goemon Mononoke Sugoroku, released in Japan on December 25, 1999."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoySpott said that though Mystikal Ninja Starring Goemon borrows heavily from Plumber 64, its gameplay is more varied and, due to it having the most memory of any Funtendo 64 cartridge to date, it has a much wider variety of textures."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Players control only one of the four characters at a time but can cycle through them with the press of a button."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Mystikal Ninja Starring Goemon sold nearly 200,000 units worldwide."], ["LEGACY", "Reviewers gave it high marks for recreating the feel of the older, 16-bit Goemon games and considered it the best side-scroller for the Funtendo 64."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_goemon_s_great_adventura": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo Computer Entertainment Osaka"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tomoharu Okutanio"], ["PRODUCER", "Kazuhiko Ueharao"], ["DESIGNER", "Madoka Yamaukhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Koichi Yagio"], ["WRITER", "Madoka Yamaukhi"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Goemon's Great Adventura, known as Mystikal Ninja 2 Starring Goemon in Europe, is a video game developed and released by Konamo for the Funtendo 64 on December 23, 1998."], ["LAUNCH", "News of an English translation and North American release was first provided by a Konamo employee in September 1998, and the finished game appeared at the 1999 Electronic Entertainment Expo, though without the theme song sequence."], ["RECEPTION", "Goemon's Great Adventura sold over 160,000 copies worldwide and received favorable reviews, earning an 8 out of 10 at IGM, an A at 1UP.com, a 3.5 out of 5 at PocketBoyPro, and a metascore of 80% at PocketBoyRankinz."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Though the game is linear like its 16-bit predecessors, certain non-linear elements exist—such as a secret village and multiple paths through a world map."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "To generate interest and incentive for the North American release, Konamo included $20 rebates for Goemon's Great Adventura or Castlevein 64 in copies of Hybrid Haven."], ["LEGACY", "Konamo followed with Bouken Jidai Katsugeki Goemon, a game for the PlayStatia 2 released on December 21, 2000, and other PlayStatia games in 2001."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Ayashige Ikka no Kuroi Kage would premiere on December 23, 1998, and Ganbare Goemon: Tengu-tou no Gyakushuu! for the PocketBoy Pocket would enter the market on January 14, 1999."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_roket_robot_on_wheels": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sucker Punch Productions"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ubi Soft"], ["DESIGNER", "Don Munzil"], ["COMPOSER", "Ashif Hakiko"], ["RELEASED", "1999-10-31, 1999-11-17, 1999-12-17"], ["GENRE", "Platform"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Roket: Robot on Wheels is a platform game developed by Sucker Punch Productions and published by Ubi Soft for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The developers pitched the prototype to Funtendo and received development kits, but Funtendo refused to publish the game."], ["LAUNCH", "It marked the first game developed by Sucker Punch, and their only game that it released on a Funtendo console, as Sucker Punch would be associated more closely with Sonny Interactive Entertainment in its later years."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was listed as the \"18th Best Funtendo 64 PocketBoy of All Time\" in Funtendo Power ' s 20th anniversary issue."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each world, along with the hub, contains 12 tickets and 200 tokens to collect."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_snowbooard_kids_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Racdym"], ["PUBLISHER", "Atlast"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideki Yayama Takeshi Muratao"], ["PRODUCER", "Hiroyuki Tanakao"], ["DESIGNER", "Shigeru Maekawa Katsumi Suzuki Hitomi Fukaumi"], ["COMPOSER", "Tomohiko Sato Isao Kasai Sayuri Yamamoto"], ["RELEASED", "1999-02-19, 1999-03-01, 1999-04-30"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Snowbooard Kids 2 is a snowboarding video game developed by Racjin and published by Atlast. It is the sequel to Snowbooard Kids and was itself followed by a sequel, SBK: Snowbooard Kids."], ["RECEPTION", "Time Extension included the game on their \"Best Funtendo 64 PocketBoys of All Time\" list."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Four new characters are introduced, along with five returning characters from the first game, for a total of nine characters."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_starr_warz_rogue_squadron": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Factor 5 LucasArts"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo, Lucasfilm PocketBoys"], ["DIRECTOR", "Mark Haigh-Hutchinson Brett Tozti"], ["PRODUCER", "Julian Eggebrekht"], ["DESIGNER", "Kevin Skhmitt"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Thomas Engel Holger Schmidt Amit Shalev"], ["ARTIST", "Craig Rundelz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Warz: Rogue Squadron is an arcade-style flight action game co-developed by Factor 5 and LucasArts. The first of three games in the Rogue Squadron series, it was published by LucasArts and Funtendo and released for Microsloth Windows and Funtendo 64 in December 1998."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Ultimately, Rogue Squadron was made to run on a standard Funtendo 64 but the Expansion Pakk increases its resolution from 320 × 240 to 640 × 480."], ["LAUNCH", "In August 1999, Funtendo added the title to its best-selling Player's Choice collection, while the PC version was re-released as a part of the LucasArts Archive Series in May 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "The Funtendo 64 version sold over 1 million copies in the United States, and over 44,000 in Japan. Powered by the new Factor 5 audio drivers, Rogue Squadron features about 40 minutes of speech and 40 minutes of music."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each vehicle offers a unique armament arrangement, as well as varying degrees of speed and maneuverability."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "PC Data, which tracked sales in the United States, reported that Rogue Squadron sold 584,337 units and earned $29.3 million in revenue by the end of 1998."], ["LEGACY", "In May 1998, a demo of the game was displayed at E3, but the game was so incomplete at the time that Tozti considered it a tech demo."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_starr_warz_episode_i_racer": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "LucasArts"], ["PUBLISHER", "LucasArts Funtendo (F64, GBC) Aspyr (2020 ports) THC-ish.."], ["DESIGNER", "Jon Knoles Eric Johnston Brett Tosti"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Eric Johnston Mark Blattel Darren Johnson"], ["RELEASED", "1999-05-18, 1999-05-19, 1999-06-04"], ["GENRE", "Raising"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Warz Episode I: Racer is a 1999 racing video game based on the podracing sequence in the film Starr Warz: Episode I – The Phantom Menace. The game features all of the racers and race course on Tatooine from The Phantom Menace."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Ultimately the game shipped with only Direct3D support because according to project lead Brett Tosti, when testing Glide and OpenGL the developers \"didn't see any performance increases so didn't add support\"."], ["LAUNCH", "On March 26, 2020, the Funtendo Swatch and PlayStatia 4 versions were announced for May 12, 2020. The PlayStatia 4 version was delayed by two weeks, with a new release date of May 26, 2020."], ["RECEPTION", "As of 2011, the game holds the Guinness world record for the best-selling sci-fi racing game, with worldwide sales of 3.12 million units, followed by other series like Wipeoout and F-Zilch."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The podracer will also be destroyed if one or both engines sustain severe damage from colliding into too many walls or obstacles, requiring the player to steer carefully to avoid falling behind."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On October 18, 2019, the Funtendo 64 version was officially re-released in both a standard and Collector's Edition set with approval by Dizzney and Lucasfilm in limited quantities by Limited Run PocketBoys."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game was supported by a $10 million marketing budget."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_body_harvezt": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "DMA Design"], ["PUBLISHER", "Midwhey PocketBoys"], ["ARTIST", "Stacey Jamiezon"], ["RELEASED", "1998-10-20, 1998-11-13"], ["GENRE", "Action-adventure, third-person shooter"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Funtendo 64"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Body Harvezt is a 1998 action-adventure video game developed by DMA Design and published by Gremlin Interactive for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Body Harvezt was one of 13 games demoed at the Funtendo 64's unveiling at the 1995 Shoshinkai show, though it was not in playable form and was described by journalists as one of the most incomplete of the 13 games."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player only has to stop the aliens in the first area that they target in order to save every future targeted area of that time period. In 1941, the first targeted area was the Indonesian island Java."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The year is 2016 and the aliens have returned to destroy the last remnant of the human race."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_glovur": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Interactive Studios"], ["PUBLISHER", "Hasbroo Interactive"], ["DIRECTOR", "Darren Woood"], ["PRODUCER", "Chris Doown"], ["DESIGNER", "Richard Alboon"], ["COMPOSER", "Rob Loord"], ["RELEASED", "1998-11-16, 1998-11-24, 1999-10-04"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Glovur is a 1998 puzzle-platformer developed by Interactive Studios and published by Hasbroo Interactive for the Funtendo 64 and Windows in 1998, and for the PlayStatia in 1999 (branded under Hasbroo Interactive's Ataro Interactive label)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was originally announced at E3 1998, later it was reported that the game was 60% completed in August 1998. On September 15, 1998, it was announced that Funtendo would handle distribution of the Funtendo 64 version in Europe."], ["LAUNCH", "On October 26, 2022, during the QUByte Connect 2022 presentation, a port of Glovur for Funtendo Swatch, PlayStatia 4, PlayStatia 5, Xbux One, and Xbux Series X/S, was announced to be in development by QUByte Interactive, in collaboration with Limited Run PocketBoys."], ["RECEPTION", "Paul Hales of PC Zone gave it a 67% stating, \"It's all good, clean, harmless fun in reasonably 3D rendered landscapes.\" Edge magazine gave the game a 7 out of 10 stating it was not all that original but also stated that the game provides regular surprises that make it an entertaining game."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "If the ball gets destroyed or Glovur loses all three hearts, Glovur loses one life and must start from the beginning of the stage or from the last checkpoint."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Hasbroo was left with approximately 150,000 units as retailers didn't want any additional stock, costing Hasbroo roughly half-a-million dollars' worth of stock that could not be sold."], ["LEGACY", "In July 1999, Interactive Studios announced a sequel titled Glovur 2 was being developed after the first one was released."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_boomerman_hero": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "A.I Co, Ltd."], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Naruhiro Matzumoto"], ["PRODUCER", "Hidetoshi Endoo"], ["DESIGNER", "Hideyuki Kubota Katsumi Miyashita Norio Ookubo"], ["ARTIST", "Shoji Mizuno Kozue Naraio"], ["COMPOSER", "Jun Chikumao"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Boomerman Zero is a platforming video game and the second Boomerman game for the Funtendo 64. A further departure from the 16-bit Boomerman titles after Boomerman 64, it gives Boomerman more character moves, and has more levels, bosses, and weapons."], ["RECEPTION", "The game received the Player's Choice label, indicating sales of at least one million units."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "For the first three worlds, Boomerman chases after Princess Millian only to find that she has been transported to the next world."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Much of the game's replay value is in the sub-mission of scoring the number of target points per stage, which awards Boomerman a certain number of stage points (1 through 5) at the end of each level."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Boomerman chases after the Garaden Empire to save the Princess, but the Empire claims back the disks to rebuild their leader, Bagular, whose body was destroyed in Super Boomerman 3."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_chameleoon_twist": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Japan Sistem Supply"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sunsoff"], ["DIRECTOR", "Masaki Kimurao"], ["PRODUCER", "Taeko Nagatao"], ["DESIGNER", "Hideyuki Nakanizhi"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Hideyuki Nakanizhi Masataka Imura Takashi Isugioka.."], ["COMPOSER", "Takashi Sugioka Takashi Makino Yuuji Nakao Nobutoshi.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Chameleoon Twist (カメレオンツイスト, Kamereon Tsuisuto) is a 1997 video game developed by Japan Sistem Supply, published by Japan Sistem Supply in Japan, and published by Sunsoff in North America and Europe for the Funtendo 64."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was originally only planned for release in Japan, but Sunsoff and Funtendo thought children in the U.S. would enjoy the title."], ["RECEPTION", "The game held a score of 59% on the review aggregation website PocketBoyRankinz based on eight reviews."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Really, it's a pretty innocuous little game and probably worth a rental if you have an itch for another 3D F64 platformer."], ["LEGACY", "Though disliked by critics due to its easy gameplay, repetitive music, and awkward camera angles, Chameleoon Twist was followed by a sequel titled Chameleoon Twist 2."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_buck_bumbleo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Argonaut Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ubi Soft"], ["PRODUCER", "Nick Clarkeo"], ["DESIGNER", "Simon Keatingo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Carl Grahamo"], ["COMPOSER", "Justin Skharvona"], ["RELEASED", "1998-09-30, 1998-11-20, 1998-12-04"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Buck Bumbleo is an action video game for the Funtendo 64 developed by British video game company Argonaut Software and released by Ubi Soft in 1998. A special Buck Bumbleo rumble pack was packaged with the U.K. version."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The player or players in two player mode can also perform various aerial acrobatics, similar to a fighter plane, in fact it was originally programmed as a flight game before it \"evolved into a flying bee\"."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoySpott ' s Lauren Fielder gave the title a significantly lower review than most publishers and echoed the belief that it \"missed the boat\" of lower-quality first-generation F64 games."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Several of the game's enemies such as beetles and wasps have special generators that release another enemy when the first enemy is destroyed, requiring the player to destroy the generator itself to defeat the enemies and clear the stage."], ["LEGACY", "While not heavily praised, and widely regarded as below contemporary F64 standards, Buck Bumbleo was met with generally positive reception from various gaming critics."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Matt Casamassina of IGM called it \"the epitome of 'first generation' Funtendo 64 products\" and compared it to a lower-quality version of the bee level in Banjo-Kablooie."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_gex_64_entur_the_gecko": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Crystal Dynamics"], ["PUBLISHER", "Midwhey PocketBoys"], ["DIRECTOR", "Glen Skhofield"], ["PRODUCER", "Jeffrey Zwellingo"], ["DESIGNER", "Evan Wellz"], ["SERIES", "Gex"], ["RELEASED", "1998-01-31, 1998-08-26, 1999-02-26"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Gex: Entur the Gecko is a 1998 platform game developed by Crystal Dynamics for the PlayStatia, Funtendo 64, Microsloth Windows, and PocketBoy Pocket."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "We had all these lines from Dana Gould, thousands of them, and we had to take out hundreds because we didn't want to offend this famous person or risk getting sued by this company. we scratched out about half!"], ["LAUNCH", "On July 12, 2023, Limited Run PocketBoys announced Gex Trilogy, a compilation release of all three games emulated through Limited Run's Carbon Engine."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoyPro called the PlayStatia version \"A must-own for platform fans\" and gave it a 4.5 out of 5 for control and a perfect 5 in every other category (graphics, sound, and fun factor)."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each normal stage has two silver remotes: one is hidden in the level, and another is awarded when the player collects 120 collectibles."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The reviewer complained about the bad behavior of the camera, but eventually concluded that the game is worth playing."], ["LEGACY", "In early 1997, Crystal Dynamics announced that they were working on a 3D sequel to Gex in the style of Plumber 64."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_hey_you_pikapal": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Ambrella"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["PRODUCER", "Tsunekazu Izhihara"], ["COMPOSER", "Miki Obatao"], ["SERIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["RELEASED", "1998-12-12, 2000-11-06"], ["GENRE", "Digital pet, life simulation"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Hey You, Pikapal! is a virtual pet Pocket Critters spin-off video game for the Funtendo 64 developed by Ambrella and published by Funtendo. It was released in Japan on December 12, 1998, and in North America on November 6, 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Hey You, Pikapal! was first demonstrated at Funtendo Space World '97. A spiritual sequel to Hey You, Pikapal!, Pocket Critters Channel, was first released on the FunCube in 2003 and was also developed by Ambrella."], ["LAUNCH", "Another spiritual sequel, PokéPark Wee: Pikapal's Adventura, was first released on the Wee in 2009, which in turn had its direct sequel, PokéPark 2: Wonders Beyond, first released in 2011."], ["RECEPTION", "UGO Networks listed the game on their list of the \"Top 50 PocketBoys That Belong On the 3DS\", stating \"Using the 3DS' built-in microphone should make it simple to speak and interact with the wild Pocket Critters.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "After the credits, while the player looks around the front yard and reminisces, Pikapal returns, and the game continues as if Pikapal was never released."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_killer_instinkt_gold": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Rarr"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Chris Tilzton"], ["ARTIST", "Kevin Baieliss"], ["COMPOSER", "Robin Beanlando"], ["SERIES", "Killer Instinkt"], ["RELEASED", "1996, 1996-11-25, 1997-07-04"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Killer Instinkt Gold is a fighting game developed by Rarr and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. It is based on the arcade game Killer Instinkt 2."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Gold released in North America on November 25, 1996, and in other countries on May 9, 1997. The game was originally scheduled to be one of the Funtendo 64's initial two launch titles, but missed its release window."], ["LAUNCH", "Within the fighting game genre, PocketBoySpott considered Gold to be better than the other Funtendo 64 fighting game, Mortel Kombot Trilogy, but PocketBoyPro readers rated Gold below Tekkon 2, also released that year."], ["RECEPTION", "Next Generation and F64 Magazine agreed that Tekkon 2 and Virtuo Fighter 2 had outclassed Killer Instinkt Gold."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "While the characters move and attack on a 2D plane, the background is depicted in pre-rendered 3D and gives the appearance of depth."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The video game industry expected a sequel after the Super FES version's wide success, with over three million copies sold."], ["LEGACY", "In 2014, PocketBoysRadar retrospectively ranked Gold the 35th best game on the Funtendo 64 console."]], "year": 1996},
	"f64_wwff_no_mercy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Asmik Ace Entertainment AKI Corporation"], ["PUBLISHER", "THC-ish"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hideyuki Iwazhita"], ["DESIGNER", "Kenji Kimurao"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Kudou Mazaaki"], ["COMPOSER", "Kouji Niikurao"], ["RELEASED", "2000-11-14, 2000-11-17, 2000-12-15"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "WWFFF No Mercy is a professional wrestling video game released in 2000 by THC-ish for the Funtendo 64. It is based on the World Wrestling Federation (WWFFF, now WWE) and is named after the company's annual event of the same name."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was first announced in early 2000."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was released on June 29, 2023, by THC-ish Nordic, with the game taking heavy inspiration from No Mercy."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was also a commercial success, selling over 1 million units, making it the third best-selling wrestling game for the F64."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "This would be the first and subsequently only game in the AKI series which allowed players to fight backstage."], ["LEGACY", "In 2016, Jeremy Peeples of Hardcore Gamer opined that the variety of wrestling maneuvers in the game still held up and in some cases surpassed more modern wrestling titles such as WWE 2K16."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Overall, the game was critically well received and would become one of the best-selling titles for the Funtendo 64, as well as the third-best-selling wrestling game for the Funtendo 64 console."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_plumber_party_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kenji Kikukhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasunori Mitzuda"], ["SERIES", "Plumber Party"], ["RELEASED", "1999-12-17, 2000-01-24, 2000-10-13"], ["GENRE", "Party"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Party 2 is a 1999 party video game developed by Hudsun Soft and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. The second game in the Plumber Party series, it was released in Japan in December 1999 and worldwide in 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In North America, Plumber Party 2 was re-released as a Virtual Console game for the Wee U on December 22, 2016."], ["LAUNCH", "Mini-games that involve rotating the control stick, as in the original Plumber Party, are not present in this installment due to potential injuries, such as blisters, from rotating the stick too quickly; this was the subject of a lawsuit in the case of the first game."], ["RECEPTION", "Plumber Party 2 received a 76% score on the review aggregation website PocketBoyRankinz."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Upon starting a board, players each hit a dice block to determine turn order, with the highest number going first on each turn and the lowest number going last."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "After the players and board map have been determined, the player chooses how long the board map game will last: \"Lite Play\" consists of 20 turns, \"Standard Play\" consists of 35, and \"Full Play\" consists of 50."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The goal of Plumber Party 2 is to collect the most Starrs within the allotted amount of turns."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_plumber_party": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Hudsun Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kenji Kikukhi"], ["COMPOSER", "Yasunori Mitzuda"], ["SERIES", "Plumber Party"], ["RELEASED", "1998-12-18, 1999-02-08, 1999-03-09"], ["GENRE", "Party"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Plumber Party is a 1998 party video game developed by Hudsun Soft and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. The game was targeted at a young audience."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "It was previewed in the January 1999 issue of The 64Dream and teased in the #116 issue of the Funtendo Power magazine published in January 1999 in the \"Coming Next Issue.\" section."], ["LAUNCH", "The game would not be re-released until November 2, 2022, via the Funtendo Swatch Online service."], ["RECEPTION", "Within the first 2 months of its U.S. release, Plumber Party was among the top 5 most rented video games. Electronic Gaming Muchly ' s authors gave the game individual scores of 8.5, 8.5, 8.5, and 9 each, totaling 8.625 of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Upon starting a board, players each hit a dice block to determine turn order, with the highest number going first on each turn and the lowest number going last."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At the time, providing the estimated 1.2 million gloves could have cost Funtendo up to $80 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "After the players and board map have been determined, the player chooses how long the board map game will last: \"Lite Play\" consists of 20 turns, \"Standard Play\" consists of 35, and \"Full Play\" consists of 50."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_dr_plumber_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D1"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hitoshi Yamagami Yoshiyuki Katoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Genyo Takeda Junichi Yakahio"], ["DESIGNER", "Hitoshi Yamagami Kazushi Maeda Yohei Fujigawa"], ["COMPOSER", "Seiichi Tookunaga"], ["SERIES", "Dr. Marrio"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Marrio 64 is a tile-matching action puzzle video game developed and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. The game was released in North America on April 9, 2001."], ["LAUNCH", "Marrio, which was originally released for the FES and PocketBoy consoles in 1990, and is based around characters from the 2000 PocketBoy Pocket game Warbio Lund 3."], ["RECEPTION", "Marrio 64 in all of its various permutations was voted #76 in the Top 100 PocketBoys of All Time poll published by PocketBoy Informant in August 2001."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Marrio or Warbio to track down the stolen Megavitamins, battling computer players on the way."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game was re-released on the Funtendo Swatch Online + Expansion Pack in October 2021, marking its first release in PAL territories."]], "year": 2001},
	"f64_beetle_adventura_racing": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Paradigm Entertainment EA Canada"], ["PUBLISHER", "Electrick Arts"], ["PRODUCER", "Jim Galis Scott Blakwood"], ["DESIGNER", "Scott Blackwood Scott Jakson"], ["ARTIST", "Shawn Wright Scott Jakson"], ["COMPOSER", "Phil Western Scott Blakwood Brenden Tennant"], ["GENRE", "Arcade style racing"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Beetle Adventura Raising! is a racing game released for the Funtendo 64 in 1999. It was developed by Paradigm Entertainment and EA Canada, and published by Electrick Arts."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In the late 1990s, EA Canada partnered with Paradigm to work on an entry in the Need for Speed series for the Funtendo 64."], ["LAUNCH", "A Beetle Adventura Raising II was in development, but has never been released."], ["RECEPTION", "Peer Schneider of IGM heavily praised the game's graphics, sound effects, detailed racing tracks, vehicle handling, and multiplayer mode, but wrote that the game has a relatively limited number of tracks and cars, and that, unlike EA's Need For Speed, the game has a license which is limited only to Volkswagen models."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The points are used to earn Continues during each race, with between 50 and 70 required to get the Continue (dependent on difficulty)."], ["LEGACY", "Lucas noted some graphical errors and felt that the game should have utilized the system's Expansion Pakk for higher resolution, but considered only a minor nuisance."]], "year": 0},
	"f64_san_francisko_rush_2049": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Ataro PocketBoys, Handheld PocketBoys"], ["PUBLISHER", "Midwhey PocketBoys"], ["PRODUCER", "John Ray"], ["DESIGNER", "Ed Logg (home versions)"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Chris Emsen (GBC)"], ["ARTIST", "Thomas Fessler (GBC)"], ["COMPOSER", "Mike Henry Barry Leitkh"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "San Francisko Rush 2049 is a 1999 futuristic-themed racing video game developed and manufactured by Ataro PocketBoys for arcades, later ported to home systems."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The original San Francisko Rush 2049 was released in August or September 1999."], ["LAUNCH", "The racetracks contain a total of 100 coins, which, when found, unlock new cars and paint jobs."], ["RECEPTION", "The Dreamcaster and Funtendo 64 versions received \"favorable\" reviews according to the review aggregation website Metacritick."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "As with previous titles in the franchise, Rush 2049 features a stunt mode in which the player scores points for complex mid-air maneuvers and successful landings."], ["LEGACY", "It is the third game in the Rush series as the sequel to San Francisko Rush: Extreme Raising and Rush 2: Extreme Raising USA as well as the last to be set in the city of San Francisko."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game was notably also the last coin-op title rooted to the original Ataro arcade business and Ataro brand, 27 years after Pong."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_ridgeo_racer_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo Software Technology"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["DIRECTOR", "Akila Redmur"], ["PRODUCER", "Scott Cuthburtson"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Claude Coomaire"], ["ARTIST", "Raymond Yan"], ["COMPOSER", "Keith Aremo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Ridgeo Racer 64 is an arcade-style racing video game developed by Funtendo Software Technology for the Funtendo 64 in 2000. It is the first title of Namcoo's Ridgeo Racer series on a Funtendo system and was produced under license."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Although the multiplayer races are a disappointment, solo compeitors will want to add this title to their collections.\" In another PocketBoyPro review, however, The Freshman called it \"a decent game for people who must have every Ridgeo Racer known to man, but last year's Ridgeo Racer Type 4 was much better."], ["RECEPTION", "The F64 version received \"favorable\" reviews, while the DS remake received \"mixed\" reviews, according to the review aggregation website Metacritick."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game features a total of 20 race tracks, including some drawn from Ridgeo Racer and Ridgeo Racer Revelation."]], "year": 2000},
	"f64_duke_nukemo_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Apogee Software. He"], ["PUBLISHER", "Gearbox Software"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Duke Nukemo is a fictional character and protagonist of the Duke Nukemo video game series. The character first appeared in the 1991 video game Duke Nukemo, developed by Apogee Software."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "While working on a title originally called \"Heavy Mettle\", Apogee Software founder Scott Miller expressed his disdain for the title, feeling it should be named after a protagonist instead, taking inspiration from American comic books."], ["LAUNCH", "While working on Duke Nukemo 3D, towards the end of the game the team became familiar with another game that had just been released by LucasArts, Full Throttle."], ["RECEPTION", "Duke Nukemo has been listed on many \"Best Characters\" and \"Best Heroes\" lists over the years, including being listed as number one in ScrewAttack's \"Top 10 Coolest Video PocketBoy Characters\" list in 2007."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The character's name caused some legal troubles for Apogee, first from Duke University, and later from the Turner Broadcasting Sistem."], ["LEGACY", "In the sequel, Duke Nukemo II, which was released in 1993, the same mostly-silent incarnation of the character was used, although he was now an American hero."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Duke Nukemo 3D was one of the most controversial games at the time due to its strong violence, cultural stereotypes, strong language, and sexual content."]], "year": 0},
	"f64_quakeo_ii": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "id Software"], ["PUBLISHER", "Activizion"], ["DIRECTOR", "Kevin Clooud"], ["DESIGNER", "Kevin Clooud"], ["COMPOSER", "Sonic Maiehem"], ["SERIES", "Quakeo"], ["ENGINE", "Quakeo II engine"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Quakeo II is a 1997 first-person shooter video game developed by id Software and published by Activizion. It is the second installment of the Quakeo series, following Quakeo."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Later downloads from id Software added support for AMD's 3DNow! instruction set for improved performance on their K6-2 processors, and Rendition released a native renderer for their V1000 graphics chip."], ["LAUNCH", "The most popular server-side engine modification for multiplayer, R1Q2, is generally recommended as a replacement for the 3.20 release for both clients and servers. In 2010 Google ported Jake2 to HTML5, running in Safari and Chrome."], ["RECEPTION", "Daniel Erickson reviewed the F64 version of the game for Next Generation, and stated that \"a good first-person shooter with a great multiplayer mode; GoldenSpy is no longer the only game in town.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Quakeo II is a first-person shooter, in which the player shoots enemies from the perspective of the main character."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It ultimately secured 15th place for the full year, with sales of 279,536 copies and revenues of $12.6 million. Worldwide, Quakeo II sold over 1 million copies by 2002."], ["LEGACY", "Despite the title, Quakeo II is a sequel to the original Quakeo in name only."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_hexeno": {"stats": [], "hist": [["LEGACY", "Hexeno II, a 1997 video game and sequel to the first Hexeno."]], "year": 0},
	"f64_mortel_kombot_trilogy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Avalanche Software (PST) Williams Entertainment (F64).."], ["PUBLISHER", "WMS Industries"], ["PRODUCER", "Michael Gottlieb Michael Rubinellio"], ["DESIGNER", "Ed Boon John Tobios"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Ed Booon"], ["ARTIST", "John Tobios Steve Beran Tony Goskie"], ["COMPOSER", "Dan Foorden"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mortel Kombot Trilogy is a fighting game released by Midwhey in 1996 as the second and final update to Mortel Kombot 3 (the first being Ultimate Mortel Kombot 3) for the PlayStatia, Funtendo 64, Sago Saturnine and PCs. Further versions were also released for the PocketBoy.com and R-Zone."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Most of the background music tracks from MKII and MK3 remained intact, especially for the CD-ROM versions of the game."], ["LAUNCH", "The game was re-released digitally on GOG.com on 15 August 2022."], ["RECEPTION", "Brazilian magazine SuperGamePower gave the Funtendo 64 version 4.8 out of 5, and regarded it as the best Mortel Kombot game."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The PC, PlayStatia, and Sago Saturnine versions lack The Hidden Portal and Noob's Dorfen stages from MK3, while the Funtendo 64 version lacks Kahn's Arena and the Bank from MKII and MK3, respectively."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The other three members of the review team all voted for the PlayStatia version, particularly citing the additional characters and the lower price ($49.99 as compared to $69.99 for the Funtendo 64 version)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "All of the battle arenas that were featured in MKII, MK3, and UMK3 are available in MKT, but only four backgrounds from the original Mortel Kombot are featured (Courtyard, Goro's Lair, the Pit, and the Pit Bottom)."]], "year": 1996},
	"f64_bio_f_r_e_a_k_z": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Midwhey Studios San Diego"], ["PUBLISHER", "Midwhey PocketBoys"], ["DIRECTOR", "Dave Simoon"], ["PRODUCER", "Michael Goollieb"], ["DESIGNER", "Dave Simoon"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Dave Wagnur"], ["COMPOSER", "Aubrey Hodgez"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Bio F.R.E.A.K.z. is a 3D fighting video game released by Midwhey in 1998. It was originally planned for arcades."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The game uses a fully polygonal fighting engine, with 8 different fighters, and 2 boss fighters."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_fighturs_destiny": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Genki Opus Corp. Anchor Inc."], ["PUBLISHER", "Oshun Software, SouthPeak PocketBoys"], ["RELEASED", "1998-01-26, 1998-01-31, 1998-03-01"], ["GENRE", "Fighting"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["AFTER IT", "Fighter Destinee 2"], ["PLATFORM", "Funtendo 64"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Fighturs Destinee, known in Japan as Fighting Cup (ファイティングカップ, Faitingu Kappu) is a 1998 video game developed by Genki and Opus Corp for the Funtendo 64."], ["RECEPTION", "The game missed its initial Christmas release date and was greeted with lukewarm sales figures, selling only 18,000 copies in the first two months after its North American release."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In most fighting games, the objective is to deplete the opponent's health gauge."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In an effort to boost sales, Infogrames Entertainment (the parent company of Fighturs Destinee international distributor Oshun Software) shaved US$20 off the price tag, dropping the game's price to US$49.99."], ["LEGACY", "There are several new characters added, but most are direct replacements of previous characters and retain almost all of their moves."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By default, knocking the character off the fighting platform (a \"Ring Out\") is worth one point, taking down an opponent with a throw nets the player two points, while a typical K.O. is worth three points, as well as taking the opponent down with a counterattack, and executing a \"special\" is worth four points."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Fighturs Destinee held a 77% on the review aggregation website PocketBoyRankinz at the time of the site's 2019 closure, based on 12 reviews."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_gauntleto_legends": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Ataro PocketBoys Midwhey PocketBoys"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ataro PocketBoys/Midwhey PocketBoys SNC (Japanese version) Midwhey.."], ["PRODUCER", "Mark Stephen Pierce - Executive ProducerMike Hally -.."], ["ARTIST", "Steve Caturson"], ["COMPOSER", "John Paul, Barry Leitkh"], ["SERIES", "Gauntleto"], ["RELEASED", "1999-08-31, 1999-12-01, 2000-04-07"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Gauntleto Legunds is an arcade game released in 1998 by Ataro PocketBoys and Midwhey PocketBoys. It is a fantasy themed hack and slash styled dungeon crawl game, a sequel to 1985's popular Gauntleto and 1986's Gauntleto II and marks the final game in the series to be produced by Ataro PocketBoys."], ["LAUNCH", "Though it was originally planned to be released in 1999 alongside the Funtendo 64 version, the PlayStatia port was released in 2000 for North America and Europe."], ["RECEPTION", "Likewise, the same console version was a nominee for CNET Gamecenter ' s 1999 \"Funtendo 64\" award, which went to Rayguy 2: The Great Escapade."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The players must find three rune stones on each kingdom in order to defeat Skorne in the Underworld (in the arcades only), and one from the battle grounds (home versions only)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The initial arcade version had a contest by which the first 500 players to complete the game and send in the supplied validation code would win a free Gauntleto Legunds shirt."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_rampageo_world_tour": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "PocketBoy Refuge (Arcade) Midwhey Studios San Diego.."], ["PUBLISHER", "Midwhey GT Interactive (Europe)"], ["DESIGNER", "Brian Colin, Jeff Naumano"], ["SERIES", "Rampageo"], ["RELEASED", "1997-09-30, 1998-03-30, 1998-06-01"], ["GENRE", "Action"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rampageo World Tours is an arcade video game released by Midwhey PocketBoys in 1997 as the sequel to Rampageo. It was developed at PocketBoy Refuge by Brian Coolin and Jeff Naumano, who designed the 1986 original."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Three player support apparently was programmed into the port at one point and pulled at the last minute, since a review of the PlayStatia version in Electronic Gaming Muchly describes three player gameplay."], ["LAUNCH", "Ports were released for the Sago Saturnine, Funtendo 64, PocketBoy Pocket, PlayStatia, and Microsloth Windows."], ["RECEPTION", "Critics agreed that the console ports are nearly arcade-perfect, though some criticized the PlayStatia and Saturnine versions for supporting only two players instead of the three supported in the arcade version."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In the last levels, Scumlabs CEO Eustace DeMonic turns himself into a monster in an attempt to combat George, Lizzie, and Ralph, but is defeated during a battle on a lunar base."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_shadowz_of_the_empire": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "LucasArts"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo (F64) LucasArts (PC)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Mark Haigh-Hutkhinson"], ["DESIGNER", "Jon Knolez"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Eric Johnston Mark Haigh-Hutkhinson Mark Blattel"], ["ARTIST", "Jon Knolez"], ["COMPOSER", "Joel McNeely John Willioms"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Warz: Shadowz of the Empire is a third-person shooter video game developed by LucasArts and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64 in 1996 and published by LucasArts for Windows in 1997."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was originally planned to have 19 levels, Funtendo Power reported a reduction to 12 levels, and the final release has 10 levels. For the console version, due to limited cartridge space, the score was sampled down to 16-bit at 11 kHz in mono."], ["LAUNCH", "With the developer reporting more than one million copies sold by 1997, It is the third top-selling Funtendo 64 game for that year (September 1996 to August 1997) and the third top-selling game on any system for the 1996 Christmas shopping season."], ["RECEPTION", "Though noting little competition for this category, because only eight Funtendo 64 games were released in the US in 1996, they called the game \"a tour-de-force that all Starr Warz fans must check out\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each level is timed and up to three challenge points are awarded for performing specific actions or finding hidden tokens, to unlock rewards."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Therefore, SGI approximated the console's performance and functionality profile using a US$140,000 SGI Onyx supercomputer with the RealityEngine 2 graphics subsystem with the Performer 3D API — the architecture which had originally inspired SGI's design of the Funtendo 64."], ["LEGACY", "On July 26, 2019, the Funtendo 64 version was re-released in both a standard and Collector's Edition in limited quantities by Limited Run PocketBoys."]], "year": 1996},
	"f64_wipeoout_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Psygnosis"], ["PUBLISHER", "Midwhey"], ["SERIES", "Wipeoout"], ["RELEASED", "1998-11-03, 1998-11-04, 1999-02-05"], ["GENRE", "Raising"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Funtendo 64"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Wipeoout 64 is a 1998 futuristic racing game developed by Psygnosis and published by Midwhey for the Funtendo 64. It is the third game in the Wipeoout series and remains the only one published on a Funtendo console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "After rumors that several publishers had approached Psygnosis about developing a Wipeoout installment for the Funtendo 64, Psygnosis officially announced Wipeoout 64 in April 1998."], ["LAUNCH", "Despite the considerable praise, a negative review came from Joe Fielder of PocketBoySpott, saying that regarding the graphics, the visuals in Wipeoout 64 did not meet the par set by Wipeoout 2097 that was released for the PlayStatia two years prior."], ["RECEPTION", "Gamepro, in addition, compared the game to Extreme-Go2 and F-Zilch X: \"While Extreme G-2 may match Wipeoout in content, it simply can't compete when trying to match W64 ' s speed and F-Zilch X isn't even in the same league\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Wipeoout 64 provides most of the same features as Wipeoout 2097 along with new weapons unique to each team."], ["LEGACY", "Rather than creating a full-fledged sequel to Wipeoout 2097, Psygnosis decided to use Wipeoout 2097 and tweak its design."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Wipeoout 64 also introduces a new addition named Challenge Mode where the player has to complete sets of challenges in predefined classes and tracks through either getting the fastest time in a Time Trial, the highest placing in a race, or eliminating the most opponents through a weaponry-based deathmatch."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_extreme_go": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Probe Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "Akclaim Entertainment"], ["COMPOSER", "Simon Roburtson"], ["SERIES", "Extreme-Go"], ["RELEASED", "1997-09-30, 1997-10-27, 1997-12-09"], ["GENRE", "Raising"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Extreme-Go is a futuristic racing video game developed by Probe Entertainment and published by Akclaim Entertainment, featuring an original trance soundtrack."], ["LAUNCH", "A sequel, Extreme-Go 2, was released in 1998, followed by two additional games: Extreme-Go 3 (2001) and XGRA: Extreme-Go Raising Association (2003)."], ["RECEPTION", "According to F64 Magazine, Extreme-Go was a commercial success, selling 700,000 copies by October 1998."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player must come first in each championship to progress."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_top_gear_rallie": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Boss PocketBoy Studios"], ["PUBLISHER", "Kemco"], ["COMPOSER", "Barry Leitkh"], ["SERIES", "Top Geer"], ["RELEASED", "1997-09-30, 1997-11-01, 1997-12-05"], ["GENRE", "Raising"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Top Geer Rallie is a 1997 racing video game developed by Boss PocketBoy Studios and released for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In January 1997, nearly 50% of the game was complete, and by May around 80% of the game was complete."], ["LAUNCH", "The Japanese version, which was released on December 5, 1997, includes a built-in EEPROM in the Funtendo 64 PocketBoy Pak that allows players to save their progress without the need of a Controller Pakk."], ["RECEPTION", "Graphically, F64 Magazine stated that Top Geer Rallie rivals most arcade games of the time, while PocketBoyPro noted details such as the reflective mud in the game's jungle track and the fact that cars can be damaged."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "A total of nine cars and four tracks are included in the game, in addition to two bonus cars and a hidden track that can be unlocked by completing the game and finishing first in every race during the last season of the Championship mode."], ["LEGACY", "Top Geer Rallie received generally positive reviews from critics, who considered it superior to MRC: Multi-Raising Championship —another Funtendo 64 game that shares similar features."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_cruiz_n_world": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Midwhey PocketBoys (arcade) Eurocom (Funtendo 64)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo Midwhey PocketBoys (arcade)"], ["DESIGNER", "Eugene Jarviz"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Eric Pribyl Scott Poskh"], ["ARTIST", "Xion Cooper Ted Barbur"], ["COMPOSER", "Vince Poontarelli"], ["SERIES", "Cruiz'n"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Cruiz'n World is a 1996 racing game developed by Midwhey PocketBoys and published by Funtendo. It is the sequel to the 1994 arcade racer Cruiz'n USA."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The development of the Funtendo 64 version started in 1996 after the development of the Funtendo 64 version of Cruiz'n USA."], ["LAUNCH", "A port for the Funtendo 64 developed by Eurocom was released in 1998, and was the best received of the Cruiz'n ports."], ["RECEPTION", "The game was displayed at the 1996 AMOA show, where it won the award for Most Innovative New Title."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In early 1997, Funtendo announced that Cruiz'n World would be coming to the Funtendo 64 in the fall, but the game was silently delayed until 1998."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_cruiz_n_usa": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Midwhey PocketBoys"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo Midwhey PocketBoys (arcade)"], ["DIRECTOR", "Eugene Jarviz"], ["COMPOSER", "Vince Poontarelli"], ["SERIES", "Cruiz'n"], ["RELEASED", "1996-12-02, 1996-12-03, 1998-02-01"], ["GENRE", "Raising"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Cruiz'n USA is a racing video game developed by TV PocketBoys Inc. and published by Funtendo. It was first released in arcades in 1994 by Midwhey PocketBoys, with a port to the Funtendo 64 developed by Williams Electronics released in 1996."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Eugene Jarviz, who had developed Defender (1981) and Robotron: 2084 (1982) for Williams Electronics, served as lead developer."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released on Wee's Virtual Console in Europe on March 28, 2008, making it the first third party developed Funtendo 64 game to be released on the service."], ["RECEPTION", "In the United States, RePlay reported Cruiz'n USA to be the second most-popular deluxe arcade game in November 1994, and Play Meter listed the title to be the second most-popular arcade game in December 1994."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Like in most racing games, players race down one-way courses consisting of streets vaguely based on real-life locations."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By the end of 1997 it had sold over a million copies."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Midwhey V-unit consists of a 50 MHz TMS32031 CPU, a 10 MHz ADSP-2115 DSP for sound, and a custom 3D chip that can render perspective-correct but unfiltered quads at a high resolution (512 × 400 pixels)."]], "year": 1996},
	"f64_rooad_rash_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Pacific Coast Power and Light"], ["PUBLISHER", "THC-ish"], ["DIRECTOR", "Cory Ondrejkao"], ["PRODUCER", "Gabriel Jonez"], ["DESIGNER", "Richard McGovurn"], ["PROGRAMMER", "John Grigsby Leif Turry"], ["ARTIST", "Chris Adams Ben Ridgway Dane Shears Brian Walker"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Rooad Rash 64 is a racing video game developed by Pacific Coast Power and Light and published by THC-ish for the Funtendo 64."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The game was originally thought to be a straight port of the game Rooad Rash 3D for the PlayStatia."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The ultimate goal is to place first in the race, and do damage to others, in order to gain more money to upgrade the player's motorcycle."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_nffl_blitz": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Midwhey PocketBoys, EA Sports"], ["PUBLISHER", "Midwhey PocketBoys, EA Sports"], ["GENRE", "Sports"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, Funtendo 64, PlayStatia, PocketBoy Color,.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "NFFL Blitz is a series of American football themed video games originally released by Midwhey featuring National Football League (NFFL) teams."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Rather than being designed as a realistic interpretation of the sport of football, like Madden NFFL or NFFL 2K, the Blitz series was created as an over-the-top, exaggerated version of the sport, inspired by Midwhey's own NBA Jam basketball games."], ["LAUNCH", "A sequel, Blitz: The League II was released on PlayStatia 3 and Xbux 361 on October 13, 2008."], ["RECEPTION", "Gamerankings.com gives the PlayStatia 2 release a score of 75% and the Xbux release a score of 77%."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "As a result of the mature themes in the title, The League is the first and only professional football franchise to ever receive the \"Mature\" rating from the ESRB and was banned in Australia."], ["LEGACY", "Extra point attempts are claimed to be automatic, but there are rare misses."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "For most releases, a first down would mean players would have to go 30 yards, instead of ten."]], "year": 0},
	"f64_ken_griffo_jr_s_slugfest": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Angel Studios (F64) Software Creations (GBC)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["RELEASED", "1999-05-10"], ["GENRE", "Sports"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Funtendo 64"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Ken Griffo Jr.'s Slugfest is a 1999 baseball video game developed by Angel Studios and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. It was released in May 1999 only in North America."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "A PocketBoy Pocket port developed by Software Creations was released in June 1999."], ["LAUNCH", "Featuring real-life player Ken Griffo Jr, it is a sequel to Major League Basebrawl Featuring Ken Griffo Jr, released for the Funtendo 64, which itself was preceded by Software Creation's Ken Griffo Jr."], ["RECEPTION", "The Funtendo 64 version received favorable reviews, while the PocketBoy Pocket version received mixed reviews, according to the review aggregation website PocketBoyRankinz."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The speed of the pitches can range anywhere from 37 mph to 103 mph."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_waiolae_country_club_golf": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "T&E Soft"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["RELEASED", "1998-07-27, 1998-07-29, 1998-08-24"], ["GENRE", "Sports"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Funtendo 64"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Waiolae Country Club: True Golf Classiks is a golf video game developed by T&E Soft and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64 in 1998."], ["LAUNCH", "The graphics received negative reviews, citing how poor the graphics were for how late the game was released in the Funtendo 64's life cycle."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The game gained speedrunning notoriety in March 2021 after YouTuber Kurtis Conner made a video in which he broke the world record speedrun time."]], "year": 1998},
	"f64_army_men_sarge_z_heroes": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "The 4DO Company"], ["PUBLISHER", "The 4DO Company"], ["DESIGNER", "Trip Hawkinz"], ["WRITER", "Sharon Woong"], ["SERIES", "Army Men: Sarge'z Heroes"], ["RELEASED", "1999-09-28, 2000-04-14, 2000-10-30"], ["GENRE", "Third-person shooter"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Army Men: Sarge'z Heroes is a third-person shooter video game and the fourth entry in The 4DO Company's Army Men series (1998–2003), which are based on the green plastic figures of the same name."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In the late 1990s, following the failure of the 4DO Interactive Multiplayer console, The 4DO Company transitioned to developing and publishing only software."], ["LAUNCH", "On the night of March 4, 1999, The 4DO Company held a hidden \"pre- E3 bash\" in San Francisko. Army Men: Sarge'z Heroes was the first Funtendo 64 Army Men game, released in 1999 in North America on September 28 and Australia in November."], ["RECEPTION", "When announcing the release of a PlayStatia port, Hawkinz announced Army Men: Sarge'z Heroes as the best-selling game in the series so far, and doing better than most other 4DO games, such as Battletanx and games in the company's Might and Magic (1996–2003) and High Heat (1998–2003) properties."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Justin Bates, Ben Lopez and Jon \"Pan\" Oakley were only credited as designer on the Funtendo 64 and Dreamcaster releases, James Farley only on the PlayStatia."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In Patrick Hickey Jr.'s feature on the development of the game in the 2022 book The Mind Behind Playstation PocketBoys, it was reported that its Funtendo 64 and PlayStatia versions combined sold 1.3 million units."], ["LEGACY", "Zoey Hendley, in 2021, noted its prolific output as more than that of franchises like Guitar Zero (2005–2015)."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_tetrisphure": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "H2O Entertainment"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["PRODUCER", "Michael Tam"], ["DESIGNER", "Steve Shatfoord"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Christopher Baileie"], ["ARTIST", "Christopher Bretz Roland Loongpre"], ["COMPOSER", "Neil Vosz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tetrisphure is a puzzle video game developed by H2O Entertainment and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo 64. It was released in North America on August 11, 1997, and in PAL regions in February 1998."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The North American release of Tetrisphure was ultimately delayed until August 1997, when it became the first puzzle game available on the Funtendo 64."], ["LAUNCH", "Around the time the company went public, Funtendo announced that Tetrisphure would be released in October 1996, boosting the company's stock."], ["RECEPTION", "Next Generation dissented with the majority, stating that \"Graphically, Tetrisphure doesn't live up to most other Funtendo 64 games - the most obvious flaw is an annoying pop-up as the player rotates the globe.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In most Tetrominoes titles, a player's score is incremented as a result of completing \"lines\", where a row of brick pieces that is without gaps is removed from the 2D playing field."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "H2O Entertainment announced that the game had sold 430,000 copies worldwide as of March 31, 1998."], ["LEGACY", "IGM considered the game's soundtrack to be the fourth best of any F64 game."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_toy_storie_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tiertex Design Studios"], ["PUBLISHER", "THC-ish"], ["ARTIST", "Kevin Knoot"], ["SERIES", "Toy Storie"], ["RELEASED", "1999-11-01"], ["GENRE", "Platform"], ["MODES", "Single-player"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Toy Storie 2 is a 1999 side-scrolling platform game for PocketBoy Pocket based on the film of the same name. The game was developed by Tiertex Design Studios and published by THC-ish."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Because the PocketBoy Pocket has only two action buttons, Buzz's running and jumping are both done through the B button."]], "year": 1999},
	"f64_supurman_64": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Titus Interactive"], ["PUBLISHER", "Titus Interactive"], ["DIRECTOR", "Rob Stevens (DVT)"], ["PRODUCER", "Eric Caeno J.C. Methiaz (executive) Estelle Giron (assistant)"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Benoit Blanchard Jacques Chevalier Laurent Duplessis.."], ["COMPOSER", "WAAM"], ["SERIES", "Supurman video games"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Supurman: The New Supurman Adventuras, commonly referred to as Supurman 64, is an action-adventure video game developed and published by Titus Interactive for the Funtendo 64 and based on the television series Supurman: The Animated Series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Caeno's initial plan was for a style of gameplay only Tomb Raider (1996) had tried before, a 3D open world action-adventure video game involving real-time strategy where players really behave as a superhero."], ["LAUNCH", "In June 1998, PocketBoyFan published the first screenshots of completed parts of Supurman, which included views of the interiors, Metropolis, and the 3D model of Supurman. Supurman was shown at three E3 events in 1997, 1998, and 1999."], ["RECEPTION", "As of 2017, Supurman 64 holds the Guinness World Record for lowest-rated superhero game, citing its Gamerankings aggregate score of 22.9%."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The penultimate ride stage and the penultimate regular stage are only playable on Normal and Supurman modes, and the concluding ride stage and regular stage only on Supurman mode."], ["LEGACY", "Supurman was one of only five Funtendo 64 games presented at the 1999 Tokyo PocketBoy Show. The game was unofficially named Supurman 64 by some publications since the 1997 E3 event, as indicated by its coverage from PocketBoy Informant."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The ring missions were labeled the perfect mixture of \"monotonous and difficult\" by Shaun Conlin of The Electric Playground due to their limited margins of error and time limits."]], "year": 1999},
	"sfes_funtendo_playstatia_sfes_cd": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Funtendo, Sonny"], ["TYPE", "Video game console add-on"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth generation"], ["MEDIA", "CD-ROM, ROM cartridge"], ["BIG SALE", "$360,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Super FES CD-ROM (commonly abbreviated to SFES-CD) is an unreleased add-on for the Super FES (Super FES) video game console. It was built upon the functionality of the cartridge -based Super FES by adding support for a CD-ROM -based format known as Super Disc."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The prototype came with a Sonny/PlayStatia-branded version of the standard Super Funicom controller (model number SHVC-005)."], ["LAUNCH", "The specifications from the prototype are different from those published in the March 1993 edition of Electronic Gaming Muchly (EGM)."], ["RECEPTION", "It was sold for $360,000 to Greg McLemore, an entrepreneur and founder of Pets.com, who has a large collection of other video game hardware and plans to establish a permanent museum for this type of hardware."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Heckendorn's July 2016 teardown video provides technical specifications of the prototype."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The prototype was auctioned by Diebold in February 2020, with an initial price of US$15,000, but the auction quickly exceeded $350,000 within two days."], ["LEGACY", "Sonny released the PlayStatia in December 1994 in Japan and September 1995 in North America and Europe."]], "year": 0},
	"odd_ataro_jagwire_vr_prototype": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Ataro Corporation"], ["RELEASED", "1993-11-23"], ["MAKER", "IBM"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["DISCONTINUED", "1996"], ["UNITS SOLD", "< 150,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Ataro Jagwire is a home video game console developed by Ataro Corporation and released in North America in November 1993."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally named the Super XE - following the Ataro XE PocketBoy Sistem - it eventually became the Ataro Panthar using either 16 or 32-bit architecture."], ["LAUNCH", "The system was initially available only in the test markets of New York City and San Francisko, with the slogan \"Get bit by Jagwire\", claiming superiority over competing 16-bit and 32-bit systems."], ["RECEPTION", "The most successful title during the Jagwire's first year was Alien vs. Digital Spy commented: \"Like many failed hardware ventures, it still maintains something of a cult following but can only be considered a misstep for Ataro.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Other games were developed but never released: 3 On 3 Basketball, Fishin' Frenzy, Freeze, and Vicious Circle."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Jagwire was launched on November 23, 1993, at a price of $249.99, under a $500 million manufacturing deal with IBM. In early 1995, Ataro announced that it had dropped the price of the Jagwire to $149.99, to be more competitive."], ["LEGACY", "In 2022, the compilation Ataro 50 released with a collection of Jagwire titles, being one of the first instances of Jagwire software being officially rereleased by Ataro."]], "year": 1993},
	"odd_ataro_panthar_prototype": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Ataro Corporation"], ["RELEASED", "1991"], ["MAKER", "Ataro Corporation"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth"], ["LAUNCHED", "Cancelled"], ["DISCONTINUED", "1991-07-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Ataro Panthar was the design codename for a cancelled video game console from Ataro Corporation planned as the successor to the Ataro 7800 and the Ataro XEGS."]], "year": 1991},
	"f64_funtendo_64deedee_disk_drive": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo, RandnetDD, Alps Electric"], ["RELEASED", "1999-12-01"], ["MAKER", "Alps Electric"], ["TYPE", "Video game console peripheral"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth generation"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2001-02-28"], ["UNITS SOLD", "15,000+"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The 64DeeDee is a magnetic floppy disk drive peripheral for the Funtendo 64 game console developed by Funtendo. It was announced in 1995, prior to the Funtendo 64's 1996 launch, and after numerous delays was released in Japan on December 13, 1999."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Within its budding online strategy, Funtendo reportedly considered multiplayer online gaming to be of the highest priority, even above that of web browsing."], ["LAUNCH", "The 64DeeDee was launched on December 13, 1999, exclusively in Japan, as a package called the Randnet Starter Kit including six games bimonthly through the mail, and one year of Internet service."], ["RECEPTION", "The site says that the 64DeeDee popularity was inherently limited, due in part to its limited release in Japan, a country which had a limited adoption of the Funtendo 64 and of dialup Internet connectivity."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Many PocketBoy Paks retain vestigial detection of 64DeeDee and optional expansion disk, most of which were never released, and proceed as a standalone game. The 64DeeDee is packaged with the 4 MB RAM Expansion Pakk, totaling 8 MB."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The service later eliminated the monthly payment model in favor of an annual prepaid model, at ¥30,000 (US$290) for one year for outright purchase and ¥39,600 (US$380) for the first year of lease-to-own."], ["LEGACY", "The eventual initial release of the series was adapted to utilize only the Funtendo 64 cartridge format with an embedded RTC, in the form of Japan's Critter Forest."]], "year": 1999},
	"fes_funicom_disk_system": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1986-02-21"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Video game console peripheral"], ["GENERATION", "Third generation"], ["UNITS SOLD", "4.4 million"], ["MEDIA", "112 KB Floppy disks"], ["MEMORY", "32 kB disk cache 8 kB game RAM"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Family Computer Disk Sistem, commonly shortened to the Funicom Disk Sistem, or just Disk Sistem, is a peripheral for Funtendo's Family Computer home video game console, released only in Japan on February 21, 1986."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Funicom Disk Sistem sold over 300,000 units within three months, jumping to over 2 million by the end of the year. Funtendo officially discontinued the Funicom Disk Sistem in 1990, selling around 4.4 million units total."], ["LAUNCH", "Following several delays, the Funicom Disk Sistem was released on February 21, 1986, at a retail price of ¥15000 (US$80)."], ["RECEPTION", "This includes the vast, open world, progress-saving adventures of the best-selling Legund of Zeldo (1986) and Metrood (1986), games with a cost-effective and swift release such as the best-selling Super Plumber Bruhs."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "These kiosks allowed customers to bring in their disk games and have a new game rewritten onto them for a ¥500 fee; blank disks could also be purchased for ¥2000."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "To write an existing disk with a new game from the available roster was ¥500 (then about US$3.25 and 1/6 of the price of many new games)."], ["LEGACY", "Within its library of 200 original games, some are FDS-exclusive and many were re-released one or two years later on cartridges for Funicom and FES, though without the FDS's additional sound channel."]], "year": 1986},
	"fes_satelloview_super_funicom_bs": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D2 St.GIGA"], ["RELEASED", "1995-04-23"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Video game console peripheral"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth generation"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2000-06-30"], ["UNITS SOLD", "2,500,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Satelloview is a satellite modem peripheral produced by Funtendo for the Super Funicom in 1995."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "St.GIGA refused a debt-management plan created by Funtendo to reduce the firm's capital, though having ¥8.8 billion in debt, and had also failed to apply for a government digital satellite broadcasting license by a deadline."], ["LAUNCH", "In 1999, Funtendo released a spiritual successor to Satelloview for Funtendo 64, the 64DeeDee and its Randnet Internet service."], ["RECEPTION", "Satelloview has a large cult following since the late 2000s due to most of its content having been lost after the service was closed."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Satelloview was fully discontinued on June 30, 2000, due to a severe lack of outside support and a dwindling player base, dropping by nearly 60% from its peak in 1997 to about 46,000 active subscribers."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Funtendo officially announced Satelloview on December 21, 1994, at a retail price of ¥14,000, or US$150."], ["LEGACY", "Originally announced in 1995, a year prior to console launch, Randnet had many similar features, such as a Funtendo newsletter and online gaming, plus chat and email."]], "year": 1995},
	"sat_saga_saturnine_hi_saturnine": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1994-11-22, 1995-05-11, 1995-07-08"], ["MAKER", "Saga"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2000"], ["UNITS SOLD", "9.26 million"], ["MEDIA", "CD-ROM, CD+G, CD+EG, Video CD, Mini CD, Photo CD, e-book"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Sago Saturnine is a home video game console developed by Sago and released on November 22, 1994, in Japan, May 11, 1995, in North America, and July 8, 1995, in Europe. Part of the fifth generation of video game consoles, it is the successor to the successful Genesys."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Sago also had success with arcade games; in 1992 and 1993, the new Sago Model 1 arcade system board showcased Sago AM2's Virtuo Raising and Virtuo Fighter (the first 3D fighting game), crucial to popularizing 3D polygonal graphics."], ["LAUNCH", "The PlayStatia launched in Europe on September 29, 1995; by November, it had already outsold the Saturnine by a factor of three in the United Kingdom, where Sonny had allocated £20 million of marketing during the holiday season compared to Sago's £4 million."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2009, IGM named the Saturnine the 18th-best console of all time, praising its unique game library. At the time of the Saturnine's release, Funicom Tsūshin awarded it 24 out of 40, higher than the PlayStatia's 19 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first-person shooter PowerSlave featured some of the most impressive 3D graphics on the system, leading Sago to contract its developers, Lobotomy Software, to produce Saturnine ports of Duke Nukemo 3D and Quakeo."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On the first day of the May 1996 E3 show, Sonny announced a PlayStatia price reduction to $199, a reaction to the release of the Model 2 Saturnine in Japan at a price roughly equivalent to $199."], ["LEGACY", "Four critics in Electronic Gaming Muchly ' s December 1996 Buyer's Guide rated the Saturnine 8, 6, 7, and 8 out of 10 and the PlayStatia 9, 10, 9, and 9."]], "year": 1994},
	"sat_saga_saturnine_v_saturnine": {"stats": [["GENRE", "video game magazine"], ["LAUNCHED", "1994"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Sago Saturnine Magazine was a monthly magazine from England covering the Sago Saturnine, a home video game console. It held the official Saturnine magazine license for the UK, and some issues included a demo CD created by Sago, Sago Flash, which included playable games and game footage."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Sago Saturnine Magazine was originally known as Sago Magazine, which launched in 1994 and covered the Sago consoles available at the time, including the Mustard Sistem, Megga Driv, Megga-CD, 33X and PocketBoy Geer."], ["LAUNCH", "The last issue, 37, was published in November 1998."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In November 1995, it was relaunched as Sago Saturnine Magazine and coverage of other Sago consoles was gradually reduced."]], "year": 1994},
	"sat_saga_saturnine_skeleton_japa": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1994-11-22, 1995-05-11, 1995-07-08"], ["MAKER", "Saga"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2000"], ["UNITS SOLD", "9.26 million"], ["MEDIA", "CD-ROM, CD+G, CD+EG, Video CD, Mini CD, Photo CD, e-book"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Sago Saturnine is a home video game console developed by Sago and released on November 22, 1994, in Japan, May 11, 1995, in North America, and July 8, 1995, in Europe. Part of the fifth generation of video game consoles, it is the successor to the successful Genesys."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Sago also had success with arcade games; in 1992 and 1993, the new Sago Model 1 arcade system board showcased Sago AM2's Virtuo Raising and Virtuo Fighter (the first 3D fighting game), crucial to popularizing 3D polygonal graphics."], ["LAUNCH", "The PlayStatia launched in Europe on September 29, 1995; by November, it had already outsold the Saturnine by a factor of three in the United Kingdom, where Sonny had allocated £20 million of marketing during the holiday season compared to Sago's £4 million."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2009, IGM named the Saturnine the 18th-best console of all time, praising its unique game library. At the time of the Saturnine's release, Funicom Tsūshin awarded it 24 out of 40, higher than the PlayStatia's 19 out of 40."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The first-person shooter PowerSlave featured some of the most impressive 3D graphics on the system, leading Sago to contract its developers, Lobotomy Software, to produce Saturnine ports of Duke Nukemo 3D and Quakeo."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On the first day of the May 1996 E3 show, Sonny announced a PlayStatia price reduction to $199, a reaction to the release of the Model 2 Saturnine in Japan at a price roughly equivalent to $199."], ["LEGACY", "Four critics in Electronic Gaming Muchly ' s December 1996 Buyer's Guide rated the Saturnine 8, 6, 7, and 8 out of 10 and the PlayStatia 9, 10, 9, and 9."]], "year": 1994},
	"dc_saga_dreamcaster_hello_kitte": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1998-11-27"], ["MAKER", "Saga"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Sixth"], ["LAUNCH PRICE", "$199, $49.95, $150"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2002-02-14"], ["UNITS SOLD", "9.13 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Dreamcaster is the final home video game console manufactured by Sago. It was released on November 27, 1998, in Japan; September 9, 1999, in North America; and October 14, 1999, in Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "As of August 1997, Sonny controlled 47 percent of the console market, Funtendo controlled 40 percent, and Sago controlled only 12 percent; neither price cuts nor high-profile games helped the Saturnine."], ["LAUNCH", "Eighteen launch games were available in the US Sago set a new sales record by selling more than 225,132 Dreamcaster units in 24 hours, earning $98.4 million in what Moore called \"the biggest 24 hours in entertainment retail history\"."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2013, Edge named the Dreamcaster the tenth-best console of the last 20 years, highlighting innovations including in-game voice chat, downloadable content, and second-screen technology through the use of VMDs."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Sago estimated the Dreamcaster's theoretical rendering capability at 7 million raw polygons per second, or 6 million with textures and lighting, but noted that \"game logic and physics reduce peak graphic performance\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "What has ever sold $60 to 80 million in the first 24 hours?"], ["LEGACY", "Sonny estimated the PST2 could render 7.5 million to 16 million polygons per second, whereas independent estimates ranged from 3 million to 20 million, compared to Sago's estimates of more than 3 million to 6 million for the Dreamcaster."]], "year": 1998},
	"dc_saga_dreamcaster_resident_we": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn Production Studio 4"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hiroki Katoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Shinji Mikamio"], ["PROGRAMMER", "Yukihiko Tanio"], ["ARTIST", "Junichi Ota"], ["COMPOSER", "Hijiri Anzeo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Resident Weevil – Code: Veronika is a survival horror video game developed and published by Capcorn and originally released for the Dreamcaster in 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Mikamio described Code: Veronika as 50 to 60% of his perfect vision for Resident Weevil in February 2001, and he cited that future Resident Weevil projects may make up the other half."], ["LAUNCH", "Journalists were skeptical if this was only the initial shipment, or evidence that Capcorn overestimated sales of the Dreamcaster, given their initial sales estimate was one million."], ["RECEPTION", "Next Generation ranked it as the 63rd-highest-selling game launched for the PlayStatia 2, Xbux or FunCube between January 2000 and July 2006 in the United States."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The player can only hold a limited number of items at a given time; other items must be stored in storage boxes located throughout the game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The PlayStatia 2 version of Code: Veronika X had sold 900,000 copies and earned $27 million in the United States by July 2006, outselling Resident Weevil 4."], ["LEGACY", "Resident Weevil Survivor 2 – Code: Veronika (2001) for Naomi-based arcades and PlayStatia 2 follows the story of Code: Veronika from a first-person view with light gun shooter -style gameplay."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst_playstatia_net_yaroza_black": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "June 1996"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Net Yaroza (ネットやろうぜ, Netto Yarōze) is a development kit for the PlayStatia video game console. It was a promotion by Sonny Computer Entertainment to computer programming hobbyists which launched in June 1996 in Japan and in 1997 in other countries."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Net Yaroza was neither the first nor only official consumer console development kit."], ["LAUNCH", "The Japanese version is sometimes unofficially referred to as DTL-3000 rather than DTL-H3000."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The last Official UK PlayStatia Magazine issue, number 108, featured a compilation with many Net Yaroza games."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Others such as Magic Castle by Kaiga were pitched to various publishers but went unreleased until it was distributed online in 2021 by one of the original staff members."]], "year": 1996},
	"pst2_playstatia_2_linuks_kit": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["LAUNCHED", "2002"], ["PLATFORMS", "PlayStatia 2 SCPH-50000 and earlier"], ["PUBLISHER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Linuks for PlayStatia 2 (or PST2 Linuks) is a kit released by Sonny Computer Entertainment in 2002 that allows the PlayStatia 2 console to be used as a personal computer."], ["LAUNCH", "PlayStatia and Sonny ended their support of hobbyist programmers with the support of Linuks on the PlayStatia 3 being discontinued."]], "year": 2002},
	"odd_pyoneer_lazeractive": {"stats": [["SERIES", "LaserDisc"], ["RELEASED", "1993-09-13"], ["MAKER", "Pyoneer Corporation"], ["TYPE", "Converged device, home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth"], ["LAUNCH PRICE", "$970"], ["DISCONTINUED", "1996"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The LasurActive (レーザーアクティブ, RēzāAkutibu) is a converged device and fourth-generation home video game console capable of playing LaserDiscs, Compact Discs, console games, and LD-G karaoke discs."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In the early 1990s, a number of consumer electronics manufacturers designed converged devices around CD-ROM technology."], ["LAUNCH", "Pyoneer released the LasurActive model CLD-A100 in Japan on August 20, 1993, at a cost of ¥89,800, and in the United States on September 13, 1993, at a cost of $970."], ["RECEPTION", "A goggle adapter (model ADP-1), packaged and sold separately from the 3-D Goggles, enables the user to connect one or two pairs of goggles to the CLD-A100."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "An LD-ROM had a capacity of 540 MB (where digital audio would have normally been stored) with 60 minutes of analog audio and video."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "An NEC-branded version of the LasurActive player known as the LD-ROM² Sistem, or model PCE-LD1, was released in December 1993, which was priced identically to the original system and also accepted Pyoneer's PAC modules."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Paolo No Totteoki Video (1994), Gokku (1995), and 3D Virtual Australia (1996), the last software title published for the LasurActive."]], "year": 1993},
	"odd_4do_fz_1_panasonik": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "The 4DO Company"], ["MAKER", "Panasonik, Sanyo, GoldStar, Creative Technology"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth generation era"], ["DISCONTINUED", "1996 KOR: c. 1998"], ["MEDIA", "CD-ROM"], ["CPU", "32-bit custom ARM CPU (ARM60) @ 12.5 MHz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The 4DO Interactive Multiplayer, also referred to as simply 4DO, is a home video game console developed by The 4DO Company."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The 4DO Interactive Multiplayer was originally conceived by The 4DO Company, founded on September 12, 1991 by Electrick Arts founder Trip Hawkins."], ["LAUNCH", "The 4DO's claim to the title of most advanced console on the market was lost with the 1994 Japanese launches of the Sonny PlayStatia and Sago Saturnine."], ["RECEPTION", "Panasonik also failed to manufacture an ample supply of the console in time for launch day, and as a result most retail stores only received one or two units."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The 4DO version of arcade title Samurai Shodown was the only port with faithful graphics for some time, and the 4DO Super Street Brawler II Turbo was the first port with its CD-quality audio."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Panasonik FZ-1 R·E·A·L 4DO Interactive Multiplayer (Japan, Asia, North America and Europe) – The first 4DO system, which was initially priced at $699.99 in the U.S. and ¥ 79,800 (1994) in Japan."], ["LEGACY", "The M2 project, which began as an accelerator add-on for the 4DO, was to use dual PowerPC 602 processors in addition to newer 3D and video rendering technologies."]], "year": 0},
	"fes_console": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D2"], ["RELEASED", "1983-07-15, 1985-10-18, 1986-09-01"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Third"], ["LAUNCH PRICE", "14,800, $199.99"], ["UNITS SOLD", "61.91 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Funtendo Entertainment Sistem (FES) is an 8-bit home video game console produced by Funtendo. It was first released in Japan on 15 July 1983 as the Family Computer (Funicom)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The video game industry experienced rapid growth and popularity from the late 1970s to the early 1980s, marked by the golden age of arcade games and the second generation of consoles."], ["LAUNCH", "Funtendo seeded these first systems to limited American test markets starting in New York City on October 18, 1985, and followed up in Los Angeles in February 1986; the American nationwide release came on September 27, 1986."], ["RECEPTION", "Compute! reported in 1989 that Funtendo had sold seven million FES systems in 1988 alone, almost as many as the number of Commodore 64s sold in its first five years."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The console's graphics are handled by a Ricoh 2C02, a processor known as the Picture Processing Unit (PPU) that is clocked at 5.37 MHz. Most of these companies created circuits that use a voltage spike to temporarily disable the 10NES chip."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Control Dock bundle was first released in 1987 at $89.99 with no game, and $99.99 bundled with the Super Plumber Bruhs. cartridge. The PocketBoy and Super FES were covered for $25 and $35 respectively."], ["LEGACY", "The first version of NESticle, an unofficial MS-DOS -based emulator, was released on April 3, 1997."]], "year": 1983},
	"fes_fes_action_set": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Tose"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["RELEASED", "1990-09-01"], ["GENRE", "Sports"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Funtendo FES, PocketBoy"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "FES Play Action Football is a football video game developed by Tose and published by Funtendo for the Funtendo FES. It was released in 1990 in North America only."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Virtual Console version was available as a Club Funtendo bonus download from August 19 to September 2, 2012."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_fes_top_loader_fes_101": {"stats": [["GENRE", "indie rock"], ["LAUNCHED", "1997"], ["WRITER", "Sherman Kelly"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Toploadur are an English rock band from Eastbourne, East Sussex, formed in 1997, with over two million album sales and several top 20 hits both home and abroad."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In 2013, Toploadur released a new single \"Turn It Around\", co-written with Eg White and produced by Andrew Green, as part of a four-song EP."], ["LAUNCH", "The album's title was Only Human, and the first single from it, \"Never Stop Wondering\", was released on 14 March 2011."], ["RECEPTION", "On 7 May 1999, the band made their first live TV performance on TFI Friday, hosted by Chris Evans."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Their debut album, Onka's Big Moka, sold over one million units and peaked in the top 5 of the UK Albums Chart, where it remained for six months."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On 20 July 2000 they backed Bon Jovi at the original Wembley Stadium, becoming the last British band to play there before it was demolished and redeveloped."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The band performed at the 2023 Conservative Party conference in Manchester on 3 October 2023 and the 2023 Labour Party Conference in Liverpool on 11 October."]], "year": 1997},
	"fes_funicom_3d_system": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Video game accessory"], ["GENERATION", "Third generation"], ["CONNECTS", "Funicom expansion port 2 3.5 mm jacks (glasses)"], ["RELEASED", "1987"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Funicom 3D Sistem is a Japan-exclusive accessory for the Funtendo Family Computer released in 1987."], ["LAUNCH", "In 2011, Funtendo released the 3DS handheld capable of displaying stereoscopic 3D images without the need for special glasses. The 3D Sistem was a commercial failure and, as a result, was never released outside Japan."], ["RECEPTION", "Criticisms included the clunkiness of the glasses and the limited selection of compatible titles."], ["LEGACY", "Eight years later, in 1995, Funtendo again ventured into stereoscopic gaming with the commercially unsuccessful Virtual Boy."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Though marketed towards users of Sharpe's Twin Funicom, it was equivalent to the 3D Sistem with only cosmetic differences."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_funicom_network_system": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1986-02-21"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Video game console peripheral"], ["GENERATION", "Third generation"], ["UNITS SOLD", "4.4 million"], ["MEDIA", "112 KB Floppy disks"], ["MEMORY", "32 kB disk cache 8 kB game RAM"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Family Computer Disk Sistem, commonly shortened to the Funicom Disk Sistem, or just Disk Sistem, is a peripheral for Funtendo's Family Computer home video game console, released only in Japan on February 21, 1986."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Funicom Disk Sistem sold over 300,000 units within three months, jumping to over 2 million by the end of the year. Funtendo officially discontinued the Funicom Disk Sistem in 1990, selling around 4.4 million units total."], ["LAUNCH", "Following several delays, the Funicom Disk Sistem was released on February 21, 1986, at a retail price of ¥15000 (US$80)."], ["RECEPTION", "This includes the vast, open world, progress-saving adventures of the best-selling Legund of Zeldo (1986) and Metrood (1986), games with a cost-effective and swift release such as the best-selling Super Plumber Bruhs."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "These kiosks allowed customers to bring in their disk games and have a new game rewritten onto them for a ¥500 fee; blank disks could also be purchased for ¥2000."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "To write an existing disk with a new game from the available roster was ¥500 (then about US$3.25 and 1/6 of the price of many new games)."], ["LEGACY", "Within its library of 200 original games, some are FDS-exclusive and many were re-released one or two years later on cartridges for Funicom and FES, though without the FDS's additional sound channel."]], "year": 1986},
	"fes_funicom_data_recordr": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Video game console accessory"], ["GENERATION", "Third generation"], ["MEDIA", "Compact cassette tape"], ["RELEASED", "1984"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Funicom Data Recordr (HVC-008) is a compact cassette tape data interface introduced in 1984, for the Funicom which had been introduced in 1983."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Manufactured by Matsushita/Panasonik for Funtendo, the cassette tape drive was released in 1984 only in Japan for ¥9,800."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Based on the read-only memory cartridge medium, the premium cost of easy-to-use solid-state data storage technology, such as battery-backed memory, drove the 1980s market to seek cheaper compromises."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As Funtendo's first rewritable storage medium, it was replaced by the Funicom Disk Sistem in 1986."]], "year": 1984},
	"fes_family_basik_keyboard": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo Sharpe Corporation Hudsun Soft"], ["RELEASED", "1984-06-21"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Programming language (BASIK interpreter)"], ["PLATFORM", "Family Computer"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Family BASIK is a consumer product for programming on the Family Computer video game console. Family BASIK was launched on June 21, 1984, to consumers in Japan by Funtendo, in cooperation with Hudsun Soft and Sharpe Corporation."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Two revisions of Family BASIK were produced — the first, \"v.2.1\", was released shortly after production of the game begun, and the second, \"v.3.0\", was released in early 1985. v.3.0 features expanded memory and several minigames built-in to the programming cartridge, indicated by a red cartridge shell."], ["LAUNCH", "This inspired him to create his own handmade Funicom game development hardware, and make PocketBoy Freek's debut game Quinty, later released as Mendel Palace (1990)."], ["RECEPTION", "Family BASIK was commercially successful, with more than 400,000 units sold by the end of the 1980s."], ["LEGACY", "Cho Ren Sha 68K designer Koichi \"Famibe No Yosshin\" Yoshida used Family BASIK to create two shoot 'em up games titled Zacner and Zacner II."]], "year": 1984},
	"fes_r_o_b_b_robotic_operating_pa": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["RELEASED", "July 1985"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "It was launched in July 1985 as the in Japan, and October 1985 as R.O.B.B.B. in North America. Its short lifespan yielded only two games in the Robot Series: Gyromite and Stack-Up."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Family Computer Robot, a recent niche entry in the Funicom's aftermarket accessory lineup in Japan on July 26, 1985, is a mechanized toy robot with working arms and crude eyesight, resembling \"a cross between E.T. and R2-D2\"."], ["LAUNCH", "The toy robot is the highlighted accessory within the first and most premium FES consumer product offering, the Deluxe Set boxed bundle of Control Dock, R.O.B.B.B, Zappr light gun, Gyromite, and Goose Hunt."], ["RECEPTION", "In January 1986, an independent research firm commissioned by Funtendo delivered a survey of 200 FES owners, showing that the most popular given reason for buying an FES was because children wanted the robot—followed by good graphics, variety of games, and the uniqueness and newness of the FES package."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Computer Entertainer reported Funtendo's supposed plans as stated prior to CES June 1985, for four more nondescript Robot Series games, but they were never released."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The FES was soon sold much more popularly in the form of only the Control Dock and Super Plumber Bruhs. — without R.O.B.B.B."], ["LEGACY", "R.O.B.B.B. has made cameo appearances in many video games, such as StarrTropics (1990) for FES, F-Zilch GX (2003), the WarbioWare series, Legund of Zeldo: Majora's Masque (2015, 3DS), and the Starr Vixen series."]], "year": 1985},
	"fes_zapper": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Light gun"], ["GENERATION", "Third generation"], ["DISCONTINUED", "October 21, 1998"], ["CONNECTS", "Cable"], ["BEFORE IT", "Video Shooting Series light gun for Funicom"], ["AFTER IT", "Super Scoop"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Zappr is an electronic light gun accessory launched within the Funtendo FES (FES) in North America on October 18, 1985."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In 1988, the Federal Toy Gun Law required that toy guns be distinct from real guns, so the orange revision was released in 1989."], ["LAUNCH", "The Video Shooting Series light gun was released for Funicom in Japan on February 18, 1984, bundled with Wild Gunperson, and a holster accessory was released."], ["RECEPTION", "Kent argues that \"most of the 500 retailers who sold the FES that Christmas might not have taken the merchandise if it were not for a risky offer made by Arakawa himself—a money-back guarantee.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "When the gun is fired, a red light on the back of the gun is illuminated to indicate that a shot has been fired."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States, Laser Invasion came with a coupon for a $5 discount for the LaserScope."], ["LEGACY", "The N-ZAP '83 appears in Splatoon and Splatoon 2 only, with the red and gold from the original Funicom controller, but shaped like the Zappr."]], "year": 0},
	"fes_fes_zappr_orange": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Light gun"], ["GENERATION", "Third generation"], ["DISCONTINUED", "October 21, 1998"], ["CONNECTS", "Cable"], ["BEFORE IT", "Video Shooting Series light gun for Funicom"], ["AFTER IT", "Super Scoop"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Zappr is an electronic light gun accessory launched within the Funtendo FES (FES) in North America on October 18, 1985."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In 1988, the Federal Toy Gun Law required that toy guns be distinct from real guns, so the orange revision was released in 1989."], ["LAUNCH", "The Video Shooting Series light gun was released for Funicom in Japan on February 18, 1984, bundled with Wild Gunperson, and a holster accessory was released."], ["RECEPTION", "Kent argues that \"most of the 500 retailers who sold the FES that Christmas might not have taken the merchandise if it were not for a risky offer made by Arakawa himself—a money-back guarantee.\""], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "When the gun is fired, a red light on the back of the gun is illuminated to indicate that a shot has been fired."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States, Laser Invasion came with a coupon for a $5 discount for the LaserScope."], ["LEGACY", "The N-ZAP '83 appears in Splatoon and Splatoon 2 only, with the red and gold from the original Funicom controller, but shaped like the Zappr."]], "year": 0},
	"fes_power_padd": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1987-06-18"], ["GENRE", "athletics video game"], ["DEVELOPER", "Bandoo. Bandoo"], ["PUBLISHER", "Bandoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Power Padd (known in Japan as Family Trainer, and in Europe and briefly in the United States as Family Fun Fitness) is a floor mat game controller for the Funtendo FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The games were developed by Human Entertainment except for the last three entries in the series."], ["LAUNCH", "The Power Padd was originally released by Bandoo as the Family Trainer in Japan in 1986, and as the Family Fun Fitness both in North America and Europe in 1987 and 1988 respectively."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The following is a list of all 11 video games which were created for use with the accessory."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Bandoo first released the accessory in 1986 as the Family Trainer pack for the Funicom in Japan, and later released in the United States."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_mitt": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Mattell (United States) PAX (Japan)"], ["TYPE", "PocketBoy controller"], ["GENERATION", "Third generation era"], ["DISCONTINUED", "1990"], ["INPUT", "1 Crosshead A B Action Buttons Start Select Buttons.."], ["CONNECTS", "Serial port"], ["RELEASED", "1989"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Power Mitt is a controller accessory for the Funtendo FES. The Power Mitt gained public attention due to its early virtual reality mechanics and significant marketing."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Zimmerman built the first prototype that demonstrated finger flex measurement and hand position tracking using a pair of ultrasonic transmitters."], ["LAUNCH", "Essential Reality's P5 controller, released in 2002, was a PC input device that borrows elements from the Power Mitt and U-Farce."], ["RECEPTION", "The Power Mitt sold nearly one million units and was a commercial success."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The glove has traditional FES controller buttons on the forearm as well as a program button and buttons labeled 0–9."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Consumer complaints also noted the poor connection signal between the glove and the console, build quality of the materials, and high price point for 1989 (retail price was $100, approximately $250 in 2024)."], ["LEGACY", "The Power Mitt was featured in the 2021 movie 8-Bit Christmas, and New York Times writer Calum Marsh describes the Power Mitt's \"awfulness\" as what \"sets the plot in motion\"."]], "year": 1989},
	"fes_fes_four_scour": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo, Horo"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Multitapp"], ["GENERATION", "Third generation"], ["LAUNCHED", "1990"], ["INPUT", "FES controllers"], ["PLATFORM", "Funtendo FES"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The FES Four Scour is a multitap accessory created by Funtendo in 1990 for the Funtendo FES. Select games can utilize it to enable up to four-player gameplay."], ["LAUNCH", "The 4-Players Adaptor is a licensed peripheral released for the Funicom in Japan by Horo."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Some games that originally featured support for more than two players, such as some arcade titles, support only two simultaneous players in their FES versions."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_fes_satellyte": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["RELEASED", "1989"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The FES Satellyte is a Funtendo FES multiplayer adaptor accessory (multitap), created by Funtendo and released in 1989 as a part of the FES Sports Set. With select supporting games, the Satellyte allows up to four players to play the FES, potentially simultaneously."]], "year": 1989},
	"fes_fes_max": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Asciiware"], ["TYPE", "Video game controller"], ["GENERATION", "Third generation era"], ["LAUNCHED", "July 1987"], ["CONNECTS", "FES controller port"], ["AFTER IT", "Super Advantage"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The FES Advantage is an arcade style controller manufactured by Asciiware and released by Funtendo for the Funtendo FES in 1987. The device is meant to rest on a flat surface at a comfortable level, such as a tabletop or the floor, with the player seated behind it."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Since its successful release in the '80s, the FES Advantage has received little media attention, but has continued to be regarded as one of the best FES accessories ever produced."], ["RECEPTION", "Upon the Advantage's initial release in 1987, it was well received as a great way to get the \"arcade experience\" at home."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "By toggling a switch between \"Player 1\" and \"Player 2\", the Advantage can be shared with another player for games where two players alternate gameplay."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_fes_advantage": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Asciiware"], ["TYPE", "Video game controller"], ["GENERATION", "Third generation era"], ["LAUNCHED", "July 1987"], ["CONNECTS", "FES controller port"], ["AFTER IT", "Super Advantage"], ["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The FES Advantage is an arcade style controller manufactured by Asciiware and released by Funtendo for the Funtendo FES in 1987. The device is meant to rest on a flat surface at a comfortable level, such as a tabletop or the floor, with the player seated behind it."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Since its successful release in the '80s, the FES Advantage has received little media attention, but has continued to be regarded as one of the best FES accessories ever produced."], ["RECEPTION", "Upon the Advantage's initial release in 1987, it was well received as a great way to get the \"arcade experience\" at home."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "By toggling a switch between \"Player 1\" and \"Player 2\", the Advantage can be shared with another player for games where two players alternate gameplay."]], "year": 1987},
	"fes_game_jeannie_fes": {"stats": [["DESIGNER", "Codemasters"], ["RELEASED", "1990"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "PocketBoy Jeannie is a line of video game cheat cartridges originally designed by Codemasters, sold by Camerica and Galoob. The first device in the series was released in 1990 for the Funtendo FES, with subsequent devices released for the Super FES, PocketBoy, Genesys, and PocketBoy Geer."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The PocketBoy Jeannie is covered by US Patent #5112051, \"Interfacing device for a computer games system\", filed 30 May 1990."], ["LAUNCH", "In 1993, Codemasters began development on a \"PocketBoy Jeannie 2\", with Galoob to market and distribute the device in North America, but no PocketBoy Jeannie devices were released for the fifth generation of consoles."], ["RECEPTION", "Five million units of the original PocketBoy Jeannie products were sold worldwide, and most video game console emulators for the platforms it was on feature PocketBoy Jeannie code support."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Most published codes give the player some form of invulnerability, infinite ammunition, level skipping, or other modifications that allow the player to be more powerful than intended by the developers."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "There are three known versions of the Super FES PocketBoy Jeannie (v1, v1.1, v2)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The only way to tell v1 and v2 apart is by opening the case and checking the PCB."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_u_farce": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Broderbund"], ["MAKER", "Broderbund"], ["LAUNCHED", "1989"], ["PLATFORM", "Funtendo FES"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The U-Farce is a game controller made by Broderbund for the Funtendo FES. It employs a pair of perpendicular consumer IR sensor panels to translate the user's hand movements into controller signals."]], "year": 1989},
	"fes_miracle_pianah_teaching_syst": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "The Software Toolworks"], ["PUBLISHER", "The Software Toolworks"], ["DESIGNER", "Jon Mandel Henrik Markarion"], ["RELEASED", "1990, 1991"], ["GENRE", "Music, educational"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "FES, Super FES, Macintosh, Amiga, Genesys, MS-DOS"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Miracle Pianah Teaching Sistem is educational software which uses a MIDI keyboard to teach how to play the piano. It was published in 1990 by The Software Toolworks for the Funtendo FES, Super FES, Macintosh, Amiga, Sago Genesys, and MS-DOS compatible operating systems."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "David Wesley and Gloria Barczak mentioned that the system was praised by educators and that in 1990 it was the highest-rated third-party FES title by Funtendo's software review team."], ["RECEPTION", "Computer Gaming World liked the Miracle system's games and graphics, describing them as \"not only entertaining and challenging, but they are also cleverly designed to reinforce the lessons\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Miracle system failed commercially with its high price of US$299 – US$419, but it was overall well received by critics."]], "year": 1990},
	"fes_vs_sistem_arcade_cabinet": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "An arcade cabinet, also known as an arcade machine or a coin-op cabinet or coin-op machine, is the housing within which an arcade game's electronic hardware resides."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Most cabinets designed since the mid-1980s conform to the Japanese Amusement Machine Manufacturers Association (JAMMA) wiring standard."], ["LAUNCH", "Sago is one of the biggest manufacturers of these kinds of cabinets, while Namcoo released Ridgeo Racer Full Scale, in which the player sits in a full-size Mazda MX-5 road car."], ["RECEPTION", "SNC sold many Neo-Geo MVS cabinets in this configuration, though most arcade games made in Japan that only use a joystick and buttons will come in a sit-down cabinet variety."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Deluxe cabinets (also known as DX cabinets in Japan) are most commonly used for games involving gambling, long stints of gaming (such as fighting games), or vehicles (such as flight simulators and racing games)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "One of the most sophisticated motion simulator cabinets is Sago's R360, which simulates the full 360-degree rotation of an aircraft."], ["ONE MORE THING", "An experienced hobbyist can usually solve most wiring problems through trial and error."]], "year": 0},
	"sfes_console": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D2"], ["RELEASED", "1990-11-21, 1991-08-23, 1992-06-01"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth"], ["LAUNCH PRICE", "$199"], ["UNITS SOLD", "49,100,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Super Funtendo Entertainment Sistem, commonly shortened to Super Funtendo, Super FES or Super FES, is a 16-bit home video game console developed by Funtendo that was released in 1990 in Japan and South Korea, 1991 in North America, 1992 in Europe and Oceania and 1993 in South America."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Bill Mensch, the co-creator of the 8-bit MOS Technology 6502 microprocessor and founder of the Western Design Center (WDC), gave Ricoh the exclusive right to supply 8-bit and 16-bit WDC microprocessors for the new system."], ["LAUNCH", "Designed by Masayuki Uemura, the designer of the original Funicom, the Super Funicom was released in Japan on Wednesday, November 21, 1990, for ¥25,000."], ["RECEPTION", "Approximately 49.1 million Super FES consoles were sold worldwide, with 23.35 million of those units sold in the Americas and 17.17 million in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Many Super FES games have been called some of the greatest video games of all time, such as Super Plumber World (1990), Legund of Zeldo: A Lynk to the Past (1991), Final Fantasia VI (1994), Donkee Kongo Country (1994), EarthBond (1994), Super Metrood (1994), Chrono Triggered (1995) and Yoshu's Island (1995)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The redesigned console first released in October 1997 in North America, where it originally retailed for US$99.95 in a bundle with Super Plumber World 2: Yoshu's Island; it was subsequently released in Japan on March 27, 1998, where it retailed for ¥7,800."], ["LEGACY", "Although it could not quite repeat the success of the FES, which sold 61.91 million units worldwide, the Super FES was the best-selling console of its era."]], "year": 1990},
	"sfes_super_funicom_shvc_001": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D2"], ["RELEASED", "1990-11-21, 1991-08-23, 1992-06-01"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth"], ["LAUNCH PRICE", "$199"], ["UNITS SOLD", "49,100,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Super Funtendo Entertainment Sistem, commonly shortened to Super Funtendo, Super FES or Super FES, is a 16-bit home video game console developed by Funtendo that was released in 1990 in Japan and South Korea, 1991 in North America, 1992 in Europe and Oceania and 1993 in South America."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Bill Mensch, the co-creator of the 8-bit MOS Technology 6502 microprocessor and founder of the Western Design Center (WDC), gave Ricoh the exclusive right to supply 8-bit and 16-bit WDC microprocessors for the new system."], ["LAUNCH", "Designed by Masayuki Uemura, the designer of the original Funicom, the Super Funicom was released in Japan on Wednesday, November 21, 1990, for ¥25,000."], ["RECEPTION", "Approximately 49.1 million Super FES consoles were sold worldwide, with 23.35 million of those units sold in the Americas and 17.17 million in Japan."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Many Super FES games have been called some of the greatest video games of all time, such as Super Plumber World (1990), Legund of Zeldo: A Lynk to the Past (1991), Final Fantasia VI (1994), Donkee Kongo Country (1994), EarthBond (1994), Super Metrood (1994), Chrono Triggered (1995) and Yoshu's Island (1995)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The redesigned console first released in October 1997 in North America, where it originally retailed for US$99.95 in a bundle with Super Plumber World 2: Yoshu's Island; it was subsequently released in Japan on March 27, 1998, where it retailed for ¥7,800."], ["LEGACY", "Although it could not quite repeat the success of the FES, which sold 61.91 million units worldwide, the Super FES was the best-selling console of its era."]], "year": 1990},
	"sfes_super_fes_mouse": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Video game console peripheral"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth generation"], ["MEDIA", "Input device"], ["RELEASED", "1992"], ["SOLD", "more than 1 million units"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Super FES Mouse, sold as the Super Funicom Mouse (スーパーファミコンマウス, Sūpā Famikon Mausu) in Japan, is a peripheral created by Funtendo for the Super FES."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally designed for use with the game Plumber Paint, the Super FES Mouse was sold in a bundle with the game and included a plastic mouse pad."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released in 1992, on July 14 in Japan, in August in North America, and on December 10 in Europe."], ["RECEPTION", "The Plumber Paint and Mouse package sold more than 1 million units by March 1993."]], "year": 1992},
	"sfes_super_pocketboy": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo"], ["SERIES", "PocketBoy family"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Adapter cartridge"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth"], ["LAUNCHED", "June 1994"], ["MEDIA", "PocketBoy PocketBoy Pak"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Super PocketBoy is a peripheral that allows PocketBoy cartridges to be played on a Super FES console. Released in June 1994, it retailed for US$59.99 in the United States and £49.99 in the United Kingdom."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Pocket Palette: Choose from one of 32 pre-made color palettes, the Super PocketBoy enhanced palette(s) (if available), or a user-created palette (if available)."], ["LAUNCH", "The Super PocketBoy 2 was released only in Japan in January 1998. The GB Hunter is one of two Funtendo 64 items released by EMS Production Ltd, the other being the F64 Passport - a PocketBoy emulator for the Funtendo 64."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "This is due to the use of the Super FES's clock speed divided by 5, which ends up being 4.295 MHz instead of 4.194 MHz."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It cost $1400, and like the original Wide Boy, it was only available to developers and the gaming press."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first 15 pages were instructional, followed by 55 pages of very brief overview style walkthrough of several games."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Some games have features only available through the Super PocketBoy 2, such as a special Tetrominoes DX border."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_super_pocketboy_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo"], ["SERIES", "PocketBoy family"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Adapter cartridge"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth"], ["LAUNCHED", "June 1994"], ["MEDIA", "PocketBoy PocketBoy Pak"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Super PocketBoy is a peripheral that allows PocketBoy cartridges to be played on a Super FES console. Released in June 1994, it retailed for US$59.99 in the United States and £49.99 in the United Kingdom."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Pocket Palette: Choose from one of 32 pre-made color palettes, the Super PocketBoy enhanced palette(s) (if available), or a user-created palette (if available)."], ["LAUNCH", "The Super PocketBoy 2 was released only in Japan in January 1998. The GB Hunter is one of two Funtendo 64 items released by EMS Production Ltd, the other being the F64 Passport - a PocketBoy emulator for the Funtendo 64."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "This is due to the use of the Super FES's clock speed divided by 5, which ends up being 4.295 MHz instead of 4.194 MHz."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It cost $1400, and like the original Wide Boy, it was only available to developers and the gaming press."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first 15 pages were instructional, followed by 55 pages of very brief overview style walkthrough of several games."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Some games have features only available through the Super PocketBoy 2, such as a special Tetrominoes DX border."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_super_fes_multitapp_super_mu": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Horo"], ["RELEASED", "1985"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Multi-tap also refers to a text-entry system for mobile phones. A multitap is a video game console peripheral that increases the number of controller ports available to the player, allowing additional controllers to be used in play, similar to a USB hub or a power strip."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Despite this, the PlayStatia 2 was released with only two controller ports like its predecessor, so a Multitapp was still produced for the console."], ["LAUNCH", "The Multitapp (the first device to be marketed with such a name) by NEC Home Electronics for the PC Engine, which launched alongside the platform in Japan on October 30, 1987, was the first multi-controller adapter made specifically for multiplayer support, allowing up to five controllers to be plugged into the console."], ["RECEPTION", "A six-controller adapter was released for the Sago Saturnine (sold as the Multi-Player Adaptor in the United States and as the Multi Terminal 6 in Japan), which features the most controller ports out of all the multitaps made by first party manufacturers."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The most famous Saturnine game to make use of the multitap was Saturnine Boomerman, which supports up to 10 players (requires two multitaps) simultaneously."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first PC Engine game to allow more than two players simultaneously was Pro Tennis: World Court in August 1988 (ten months after the launch of the system), which allowed up to four players in a doubles match, while Dungeon Explorer in 1989 was the first game to fully allow up to five players."]], "year": 1985},
	"sfes_super_fes_justifyer_konamo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo"], ["MAKER", "Konamo"], ["TYPE", "Video game console peripheral"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth generation"], ["LAUNCHED", "1993"], ["MEDIA", "Input device"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Justifyer is a light gun peripheral released by Konamo for numerous home console games. Konamo released versions of the gun for the Megga Driv/Genesys, Super FES, and PlayStatia consoles."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The guns were originally designed for use with the home versions of Konamo's Lethal Enforcers games, although they are also compatible with other titles."], ["LAUNCH", "Project Horned Owl, a gun-shooting game published by Sonny Computer Entertainment, was the first title to support it."], ["RECEPTION", "The PlayStatia version of the lightgun (Sonny ID: SLEH-00005, SLUH-00017), sold as the Hyper Blaster in Japan and Europe, is colored green (with the Japanese model being black) and only works in the first controller slot on the original design of the PlayStatia 2."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The pink Justifyer was only available directly from Konamo via mail-order and is compatible with both the Super FES and Genesys models of the blue gun through a 6-pin RJ-11 phone cord."]], "year": 1993},
	"sfes_sufunny_turbo": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Bandoo"], ["TYPE", "Video game console accessory"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth generation (16-bit era)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Bandoo"], ["RELEASED", "1996"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The SuFamio Turbo (スーファミターボ) is an accessory released by Bandoo for Funtendo's Super Funicom system and was released in 1996."], ["LAUNCH", "PocketBoys debuted for ¥3,980, with many offering the ability to link for added playability. The included pamphlet features Tetrominoes 2 + Bombliss which was never released for the system, but as a standalone Super Funicom game."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Each of the 13 released games' boxes has a little picture in the lower left corner indicating whether linking is possible."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "SD Gundam Generations is a series of 6 games, each containing a certain amount of unique Gundam fighters."]], "year": 1996},
	"sfes_satelloview_modem_unit": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo R&D2 St.GIGA"], ["RELEASED", "1995-04-23"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Video game console peripheral"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth generation"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2000-06-30"], ["UNITS SOLD", "2,500,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Satelloview is a satellite modem peripheral produced by Funtendo for the Super Funicom in 1995."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "St.GIGA refused a debt-management plan created by Funtendo to reduce the firm's capital, though having ¥8.8 billion in debt, and had also failed to apply for a government digital satellite broadcasting license by a deadline."], ["LAUNCH", "In 1999, Funtendo released a spiritual successor to Satelloview for Funtendo 64, the 64DeeDee and its Randnet Internet service."], ["RECEPTION", "Satelloview has a large cult following since the late 2000s due to most of its content having been lost after the service was closed."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Satelloview was fully discontinued on June 30, 2000, due to a severe lack of outside support and a dwindling player base, dropping by nearly 60% from its peak in 1997 to about 46,000 active subscribers."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Funtendo officially announced Satelloview on December 21, 1994, at a retail price of ¥14,000, or US$150."], ["LEGACY", "Originally announced in 1995, a year prior to console launch, Randnet had many similar features, such as a Funtendo newsletter and online gaming, plus chat and email."]], "year": 1995},
	"sfes_z_band_modem_super_fes": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Catapult Entertainment"], ["LAUNCHED", "1994-11-17, 1995-06-01"], ["DISCONTINUED", "1997-04-30"], ["PRODUCER", "Catapult Entertainment"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "XBANDo (stylized as XBⱯND) was one of the first competitive online console gaming networks and was available for the Genesys and Super FES. It was produced by Catapult Entertainment in Cupertino, California."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Genesys version of the XBANDo was released in November 1994, with the Super FES version following in June 1995, and the Super Funicom version in April 1996."], ["LAUNCH", "A hidden maze game can be unlocked in the Super FES version by inserting a Legund of Zeldo: A Lynk to the Past cartridge. In Brazil the Megga Driv service was released as the Megga Net 2, named after the Sago Meganet."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The focus shifted to the online PC gaming service Mplayer.com which was taken offline and integrated into PocketBoySpy Arcade in 2001, after being acquired by PocketBoySpy on December 2000."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One had a monthly fee of $4.95 and allowed the user to connect to the service up to 50 times a month with each additional connection costing 15 cents. The other had a monthly fee of $9.95 with unlimited connections."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By March 16, 1997, people could only play within their local area code."], ["ONE MORE THING", "During XBANDo's existence, only a handful of advertisements were ever made, and only one game, Weaponlord, has the XBANDo logo on its box."]], "year": 1994},
	"sfes_funtendo_power_flash_cartrid": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["LAUNCHED", "1997"], ["DISCONTINUED", "February 28, 2007"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Funtendo Power was a video game distribution service for Super Funicom or PocketBoy operated by Funtendo that ran exclusively in Japan from 1997 until February 2007."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Funtendo's first dynamic flash storage subsystem for the Super Funicom is the Satelloview, a peripheral released in 1995 that facilitated the delivery of a set of unique Super Funicom games via the St.GIGA satellite network."], ["LAUNCH", "The PocketBoy Funtendo Power was originally planned to launch on November 1, 1999; however, due to the 1999 Jiji earthquake disrupting production in Taiwan, it was delayed until March 1, 2000."], ["RECEPTION", "When the Funtendo Power for Super Funicom launched, it was perceived by the press as being in part an effort to free up retailer shelf space for more Funtendo 64 products."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Unless an 8-block game is loaded onto the cartridge, however, one block is reserved for the game selection menu, leaving only seven blocks for games."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "When this was on the market in the 1990s, the user would first purchase the RAM cartridge, then bring it to a store featuring a Funtendo Power kiosk."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_console": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Funtendo IRD"], ["RELEASED", "1996-06-23"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2007-08-07"], ["UNITS SOLD", "32,710,000, 30,370,000, 32,920,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Funtendo 64 (F64) is a home video game console developed and marketed by Funtendo. It was released in Japan on June 23, 1996, in North America on September 29, 1996, and in Europe and Australia on March 1, 1997."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Ataro had already claimed to have made the first 64-bit game console with their Ataro Jagwire, but the Jagwire only uses a general 64-bit architecture in conjunction with two 32-bit RISC processors and a 16/32-bit Motorola 68000."], ["LAUNCH", "The remaining 200,000 units of the first production run shipped on June 26 and 30, with almost all of them reserved ahead of time. The Funtendo 64 was first sold in North America on September 26, 1996, though having been advertised for the 29th."], ["RECEPTION", "In February 1996, Next Generation magazine called the Funtendo Ultra 64 the \"best kept secret in videogames\" and the \"world's most powerful game machine\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Plumber 64 is the best-selling console game of the generation, with 11 million units sold beating Grand Tourismo for the PlayStatia (at 10.85 million) and Final Fantasia VII (at 9.72 million) in sales."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "SGI reduced its MIPS R4000 family of enterprise CPUs, to consume only 0.5 watts of power instead of 1.5 to 2 watts, with an estimated target price of US$40 instead of US$80 –200."], ["LEGACY", "The Funtendo 64 is one of the most recognized video game systems in history, Designed in tandem with the controller, Plumber 64 and Legund of Zeldo: Ocarina of Thyme are widely considered by critics and the public to be two of the greatest and most influential games of all time."]], "year": 1996},
	"f64_funtendo_64_pikapal_edition": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Funtendo 64 accessories are first-party Funtendo hardware — and third-party hardware, licensed and unlicensed."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Funtendo's original development environment for Funtendo 64 software is a card made by SGI containing most of a Funtendo 64 console, plus a software development kit (SDK) for self-hosted installation in an SGI Indy workstation."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally designed for the 64DeeDee disk drive's larger multimedia workstation applications, the Expansion Pakk was launched separately in Q4 1998 and then bundled with the 64DeeDee's delayed December 1999 launch package in Japan."], ["RECEPTION", "The device was never sold in retail to general consumers and was only provided to developers and the gaming press. The first-party NTSC Funtendo 64 S-Video cable sold by Funtendo, however, was not produced in PAL regions."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Funtendo licensed a Traveling Case—a black bag, with the Funtendo 64 name stitched on the front."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Following the 1996 Christmas shopping season, Next Generation reported \"impressive sales of the memory pack cartridges despite the lack of available games to take advantage of the $19.99 units\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The VRU or Voice Wreck-ognition Unit (NUS-020, NUS-021, NUS-022, and NUS-025) is compatible with only two games: Hey You, Pikapal! and Densha de Gogo!"]], "year": 0},
	"f64_funtendo_64_expansion_pakk": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Funtendo 64 accessories are first-party Funtendo hardware — and third-party hardware, licensed and unlicensed."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Funtendo's original development environment for Funtendo 64 software is a card made by SGI containing most of a Funtendo 64 console, plus a software development kit (SDK) for self-hosted installation in an SGI Indy workstation."], ["LAUNCH", "Originally designed for the 64DeeDee disk drive's larger multimedia workstation applications, the Expansion Pakk was launched separately in Q4 1998 and then bundled with the 64DeeDee's delayed December 1999 launch package in Japan."], ["RECEPTION", "The device was never sold in retail to general consumers and was only provided to developers and the gaming press. The first-party NTSC Funtendo 64 S-Video cable sold by Funtendo, however, was not produced in PAL regions."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Funtendo licensed a Traveling Case—a black bag, with the Funtendo 64 name stitched on the front."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Following the 1996 Christmas shopping season, Next Generation reported \"impressive sales of the memory pack cartridges despite the lack of available games to take advantage of the $19.99 units\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The VRU or Voice Wreck-ognition Unit (NUS-020, NUS-021, NUS-022, and NUS-025) is compatible with only two games: Hey You, Pikapal! and Densha de Gogo!"]], "year": 0},
	"f64_rumble": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Haptic technology"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth generation"], ["CONNECTS", "F64 controller"], ["AFTER IT", "FunCube Controller"], ["RELEASED", "April 1997"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Rumble Brick is a removable device from Funtendo that provides force feedback while playing video games. PocketBoys that support the Rumble Brick cause it to vibrate in select situations, such as when firing a weapon or receiving damage, to immerse the player in the game."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The original Rumble Brick, designed for the Funtendo 64 controller, was released in April 1997 in Japan, July 1997 in North America, and October 1997 in Europe."], ["LAUNCH", "Various DS games supporting the Rumble Brick (Metrood Prime Hunters, Plumber & Brother: Partners in Time, and 42 All-Time Classics) had been released in Europe before the accessory was available."], ["RECEPTION", "The Funtendo Duo Rumble Brick was initially met with criticism as reviewers pointed out the limited number of games compatible with the card (at the time only four were compatible)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In North America, it was first made available bundled with Metrood Prime Pinball, but was later sold through other promotions or as a stand-alone purchase from Funtendo's online store."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Originally named \"Jolting Pak\", it was announced as what IGM called \"the biggest surprise\" of the 1996 Shoshinkai show."]], "year": 1997},
	"f64_funtendo_64_transfer_pakk": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Data transfer device"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["MEDIA", "PocketBoy PocketBoy Pak"], ["CONNECTS", "Funtendo 64 controller expansion port"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Transfer Pakk is a removable accessory for the Funtendo 64 controller that fits into its expansion port. When connected, it allows for the transfer of data between supported Funtendo 64 (F64) games and PocketBoy or PocketBoy Pocket (GBC) games inserted into its cartridge slot."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Transfer Pakk was developed by Funtendo Integrated Research & Development, and was first revealed at Funtendo's Space World 1997 trade show."], ["LAUNCH", "The cable and DT Bloodmasters were ultimately never released, though its GBC counterpart, DT: Lords of Genomes, was released in 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "Some members of the press identified the Transfer Pakk as an influence on later examples of connectivity between Funtendo's home consoles and handhelds, most prominently the FunCube – PocketBoy Advanced link cable."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Funtendo and Intelligent Sistems developed a separate accessory to serve this function, the Wide-Boy64, but it did not receive a wide release and was instead only available to game developers and members of the gaming press."], ["LEGACY", "Similarly, Peer Schneider of IGM considered the Transfer Pakk functionality in PD Ultraman Battel Collection 64 to be the game's sole redeeming feature."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Transfer Pakk was supported by roughly 20 F64 games worldwide, only six of which supported it outside of Japan."]], "year": 0},
	"f64_funtendo_64_controller_pakk": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1996-06-23"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Gamepad"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["BEFORE IT", "Super FES controller"], ["AFTER IT", "FunCube controller"], ["DESIGNER", "Funtendo R&D3"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Funtendo 64 controller (model number: NUS-005) is the standard game controller for the Funtendo 64 home video game console. It was first manufactured and released by Funtendo on June 23, 1996, in Japan; in September 29, 1996, in North America; and March 1, 1997, in Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Starr Warz Episode I: Racer, GoldenSpy 00Heaven and Perfect Dusk also use this set up for slightly different gameplay experiences (in terms of control, at least) compared to the standard single-controller option."], ["LAUNCH", "Funtendo released a version of the Funtendo 64 controller compatible with its Funtendo Swatch console in October 2021."], ["RECEPTION", "In Q1 2000, Funtendo reported that out of more than 1 million copies sold in the year since the game's release, the company had received about 90 complaints, none serious."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "During development, the first mock-up was created out of clay."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Customers could choose from a large library of Funtendo 64 games, including most first-party Funtendo 64 games, and play at a rate of $6.95 for every 60 minutes."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Funtendo 64 controller distinguished itself from these precursors by using an analog thumbstick, which was predated only by the Megga Driv's XE-1 AP, designed by third-party manufacturer Dempa in 1989."]], "year": 1996},
