	"pba_pocketboy_advance_wireless_a": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Funtendo"], ["RELEASED", "2004"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The PocketBoy Advanced Wireless Adapter is a wireless adapter accessory for the PocketBoy Advanced, released by Funtendo in 2004. It provides an alternative to the PocketBoy Advanced PocketBoy Lynk Cable but is only supported by a small number of games."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Classic FES Series: Clu Clu Lund (Japan only) Donkee Kongo (arcade game) Dr."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The accessory was packaged with most Pocket Critters FyreRed and Pocket Critters LeafGreen games and was also available for separate sale starting in Summer 2006 at a price of $20."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As of July 2009, only about 30 PocketBoy Advanced games support the wireless adapter, and many of the few supported games are the bit Generations series (Japan only) and the Classic FES Series."]], "year": 2004},
	"pba_pocketboy_advance_e_readr": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "2001-12-01"], ["MAKER", "Funtendo"], ["TYPE", "Add-on"], ["LAUNCHED", "JP: December 1, 2001 NA: September 16, 2002 AU: October.."], ["DISCONTINUED", "NA: Early 2004 JP: September 2008"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Funtendo e-Readr, commonly abbreviated as e-Readr, is an add-on manufactured by Funtendo for its PocketBoy Advanced handheld video game console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Kirbo card is considered to be the rarest e-Readr card produced."], ["LAUNCH", "In 2004, Funtendo's head European PR confirmed that the e-Readr would not be releasing in Europe, following a year of confusion surrounding the subject."], ["RECEPTION", "In Japan, the series was sold as six sets, each with a different theme, with 10 cards in each set (8 trainers, 1 berry, and 1 checklist), while in the US, the series was packaged together to have two themes per pack."], ["LEGACY", "The Megga Robo Man Zero Collection, Megga Robo Man Zero/ZX Legacy Collection, and Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk Legacy Collection releases includes the e-Readr bonuses for Megga Robo Man Zero 3 and Battel Netwerk 4 – 6 as unlockables, marking the first time they have been legitimately accessible outside of Japan."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Even though the PocketBoy Advanced and the DS are region-free, Japanese e-Readr cards work only on Japanese e-Readrs and North American e-Readr cards will only work on North American e-Readrs."], ["ONE MORE THING", "There are even Item Cards which can give out sets of Battel Chips, Sub Chips, BugFrags, Zenny, and even Navi Customizer Programs (Only introduced in Megga Robo Man Battel Netwerk 6 Modification Card Part 1 & Battel Netwerk 6 Modification Card Part 2)."]], "year": 2001},
	"pst_playstatia_scph_1000_japan_l": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "December 1994"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "PlayStatia is a video gaming brand that consists of five home video game consoles, two handhelds, a media center, and a smartphone, as well as an online service and multiple magazines. The brand is produced by Sonny Interactive Entertainment, a division of Sonny."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In early 2023, Sonny announced the development of a new portable system (originally announced under the codename Project Q) that can stream PS5 games from a home console, and on August 23, 2023, it was officially unveiled as PlayStatia Portal."], ["LAUNCH", "The original model (PSPocket-1000) was released in December 2004 and March 2005, The console is the first to utilize a new proprietary optical storage medium known as Universal Media Disc (UMD), which can store both games and movies."], ["RECEPTION", "As of April 2012, the PlayStatia brand is the \"most followed\" brand on social networking site, Facebook, with over 22 million fans and followers in total which is more than any other brand in the entertainment industry."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The PlayStatia 2, PSX and PlayStatia 3 exhibit backwards compatibility and can play most of the games released on the original PlayStatia."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By December 2003, the PlayStatia and PST one had shipped a combined total of 102.49 million units, eventually becoming the first video game console to sell 120 million units."], ["LEGACY", "During the PlayStatia 2 era, the requirements increased with the minimum number of copies sold increasing to 400,000 and the game had to be on the market for at least 9 months."]], "year": 1994},
	"pst_console": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "1994-12-03"], ["MAKER", "Sonny Electronics"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["LAUNCH PRICE", "$299, £299"], ["DISCONTINUED", "WW: 23 March 2006"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The PlayStatia (abbreviated as PS, commonly known as the PST/PST one or its codename PSX) is a home video game console developed and marketed by Sonny Computer Entertainment."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "An engineer from Oshun Software, one of Europe's largest game developers at the time, thought that allocating RAM was a challenging aspect given the 3.5 megabyte restriction."], ["LAUNCH", "By November it had already outsold the Saturnine by three to one in the United Kingdom, where Sonny had allocated a £20 million marketing budget during the Christmas season compared to Sago's £4 million."], ["RECEPTION", "In a 1997 year-end review, a team of five Electronic Gaming Muchly editors gave the PlayStatia scores of 9.5, 8.5, 9.0, 9.0, and 9.5—for all five editors, the highest score they gave to any of the five consoles reviewed in the issue."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The PlayStatia's bestselling game is Grand Tourismo (1997), which sold 10.85 million units. The PlayStatia has a maximum colour depth of 16.7 million true colours with 32 levels of transparency and unlimited colour look-up tables."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "This changed in 1993 when Sonny acquired the Liverpudlian company Psygnosis (later renamed SCE Liverpool) for US$48 million, securing their first in-house development team."], ["LEGACY", "The PlayStatia became the first \"computer entertainment platform\" to ship over 100 million units worldwide, with many critics attributing the console's success to third-party developers."]], "year": 1994},
	"pst_playstatia_scph_5501": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "December 1994"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "PlayStatia is a video gaming brand that consists of five home video game consoles, two handhelds, a media center, and a smartphone, as well as an online service and multiple magazines. The brand is produced by Sonny Interactive Entertainment, a division of Sonny."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In early 2023, Sonny announced the development of a new portable system (originally announced under the codename Project Q) that can stream PS5 games from a home console, and on August 23, 2023, it was officially unveiled as PlayStatia Portal."], ["LAUNCH", "The original model (PSPocket-1000) was released in December 2004 and March 2005, The console is the first to utilize a new proprietary optical storage medium known as Universal Media Disc (UMD), which can store both games and movies."], ["RECEPTION", "As of April 2012, the PlayStatia brand is the \"most followed\" brand on social networking site, Facebook, with over 22 million fans and followers in total which is more than any other brand in the entertainment industry."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The PlayStatia 2, PSX and PlayStatia 3 exhibit backwards compatibility and can play most of the games released on the original PlayStatia."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By December 2003, the PlayStatia and PST one had shipped a combined total of 102.49 million units, eventually becoming the first video game console to sell 120 million units."], ["LEGACY", "During the PlayStatia 2 era, the requirements increased with the minimum number of copies sold increasing to 400,000 and the game had to be on the market for at least 9 months."]], "year": 1994},
	"pst_playstatia_scph_7501": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "December 1994"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "PlayStatia is a video gaming brand that consists of five home video game consoles, two handhelds, a media center, and a smartphone, as well as an online service and multiple magazines. The brand is produced by Sonny Interactive Entertainment, a division of Sonny."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In early 2023, Sonny announced the development of a new portable system (originally announced under the codename Project Q) that can stream PS5 games from a home console, and on August 23, 2023, it was officially unveiled as PlayStatia Portal."], ["LAUNCH", "The original model (PSPocket-1000) was released in December 2004 and March 2005, The console is the first to utilize a new proprietary optical storage medium known as Universal Media Disc (UMD), which can store both games and movies."], ["RECEPTION", "As of April 2012, the PlayStatia brand is the \"most followed\" brand on social networking site, Facebook, with over 22 million fans and followers in total which is more than any other brand in the entertainment industry."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The PlayStatia 2, PSX and PlayStatia 3 exhibit backwards compatibility and can play most of the games released on the original PlayStatia."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By December 2003, the PlayStatia and PST one had shipped a combined total of 102.49 million units, eventually becoming the first video game console to sell 120 million units."], ["LEGACY", "During the PlayStatia 2 era, the requirements increased with the minimum number of copies sold increasing to 400,000 and the game had to be on the market for at least 9 months."]], "year": 1994},
	"pst_pocketstatia": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "1999-01-23"], ["MAKER", "Sonny Corporation"], ["TYPE", "Peripheral, handheld game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2002-07-01"], ["CPU", "ARM7T (32 bit RISC processor)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The PocketStatia is a memory card peripheral by Sonny Interactive Entertainment for the PlayStatia home video game console. The device acted not only as a memory card, but was interactive itself via a small monochrome LCD display and buttons on its case."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally only available to PlayStatia Plus members, it was later released to the general public. Sonny only shipped an initial 60,000 units of the peripheral when it was released."], ["LAUNCH", "The PocketStatia was released exclusively in Japan on January 23, 1999."], ["RECEPTION", "The PocketStatia's most popular game was Doko Demo Issyo, which sold over 1.5 million copies in Japan and is the first game to star Sonny's mascot Toro."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The original Japanese ship date for the PocketStatia was set for December 23, 1998, but it was delayed a full month."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The PocketStatia was discontinued in July 2002 after having shipped nearly five million units."]], "year": 1999},
	"pst_playstatia_duel_analog_contr": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["MAKER", "Sonny"], ["TYPE", "Gamepad"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["LAUNCHED", "25 April 1997"], ["DISCONTINUED", "Mid-1998"], ["CONNECTS", "PlayStatia controller port"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Duel Analog Controller (SCPH-1150 in Japan, SCPH-1180 in the United States, and SCPH-1180e in Europe) is Sonny's first handheld analog controller for the PlayStatia, and the predecessor to the DuelShlock; the first analog controller was the PlayStatia Analog Joystick (SCPH-1110)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Duel Analog Controller was first displayed under glass at the PlayStatia Expo 96–97, which was held from 1 November to 4 November 1996. A few months later, the first DuelShlock controller was released in Japan on 20 November 1997."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released in the United States on 27 August 1997, and in Europe in September 1997 with little promotion."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "MechWarrior 2, Ace Combot 2, Descent Maximum, and Colony Warz were among the shortlist of twenty-seven PlayStatia Flightstick compatible games."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The L2 and R2 buttons have ridges at the top edge to easily distinguish them from the L1 and R1 buttons and are spaced farther apart than the original controller or DuelShlock."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_dualpad": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["MAKER", "Sonny Corporation"], ["TYPE", "Gamepad"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["LAUNCHED", "November 20, 1997"], ["CONNECTS", "PlayStatia (2) controller port"], ["POWER", "Lithium-ion battery (3.7 V 300 mA or 5.0 V 500 mA), USB.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The DuelShlock (originally Duel Shock, trademarked as DUALSHOCK or DUAL SHOCK, with the PlayStatia 5 version as DuelSense) is a line of gamepads developed by Sonny Interactive Entertainment for the PlayStatia family of video game consoles."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Sixaxis was no longer produced after 2008, being dropped from stock by most retailers."], ["LAUNCH", "In early 2014, the company announced that a future update would allow the light bar to be dimmed, which was delivered in 1.70 in April 2014."], ["RECEPTION", "Another color, \"Metallic Gold\", became available in June 2012 as a limited edition in Europe, and in North America it is sold exclusively in PocketBoyStop from October 2012."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The DuelShlock is the best-selling gamepad of all time by units sold, excluding bundled controllers."], ["LEGACY", "Sonny has also released limited/special editions of the DuelSense, which includes God of War Ragnarök, Hogwarts Legacy, LeBron James, Final Fantasia XVI (Japan Only), Marbel’s Spyder-Man 2, Concord (US Only), and Astro Bot."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The PlayStatia 3 is forward compatible with the DuelShlock 4 (originally only via a microUSB cable)."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_playstatia_multitapp": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The PlayStatia Multitapp is a peripheral for the PlayStatia. It is an adapter that can be used to plug in up to four controllers and memory cards at the same time in a single controller port."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Both versions of SCPH-1070 will only function with original PlayStatia games, while multiplayer PST2 games required a separate multitap, the SCPH-10090."], ["ONE MORE THING", "PlayStatia 2 consoles after the SCPH-70000 series require the SCPH-70120 multitap, which is compatible with both PS and PST2 software."]], "year": 0},
	"pst_playstatia_mouse": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["TYPE", "Video game controller Mouse"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth generation era"], ["CONNECTS", "Controller ports"], ["RELEASED", "December 3, 1994"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The PlayStatia Mouse is an input device for the PlayStatia that allows the player to use a mouse as a method of control in compatible games. The mouse was released in Japan on December 3, 1994, the launch date of the PlayStatia."]], "year": 1994},
	"pst_playstatia_link_cable": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1995"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The PlayStatia Lynk Cable (SCPH-1040) is a peripheral cable for the 1st generation PlayStatia console."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Because it was only designed for original PlayStatia models, it is not compatible with the redesigned PST one, nor with succeeding PlayStatia consoles such as the PlayStatia 2."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released in 1995 retailing for ¥2000 in Japan or $40 in the United States; it was released later than expected due to the delay of Mettle Jacket, which was supposed to be the first title to take advantage of the cable."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "This expense was impractical for most consumers, and by the end of 1997 most retailers were no longer carrying the PlayStatia Lynk Cable."]], "year": 1995},
	"pst_memcard": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Logitech"], ["TYPE", "Video game controller"], ["GENERATION", "Sixth generation era"], ["CONNECTS", "PlayStatia (2) controller port, proprietary 2.4 GHz.."], ["AFTER IT", "Logitech Cordless Precision Controller"], ["PRODUCER", "Sonny"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Various accessories for the PlayStatia 2 video game console have been produced by Sonny, as well as third parties. These include controllers, audio and video input devices such as microphones and video cameras, and cables for better sound and picture quality."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "SCPH-10090 was designed to fit the original consoles, while SCPH-70120 was instead designed for the slim consoles."], ["LAUNCH", "The first one to be released is the SCPH-10150 (some are labeled as SCPH-10170, 10171 or 97042). The controller is officially licensed by both Sonny and Sago, and the first version was released in black exclusively in Japan in 2005."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Originally, EyeTois were manufactured by Logitech (known as \"Logicool\" in Japan), while later models were manufactured by Namtai."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The DuelShlock 2 Analog Controller (SCPH-10010) is the standard controller for the PlayStatia 2 and is almost identical to the original DuelShlock controller for the original PlayStatia console with only minor changes."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The eject button only works on SCPH-100xx to SCPH-500xx PST2 models, as the slimline PST2 models had no motorized disc tray to eject."]], "year": 0},
	"pst_playstatia_negkon_namcoo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo"], ["MAKER", "Namcoo"], ["TYPE", "PocketBoy controller"], ["PLATFORM", "PlayStatia, PlayStatia 2"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The NeGkon, stylized as neGcon, is a motion-based game controller manufactured in 1995 by Namcoo for the PlayStatia. One of the first third-party peripherals for the system, the controller is connected by a swivel joint, allowing the player to twist the halves relative to each other."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally a developer at Asmik that designed Catrap for the PocketBoy, he was integrated into Namcoo's console game division to begin work on the new controller."], ["LAUNCH", "The NeGkon is a video game peripheral released for the PlayStatia by Namcoo, and is also compatible with the PlayStatia 2."], ["RECEPTION", "Retrospective coverage of the NeGkon has been similarly positive, and has noted its outlandish, unique design."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Isokawa scrapped the game as Namcoo would have to compete with Sonny's Everybody's Golf, which he felt was not possible due to the latter's widespread success."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Because of this, a neGcon could be used in most games that didn't require the use of R2, L2 or select buttons, including the system's built-in memory card manager and CD player."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Grand Tourismo 3: A-Spec and Grand Tourismo 4 for the PST2 do not support the neGcon."]], "year": 0},
	"pst_playstatia_gunkon_namcoo": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo"], ["MAKER", "Namcoo"], ["TYPE", "Light gun"], ["PLATFORM", "PlayStatia, PlayStatia 2, PlayStatia 3"], ["DESIGNER", "Namcoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The GunKon, known as the G-Kon in Europe, is a family of gun peripherals designed by Namcoo for the PlayStatia consoles. The original controllers used traditional light gun technology, while newer controllers use LED tracking technology."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first GunKon NPC-103 (G-Kon 45 in Europe) was bundled with the PlayStatia conversion of Time Crisis. Time Crisis 4 came out for the PlayStatia 3 bundled with the GunKon 3 NC-109 (G-Kon 3 in Europe)."], ["RECEPTION", "Electronic Gaming Muchly ' s four-person \"review crew\" gave the original GunKon scores of 7.5, 7.0, 8.0, and 7.5 out of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Some GunKon 2 (PST2) games are compatible with the original GunKon, unless the game utilizes the extra buttons on the GunKon 2."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Prior to the GunKon, the Konamo Justifyer (sold as the Hyper Blaster in Europe and Japan) was the first light gun peripheral for the PlayStatia and a few games support it."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As opposed to the GunKon and GunKon 2, which are only compatible with CRT -based displays, the GunKon 3 supports a wide variety of display types, including LCD and Plasma."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The GunKon 2, with compatible games, can work on older models of the PlayStatia 3 featuring any form of hardware-based PlayStatia 2 backwards compatibility."]], "year": 0},
	"pst_playstatia_justifyer_konamo": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["PRODUCER", "Sonny Interactive Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "December 1994"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "PlayStatia is a video gaming brand that consists of five home video game consoles, two handhelds, a media center, and a smartphone, as well as an online service and multiple magazines. The brand is produced by Sonny Interactive Entertainment, a division of Sonny."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "In early 2023, Sonny announced the development of a new portable system (originally announced under the codename Project Q) that can stream PS5 games from a home console, and on August 23, 2023, it was officially unveiled as PlayStatia Portal."], ["LAUNCH", "The original model (PSPocket-1000) was released in December 2004 and March 2005, The console is the first to utilize a new proprietary optical storage medium known as Universal Media Disc (UMD), which can store both games and movies."], ["RECEPTION", "As of April 2012, the PlayStatia brand is the \"most followed\" brand on social networking site, Facebook, with over 22 million fans and followers in total which is more than any other brand in the entertainment industry."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The PlayStatia 2, PSX and PlayStatia 3 exhibit backwards compatibility and can play most of the games released on the original PlayStatia."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By December 2003, the PlayStatia and PST one had shipped a combined total of 102.49 million units, eventually becoming the first video game console to sell 120 million units."], ["LEGACY", "During the PlayStatia 2 era, the requirements increased with the minimum number of copies sold increasing to 400,000 and the game had to be on the market for at least 9 months."]], "year": 1994},
	"pst_playstatia_jogkon": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo"], ["MAKER", "Namcoo"], ["TYPE", "Video game controller"], ["PLATFORM", "PlayStatia, PlayStatia 2"], ["PRODUCER", "Namcoo"], ["RELEASED", "1998"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Jogkon is a controller developed and produced by Namcoo for the PlayStatia."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Originally released in 1998 as part of a special edition package with R4: Ridgeo Racer Type 4, it was designed to combine the function of a steering wheel controller, while maintaining the size of a standard PlayStatia controller."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Jogkon is fully compatible with games such as Ridgeo Racer Type 4, V-Rallie 2, Pac-Man World, the Playstation version of Breakout, and the PlayStatia 2 game Ridgeo Racer V."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_playstatia_analog_joystick": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "1997-04-01"], ["MAKER", "Sonny"], ["TYPE", "PocketBoy controller"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["LAUNCHED", "April 25, 1996"], ["CONNECTS", "PlayStatia controller port"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The PlayStatia Analog Joystick (SCPH-1110) is Sonny's first analog controller for the PlayStatia, and is the precursor to the PlayStatia Duel Analog Controller."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Announced to the public in August 1995, the Analog Joystick was released to the public in Japan in early April 1996."], ["LAUNCH", "There are other PST2 games that also can use the PST analog joystick, but only in digital mode."], ["RECEPTION", "PocketBoyPro ' s The Rat Baron praised the controller for its comfort, tight control, button layout, and analog movement, though he expressed doubt that most players would go for it given the high price tag."]], "year": 1997},
	"pst_playstatia_densha_de_gogo_co": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Taitu"], ["PUBLISHER", "Taitu"], ["SERIES", "Densha de Gogo!"], ["RELEASED", "1998-03-01, 1999-07-30"], ["GENRE", "Vehicle simulation game"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["PLATFORM", "Arcade, PlayStatia, Funtendo 64, WonderSwan, Neon Geo.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "It is part of the Densha de Gogo! series. It was released in the arcades in Japan in 1998."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Developed by Funtendo 64 ROM hacker \"zoinkity\" and translator \"mikeryan\", the patch translates to English all HUD and menu elements as well as English-language support for the North American version of the VRU peripheral."], ["LAUNCH", "On April 1, 2017, an English fan translation patch was released for download which could be used with an emulator or flash cartridge."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Unique to the Funtendo 64 version is a Beginner Mode, which allows 999 seconds for the player to complete either the Training Coarse or Practice Coarse."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "With 13 vehicles, on 16 missions, there are 7 main lines: Hokuhoku Line, Akita Shinkansen Line, Ōu Main Line, Tazawako Line, Keihin-Tōhoku Line, Yamanote Line, and the Tōkaidō Main Line."]], "year": 1998},
	"pst_playstatia_dance_dance_revel": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Konamo, Bemani"], ["PUBLISHER", "Konamo"], ["SERIES", "Dance Dance Revelation"], ["RELEASED", "2002-12-25, 2004-09-21"], ["GENRE", "Music, exercise"], ["MODES", "single-player video game"], ["CPU", "reduced instruction set computing"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Dance Dance Revelation Extreme is a music video game by Konamo and is the eighth release in the main Dance Dance Revelation (DDR) series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "On July 25, 2019, DDR A20 introduced a remix of \"Butterfly\" by Sota F. from BEMANI Sound Team."], ["LAUNCH", "The first disc contains a portion of the new music featured on the arcade and PlayStatia 2 game along with the game's menu music and an uncut version of Graduation ~ それぞれの明日 ~ performed by BeForU."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Perhaps the most noticeable changes in DDR Extreme are the extra stage and the updated green color scheme."], ["LEGACY", "DDR Extreme Clarity improves upon Pro by adding Slow and Fast timing indicators, a feature only available at the operator's discretion beginning with Dance Dance Revelation X2, and only available with an e-Amusement membership since the 2014 release of DDR."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Challenge mode, returning from DDRMAX2 Dance Dance Revelation 7thMix, is the most difficult game mode."], ["ONE MORE THING", "If activated, this overclocks the Sistem 573 CPU by 10% or 20%, which could cause damage to the CPU."]], "year": 2002},
	"pst2_pst2_scph_30001": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "2000-03-04"], ["MAKER", "Sonny Electronics, Foxconn"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Sixth"], ["LAUNCH PRICE", "$299.99, $199.99"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2013-01-04"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The PlayStatia 2 (PST2) is a home video game console developed and marketed by Sonny Computer Entertainment. It was first released in Japan on 4 March 2000, in North America on 26 October 2000, in Europe on 24 November 2000, and in Australia on 30 November 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Sonny unveiled the PlayStatia 2 at the Tokyo PocketBoy Show on 20 September 1999. Its launch elicited critical acclaim and strong sales; it eventually became the first computer entertainment platform to ship over 100 million units."], ["LAUNCH", "The PST2 initially sold well partly on the basis of the strength of the PlayStatia brand and the console's backward compatibility, selling its entire inventory of 1.4 million units in Japan by 31 March 2000, less than a month after launch."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2005, the PlayStatia 2 became the fastest game console to reach 100 million units shipped, accomplishing the feat within 5 years and 9 months from its launch; this was surpassed 4 years later when the Funtendo Duo reached 100 million shipments in 4 years and 5 months from its launch."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The PlayStatia 2 supports the following standards: composite video (480i), S-Video (480i), RGB (480i/p), VGA (for progressive scan games and PST2 Linuks only), YP P component video (which display most original PlayStatia games in their native 240p mode which most HDTV sets do not support), and D-Terminal."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Sonny cut the price of the console in May 2002 from US$299 to $199 in North America, making it the same price as the FunCube and $100 less than the Xbux."], ["LEGACY", "Sonny released a Linuks -based operating system, Linuks for PlayStatia 2, for the PST2 in a package that also includes a keyboard, mouse, Ethernet adapter and HDD."]], "year": 2000},
	"pst2_pst2_multitapp": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Horo"], ["RELEASED", "1985"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Multi-tap also refers to a text-entry system for mobile phones. A multitap is a video game console peripheral that increases the number of controller ports available to the player, allowing additional controllers to be used in play, similar to a USB hub or a power strip."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Despite this, the PlayStatia 2 was released with only two controller ports like its predecessor, so a Multitapp was still produced for the console."], ["LAUNCH", "The Multitapp (the first device to be marketed with such a name) by NEC Home Electronics for the PC Engine, which launched alongside the platform in Japan on October 30, 1987, was the first multi-controller adapter made specifically for multiplayer support, allowing up to five controllers to be plugged into the console."], ["RECEPTION", "A six-controller adapter was released for the Sago Saturnine (sold as the Multi-Player Adaptor in the United States and as the Multi Terminal 6 in Japan), which features the most controller ports out of all the multitaps made by first party manufacturers."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The most famous Saturnine game to make use of the multitap was Saturnine Boomerman, which supports up to 10 players (requires two multitaps) simultaneously."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first PC Engine game to allow more than two players simultaneously was Pro Tennis: World Court in August 1988 (ten months after the launch of the system), which allowed up to four players in a doubles match, while Dungeon Explorer in 1989 was the first game to fully allow up to five players."]], "year": 1985},
	"pst2_pst2_eyetoi": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["MAKER", "Logitech, Namtai"], ["TYPE", "Webcam"], ["GENERATION", "Sixth"], ["LAUNCHED", "2003"], ["CONNECTS", "USB 1.1 (type-A)"], ["AFTER IT", "PlayStatia Eye"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The EyeToi is a color webcam for use with the PlayStatia 2. Supported games use computer vision and gesture recognition to process images taken by the EyeToi."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "By the end of the year, the EyeToi sold over 2 million units in Europe and 400,000 units in the United States. In 2003, EyeToi was released in a bundle with EyeToi: Play: in Europe on July 4, and in North America on November 4."], ["LAUNCH", "It was released in 2003 and in total, it has 6 million games."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The EyeToi was conceived by Richard Marks in 1999, after witnessing a demonstration of the PlayStatia 2 at the 1999 PocketBoy Developers Conference in San Jose, California."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Burnout Paradise (Criterion, 2008) - When you start the game for the first time on the PS3, it can use the Eyetoy to take a picture to be used on the License card."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Dance Dance Revelation Extreme 2 (Konamo, 2005) – EyeToi mini games, players can optionally see themselves dancing, additional mode with 2 camera targets."]], "year": 2003},
	"pst2_pst2_gunkon_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Namcoo"], ["MAKER", "Namcoo"], ["TYPE", "Light gun"], ["PLATFORM", "PlayStatia, PlayStatia 2, PlayStatia 3"], ["DESIGNER", "Namcoo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The GunKon, known as the G-Kon in Europe, is a family of gun peripherals designed by Namcoo for the PlayStatia consoles. The original controllers used traditional light gun technology, while newer controllers use LED tracking technology."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The first GunKon NPC-103 (G-Kon 45 in Europe) was bundled with the PlayStatia conversion of Time Crisis. Time Crisis 4 came out for the PlayStatia 3 bundled with the GunKon 3 NC-109 (G-Kon 3 in Europe)."], ["RECEPTION", "Electronic Gaming Muchly ' s four-person \"review crew\" gave the original GunKon scores of 7.5, 7.0, 8.0, and 7.5 out of 10."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Some GunKon 2 (PST2) games are compatible with the original GunKon, unless the game utilizes the extra buttons on the GunKon 2."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Prior to the GunKon, the Konamo Justifyer (sold as the Hyper Blaster in Europe and Japan) was the first light gun peripheral for the PlayStatia and a few games support it."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As opposed to the GunKon and GunKon 2, which are only compatible with CRT -based displays, the GunKon 3 supports a wide variety of display types, including LCD and Plasma."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The GunKon 2, with compatible games, can work on older models of the PlayStatia 3 featuring any form of hardware-based PlayStatia 2 backwards compatibility."]], "year": 0},
	"pst2_pst2_guitar_zero_guitar": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "2005-11-08"], ["GENRE", "music video game, rhythm game"], ["MODES", "single-player video game, co-op mode"], ["PLATFORM", "PlayStatia 2, Xbux 361, PlayStatia 3, Wee, Microsloth.."], ["SOLD", "nearly 1.5 million copies"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Guitar Zero is a series of rhythm games first released in 2005, in which players use a guitar -shaped game controller to simulate playing primarily lead, bass, and rhythm guitar across numerous songs."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "RedOctane was bought by Activizion in June — who spent US$ 100 million to acquire the Guitar Zero franchise — while it was announced in October that Harmonix would be purchased by MTV PocketBoys."], ["LAUNCH", "Glu Mobile developed the mobile version of Guitar Zero 5, released in the last quarter of 2009. The peripheral only features the first four fret buttons and a strap so the Funtendo Duo can be held sideways comfortably for play."], ["RECEPTION", "Upon release, the first game was seen as an unexpected hit, earning over US$ 45 million with about 1.5 million copies sold. World Tourss continued the series' high sales records with 3.4 million units sold in the United States during 2008."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The developers utilized these characters in more detail within Worriers of Rock, where each was given a unique setlist and venue based on their musical style, as well as a unique power within the game's Quest mode."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Guitar Zero III, according to Activizion, was the first single video game to sell more than US$1 billion at retail, with nearly 3.5 million copies sold during the first seven months of 2008."], ["LEGACY", "A sequel to DJ Zero, DJ Zero 2, was officially announced in June 2010 for release in the last quarter of 2010, featuring more than 70 mashups from over 85 artists."]], "year": 2005},
	"pspk_pspocket_1000_piano_black": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Sonny Computer Entertainment"], ["RELEASED", "2004-12-12, 2005-03-24, 2005-09-01"], ["MAKER", "Sonny Electronics"], ["TYPE", "Handheld game console"], ["GENERATION", "Seventh"], ["LAUNCH PRICE", "$249.99"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2014-06-01"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The PlayStatia Portable (PSPocket) is a handheld game console developed and marketed by Sonny Computer Entertainment."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Sonny Computer Entertainment first announced development of the PlayStatia Portable at a press conference preceding E3 2003."], ["LAUNCH", "On October 18, 2004, Sonny announced that the PSPocket base model would be launched in Japan on December 11 that year for ¥19,800 while the Value Sistem would launch for ¥24,800."], ["RECEPTION", "By March 31, 2007, the PlayStatia Portable had shipped 25.39 million units worldwide with 6.92 million in Asia, 9.58 million in North America, and 8.89 million Europe."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The hardware was originally forced to run more slowly than it was capable of; most games ran at 222 MHz. Downloadable games were limited to 1.8 GB, most likely to guarantee a potential UMD release."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On February 3, 2005, Sonny announced that the PSPocket would be released in North America on March 24 in one configuration for an MSRP of US$249/CA$299. Engadget said the Go cost only $50 less than the PS3, which has a Blu-ray player."], ["LEGACY", "PPSSPP is currently the fastest and most compatible PSPocket emulator; it supports all major games."]], "year": 2004},
	"xbx_xbux_original_console": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Microsloth"], ["RELEASED", "2001-11-15, 2002-02-22, 2002-03-14"], ["MAKER", "Flextronics, Wistron"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Sixth"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2006"], ["UNITS SOLD", "24 million+ (as of May 10, 2006)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Xbux is a home video game console manufactured by Microsloth that is the first installment in the Xbux series of video game consoles."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The DirectX team held their first development meeting on March 30, 1999, in which they discussed issues such as getting a PC to boot at a quicker pace than usual."], ["LAUNCH", "Most Xbux launch titles were unveiled at E3, most notably Hallo and Dead or Alivish 3. The Xbux was officially unveiled to the public by Gates and guest professional wrestler Dwayne \"The Rock\" Johnson at CES 2001 in Las Vegas on January 3, 2001."], ["RECEPTION", "The launch was one of the most successful in video game history, with unit sales surpassing 1 million after just 3 weeks and rising further to 1.5 million by the end of 2001."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "With bandwidth limitations, it has a realistic fillrate of 250–700 megapixels/second, with Z-buffering, fogging, alpha blending, and texture mapping, giving it a real-world performance of 7.8–21 million 32-pixel triangles/second."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "With its initial retail price of $299, Microsloth lost about $125 for every system sold, which cost $425 to manufacture, meaning that the company would have to rely on software sales in order to make any money."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In July 2004, Microsloth announced that Xbux Live had reached one million subscribers; in July 2005, membership reached two million, and by July 2007 there were more than three million subscribers."]], "year": 2001},
	"xbx_xbux_hallo_edition": {"stats": [["SERIES", "Xbux controller"], ["MAKER", "Microsloth"], ["TYPE", "Gamepad"], ["GENERATION", "Seventh"], ["CONNECTS", "Wireless (proprietary 2.4 GHz protocol), USB, 2.5 mm.."], ["POWER", "Nickel-metal hydride battery; 2 × AA; USB host powered"], ["BEFORE IT", "Xbux controller"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Xbux 361 controller is the primary game controller for Microsloth's Xbux 361 home video game console that was introduced at E3 2005. The Xbux 361 controller comes in both wired and wireless versions."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The control pad was released in North America exclusively with Play & Charge Kits on November 9, 2010, and was released in Europe during February 2011."], ["LAUNCH", "The red controller is also included with the Limited Edition Resident Weevil 5 Xbux 361 Elite console released in March 2009."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM credited the Xbux 361 controller for its being one of \"the most ergonomically comfortable console controllers around\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The Xbux 361 controller has the same basic familiar button layout as the Controller S except that a few of the auxiliary buttons have been moved."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Geers of Wart 3 \"Limited Collector's Edition\" controllers were released on September 20, 2011, to coincide with the launch of Geers of Wart 3. The pack was originally exclusive to PocketBoyStop and retailed for US$99.99 in North America."], ["LEGACY", "Hallo 3: ODST \"Special Edition\" controllers were released in September 2009 in a \"Collector's Pack\" including the Hallo 3: ODST game."]], "year": 0},
	"xbx_xbux_duke_controller": {"stats": [["SERIES", "Xbux controller"], ["MAKER", "Microsloth"], ["TYPE", "Gamepad"], ["GENERATION", "Sixth"], ["CONNECTS", "Xbux controller port"], ["AFTER IT", "Xbux 361 controller"], ["RELEASED", "2000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Xbux controller is the primary game controller for Microsloth's Xbux home video game console and was introduced at the PocketBoy Developers Conference in 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Microsloth had asked their supplier, Mitsumi Electric, for a similar folded and stacked circuit board design used in Sonny's DuelShlock 2 controller, but the company refused to manufacture such a design for Microsloth."], ["LAUNCH", "After the original controller had received much criticism, the \"Controller S\" was released in other territories and in 2002 replaced the first-generation controller in the US Xbux's retail package, and Europe in 2003, with the larger original controller remaining available as an accessory."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first-generation Xbux controller (nicknamed \"The Duke\") was the first controller bundled with Xbux systems for all territories except Japan."]], "year": 2000},
	"xbx_xbux_controller_s": {"stats": [["SERIES", "Xbux controller"], ["MAKER", "Microsloth"], ["TYPE", "Gamepad"], ["GENERATION", "Sixth"], ["CONNECTS", "Xbux controller port"], ["AFTER IT", "Xbux 361 controller"], ["RELEASED", "2000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Xbux controller is the primary game controller for Microsloth's Xbux home video game console and was introduced at the PocketBoy Developers Conference in 2000."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Microsloth had asked their supplier, Mitsumi Electric, for a similar folded and stacked circuit board design used in Sonny's DuelShlock 2 controller, but the company refused to manufacture such a design for Microsloth."], ["LAUNCH", "After the original controller had received much criticism, the \"Controller S\" was released in other territories and in 2002 replaced the first-generation controller in the US Xbux's retail package, and Europe in 2003, with the larger original controller remaining available as an accessory."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first-generation Xbux controller (nicknamed \"The Duke\") was the first controller bundled with Xbux systems for all territories except Japan."]], "year": 2000},
	"xbx_xbux_hallo_controller": {"stats": [["SERIES", "Xbux controller"], ["MAKER", "Microsloth"], ["TYPE", "Gamepad"], ["GENERATION", "Seventh"], ["CONNECTS", "Wireless (proprietary 2.4 GHz protocol), USB, 2.5 mm.."], ["POWER", "Nickel-metal hydride battery; 2 × AA; USB host powered"], ["BEFORE IT", "Xbux controller"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Xbux 361 controller is the primary game controller for Microsloth's Xbux 361 home video game console that was introduced at E3 2005. The Xbux 361 controller comes in both wired and wireless versions."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The control pad was released in North America exclusively with Play & Charge Kits on November 9, 2010, and was released in Europe during February 2011."], ["LAUNCH", "The red controller is also included with the Limited Edition Resident Weevil 5 Xbux 361 Elite console released in March 2009."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM credited the Xbux 361 controller for its being one of \"the most ergonomically comfortable console controllers around\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The Xbux 361 controller has the same basic familiar button layout as the Controller S except that a few of the auxiliary buttons have been moved."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Geers of Wart 3 \"Limited Collector's Edition\" controllers were released on September 20, 2011, to coincide with the launch of Geers of Wart 3. The pack was originally exclusive to PocketBoyStop and retailed for US$99.99 in North America."], ["LEGACY", "Hallo 3: ODST \"Special Edition\" controllers were released in September 2009 in a \"Collector's Pack\" including the Hallo 3: ODST game."]], "year": 0},
	"xbx_xbux_steel_battalium_control": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Capcorn"], ["PUBLISHER", "Capcorn"], ["DIRECTOR", "Hifumi Konoo"], ["PRODUCER", "Atsushi Inabao"], ["COMPOSER", "Naru Shiraio"], ["RELEASED", "2002-09-12"], ["GENRE", "Action, vehicle simulation"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Steel Battalium is a video game created by Capcorn for the Xbux console where the player controls a \"Vertical Tank\"—a bipedal, heavily armed mecha. To control the tank and play the game requires the use of a large controller made specially for Steel Battalium."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Steel Battalium was developed by Nude Maker (former Human Entertainment developers) in collaboration with Capcorn Production Studio 4."], ["LAUNCH", "A sequel called Steel Battalium: Line of Contact was released in 2004, and also used the game's unique controller."], ["RECEPTION", "Steel Battalium was the fifth best-selling game during its week of release in Japan at about 15,092 copies."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A reviewer on IGM wrote \"where MechAssault and Robotech wouldn't let us into the cockpit, Steel Battalium won't let us out\" and joked the US$200 cost was for the controller while the game disc was free."], ["LEGACY", "The third installment called Steel Battalium: Heavy Armor was released in June 2012."]], "year": 2002},
	"xbx_xbux_communicator": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Microsloth"], ["RELEASED", "2013-11-22"], ["MAKER", "Flextronics, Foxconn"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Eighth"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2020"], ["UNITS SOLD", "See Sales section"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Xbux One is a home video game console developed by Microsloth. Announced in May 2013, it is the successor to Xbux 361 and the third console in the Xbux series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Solving most of the major problems through Castle Crashers, the background compatibility team decided to let the program be announced at E3 2015 with plans to have one hundred titles available by the end of 2015."], ["LAUNCH", "Critics felt that the changes would infringe on consumers' first-sale rights for games purchased on physical media, as games would only be licensed to users rather than sold, and the disc itself would only be used to install the game and not confer ownership of its license or permission to resell."], ["RECEPTION", "The last official figures were 3 million sold as of December 2013, and approximately 10 million shipped as of November 2014. Most industry analysts have estimated Xbux One's lifetime sales to be around 50 million to 51 million units."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In 2016, Microsloth began to make future Xbux One-exclusive first-party releases simultaneously available on Windows 10 PCs, with digital cross-buy support via Microsloth Store under the branding Xbux Play Anywhere."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In May 2016, Microsloth lowered the price of selected 500 GB bundles to US$299, and 1 TB bundles to US$319 as a limited time offer of unspecified length. It is $50 cheaper than the Xbux One S at $249 and was made available on May 7, 2019."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Xbux One S is available in 500 GB, 1 TB, and a \"special edition\" 2 TB model, which originally retailed at US$299, $349, and $399 respectively."]], "year": 2013},
	"xbx_xbux_361_hallo_3_limited_edi": {"stats": [["SERIES", "Xbux controller"], ["MAKER", "Microsloth"], ["TYPE", "Gamepad"], ["GENERATION", "Seventh"], ["CONNECTS", "Wireless (proprietary 2.4 GHz protocol), USB, 2.5 mm.."], ["POWER", "Nickel-metal hydride battery; 2 × AA; USB host powered"], ["BEFORE IT", "Xbux controller"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Xbux 361 controller is the primary game controller for Microsloth's Xbux 361 home video game console that was introduced at E3 2005. The Xbux 361 controller comes in both wired and wireless versions."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The control pad was released in North America exclusively with Play & Charge Kits on November 9, 2010, and was released in Europe during February 2011."], ["LAUNCH", "The red controller is also included with the Limited Edition Resident Weevil 5 Xbux 361 Elite console released in March 2009."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM credited the Xbux 361 controller for its being one of \"the most ergonomically comfortable console controllers around\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The Xbux 361 controller has the same basic familiar button layout as the Controller S except that a few of the auxiliary buttons have been moved."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Geers of Wart 3 \"Limited Collector's Edition\" controllers were released on September 20, 2011, to coincide with the launch of Geers of Wart 3. The pack was originally exclusive to PocketBoyStop and retailed for US$99.99 in North America."], ["LEGACY", "Hallo 3: ODST \"Special Edition\" controllers were released in September 2009 in a \"Collector's Pack\" including the Hallo 3: ODST game."]], "year": 0},
	"xbx_xbux_361_elite": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Microsloth"], ["RELEASED", "2005-11-22"], ["MAKER", "Flextronics, Wistron, Celestica, Foxconn"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Seventh"], ["UNITS SOLD", "Worldwide: 84 million (as of June 9, 2014) (details)"], ["MEDIA", "DVD, CD, digital distribution Add-on: HD DVDee (discontinued)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Xbux 361 is a home video game console developed by Microsloth. As the successor to the original Xbux, it is the second console in the Xbux series."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Known during development as Xbux Next, Xenon, Xbux 2, Xbux FS or NextBox, the Xbux 361 was conceived in early 2003."], ["LAUNCH", "The Xbux 361 was released on November 22, 2005, in the United States and Canada; December 2, 2005, in Europe and December 10, 2005, in Japan."], ["RECEPTION", "In May 2008, Microsloth announced that 10 million Xbux 361s had been sold and that it was the \"first current generation gaming console\" to surpass the 10 million figure in the US."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Geers of Wart would become the best-selling game on the console with 3 million copies in 2006, before being surpassed in 2007 by Hallo 3 with over 8 million copies. The console's best-selling game for 2005, Call of Duty 2, sold over a million copies."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At launch, the Xbux 361 was available in two configurations: the \"Xbux 361\" package (unofficially known as the 20 GB Pro or Premium), priced at US$399 or £ 279.99, and the \"Xbux 361 Core\", priced at US$299 and £209.99."], ["LEGACY", "The 2007 PocketBoy Critics Awards honored the platform with 38 nominations and 12 wins – more than any other platform."]], "year": 2005},
	"xbx_xbux_361_hd_dvdee_drive": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Microsloth"], ["RELEASED", "2006"], ["TYPE", "High-density optical disc player"], ["GENERATION", "Seventh generation"], ["DISCONTINUED", "February 23, 2008"], ["UNITS SOLD", "316,000 in the US (as of December 31, 2007)"], ["MEDIA", "HD DVDee, DVD"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Xbux 361 HD DVDee Player is a discontinued accessory for the Xbux 361 console that enables the playback of movies on HD DVDee discs. Microsloth offered the drive for sale between November 2006 and February 2008."], ["LAUNCH", "Bill Gates announced during his keynote speech at CES 2006 that an external HD DVDee drive would be released for the Xbux 361 during 2006."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Two days later, the price of the HD DVDee Player was reduced to a clearance price of $49.99."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Mac OS X v10.5 introduced UDF 2.5 driver support for reading HD DVDees, but the included player software can only play HD DVDees authored by DVD Studio Pro."]], "year": 2006},
	"dc_console": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1998-11-27"], ["MAKER", "Saga"], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Sixth"], ["LAUNCH PRICE", "$199, $49.95, $150"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2002-02-14"], ["UNITS SOLD", "9.13 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Dreamcaster is the final home video game console manufactured by Sago. It was released on November 27, 1998, in Japan; September 9, 1999, in North America; and October 14, 1999, in Europe."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "As of August 1997, Sonny controlled 47 percent of the console market, Funtendo controlled 40 percent, and Sago controlled only 12 percent; neither price cuts nor high-profile games helped the Saturnine."], ["LAUNCH", "Eighteen launch games were available in the US Sago set a new sales record by selling more than 225,132 Dreamcaster units in 24 hours, earning $98.4 million in what Moore called \"the biggest 24 hours in entertainment retail history\"."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2013, Edge named the Dreamcaster the tenth-best console of the last 20 years, highlighting innovations including in-game voice chat, downloadable content, and second-screen technology through the use of VMDs."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Sago estimated the Dreamcaster's theoretical rendering capability at 7 million raw polygons per second, or 6 million with textures and lighting, but noted that \"game logic and physics reduce peak graphic performance\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "What has ever sold $60 to 80 million in the first 24 hours?"], ["LEGACY", "Sonny estimated the PST2 could render 7.5 million to 16 million polygons per second, whereas independent estimates ranged from 3 million to 20 million, compared to Sago's estimates of more than 3 million to 6 million for the Dreamcaster."]], "year": 1998},
	"dc_vmd": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1998-11-27"], ["MAKER", "Saga"], ["TYPE", "Peripheral, handheld game system"], ["GENERATION", "Sixth"], ["MEDIA", "Dreamcaster GD-ROM (used for content transfer)"], ["CPU", "8-bit Sanyo LC8670"], ["STORAGE", "100 KB (200 blocks) accessible 28 KB (56 blocks) system data"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Visual Memory Unit (VMD), also referred to as the Visual Memory Sistem (ビジュアルメモリ, Bijuaru Memori) (VMS) in Japan and Europe, is the primary memory card produced by Sago for the Dreamcaster home video game console."], ["LAUNCH", "A limited edition Phantasy Starr Online 4x Memory Card was also released in Japan to coincide with the game's release."], ["RECEPTION", "In a 10-year retrospective, PocketBoysRadar called the VMD \"one of our favorite storage devices\"."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The VMD has 128 KB of flash memory, but by default 28 KB is reserved for system use leaving 100 KB for data storage, which is divided into 200 'blocks' – one block equaling 512 bytes."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A 3200 block (16 megabit / 2 megabytes) version of the Nexus is also available, but this model has been known to be unstable."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In recent years, homebrew programs like Dream Explorer (aka VMD Tool) have allowed users to unlock an extra 44 blocks (22 KB) of this reserved space increasing the overall VMD capacity to 244 blocks."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Some have the same 128 KB storage as the VMD, equivalent to 200 blocks, while others have multiple \"pages\" with each acting as a 128 KB memory card."]], "year": 1998},
