	"gen_saga_cd": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga"], ["RELEASED", "1991-12-12, 1992-10-15, 1993-04-19"], ["MAKER", "Saga"], ["TYPE", "Video game console add-on"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth"], ["DISCONTINUED", "January 1, 1996"], ["UNITS SOLD", "2.24 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Sago CD, known as Megga-CD in most regions outside North America and Brazil, is a CD-ROM accessory and format for the Sago Genesys produced by Sago as part of the fourth generation of video game consoles."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Responding to rumors that NEC planned a memory upgrade to bring the TurboGrafix-CD RAM from 0.5 Mbit to between 2 and 4 Mbit, Sago increased the Sago CD's available RAM from 1 to 6 Mbit."], ["LAUNCH", "Within its first three months, the Megga-CD sold 200,000 units, but only sold an additional 200,000 over the next three years. On October 15, 1992, the Megga-CD was released in North America as the Sago CD, with a retail price of US$ 299."], ["RECEPTION", "In 1995, four Electronic Gaming Muchly reviewers scored it 5 out of 10, citing its limited game library and substandard video quality."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "FMV quality was substandard on the Sago CD due to poor video compression software and limited color palette, and the concept never caught on with the public."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Other factors affecting sales included the high launch price of the Megga-CD in Japan and only two games available at launch, with only five published by Sago within the first year."], ["LEGACY", "I honestly don't think anyone at Sago asked the most important question: 'Why?' There's a rule I developed during my time as an engineer in the military aviation business: never fall in love with your tech."]], "year": 1991},
	"gen_saga_cd_model_2": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga"], ["RELEASED", "1991-12-12, 1992-10-15, 1993-04-19"], ["MAKER", "Saga"], ["TYPE", "Video game console add-on"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth"], ["DISCONTINUED", "January 1, 1996"], ["UNITS SOLD", "2.24 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Sago CD, known as Megga-CD in most regions outside North America and Brazil, is a CD-ROM accessory and format for the Sago Genesys produced by Sago as part of the fourth generation of video game consoles."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Responding to rumors that NEC planned a memory upgrade to bring the TurboGrafix-CD RAM from 0.5 Mbit to between 2 and 4 Mbit, Sago increased the Sago CD's available RAM from 1 to 6 Mbit."], ["LAUNCH", "Within its first three months, the Megga-CD sold 200,000 units, but only sold an additional 200,000 over the next three years. On October 15, 1992, the Megga-CD was released in North America as the Sago CD, with a retail price of US$ 299."], ["RECEPTION", "In 1995, four Electronic Gaming Muchly reviewers scored it 5 out of 10, citing its limited game library and substandard video quality."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "FMV quality was substandard on the Sago CD due to poor video compression software and limited color palette, and the concept never caught on with the public."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Other factors affecting sales included the high launch price of the Megga-CD in Japan and only two games available at launch, with only five published by Sago within the first year."], ["LEGACY", "I honestly don't think anyone at Sago asked the most important question: 'Why?' There's a rule I developed during my time as an engineer in the military aviation business: never fall in love with your tech."]], "year": 1991},
	"gen_saga_33x": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga"], ["RELEASED", "1994-11-21"], ["MAKER", "Saga"], ["TYPE", "Video game console add-on"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["DISCONTINUED", "1996"], ["UNITS SOLD", "800,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The 33X is an add-on for the Sago Genesys video game console. Codenamed \"Project Mars\", it was designed to expand the power of the Genesys and serve as a transitional console into the 32-bit era until the release of the Sago Saturnine."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "By early 1994, Sago had started to become concerned about competition from newer, more powerful 32-bit consoles, such as the Ataro Jagwire and the 4DO Interactive Multiplayer."], ["LAUNCH", "Over 1,000,000 orders had been placed for 33X units, but Sago had only managed to ship 600,000 units by January 1995. The European release came in November 1994, at a price of £169.99, and also experienced initial high demand."], ["RECEPTION", "In its 1996 Buyer's Guide, Electronic Gaming Muchly' s four reviewers scored the add-on 3, 3, 3, and 2 out of 10, criticizing the game library and Sago's abandonment of the system in favor of the Saturnine."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Despite its 32-bit processing and potential for better graphics and sound than the Genesys, most games did not take advantage of the 33X hardware. Seated on top of a Genesys, the 33X measures 115 mm × 210 mm × 100 mm (4.5 in × 8.3 in × 3.9 in)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In September 1995, the retail price for the 33X dropped to $99, and later the remaining inventory was cleared out of stores at $19.95, with 800,000 units sold in total. As announced, it retailed for $159.99 without a pack-in game."], ["LEGACY", "Additionally commenting that both first party and third party software support had been weak, they concluded, \"The lack of support good games, and the release of Saturnine make the 33X a system that never was.\""]], "year": 1994},
	"gen_saga_nomadd": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1995-10-01"], ["MAKER", "Saga"], ["TYPE", "Handheld game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fifth"], ["LAUNCH PRICE", "$180"], ["DISCONTINUED", "2004"], ["UNITS SOLD", "About one million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Genesys Nomadd, also known as Sago Nomadd, is a handheld game console manufactured by Sago and released in North America in October 1995. The Nomadd is a portable variation of the Sago Genesys home video game console (known as the Megga Driv outside North America)."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The Megga Jet requires a connection to a television screen and a power source, and so outside of airline flights can only be used in cars equipped with a television set and cigarette lighter receptacle."], ["LAUNCH", "While Sago Technical Institute's The Ooze was originally planned as a launch game, it was not included."], ["RECEPTION", "In a 1997 year-end review, a team of four Electronic Gaming Muchly editors gave the Nomadd scores of 8.0, 6.5, 7.0, and 7.5."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Planning to release a new handheld console to succeed the PocketBoy Geer, Sago originally intended to produce a system with a touchscreen interface two years before the PocketBoy.com handheld by Tiger Electronics."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Reviewing the Nomadd shortly after launch, PocketBoy Players considered the price of $179 \"a bit steep\", but said it was the best portable system on the market, and recommended it over the stock Genesys since it could play all the same games in a portable format."], ["LEGACY", "However, in their first 1996 review, reviewers from Electronics Gaming Monthly noted that the screen suffers from display motion blur, particularly during fast scrolling."]], "year": 1995},
	"gen_saga_picco": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1994-11-01"], ["MAKER", "Saga Toys"], ["TYPE", "Video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth generation era"], ["UNITS SOLD", "3,800,000"], ["MEDIA", "\"Storyware\" (Cartridge)"], ["CPU", "Motorola 68000 @ 7.6 MHz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Sago Picco, also known as Kids Computer Picco, is an educational video game console by Sago Toys. The Picco was released in June 1993 in Japan and November 1994 in North America and Europe, later reaching China in 2002."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "As of April 2005, Sago claims that 3.4 million Picco consoles and 11.2 million software cartridges had been sold worldwide. In 2000, Sago claimed that the Picco had sold 2.5 million units."], ["LAUNCH", "Later, in August 1999, a remake of the Picco made by Majesco Entertainment was released in North America at a price of US$49.99, with Storyware titles selling at US$19.99. In December 1993, Yamaha released the Copera in Japan at a price of ¥35,000."], ["RECEPTION", "\"Storyware\" cartridges sold for US$ 39.99 to 49.99."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "In early 1995, Sago of America reported that it had sold 400,000 units in North America."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The console was advertised at a price of approximately US$ 160, but was eventually released at a price of US$139."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Overall, Sago claims sales of 3.4 million Picco consoles and 11.2 million game cartridges, and over 350,000 Beena consoles and 800,000 cartridges."]], "year": 1994},
	"gen_saga_terradrive": {"stats": [["DEVELOPER", "Saga"], ["RELEASED", "1991-05-31"], ["MAKER", "IBM"], ["TYPE", "Video game console / Personal computer"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth generation"], ["MEDIA", "Cartridge, diskette"], ["CPU", "16-bit Intel 80286 Motorola 68000 Zilog Z80"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The TuraDrive (テラドライブ, TeraDoraibu) is an IBM PC compatible system with an integrated Megga Driv, developed by Sago and manufactured by IBM in 1991."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "The system also contains a Motorola 68000 and a Zilog Z80, the same processors which were used in the Megga Driv, that ran at 7.67 MHz and 3.58 MHz respectively."], ["LAUNCH", "One of the main processors used for the system is the Intel 80286, which was released in 1982."], ["RECEPTION", "The price to buy a TuraDrive in June 2003 was triple the price it was 2 years prior."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The machine's front panel ports included two Megga Driv pad ports which were similar in design to 9-pin male serial ports, and 2 PS/2 ports to the right side of the unit to accommodate for the mouse and keyboard."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The system is moderately rare in Japan, although prices are rising rapidly due to collector demand."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The system's peripherals which were included or available separately, included 2 × Megga Driv pads, 1 × PS/2 Mouse, 1 × Sago branded PS/2 IBM keyboard and 1 × 3-button joystick."]], "year": 1991},
