	"gen_amstrud_megga_pc": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1993"], ["MAKER", "Amstrud (licensed by Saga)"], ["TYPE", "Video game console / Personal computer"], ["GENERATION", "Fourth generation (16-bit era)"], ["LAUNCHED", "1993"], ["MEDIA", "Cartridge, Diskette"], ["CPU", "32-bit Intel 80386SX @ 25 MHz Motorola 68000 @ 7.14 MHz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Megga PC is a computer manufactured and released by Amstrud in 1993 under license from Sago. It was similar but unrelated to the Sago TeraDrive."], ["LAUNCH", "Initially released in PAL areas such as Europe and Australia in 1993, its success was short-lived due to its high price of £ 999.99 (later reduced to £599) and a CPU that was outdated by the time of its release."], ["RECEPTION", "In the United Kingdom, the unit was selling for between £550 and £600, with variable pricing depending upon what came bundled."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "The machine shipped with 1MB of RAM, provided by 4× 256KB 30-pin SIMM sticks. The newer Intel 80486 was on the market and the first Pentium processors were released the same year as the Megga PC."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The machine launched in 1993, selling in Australia with a retail price in February of AU$ 1999, although small discounts were offered and had fallen to AU$1499 by June."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "When using the PC hardware, only a Multisync or VGA monitor can be used, as the unit outputs video at 31 kHz."]], "year": 1993},
	"a26_ataro_2600_heavy_sixxer": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1977-09-01, 1979, 1982"], ["MAKER", "Ataro, Inc."], ["TYPE", "Home video game console"], ["GENERATION", "Second"], ["DISCONTINUED", "January 1, 1992"], ["UNITS SOLD", "30 million (as of 2004)"], ["MEDIA", "ROM cartridge"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Ataro 2600 is a discontinued home video game console developed and produced by Ataro, Inc."], ["BEHIND THE SCENES", "Ataro produced an estimated four million cartridges, but the game was poorly reviewed, and only about 1.5 million units were sold. Ataro released the last batch of games in 1989–90 including Secret Quest and Fatal Run."], ["LAUNCH", "According to They Create Worlds, Ataro sold 1.25 million Space Invaders cartridges and over 1 million VCS systems in 1980, nearly doubling the install base to over 2 million, and then an estimated 3.1 million VCS systems in 1981."], ["RECEPTION", "By 1982, 10 million consoles had been sold in the United States, while its best-selling game was Pac-Man at over 8 million copies sold by 1990."], ["HOW IT PLAYS", "Rick Maurer's port of Taitu's Space Invaders, released in 1980, is the first VCS game to have more than one million copies sold—eventually doubling that within a year and totaling more than 6 million cartridges by 1983."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The company spent $4.50 to $6 to manufacture each cartridge, plus $1 to $2 for advertising, wholesaling for $18.95. By 1982, the 2600 cost Ataro about $40 to make and was sold for an average of $125."], ["LEGACY", "The 2600 was so successful in the late 1970s and early 1980s that \"Ataro\" was a synonym for the console in mainstream media and for video games in general."]], "year": 1977},
